module.exports = [
  // ── Module 185: Building a Tokenizer from Scratch ──
  ...set(m185, l185_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Why Byte-Level Tokenization Guarantees No Unknown Tokens',
      titleKn: 'Byte-Level Tokenization ಯಾವುದೇ Unknown Tokens ಇಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ಏಕೆ ಖಾತ್ರಿಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ',
      problem: "As covered conceptually in an earlier Phase 8 module on subword tokenization, BPE starts with an initial vocabulary and merges frequent pairs. A genuinely important design choice for a from-scratch tokenizer is whether that initial vocabulary is built from CHARACTERS (which can still leave truly novel characters unrepresentable) or from raw BYTES (256 possible values, since any file/text is ultimately a sequence of bytes).\n\n1) Explain why starting from a byte-level base vocabulary (as GPT-2's tokenizer does) genuinely guarantees that ANY input text, in any language or containing any emoji/symbol, can always be tokenized without ever needing an [UNK] token -- since every possible piece of text is representable as SOME sequence of bytes, and every one of the 256 possible byte values is in your base vocabulary from the start, why does this make the tokenizer universally applicable in a way a character-level base vocabulary (which must enumerate every character it expects to see) cannot fully guarantee?\n\n2) Explain a genuine complication byte-level tokenization introduces for genuinely multi-byte-encoded scripts (e.g. Kannada text encoded in UTF-8, where a single Kannada character can require 3 bytes) -- explain why single raw bytes in the middle of a multi-byte character sequence are not meaningful on their own, and why BPE's merging process must learn to merge these bytes back into meaningful multi-byte units (effectively re-discovering character/subword boundaries) purely from the STATISTICS of which byte sequences co-occur frequently in the training corpus, without being explicitly told anything about UTF-8's encoding rules.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಹಿಂದಿನ Phase 8 module ನಲ್ಲಿ ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ಕವರ್ ಮಾಡಿದಂತೆ, BPE ಒಂದು ಆರಂಭಿಕ vocabulary ಇಂದ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸಿ ಆಗಾಗ್ಗೆ ಬರುವ pairs ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಲೀನಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ಒಂದು tokenizer ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮುಖ್ಯ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ಆಯ್ಕೆಯೆಂದರೆ ಆ ಆರಂಭಿಕ vocabulary CHARACTERS ಇಂದ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿದೆಯೇ ಅಥವಾ raw BYTES ಇಂದಲೇ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು byte-level base vocabulary ಇಂದ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸುವುದೂ ANY input text ಅನ್ನೂ ಎಂದೂ [UNK] token ಅಗತ್ಯವಿಲ್ಲದೆ tokenize ಮಾಡಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ಖಾತ್ರಿಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) byte-level tokenization ನಿಜವಾಗಿ multi-byte-encoded scripts ಗಳಿಗೆ (ಉದಾ UTF-8 ನಲ್ಲಿ encoded ಕನ್ನಡ text) ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಸಂಕೀರ್ಣತೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ, BPE ya merging process ಈ bytes ಅನ್ನೂ ಅರ್ಥಪೂರ್ಣ multi-byte units ಗೆ ಮರಳಿ ವಿಲೀನಗೊಳಿಸಲು ಕಲಿಯಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ಸೇರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Byte-Level BPE Training from Scratch',
      titleKn: 'Byte-Level BPE Training ಅನ್ನೂ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement byte-level BPE tokenizer training entirely from scratch in Python (no tokenizers library -- build the actual merge-learning algorithm yourself).\n\n1) Genuinely implement the core BPE training loop: convert your training corpus into a sequence of byte values (0-255), represent each 'word' as a sequence of byte-symbols with a word-boundary marker, count all adjacent symbol-pair frequencies across the corpus, merge the most frequent pair into a new symbol, and repeat for N merges, storing the ordered list of merge rules.\n\n2) Genuinely train this on a real text corpus (at least 2000 words) for at least 200 merges, and report the resulting vocabulary size and the first 10 learned merge rules.\n\n3) Genuinely implement the corresponding encode function (applying your learned merges in order to a new piece of text) and verify a critical property directly: genuinely tokenize a piece of text containing a word/symbol NOT seen during training (including deliberately testing with a short genuine Kannada phrase, to test the multi-byte-character behavior discussed conceptually in the previous exercise) -- report the actual resulting token sequence, and confirm it never produces an error or unknown-token failure, providing concrete empirical verification of the byte-level guarantee.",
      problemKn: "Python ನಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣವಾಗಿ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ byte-level BPE tokenizer training ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರಮುಖ BPE training loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ನಿಮ್ಮ training corpus ಅನ್ನೂ byte values ya ಒಂದು sequence ಗೆ ಪರಿವರ್ತಿಸಿ, ಎಲ್ಲಾ adjacent symbol-pair frequencies ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ, ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಆಗಾಗ್ಗೆ ಬರುವ pair ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಲೀನಗೊಳಿಸಿ, N merges ಗಾಗಿ ಪುನರಾವರ್ತಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 200 merges ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ text corpus ಮೇಲೆ ಇದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಅನುಗುಣವಾದ encode function ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಕನ್ನಡ ಪದಗುಚ್ಛ ಸೇರಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ ಇದೂ ಎಂದೂ ಒಂದು error ಅಥವಾ unknown-token ವೈಫಲ್ಯ ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Compression Ratio and Comparing Your Tokenizer to a Production One',
      titleKn: 'Compression Ratio ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ನಿಮ್ಮ Tokenizer ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು Production Tokenizer ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely evaluate your from-scratch tokenizer's quality against a real, production-trained tokenizer (e.g. GPT-2's tokenizer via Hugging Face).\n\n1) Genuinely compute the compression ratio (raw bytes divided by resulting token count) your tokenizer achieves on a held-out test text NOT used during training -- report the actual measured ratio.\n\n2) Genuinely compute the same compression ratio using a real pretrained GPT-2 tokenizer on the SAME test text -- report its ratio, and compare the two, discussing whether your tokenizer -- trained on far less data and for far fewer merges -- achieves a meaningfully worse compression ratio, and by how much.\n\n3) Genuinely test both tokenizers specifically on Kannada text (a script GPT-2's tokenizer, trained mostly on English web text, is known to handle poorly) -- report the actual tokens-per-character ratio for both tokenizers on the SAME Kannada test sentence, and discuss whether your OWN from-scratch tokenizer (if you included any Kannada text in your small training corpus) shows a meaningfully BETTER ratio on Kannada than GPT-2's tokenizer does, connecting this concrete, measured result to the earlier Phase 8 module's discussion of tokenization efficiency disparities across languages and why a tokenizer's training-data composition directly determines which languages it represents efficiently.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ from-scratch tokenizer ya ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟವನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ, production-trained tokenizer ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು held-out test text ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ tokenizer ಸಾಧಿಸುವ compression ratio ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n2) ಅದೇ test text ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ pretrained GPT-2 tokenizer ಬಳಸಿ ಅದೇ compression ratio ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ, ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡೂ tokenizers ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಕನ್ನಡ text ಮೇಲೆ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ, ನಿಜ tokens-per-character ratio ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m185, l185_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'WordPiece\'s Likelihood-Based Merge Criterion versus BPE\'s Frequency Count',
      titleKn: 'BPE ya Frequency Count ವಿರುದ್ಧ WordPiece ya Likelihood-Based Merge Criterion',
      problem: "As introduced conceptually in an earlier Phase 8 module, WordPiece differs from BPE in HOW it chooses which pair to merge at each step: BPE simply merges the pair with the highest raw FREQUENCY, while WordPiece merges the pair that most increases the LIKELIHOOD of the training data under a unigram language model built from the current vocabulary (roughly, the pair whose merged form appears more often together than would be expected if the two symbols were independent).\n\n1) Explain concretely why raw frequency and likelihood-increase can favor DIFFERENT pairs: construct a hypothetical example (with made-up but internally consistent counts) where a pair A-B has a HIGH raw frequency (because both A and B are individually extremely common symbols that would co-occur often even by pure chance) but a LOW likelihood-increase score (because their co-occurrence rate isn't much higher than chance would predict), versus a pair C-D with a lower raw frequency but a much higher likelihood-increase score (because C and D almost ALWAYS appear together specifically, well above what their individual frequencies alone would predict) -- explain which pair WordPiece would prefer to merge and why this is arguably a more meaningful signal of genuine subword-unit-hood than raw frequency alone.\n\n2) Explain why this likelihood-based criterion is computationally MORE expensive per merge step than BPE's simple frequency count -- what additional computation (estimating and comparing likelihood under the unigram model, rather than just counting occurrences) does WordPiece's criterion require that BPE's does not.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಹಿಂದಿನ Phase 8 module ನಲ್ಲಿ ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಚಯಿಸಿದಂತೆ, WordPiece ಪ್ರತಿ ಹಂತದಲ್ಲಿ ಯಾವ pair ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಲೀನಗೊಳಿಸಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ಆಯ್ಕೆಮಾಡುವುದರಲ್ಲಿ BPE ಇಂದ ಭಿನ್ನವಾಗಿದೆ: BPE ಕೇವಲ ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿನ raw FREQUENCY ಇರುವ pair ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಲೀನಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದರೆ WordPiece ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ vocabulary ಇಂದ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಒಂದು unigram language model ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ training data ya LIKELIHOOD ಅನ್ನೂ ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿಸುವ pair ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಲೀನಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಕಾಲ್ಪನಿಕ ಉದಾಹರಣೆ ರಚಿಸಿ, raw frequency ಮತ್ತು likelihood-increase ಭಿನ್ನ pairs ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಆದ್ಯತೆ ನೀಡಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ likelihood-based criterion BPE ya ಸರಳ frequency count ಗಿಂತ ಪ್ರತಿ merge ಹಂತಕ್ಕೆ ಏಕೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು computationally ದುಬಾರಿ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing WordPiece\'s Likelihood-Based Merge Scoring',
      titleKn: 'WordPiece ya Likelihood-Based Merge Scoring ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement WordPiece-style merge scoring from scratch, adapting your BPE training loop from the previous lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the WordPiece scoring formula: for each candidate adjacent pair, compute score = freq(pair) / (freq(first_symbol) * freq(second_symbol)) (a simplified pointwise-mutual-information-style approximation of the likelihood-increase criterion), and at each training step, merge the pair with the HIGHEST score rather than the highest raw frequency.\n\n2) Genuinely train this WordPiece-style tokenizer on the SAME training corpus you used for your BPE tokenizer in the previous lesson, for a comparable number of merges.\n\n3) Genuinely compare the actual learned merge sequences and resulting vocabularies between your BPE and WordPiece-style tokenizers on the SAME corpus -- report specific, concrete cases where the two approaches chose to merge DIFFERENT pairs at the same step (due to the frequency-versus-likelihood-ratio difference), and discuss whether the WordPiece-style vocabulary appears to capture more genuinely meaningful subword units (e.g. common prefixes/suffixes/roots) compared to BPE's on your specific small-scale corpus, or whether the practical difference is subtle at this scale.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ BPE training loop ಅನ್ನೂ ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ, ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ WordPiece-style merge scoring ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) WordPiece scoring formula ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ಪ್ರತಿ candidate pair ಗೆ score = freq(pair) / (freq(first_symbol) * freq(second_symbol)) ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ training ಹಂತದಲ್ಲಿ ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿನ score ಇರುವ pair ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಲೀನಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ಅದೇ training corpus ಮೇಲೆ ಈ WordPiece-style tokenizer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ BPE ಮತ್ತು WordPiece-style tokenizers ನಡುವೆ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ merge sequences ಮತ್ತು vocabularies ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing BPE and WordPiece Tokenization Efficiency Across Domains',
      titleKn: 'Domains ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ BPE ಮತ್ತು WordPiece Tokenization Efficiency ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely test whether the BPE-versus-WordPiece difference matters more or less depending on the TEXT DOMAIN being tokenized.\n\n1) Genuinely construct two different held-out test sets: one of ordinary prose text (similar in style to your training corpus), and one of a genuinely DIFFERENT domain your tokenizers were NOT trained on (e.g. source code, or technical/scientific text with unusual vocabulary).\n\n2) Genuinely compute tokens-per-character compression ratio for BOTH your BPE and WordPiece-style tokenizers from the previous lesson, on BOTH test domains.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether the compression-ratio GAP between BPE and WordPiece is larger on the in-domain test set or the out-of-domain one -- discuss a plausible explanation for whichever pattern you observe (e.g. does WordPiece's likelihood-based criterion, which is more sensitive to the STATISTICS of the specific training corpus, show a larger advantage/disadvantage specifically when applied to text unlike what it was trained on?), and connect this investigation to the broader theme (recurring since the Phase 8 tokenization module) that a tokenizer's efficiency is fundamentally tied to how well its TRAINING DATA's statistics match the text it will actually be applied to at deployment time, regardless of which specific merge-selection algorithm (BPE or WordPiece) was used to build it.",
      problemKn: "tokenize ಮಾಡಲಾಗುತ್ತಿರುವ TEXT DOMAIN ಅನ್ನೂ ಅವಲಂಬಿಸಿ BPE-vs-WordPiece ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಅಥವಾ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮುಖ್ಯವಾಗಿದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಎರಡು ಭಿನ್ನ held-out test sets ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ: ಒಂದು ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ prose text, ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಭಿನ್ನ domain (ಉದಾ source code ಅಥವಾ ವೈಜ್ಞಾನಿಕ text).\n\n2) ಎರಡೂ tokenizers ಗಾಗಿ ಎರಡೂ test domains ಮೇಲೆ tokens-per-character compression ratio ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n3) BPE ಮತ್ತು WordPiece ನಡುವಿನ compression-ratio ಅಂತರ in-domain ಮೇಲೆ ದೊಡ್ಡದಾಗಿದೆಯೇ ಅಥವಾ out-of-domain ಮೇಲೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m185, l185_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'SentencePiece\'s Unigram Model: Starting Large and Pruning Down',
      titleKn: 'SentencePiece ya Unigram Model: ದೊಡ್ಡದಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸಿ ಕೆಳಗೆ Pruning ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "As introduced conceptually in an earlier Phase 8 module, SentencePiece's Unigram algorithm works in the OPPOSITE direction from BPE/WordPiece: rather than starting small (individual bytes/characters) and merging UP to a target vocabulary size, Unigram starts with a very LARGE candidate vocabulary (e.g. all substrings up to some length, or all substrings appearing at least a few times) and iteratively PRUNES DOWN to the target size, removing the subword units that contribute least to the training data's likelihood under a probabilistic unigram model at each pruning step.\n\n1) Explain why this start-large-and-prune approach gives Unigram a genuinely different capability that pure bottom-up merging lacks: since a full probabilistic model is maintained over the CURRENT (still large) candidate vocabulary throughout training, explain why Unigram can, for a given piece of text, consider MULTIPLE different possible tokenizations (different ways of splitting the same text into subword units) and choose the one with highest likelihood -- unlike BPE/WordPiece, which apply their LEARNED merge rules in a fixed, deterministic order, producing exactly ONE possible tokenization for any given input text.\n\n2) Explain the practical implication of Unigram's multiple-possible-tokenizations property for a technique called 'subword regularization' (briefly, without needing to implement it here) -- during TRAINING of a downstream model, randomly sampling a different valid tokenization for the same text on different training passes can act as a form of data augmentation, exposing the model to multiple ways the same content could be split into subwords, potentially making the downstream model more robust to tokenization variation than a model trained with BPE/WordPiece's single fixed tokenization per input.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಹಿಂದಿನ Phase 8 module ನಲ್ಲಿ ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಚಯಿಸಿದಂತೆ, SentencePiece ya Unigram algorithm BPE/WordPiece ಗೆ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ದಿಕ್ಕಿನಲ್ಲಿ ಕೆಲಸ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ: ಚಿಕ್ಕದಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸಿ ಒಂದು target vocabulary size ಗೆ ವಿಲೀನಗೊಳಿಸುವ ಬದಲಿಗೆ, Unigram ಒಂದು ಬಹಳ ದೊಡ್ಡ candidate vocabulary ಇಂದ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸಿ target size ಗೆ ಪುನರಾವರ್ತಿತವಾಗಿ PRUNES ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ start-large-and-prune ವಿಧಾನ Unigram ಗೆ ಶುದ್ಧ bottom-up merging ಗೆ ಇಲ್ಲದ ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಭಿನ್ನ ಸಾಮರ್ಥ್ಯ ಏಕೆ ನೀಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) 'subword regularization' ಎಂಬ ತಂತ್ರಕ್ಕೆ Unigram ya multiple-possible-tokenizations ಗುಣದ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Simplified Unigram Tokenizer with Pruning',
      titleKn: 'Pruning ಜೊತೆ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ Unigram Tokenizer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement a simplified Unigram tokenizer training procedure from scratch.\n\n1) Genuinely build an initial LARGE candidate vocabulary from your training corpus (e.g. all substrings of length 1 to 5 that appear at least twice), and genuinely estimate initial unigram probabilities for each candidate subword (based on raw frequency, normalized).\n\n2) Genuinely implement the pruning loop: repeatedly compute, for each candidate subword, how much removing it would DECREASE the training data's overall log-likelihood (approximated by checking how much worse the best available tokenization of the corpus becomes without that subword available), and remove the subword(s) causing the SMALLEST likelihood decrease at each pruning step, until reaching your target vocabulary size.\n\n3) Genuinely implement a simple Viterbi-style decode function that finds the HIGHEST-likelihood tokenization of a new piece of text given your final pruned vocabulary and probabilities -- genuinely test it on a held-out sentence and report the actual resulting tokenization, and genuinely verify the multiple-possible-tokenizations property discussed in the previous exercise by modifying your decode function to return the top-3 highest-likelihood tokenizations (rather than just the single best) for the same input, reporting whether they genuinely differ in how they split the text.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ Unigram tokenizer training procedure ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ training corpus ಇಂದ ಒಂದು ಆರಂಭಿಕ LARGE candidate vocabulary ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ candidate subword ಗೆ ಆರಂಭಿಕ unigram probabilities ಅಂದಾಜು ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) pruning loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ಪ್ರತಿ candidate subword ಗೆ, ಅದನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುವುದೂ likelihood ಎಷ್ಟು ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ, ಚಿಕ್ಕ ಕುಸಿತ ಉಂಟುಮಾಡುವ subwords ಅನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಸರಳ Viterbi-style decode function ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು top-3 highest-likelihood tokenizations ಹಿಂತಿರುಗಿಸಲು ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ, ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Testing Whether Subword Regularization Improves Downstream Robustness',
      titleKn: 'Subword Regularization Downstream Robustness ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely test the subword-regularization claim from the first exercise of this lesson on a small downstream task.\n\n1) Using your Unigram tokenizer from the previous exercise, genuinely build a small text classification dataset (reusing a simple labeled dataset structure from an earlier Phase 8 module) and train TWO versions of a simple classifier: (a) using a SINGLE fixed tokenization per training example (always the highest-likelihood one, like BPE/WordPiece would produce), (b) using a RANDOMLY SAMPLED alternative tokenization (from your top-K decode function) for each training example on each epoch, genuinely implementing subword regularization.\n\n2) Genuinely train both classifier versions on the same small dataset for the same number of epochs.\n\n3) Genuinely evaluate both on a held-out test set, and ALSO genuinely construct a specific robustness test: take test examples and deliberately perturb them slightly (e.g. minor spelling variations or added typos) so their optimal tokenization might shift in ways not seen during training with the fixed-tokenization approach -- report whether the subword-regularization-trained classifier shows measurably better robustness (higher accuracy) on these perturbed test examples compared to the fixed-tokenization classifier, providing concrete empirical evidence for or against the claimed robustness benefit of subword regularization on your specific small-scale setup.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಪಾಠದ ಮೊದಲ exercise ya subword-regularization claim ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ downstream task ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ Unigram tokenizer ಬಳಸಿ, ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ text classification dataset ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಎರಡು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳ classifier ಅನ್ನೂ train ಮಾಡಿ: (a) ಪ್ರತಿ example ಗೆ ಒಂದು SINGLE fixed tokenization ಬಳಸಿ, (b) ಪ್ರತಿ epoch ನಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರತಿ example ಗೆ ಒಂದು RANDOMLY SAMPLED ಪರ್ಯಾಯ tokenization ಬಳಸಿ.\n\n2) ಎರಡೂ classifier ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ ಸಣ್ಣ dataset ಮೇಲೆ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ robustness test ಸೇರಿ ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 186: Building a Production Tokenizer ──
  ...set(m186, l186_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Special Tokens: Reserved Vocabulary Slots with Structural Meaning',
      titleKn: 'Special Tokens: Structural ಅರ್ಥ ಇರುವ Reserved Vocabulary Slots',
      problem: "Production tokenizers reserve specific vocabulary slots for SPECIAL TOKENS with structural, non-linguistic meaning: e.g. <|endoftext|> (marking a document boundary), <pad> (padding shorter sequences to a fixed batch length), and often chat-specific tokens like <|user|>/<|assistant|> markers for instruction-tuned models.\n\n1) Explain why these tokens must be reserved and PROTECTED from ever being produced by the normal BPE/WordPiece merge process on genuine text -- if a document boundary marker like <|endoftext|> could ALSO be accidentally produced by merging ordinary text characters, why would this create a genuine ambiguity/security problem (e.g. could a malicious user craft input text that, when tokenized, accidentally produces a special token, potentially confusing the model about document/conversation boundaries in an exploitable way)?\n\n2) Explain why padding tokens specifically are needed at all -- given that training and inference typically process BATCHES of multiple sequences simultaneously as one tensor (a recurring theme since early PyTorch modules in this curriculum), and different sequences in a batch will naturally have different lengths, why must shorter sequences be extended with padding tokens to make every sequence in the batch the same length, and why must the model's attention mechanism be told (via an attention mask) to IGNORE these padding positions so they don't corrupt genuine computation.",
      problemKn: "Production tokenizers ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ vocabulary slots ಅನ್ನೂ structural, non-linguistic ಅರ್ಥ ಇರುವ SPECIAL TOKENS ಗಾಗಿ ಕಾಯ್ದಿರಿಸುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) ಈ tokens ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ BPE/WordPiece merge process ಇಂದ ಎಂದೂ ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಲ್ಪಡದಂತೆ ಏಕೆ ಕಾಯ್ದಿರಿಸಬೇಕು ಮತ್ತು ರಕ್ಷಿಸಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) padding tokens ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, batches ಮತ್ತು attention masks ಗೆ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುತ್ತಾ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Special Token Protection and Padding',
      titleKn: 'Special Token Protection ಮತ್ತು Padding ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Extend your BPE tokenizer from an earlier lesson in this module with proper special-token handling.\n\n1) Genuinely add reserved special tokens (<|endoftext|>, <pad>, <|user|>, <|assistant|>) to your vocabulary with fixed, protected IDs, and implement a pre-processing step in your encode function that detects these EXACT special-token strings in the input text and encodes them directly to their reserved IDs, BYPASSING the normal BPE merge process entirely for those spans.\n\n2) Genuinely verify the protection works: construct an adversarial test string that contains the literal characters of a special token (e.g. the literal text '<|endoftext|>') embedded within otherwise normal prose, and confirm your tokenizer correctly recognizes and protects it as the special token rather than accidentally BPE-merging its individual characters into something else.\n\n3) Genuinely implement batch padding: given a list of variously-long token sequences, pad them all to the same length using your <pad> token, and genuinely construct the corresponding attention mask (a binary tensor marking which positions are real content versus padding) -- verify your padding and mask construction are correct by checking that mask values genuinely align with the real-versus-padding positions for a few test sequences of different lengths.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಒಂದು ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ BPE tokenizer ಅನ್ನೂ ಸರಿಯಾದ special-token handling ಜೊತೆ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ.\n\n1) reserved special tokens ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ vocabulary ಗೆ ಸ್ಥಿರ, ರಕ್ಷಿತ IDs ಜೊತೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸೇರಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಇವುಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ BPE process ಬೈಪಾಸ್ ಮಾಡಿ ನೇರವಾಗಿ encode ಮಾಡುವ ಒಂದು pre-processing ಹಂತ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು adversarial test string ರಚಿಸಿ protection ಕೆಲಸ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) batch padding ಮತ್ತು attention mask construction ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Security Risk of Unprotected Special Tokens',
      titleKn: 'Unprotected Special Tokens ya Security Risk ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely construct and measure a concrete demonstration of the security concern raised in the first exercise of this lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely build a DELIBERATELY VULNERABLE tokenizer variant: one WITHOUT the special-token protection you implemented in the previous exercise (special tokens are just added to the vocabulary as if they were ordinary learned merges, with no explicit bypass/protection logic).\n\n2) Genuinely construct adversarial input text designed to accidentally produce a special token's exact byte sequence through ordinary BPE merging of surrounding characters (this may require deliberately crafting text with characters positioned to trigger the relevant merges) -- test whether your vulnerable tokenizer can be tricked into emitting a special token ID from within what should be ordinary user-provided text content.\n\n3) Genuinely test the SAME adversarial input against your protected tokenizer from the previous exercise -- report whether the protection correctly prevents the special token from being accidentally emitted, and discuss the concrete downstream risk this vulnerability would create in a real deployed system (e.g. a user's input text accidentally containing what tokenizes to a role-marker token like <|assistant|>, potentially confusing a chat model about who is 'speaking' at that point in the conversation) -- connect this genuine, hands-on security investigation to why real production tokenizers treat special-token protection as a security-relevant implementation detail, not merely a convenience feature.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಪಾಠದ ಮೊದಲ exercise ya ಎತ್ತಿದ security ಕಾಳಜಿಯ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಪ್ರದರ್ಶನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ protection ಇಲ್ಲದ ಒಂದು ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ VULNERABLE tokenizer variant ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ BPE merging ಮೂಲಕ ಒಂದು special token ya ನಿಖರ byte sequence ಅನ್ನೂ ಆಕಸ್ಮಿಕವಾಗಿ ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಲು ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿದ adversarial input text ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಅದೇ adversarial input ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ protected tokenizer ವಿರುದ್ಧ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m186, l186_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Regex-Based Pre-Tokenization: Splitting Before BPE Ever Sees the Text',
      titleKn: 'Regex-Based Pre-Tokenization: BPE Text ನೋಡುವ ಮೊದಲೇ ವಿಭಜಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Production BPE tokenizers (like GPT-2's) apply a REGEX-based pre-tokenization step BEFORE running the learned BPE merges: the raw text is first split into chunks (e.g. separating whitespace-prefixed words, contractions, numbers, and punctuation into distinct pieces) using hand-written regex patterns, and BPE merging is then applied WITHIN each chunk independently (merges never cross a pre-tokenization boundary).\n\n1) Explain why this pre-tokenization step exists at all -- since your from-scratch byte-level BPE from an earlier lesson in this module could in principle learn to merge across whitespace or punctuation boundaries on its own from data, why might a production tokenizer instead want to hand-enforce that merges NEVER cross certain boundaries (e.g. never merging the end of one word with the start of the next, even if they co-occur frequently in the training corpus) -- what genuine benefit does this provide for keeping the resulting tokenization more predictable and interpretable, at the cost of removing some of BPE's data-driven flexibility?\n\n2) Explain a concrete consequence of this design choice for numbers: GPT-2's pre-tokenization regex specifically limits how digits get grouped (e.g. splitting long numbers into chunks of at most 3 digits before BPE merging) -- explain why an UNCONSTRAINED BPE process might otherwise learn highly memorized, individual multi-digit-number tokens that appeared frequently in its training data (e wasteful use of vocabulary slots for numbers that could instead be represented more generally via smaller, reusable digit-group tokens), and why this specific pre-tokenization constraint helps keep numeric representation more systematic and generalizable.",
      problemKn: "Production BPE tokenizers (GPT-2 ya ಯಂತಹ) learned BPE merges ಚಲಾಯಿಸುವ ಮೊದಲು ಒಂದು REGEX-based pre-tokenization ಹಂತ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸುತ್ತವೆ: raw text ಅನ್ನೂ ಮೊದಲು ಕೈಯಾರೆ-ಬರೆದ regex patterns ಬಳಸಿ chunks ಆಗಿ ವಿಭಜಿಸಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ, ಮತ್ತು BPE merging ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ chunk ಒಳಗೆ ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ pre-tokenization ಹಂತ ಏಕೆ ಅಸ್ತಿತ್ವದಲ್ಲಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) numbers ಗಾಗಿ ಈ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ಆಯ್ಕೆಯ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing GPT-2-Style Regex Pre-Tokenization',
      titleKn: 'GPT-2-Style Regex Pre-Tokenization ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement regex-based pre-tokenization and integrate it with your BPE tokenizer from earlier lessons in this module.\n\n1) Genuinely write a regex pattern (similar in spirit to GPT-2's, though you may simplify it) that splits raw text into chunks separating: whitespace-prefixed word-like sequences, contractions, standalone punctuation, and digit groups capped at a small maximum length.\n\n2) Genuinely modify your BPE encode function to apply this pre-tokenization FIRST, then run your learned BPE merges independently within each resulting chunk (never merging across chunk boundaries).\n\n3) Genuinely construct a test sentence containing a long number (e.g. a 10-digit number) and verify your pre-tokenization correctly splits it into smaller digit groups rather than treating it as one long chunk -- report the actual resulting token sequence, and compare it against what your ORIGINAL (no pre-tokenization) BPE tokenizer from an earlier lesson produces for the same number, discussing the observed difference in light of the numeric-representation motivation from the previous exercise.",
      problemKn: "regex-based pre-tokenization ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಈ module ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ BPE tokenizer ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n1) raw text ಅನ್ನೂ chunks ಆಗಿ ವಿಭಜಿಸುವ ಒಂದು regex pattern ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬರೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ BPE encode function ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ pre-tokenization ಮೊದಲು ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಲು ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ದೀರ್ಘ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ ಇರುವ ಒಂದು test ವಾಕ್ಯ ರಚಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ tokenization ಅನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring How Pre-Tokenization Affects Vocabulary Efficiency and Number Generalization',
      titleKn: 'Pre-Tokenization Vocabulary Efficiency ಮತ್ತು Number Generalization ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely quantify the practical benefit of pre-tokenization for numeric generalization, extending your investigation from the previous exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely train TWO versions of your BPE tokenizer on a corpus containing many DIFFERENT specific numbers (e.g. generate synthetic text with many distinct multi-digit numbers, none repeated often) -- one WITH your regex pre-tokenization from the previous exercise, one WITHOUT (your original from an earlier lesson).\n\n2) Genuinely compare the resulting vocabularies: report how many distinct number-related tokens each tokenizer learned, and whether the no-pre-tokenization version wastefully learned many single-use, near-memorized long-number tokens (a sign of poor generalization) while the pre-tokenized version reused a smaller, more general set of digit-group tokens across different numbers.\n\n3) Genuinely test generalization directly: tokenize a BRAND NEW number (not seen during training) with both tokenizers, and report whether the pre-tokenized version represents it using recognizable, reusable digit-group patterns while the non-pre-tokenized version falls back to much finer-grained (less efficient) tokenization for this unfamiliar number -- report your actual measured compression ratio for this novel number under both tokenizers, providing concrete empirical evidence for whether the pre-tokenization design choice genuinely delivers the numeric-generalization benefit discussed conceptually in the first exercise of this lesson.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ತನಿಖೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ, numeric generalization ಗಾಗಿ pre-tokenization ya ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಅನುಕೂಲವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಮಾಣಾತ್ಮಕಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಅನೇಕ ಭಿನ್ನ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ numbers ಇರುವ ಒಂದು corpus ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ BPE tokenizer ya ಎರಡು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ vocabularies ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಹೊಸ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯ ಮೇಲೆ generalization ಅನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m186, l186_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Fast Tokenization: Why Production Systems Need a Rust/C++ Implementation',
      titleKn: 'Fast Tokenization: Production Systems ಗೆ ಒಂದು Rust/C++ Implementation ಏಕೆ ಬೇಕು',
      problem: "Your from-scratch Python BPE implementation from earlier lessons in this module works correctly but is genuinely slow -- Hugging Face's 'fast' tokenizers are implemented in Rust specifically for production speed.\n\n1) Explain why the core BPE encode operation (repeatedly scanning for and applying the highest-priority applicable merge until no more merges apply) is a fundamentally sequential, loop-heavy operation that doesn't parallelize as naturally as, say, a matrix multiplication does -- why does this make raw per-token-processing SPEED (not just algorithmic complexity) a genuine bottleneck for tokenizing large amounts of text quickly, motivating a low-level systems-language implementation rather than relying on Python's interpreter overhead for the inner loop.\n\n2) Explain why tokenization speed specifically matters for LLM training/serving at scale in a way it might not matter for many other ML preprocessing steps -- since a large model consumes potentially trillions of tokens during pretraining, and every single one of those tokens must pass through this encode operation before ever reaching the model, why does even a small per-token speed difference (e.g. microseconds) compound into a genuinely significant total time difference at this scale, in a way that wouldn't matter for a task processing a comparably small dataset.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ from-scratch Python BPE implementation ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ ಕೆಲಸ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಆದರೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿಧಾನ -- Hugging Face ya 'fast' tokenizers production speed ಗಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ Rust ನಲ್ಲಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರಮುಖ BPE encode ಕಾರ್ಯಾಚರಣೆ ಒಂದು ಮೂಲಭೂತವಾಗಿ sequential, loop-heavy ಕಾರ್ಯಾಚರಣೆ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಇದೂ raw per-token-processing SPEED ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ bottleneck ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) tokenization speed LLM training/serving ಗೆ ದೊಡ್ಡ ಪ್ರಮಾಣದಲ್ಲಿ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ಮುಖ್ಯ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Benchmarking Your Pure-Python Tokenizer Against Hugging Face\'s Fast Tokenizer',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Pure-Python Tokenizer ಅನ್ನೂ Hugging Face ya Fast Tokenizer ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬೆಂಚ್ಮಾರ್ಕ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely measure the actual speed gap between your implementation and a production one.\n\n1) Genuinely load a real Hugging Face fast tokenizer (e.g. GPT-2's) and genuinely time how long it takes to tokenize a large text corpus (e.g. 1 million characters) on your machine.\n\n2) Genuinely time your OWN pure-Python BPE encode function from earlier lessons in this module tokenizing the SAME corpus (using your own trained tokenizer, or adapting to use a comparable vocabulary size for fairness).\n\n3) Genuinely report the actual measured speedup factor of the production fast tokenizer over your pure-Python implementation, and discuss whether the magnitude of this gap (likely one or more orders of magnitude) matches or exceeds what the conceptual discussion in the previous exercise would lead you to expect, connecting your concrete measurement to why this speed difference genuinely matters at LLM-training data scale.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ implementation ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು production implementation ನಡುವಿನ ನಿಜ speed ಅಂತರವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ನಿಜ Hugging Face fast tokenizer ಲೋಡ್ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ದೊಡ್ಡ text corpus tokenize ಮಾಡಲು ಎಷ್ಟು ಸಮಯ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ಈ module ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ pure-Python BPE encode function ಗಾಗಿ ಅದೇ ಅಳತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಜ ಅಳೆದ speedup factor ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Optimizing Your Python Tokenizer and Measuring the Improvement',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Python Tokenizer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Optimize ಮಾಡಿ ಸುಧಾರಣೆ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely attempt to close some of the speed gap measured in the previous exercise through standard Python optimization techniques, and measure how much they actually help.\n\n1) Genuinely profile your pure-Python BPE encode function (using Python's cProfile or similar) to identify the actual bottleneck operations -- report what fraction of time is spent in which specific parts of your implementation.\n\n2) Genuinely apply at least 2 concrete optimizations targeting your identified bottlenecks (e.g. replacing a linear scan for the highest-priority merge with a priority-queue/heap data structure, caching repeated substring lookups, or using more efficient Python data structures like arrays instead of lists where applicable).\n\n3) Genuinely re-measure your optimized tokenizer's speed on the same benchmark corpus from the previous exercise, and report the actual speedup achieved by your optimizations -- compare this optimized-Python speed against the production Rust-based tokenizer's speed from the previous exercise, and discuss whether even careful Python-level optimization can close most of the gap, or whether a fundamental speed ceiling remains that only a genuinely different implementation language could overcome -- ground this discussion in your own measured before/after numbers rather than assuming optimization results without checking.",
      problemKn: "ಪ್ರಮಾಣಿತ Python optimization techniques ಮೂಲಕ ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ಅಳೆದ speed ಅಂತರವನ್ನೂ ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ ಮುಚ್ಚಲು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಯತ್ನಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅವು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಎಷ್ಟು ಸಹಾಯ ಮಾಡುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ pure-Python BPE encode function ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ profile ಮಾಡಿ ನಿಜ bottleneck ಕಾರ್ಯಾಚರಣೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಗುರುತಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 2 ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ optimizations ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಅದೇ benchmark corpus ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ optimized tokenizer ya speed ಅನ್ನೂ ಮರುಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ speedup ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 187: Data Pipelines for Pre-Training ──
  ...set(m187, l187_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Why Deduplication Matters More Than It Might Seem',
      titleKn: 'Deduplication ಕಂಡಂತಿಗಿಂತ ಏಕೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಮುಖ್ಯ',
      problem: "Large-scale web-crawled pretraining corpora contain substantial DUPLICATE or near-duplicate content (e.g. the same article mirrored on multiple sites, boilerplate text repeated across many pages). Deduplication removes these before training.\n\n1) Explain why training on data with many exact or near-duplicates effectively means the model sees certain specific text MANY more times than the rest of the corpus, in a way that's not intentional or controlled -- why is this concerning for a model's tendency to MEMORIZE specific training examples verbatim (a genuine concern discussed in real LLM research, since memorized duplicated content is disproportionately likely to be reproducible from the trained model) rather than learning more generalizable patterns from a genuinely diverse set of examples?\n\n2) Explain why NEAR-duplicate detection (finding documents that are mostly but not exactly identical, e.g. differing only in a few words or formatting) is a genuinely harder problem than EXACT-duplicate detection (which can be solved by simple hashing) -- why does this motivate techniques like MinHash/locality-sensitive-hashing for approximate similarity detection at the scale of billions of documents, where computing exact pairwise text similarity between every pair of documents would be computationally infeasible.",
      problemKn: "ಬೃಹತ್-ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ web-crawled pretraining corpora ಗಣನೀಯ DUPLICATE ಅಥವಾ near-duplicate content ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿವೆ. Deduplication training ಮೊದಲು ಇವುಗಳನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಅನೇಕ exact ಅಥವಾ near-duplicates ಇರುವ data ಮೇಲೆ training ಮಾಡುವುದೂ model ಗೆ ಕೆಲವು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ text ಅನ್ನೂ ಇತರ corpus ಗಿಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಬಾರಿ ತೋರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಇದೂ MEMORIZATION ಕಾಳಜಿಗೆ ಏಕೆ ಸಂಬಂಧಿಸಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) NEAR-duplicate detection EXACT-duplicate detection ಗಿಂತ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ಕಷ್ಟಕರ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, MinHash/locality-sensitive-hashing techniques ಗೆ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುತ್ತಾ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Exact and Near-Duplicate Detection',
      titleKn: 'Exact ಮತ್ತು Near-Duplicate Detection ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement both exact and near-duplicate detection from scratch on a small text corpus.\n\n1) Genuinely construct a test corpus containing deliberately EXACT duplicates (identical documents appearing more than once), NEAR duplicates (documents differing only slightly, e.g. a few words changed or reformatted), and genuinely UNIQUE documents.\n\n2) Genuinely implement exact-duplicate detection via simple content hashing (hash each document, flag any hash appearing more than once).\n\n3) Genuinely implement a simplified MinHash-based near-duplicate detector: compute a set of shingles (overlapping n-grams) per document, compute MinHash signatures for each document's shingle set, and flag document pairs whose estimated Jaccard similarity (approximated via MinHash signature overlap) exceeds a threshold -- genuinely test this on your corpus and report whether it correctly identifies your deliberately-constructed near-duplicate pairs while correctly leaving your genuinely unique documents unflagged, reporting your actual measured precision/recall on this small, controllable test set.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ text corpus ಮೇಲೆ exact ಮತ್ತು near-duplicate detection ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ EXACT duplicates, NEAR duplicates, ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ UNIQUE documents ಇರುವ ಒಂದು test corpus ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಸರಳ content hashing ಮೂಲಕ exact-duplicate detection ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ MinHash-based near-duplicate detector ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring How Deduplication Threshold Affects Precision and Recall',
      titleKn: 'Deduplication Threshold Precision ಮತ್ತು Recall ಮೇಲೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate the threshold-selection tradeoff for your near-duplicate detector from the previous exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely sweep your MinHash similarity threshold across several values (e.g. very low, moderate, very high) on your test corpus.\n\n2) Genuinely compute precision and recall against your KNOWN ground truth (which pairs are genuinely near-duplicates by your own construction) at each threshold.\n\n3) Genuinely report the actual precision-recall tradeoff curve you observe, and discuss the practical consequence of choosing each extreme: a too-low threshold flags genuinely DIFFERENT documents as duplicates (destroying genuine training data diversity by wrongly removing unique content), while a too-high threshold MISSES genuine near-duplicates (leaving problematic repeated content in the training set) -- recommend a specific threshold based on your own measured tradeoff, and discuss why real large-scale deduplication pipelines (processing billions of documents) must make this same threshold choice carefully, since both over-aggressive and under-aggressive deduplication carry genuine costs for the resulting pretraining corpus's quality.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ near-duplicate detector ಗಾಗಿ threshold-selection tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ test corpus ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ MinHash similarity threshold ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಲವಾರು ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ sweep ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ threshold ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ ತಿಳಿದ ground truth ವಿರುದ್ಧ precision ಮತ್ತು recall ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಜ ಗಮನಿಸಿದ precision-recall tradeoff curve ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ threshold ಶಿಫಾರಸು ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m187, l187_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Quality Filtering: Removing Low-Value Text Before It Reaches the Model',
      titleKn: 'Quality Filtering: Model ತಲುಪುವ ಮೊದಲು Low-Value Text ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Raw web-crawled text contains substantial low-quality content: broken HTML fragments, navigation boilerplate, spam, and machine-generated gibberish. Quality filtering removes such content using heuristic rules (e.g. minimum document length, ratio of alphabetic to non-alphabetic characters, presence of too many repeated lines) before training.\n\n1) Explain why training directly on unfiltered low-quality text could genuinely harm a model's learned language distribution -- if a meaningful fraction of training tokens come from garbled or non-linguistic content, why might the model waste some of its learning capacity fitting to these patterns, or in the worst case, learn to occasionally PRODUCE similarly garbled output, rather than that capacity being spent learning genuinely useful language patterns from higher-quality text?\n\n2) Explain a genuine risk of quality filtering that's too AGGRESSIVE: since heuristic filters are necessarily simple, hand-designed rules (not a nuanced judgment of true quality), explain how an overly strict filter might systematically exclude genuinely valid but UNUSUAL text (e.g. text in a less common language, poetry with unusual formatting, or technical content with many symbols/numbers that trips a 'too many non-alphabetic characters' heuristic) -- why does this create a tension between removing genuine noise and inadvertently reducing the DIVERSITY of legitimate content the model gets to learn from.",
      problemKn: "Raw web-crawled text ಗಣನೀಯ ಕಳಪೆ-ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟದ content ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿದೆ: ಮುರಿದ HTML fragments, navigation boilerplate, spam, ಮತ್ತು machine-generated gibberish. Quality filtering training ಮೊದಲು heuristic rules ಬಳಸಿ ಇಂತಹ content ಅನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) unfiltered ಕಳಪೆ-ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟದ text ಮೇಲೆ ನೇರವಾಗಿ training ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಒಂದು model ya ಕಲಿತ language distribution ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ಹಾನಿ ಮಾಡಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಬಹಳ ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿಯಾದ quality filtering ya ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಅಪಾಯ ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಇದೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಾನ್ಯ ಆದರೆ ಅಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ text ಅನ್ನೂ ವ್ಯವಸ್ಥಿತವಾಗಿ ಹೊರಗಿಡಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ಸೇರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Heuristic Quality Filter and Measuring Its Effect',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Heuristic Quality Filter ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement a heuristic quality filter from scratch and genuinely measure what it removes and keeps.\n\n1) Genuinely construct a small, deliberately mixed-quality corpus: some genuinely high-quality prose, some deliberately garbled/broken text (e.g. random character sequences, excessive repeated lines, HTML fragments), and some genuinely unusual-but-valid text (e.g. a short piece with heavy symbol/number usage, or a short passage in a less common language).\n\n2) Genuinely implement at least 3 heuristic filter rules (minimum document length, alphabetic-character ratio threshold, repeated-line ratio threshold) and apply them to your test corpus.\n\n3) Genuinely report which documents your filter KEEPS and REMOVES, checking this against your own manual judgment of which documents genuinely deserved to be kept -- report whether your filter correctly removes the deliberately-garbled content while genuinely testing whether it ALSO incorrectly flags any of your unusual-but-valid documents as low-quality, providing concrete empirical evidence for the false-positive risk discussed conceptually in the previous exercise.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ ಒಂದು heuristic quality filter ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಏನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ ಮತ್ತು ಇಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ mixed-quality ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ corpus ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 3 heuristic filter rules ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ test corpus ಗೆ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ filter ಯಾವ documents ಇಡುತ್ತದೆ ಮತ್ತು ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Diversity Cost of Aggressive Quality Filtering',
      titleKn: 'ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿ Quality Filtering ya Diversity Cost ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely quantify the false-positive risk from the previous exercise more systematically.\n\n1) Genuinely construct a LARGER, more diverse test corpus including many different genuinely-valid but unusual document types (technical documentation, poetry, code snippets, tables, short-form social-media-style text).\n\n2) Genuinely apply your quality filter from the previous exercise at several different strictness levels (loose, moderate, strict thresholds on the same heuristic rules).\n\n3) Genuinely measure, at each strictness level, both the fraction of genuinely GARBLED content correctly removed AND the fraction of genuinely VALID-but-unusual content incorrectly removed (false positives) -- report your actual measured tradeoff curve, and recommend a specific strictness level that balances removing genuine noise against preserving legitimate diversity for your specific test corpus, discussing why real large-scale pretraining data pipelines must make and continually refine this same tradeoff decision, often supplementing simple heuristic rules with more sophisticated classifier-based quality scoring specifically to reduce this false-positive cost.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya false-positive ಅಪಾಯವನ್ನೂ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ವ್ಯವಸ್ಥಿತವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಮಾಣಾತ್ಮಕಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಅನೇಕ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ-ಮಾನ್ಯ ಆದರೆ ಅಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ document types ಸೇರಿದ ಒಂದು ದೊಡ್ಡ, ಹೆಚ್ಚು ವೈವಿಧ್ಯಮಯ test corpus ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ quality filter ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ strictness levels ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಪ್ರತಿ strictness level ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜ garbled content ಎಷ್ಟು ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿದೆ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ valid content ಎಷ್ಟು ತಪ್ಪಾಗಿ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m187, l187_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Packing Sequences: Avoiding Wasted Padding at Scale',
      titleKn: 'Sequences Packing ಮಾಡುವುದೂ: ದೊಡ್ಡ ಪ್ರಮಾಣದಲ್ಲಿ ವ್ಯರ್ಥ Padding ತಪ್ಪಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "As covered in an earlier lesson in this module, sequences of different lengths in a batch must be padded to a common length, with an attention mask telling the model to ignore padding positions. For PRE-TRAINING specifically (as opposed to fine-tuning on naturally variable-length examples), a common efficiency technique is SEQUENCE PACKING: instead of padding each individual document to a fixed length, concatenate MULTIPLE documents together (separated by a special end-of-document token) to fill each training sequence to the model's full context length, with little to no padding needed.\n\n1) Explain why padding-based batching can genuinely waste substantial compute at pretraining scale -- if your corpus documents have highly variable lengths (some very short, some near your max context length), and you pad every sequence in a batch to match the LONGEST sequence in that batch, why might a batch containing one very long document force many short documents to be padded with a large fraction of wasted, uninformative padding tokens that still cost compute to process (even though the model learns nothing useful from them)?\n\n2) Explain why packing multiple documents together into one training sequence (rather than one document per sequence with padding) essentially eliminates this waste -- since nearly every token slot in a packed sequence is genuine content from some document, why does this let the model process close to 100% useful tokens per forward pass, compared to a padding-heavy batch where a meaningful fraction of processed positions are pure padding contributing nothing to learning.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಒಂದು ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದಲ್ಲಿ ಕವರ್ ಮಾಡಿದಂತೆ, ಒಂದು batch ನಲ್ಲಿ ಭಿನ್ನ ಉದ್ದದ sequences ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ ಉದ್ದಕ್ಕೆ pad ಮಾಡಬೇಕು. PRE-TRAINING ಗಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ, ಒಂದು ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ ದಕ್ಷತೆಯ technique SEQUENCE PACKING ಆಗಿದೆ: ಪ್ರತಿ ವೈಯಕ್ತಿಕ document ಅನ್ನೂ pad ಮಾಡುವ ಬದಲಿಗೆ, model ya ಪೂರ್ಣ context length ತುಂಬಲು ಅನೇಕ documents ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಟ್ಟಿಗೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n1) padding-based batching pretraining scale ನಲ್ಲಿ ಗಣನೀಯ compute ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ವ್ಯರ್ಥಗೊಳಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಅನೇಕ documents ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಟ್ಟಿಗೆ packing ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಈ ವ್ಯರ್ಥವನ್ನೂ ಮೂಲತಃ ಏಕೆ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Sequence Packing and Measuring the Efficiency Gain',
      titleKn: 'Sequence Packing ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Efficiency ಲಾಭ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement sequence packing from scratch and genuinely measure its efficiency benefit over padding-based batching.\n\n1) Genuinely construct a small corpus of documents with highly VARIABLE lengths (some very short, some close to a chosen max context length).\n\n2) Genuinely implement a padding-based batcher (pad every document to the batch's longest length) and a packing-based batcher (concatenate documents with an end-of-document separator token until each packed sequence reaches your max context length, splitting documents across sequence boundaries when necessary).\n\n3) Genuinely compute, for both batching strategies on the same corpus, the fraction of total processed token positions that are genuine content versus padding/waste -- report the actual measured 'useful token fraction' for both approaches, providing concrete quantitative evidence for the efficiency claim discussed conceptually in the previous exercise, and report how large the gap is specifically for your deliberately variable-length corpus.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ sequence packing ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು padding-based batching ಗಿಂತ ಅದರ ದಕ್ಷತೆಯ ಅನುಕೂಲವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಬಹಳ VARIABLE ಉದ್ದಗಳ documents ya ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ corpus ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು padding-based batcher ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು packing-based batcher ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡೂ batching strategies ಗಾಗಿ, ಒಟ್ಟು ಪ್ರಕ್ರಿಯೆಗೊಳಿಸಿದ token positions ya ಎಷ್ಟು ಭಾಗ ನಿಜ content ಮತ್ತು ಎಷ್ಟು ಭಾಗ padding/ವ್ಯರ್ಥ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Testing Whether Cross-Document Attention Leakage in Packed Sequences Affects Training',
      titleKn: 'Packed Sequences ನಲ್ಲಿ Cross-Document Attention Leakage Training ಮೇಲೆ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Sequence packing introduces a subtle correctness concern: since multiple UNRELATED documents are now concatenated into one training sequence, a naive implementation's causal self-attention would let tokens from one document attend to tokens from a PREVIOUS, entirely unrelated document earlier in the same packed sequence -- genuinely investigate whether this matters.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a 'naive' packing scheme where causal attention operates across the ENTIRE packed sequence without any special handling (tokens can attend across document boundaries within the pack).\n\n2) Genuinely implement a 'correct' packing scheme using a modified attention mask that additionally prevents attention from crossing document boundaries (each document's tokens can only attend to earlier tokens WITHIN the same original document, using your end-of-document separator positions to construct this boundary-respecting mask).\n\n3) Genuinely train small language models (reusing your GPT-style architecture from an earlier Phase 10 module) with BOTH packing schemes on the same packed corpus, and compare their training loss curves and, more importantly, genuinely inspect actual generated text from both models when prompted with the start of a document similar to ones in your training set -- report whether the naive (leaky) packing scheme shows any measurable evidence of confusion/contamination from cross-document attention (e.g. generating content that seems to blend unrelated documents' topics) compared to the boundary-respecting scheme, providing concrete empirical evidence for whether this theoretical correctness concern manifests as a genuine practical problem on your specific small-scale reproduction, or whether it's a smaller effect than the conceptual description might suggest.",
      problemKn: "Sequence packing ಒಂದು ಸೂಕ್ಷ್ಮ correctness ಕಾಳಜಿಯನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: ಅನೇಕ ಅಸಂಬಂಧಿತ documents ಈಗ ಒಂದು training sequence ಗೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಲ್ಪಟ್ಟಿರುವುದರಿಂದ, ಒಂದು naive implementation ya causal self-attention ಒಂದು document ya tokens ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ packed sequence ನಲ್ಲಿ ಮೊದಲಿನ ಒಂದು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ ಅಸಂಬಂಧಿತ document ya tokens ಗೆ attend ಮಾಡಲು ಬಿಡಬಹುದು. ಇದೂ ಮುಖ್ಯವಾಗಿದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) causal attention ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ packed sequence ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಯಾವುದೇ ವಿಶೇಷ handling ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ ಒಂದು 'naive' packing scheme ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) document boundaries ದಾಟುವುದನ್ನೂ ತಡೆಯುವ ಒಂದು ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿದ attention mask ಬಳಸಿ ಒಂದು 'correct' packing scheme ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದೇ packed corpus ಮೇಲೆ BOTH packing schemes ಜೊತೆ ಸಣ್ಣ language models ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 188: Pre-Training a Mini GPT (124M) ──
  ...set(m188, l188_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Learning Rate Warmup: Why You Don\'t Start at Full Speed',
      titleKn: 'Learning Rate Warmup: ಪೂರ್ಣ ವೇಗದಲ್ಲಿ ಏಕೆ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸುವುದಿಲ್ಲ',
      problem: "Production LLM pretraining recipes universally use a learning-rate WARMUP schedule: starting the learning rate at (or near) zero and linearly increasing it to the target peak value over the first several hundred/thousand training steps, before switching to a decay schedule for the rest of training.\n\n1) Explain why beginning training at a randomly-initialized set of weights makes a LARGE learning rate genuinely dangerous at the very start -- since the model's very first gradient estimates (computed from randomly-initialized weights that have learned nothing yet) can be unusually large or poorly-scaled, why might applying a large learning rate to these early, unreliable gradients risk the SAME kind of destructively-large update problem you investigated for PPO in an earlier Phase 12 module, but here at the level of raw parameter updates rather than policy updates specifically?\n\n2) Explain why warmup's specific SLOW ramp-up (rather than an abrupt jump to full learning rate after a fixed number of steps) is preferred -- as the model's weights move away from their random initialization and its own internal statistics (e.g. activation magnitudes) start to stabilize into a more sensible range, why does a gradual increase in learning rate let the model naturally reach a point where a larger learning rate is genuinely safe, rather than forcing a fixed schedule that might apply the full learning rate before the model's internal state has actually stabilized enough to handle it.",
      problemKn: "Production LLM pretraining recipes ಸಾರ್ವತ್ರಿಕವಾಗಿ ಒಂದು learning-rate WARMUP schedule ಬಳಸುತ್ತವೆ: learning rate ಅನ್ನೂ ಶೂನ್ಯದ ಬಳಿ ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸಿ ಮೊದಲ ಹಲವಾರು ನೂರು/ಸಾವಿರ training steps ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ರೇಖೀಯವಾಗಿ ಗರಿಷ್ಠ ಮೌಲ್ಯಕ್ಕೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿಸುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) ಯಾದೃಚ್ಛಿಕವಾಗಿ-initialized weights ya ಒಂದು ಸೆಟ್ ನಲ್ಲಿ training ಪ್ರಾರಂಭಿಸುವುದೂ ಒಂದು ದೊಡ್ಡ learning rate ಅನ್ನೂ ಆರಂಭದಲ್ಲಿ ಏಕೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಪಾಯಕಾರಿ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) warmup ya ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ SLOW ramp-up ಏಕೆ ಆದ್ಯತೆ ಪಡೆಯುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Comparing Training With and Without Warmup',
      titleKn: 'Warmup ಜೊತೆ ಮತ್ತು ಇಲ್ಲದೆ Training ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely reproduce the warmup benefit by training a small GPT-style model (reusing your architecture from an earlier Phase 10 module) both with and without a warmup schedule.\n\n1) Genuinely train your mini-GPT WITHOUT warmup (jumping directly to your target peak learning rate from step 1) on a real text corpus, and track the loss curve closely over the first several hundred steps.\n\n2) Genuinely train an identical model WITH a linear warmup schedule (ramping from near-zero to the same target peak learning rate over the first few hundred steps) on the same data.\n\n3) Genuinely compare the two loss curves -- report whether the no-warmup run shows early instability (loss spikes, NaN values, or erratic behavior in the first steps) that the warmup run avoids, and report the actual final loss both runs reach after a fixed total training budget, discussing whether warmup's early caution costs any final performance or whether it's a genuinely free stabilization technique on your specific setup.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಹಿಂದಿನ Phase 10 module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ architecture ಮರುಬಳಸಿ, ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ GPT-style model ಅನ್ನೂ warmup schedule ಜೊತೆ ಮತ್ತು ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ಎರಡೂ train ಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ warmup ya ಅನುಕೂಲವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪುನರುತ್ಪಾದಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ warmup ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಮೊದಲ ಹಲವಾರು ನೂರು steps ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ loss curve ಅನ್ನೂ ಹತ್ತಿರದಿಂದ ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಅದೇ data ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ಒಂದೇ ರೀತಿಯ model ಅನ್ನೂ linear warmup schedule ಜೊತೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡು loss curves ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Sweeping Warmup Length and Measuring Its Interaction with Peak Learning Rate',
      titleKn: 'Warmup Length ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Sweep ಮಾಡಿ Peak Learning Rate ಜೊತೆ ಅದರ ಪರಸ್ಪರ ಕ್ರಿಯೆ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate how warmup LENGTH interacts with the target peak learning rate, extending your investigation from the previous exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely train your mini-GPT at a fixed, aggressive peak learning rate (higher than what worked well in the previous exercise) across several different warmup lengths (e.g. very short, moderate, very long).\n\n2) Genuinely measure and compare early-training stability (using a destructiveness metric similar in spirit to the one you built for PPO in an earlier Phase 12 module: the largest single-step loss spike observed during the first portion of training) across your warmup-length sweep.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether a longer warmup allows you to safely use a HIGHER peak learning rate than a shorter warmup would tolerate without instability -- report your actual measured relationship between warmup length and the maximum peak learning rate that remains stable, and discuss why real large-scale LLM pretraining recipes treat warmup length and peak learning rate as hyperparameters that must be tuned TOGETHER rather than independently, given the genuine interaction your own measurements reveal.",
      problemKn: "warmup LENGTH target peak learning rate ಜೊತೆ ಹೇಗೆ ಸಂವಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ, ಹಿಂದಿನ exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ತನಿಖೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸುತ್ತಾ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ, ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿ peak learning rate ನಲ್ಲಿ ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ warmup lengths ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ warmup-length sweep ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ early-training stability ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆದು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ದೀರ್ಘ warmup ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ warmup ಗಿಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿನ peak learning rate ಅನ್ನೂ ಸುರಕ್ಷಿತವಾಗಿ ಬಳಸಲು ಬಿಡುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m188, l188_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Gradient Clipping: A Safety Net Against Rare Extreme Gradients',
      titleKn: 'Gradient Clipping: ಅಪರೂಪದ Extreme Gradients ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಒಂದು Safety Net',
      problem: "Alongside warmup (from the previous lesson), production pretraining recipes universally apply GRADIENT CLIPPING: capping the total norm of the gradient vector at some maximum value (e.g. 1.0) before applying the optimizer update, rescaling the entire gradient proportionally if its norm exceeds this cap.\n\n1) Explain why even with warmup and a well-tuned learning rate, occasional individual training batches can still produce an unusually large gradient (e.g. due to a particularly unusual or extreme training example in that specific batch) -- why does gradient clipping act as a targeted SAFETY NET specifically for these rare extreme events, rather than being a general substitute for choosing a good learning rate (which handles the TYPICAL case)?\n\n2) Explain why clipping the gradient's NORM (total magnitude, rescaling the whole vector proportionally) is preferred over simply clipping each individual gradient VALUE independently (e.g. capping each parameter's gradient to some fixed range) -- why does norm-based clipping preserve the gradient's overall DIRECTION (which parameters should move relative to each other) while only reducing its overall magnitude, whereas independent per-value clipping could distort the gradient's direction by clipping some large components more aggressively (relative to their original size) than others.",
      problemKn: "warmup (ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠ) ಜೊತೆಗೆ, production pretraining recipes ಸಾರ್ವತ್ರಿಕವಾಗಿ GRADIENT CLIPPING ಅನ್ವಯಿಸುತ್ತವೆ: optimizer update ಅನ್ವಯಿಸುವ ಮೊದಲು gradient vector ya ಒಟ್ಟು norm ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಗರಿಷ್ಠ ಮೌಲ್ಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಕ್ಯಾಪ್ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) warmup ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿ-ಟ್ಯೂನ್ ಮಾಡಿದ learning rate ಜೊತೆಯೂ, ಕೆಲವೊಮ್ಮೆ ವೈಯಕ್ತಿಕ training batches ಇನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಅಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ ದೊಡ್ಡ gradient ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಬಹುದು ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) gradient ya NORM ಅನ್ನೂ clip ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಪ್ರತಿ ವೈಯಕ್ತಿಕ gradient VALUE ಅನ್ನೂ ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ clip ಮಾಡುವುದಕ್ಕಿಂತ ಏಕೆ ಆದ್ಯತೆ ಪಡೆಯುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Gradient Clipping and Verifying It Preserves Direction',
      titleKn: 'Gradient Clipping ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಇದೂ Direction ಸಂರಕ್ಷಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement gradient norm clipping from scratch (without using PyTorch's built-in clip_grad_norm_) and verify it preserves gradient direction as claimed.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the clipping formula: compute the total L2 norm across all parameter gradients, and if it exceeds your chosen max norm, rescale EVERY gradient tensor by the same factor (max_norm / actual_norm) so the total norm exactly equals max_norm.\n\n2) Genuinely verify the direction-preservation property directly: for a specific gradient tensor before and after clipping, compute the cosine similarity between the pre-clip and post-clip versions -- report whether it's exactly 1.0 (perfectly preserved direction, confirming your implementation only rescales magnitude), for several different gradient tensors in your model.\n\n3) Genuinely integrate this into your mini-GPT training loop from the previous lesson, and construct or find a training step that would produce an unusually large gradient (e.g. by using an extreme/adversarial training example) -- report whether your clipping implementation successfully caps the norm at your chosen threshold and whether this prevents a corresponding destructive loss spike, compared to the same training step run without clipping.",
      problemKn: "ಮೊದಲಿನಿಂದ gradient norm clipping ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದೂ ಹೇಳಿಕೊಂಡಂತೆ gradient direction ಸಂರಕ್ಷಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n1) clipping formula ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) direction-preservation property ಅನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಇದನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT training loop ಗೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಅಸಾಮಾನ್ಯ ದೊಡ್ಡ gradient ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುವ ಒಂದು training step ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Sweeping the Clip Threshold and Measuring Its Effect on Training Stability and Speed',
      titleKn: 'Clip Threshold ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Sweep ಮಾಡಿ Training Stability ಮತ್ತು Speed ಮೇಲಿನ ಅದರ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate the clip-threshold tradeoff, similar in spirit to the PPO clip-range investigation from an earlier Phase 12 module.\n\n1) Genuinely train your mini-GPT with gradient clipping at several different max-norm thresholds (e.g. very tight, moderate, very loose/effectively disabled) at an aggressive learning rate.\n\n2) Genuinely measure training stability (largest observed loss spike) and final training loss after a fixed budget, at each threshold.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether a too-tight threshold measurably slows convergence (since even legitimate, large-but-useful gradients get artificially shrunk) while a too-loose threshold fails to prevent destabilizing spikes -- report your genuine measured sweet-spot threshold for your specific setup, and discuss why gradient clipping's threshold, like PPO's clip range, is a genuine stability-versus-speed tradeoff requiring empirical tuning rather than a single universally-correct default value, even though a specific value (like 1.0) is commonly cited in the literature as a reasonable starting point.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಹಿಂದಿನ Phase 12 module ya PPO clip-range ತನಿಖೆಗೆ ಹೋಲುತ್ತಾ, clip-threshold tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ಭಿನ್ನ max-norm thresholds ನಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದು ಆಕ್ರಮಣಕಾರಿ learning rate ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ threshold ನಲ್ಲಿ training stability ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ budget ನಂತರ ಅಂತಿಮ training loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ setup ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಅಳೆದ sweet-spot threshold ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m188, l188_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Checkpointing: Why You Must Be Able to Resume Long Training Runs',
      titleKn: 'Checkpointing: ದೀರ್ಘ Training Runs ಅನ್ನೂ Resume ಮಾಡಲು ಸಾಧ್ಯವಾಗಬೇಕು ಏಕೆ',
      problem: "Pretraining a real LLM (even a small 124M-parameter one) takes far longer than any single uninterrupted compute session is likely to run reliably -- hardware failures, scheduled maintenance, or simply needing to pause and resume work all require the ability to save the FULL training state periodically and resume EXACTLY where training left off.\n\n1) Explain why a genuinely correct checkpoint must save more than just the model's weights -- explain why the OPTIMIZER's internal state (e.g. Adam's per-parameter running averages of gradient and squared-gradient, accumulated over all previous training steps) must ALSO be saved and restored, and what would go wrong if you resumed training from saved weights alone but with a freshly-reinitialized optimizer (the optimizer's momentum-like accumulated statistics would be lost, causing training dynamics to behave differently right after resuming than if training had never been interrupted at all).\n\n2) Explain why the exact TRAINING STEP COUNT and current position in the data pipeline (which batch of data you were about to process) must also be part of a correct checkpoint -- why would resuming from the wrong data position risk either skipping some training data entirely or reprocessing already-seen data, subtly changing what the model actually gets trained on compared to an uninterrupted run.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ನಿಜ LLM ಅನ್ನೂ pretraining ಮಾಡುವುದೂ (ಒಂದು ಸಣ್ಣ 124M-parameter ಒಂದೂ) ಯಾವುದೇ single ಅಡ್ಡಿಪಡಿಸದ compute session ಗಿಂತ ಬಹಳ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಸಮಯ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆ -- hardware failures, ನಿಗದಿತ ನಿರ್ವಹಣೆ, ಅಥವಾ ಕೇವಲ ಕೆಲಸ ವಿರಾಮಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮುಂದುವರಿಸಬೇಕಾದ ಅಗತ್ಯ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಪೂರ್ಣ training state ಅನ್ನೂ ಆವರ್ತಕವಾಗಿ ಉಳಿಸಿ ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಎಲ್ಲಿ ಬಿಟ್ಟಿತ್ತೋ ಅಲ್ಲಿಂದ ಮುಂದುವರಿಸುವ ಸಾಮರ್ಥ್ಯ ಬೇಡುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸರಿಯಾದ checkpoint ಕೇವಲ model ya weights ಗಿಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಉಳಿಸಬೇಕು ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, OPTIMIZER ya ಆಂತರಿಕ state ಸೇರಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಖರ TRAINING STEP COUNT ಮತ್ತು data pipeline ನಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ ಸ್ಥಾನ ಸಹ ಒಂದು ಸರಿಯಾದ checkpoint ya ಭಾಗವಾಗಿರಬೇಕು ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Full Checkpoint Save/Resume and Verifying Exact Continuity',
      titleKn: 'ಪೂರ್ಣ Checkpoint Save/Resume ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ನಿಖರ Continuity ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Implement complete checkpointing for your mini-GPT training loop from the previous lessons in this module.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a save function that stores model weights, optimizer state, current step count, and current data-pipeline position all together in one checkpoint file.\n\n2) Genuinely implement a resume function that restores all of these components exactly.\n\n3) Genuinely verify EXACT continuity: train your model for N steps, save a checkpoint, continue training for M more steps WITHOUT interruption and record the resulting loss values, then SEPARATELY restart from your saved checkpoint and train for the SAME M steps -- compare the loss values from the interrupted-and-resumed run against the uninterrupted run at each of the M steps, reporting whether they match EXACTLY (or very nearly, up to floating-point nondeterminism) -- report your actual measured comparison, providing a rigorous, quantitative verification that your checkpointing implementation is genuinely correct (produces training behavior indistinguishable from never having stopped) rather than merely appearing to work.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT training loop ಗಾಗಿ ಪೂರ್ಣ checkpointing ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) model weights, optimizer state, ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ step count, ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ data-pipeline ಸ್ಥಾನ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಒಟ್ಟಿಗೆ ಒಂದು checkpoint file ನಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುವ ಒಂದು save function ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಇವೆಲ್ಲವನ್ನೂ ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಮರುಸ್ಥಾಪಿಸುವ ಒಂದು resume function ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಖರ continuity ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Testing Checkpoint Robustness to Missing Optimizer State',
      titleKn: 'Missing Optimizer State ಗೆ Checkpoint Robustness ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely construct and measure the specific failure mode discussed in the first exercise of this lesson -- resuming from a checkpoint with weights but WITHOUT optimizer state.\n\n1) Genuinely train your mini-GPT for N steps and save TWO versions of a checkpoint: a COMPLETE one (weights + optimizer state + step count) and an INCOMPLETE one (weights only, discarding the optimizer's accumulated statistics).\n\n2) Genuinely resume training from BOTH checkpoint versions for the same additional M steps, using a freshly-reinitialized optimizer for the incomplete-checkpoint resume.\n\n3) Genuinely compare the loss curves for the M additional steps between the two resume approaches -- report whether the incomplete-checkpoint resume shows a measurable, temporary disruption in training dynamics (e.g. a brief loss spike or slower progress right after resuming, as the optimizer's momentum-like statistics must be re-accumulated from scratch) compared to the complete-checkpoint resume, providing concrete empirical evidence for why saving full optimizer state -- not just model weights -- is a genuinely necessary part of correct checkpointing, not merely a defensive precaution, quantifying the actual practical cost of getting this wrong.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಪಾಠದ ಮೊದಲ exercise ya ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿದ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ failure mode ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ -- weights ಜೊತೆ ಆದರೆ optimizer state ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ಒಂದು checkpoint ಇಂದ ಮುಂದುವರಿಸುವುದೂ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ N steps ಗಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು checkpoint ya ಎರಡು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ಉಳಿಸಿ: ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ ಒಂದು ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಅಪೂರ್ಣ ಒಂದು.\n\n2) BOTH checkpoint ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳಿಂದ ಅದೇ ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ M steps ಗಾಗಿ training ಮುಂದುವರಿಸಿ.\n\n3) M ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ steps ಗಾಗಿ ಎರಡು resume ವಿಧಾನಗಳ ನಡುವೆ loss curves ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),

  // ── Module 189: Distributed Training: FSDP, DeepSpeed ──
  ...set(m189, l189_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Data Parallelism: Copying the Model, Splitting the Batch',
      titleKn: 'Data Parallelism: Model Copy ಮಾಡುವುದೂ, Batch Split ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "The simplest form of distributed training, Data Parallelism, keeps a FULL COPY of the model on every GPU/device, and splits each training BATCH across the devices (each device processes a different subset of the batch's examples in parallel), then AVERAGES the resulting gradients across all devices before applying a single synchronized parameter update everywhere.\n\n1) Explain why data parallelism requires every device to hold a full copy of the model -- since each device independently computes a forward and backward pass on its own slice of the batch, why does it need the complete set of parameters to do so, rather than just a fraction of them?\n\n2) Explain why this approach's benefit (more total examples processed per unit time, since multiple devices work in parallel) has a genuine LIMIT: since every device must hold a full copy of the model, data parallelism alone cannot help you train a model LARGER than what fits in a single device's memory -- explain why adding more devices under pure data parallelism increases your effective batch size and training throughput, but does absolutely nothing to let you train a model that's too big to fit on one device in the first place, motivating the model-parallelism techniques covered in later lessons of this module.",
      problemKn: "Distributed training ya ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಸರಳ ರೂಪ, Data Parallelism, ಪ್ರತಿ GPU/device ಮೇಲೆ model ya ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ COPY ಇಡುತ್ತದೆ, ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ training BATCH ಅನ್ನೂ devices ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ವಿಭಜಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) data parallelism ಗೆ ಪ್ರತಿ device ಮಾದರಿ ya ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ ಪ್ರತಿ ಹೊಂದಿರಬೇಕು ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ವಿಧಾನ ya ಅನುಕೂಲ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ LIMIT ಹೊಂದಿದೆ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, model-parallelism techniques ಗೆ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುತ್ತಾ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Data Parallelism with Multiple Simulated Devices',
      titleKn: 'ಅನೇಕ Simulated Devices ಜೊತೆ Data Parallelism ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement the core data-parallelism mechanic on your mini-GPT from earlier lessons in this module (using CPU-based simulation of multiple 'devices' if you don't have access to multiple GPUs, to genuinely verify the algorithm's correctness).\n\n1) Genuinely implement a training step that splits a batch into K sub-batches (simulating K devices), independently computes forward and backward passes for each sub-batch using K separate copies of the SAME model weights, and AVERAGES the resulting K gradient sets before applying a single optimizer update to all K copies identically.\n\n2) Genuinely verify correctness: compare the parameter updates produced by your K-way data-parallel simulation against a SINGLE-device training step processing the FULL, unsplit batch directly -- report whether the two produce numerically identical (or very nearly identical) parameter updates, confirming your data-parallel gradient averaging is mathematically equivalent to processing the full batch on one device.\n\n3) Genuinely measure (even in this CPU-simulated setting) whether increasing K while proportionally increasing total batch size lets you process more total examples per unit wall-clock time, reporting your actual measured throughput scaling.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ data-parallelism ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು batch ಅನ್ನೂ K sub-batches ಆಗಿ ವಿಭಜಿಸುವ, ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ forward ಮತ್ತು backward passes ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುವ, ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ K gradient sets ಅನ್ನೂ ಸರಾಸರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವ ಒಂದು training step ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) correctness ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: ನಿಮ್ಮ K-way data-parallel simulation ಅನ್ನೂ ಪೂರ್ಣ batch ಅನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಒಂದೇ device ಮೇಲೆ ಪ್ರಕ್ರಿಯೆಗೊಳಿಸುವುದಕ್ಕೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಥ್ರೂಪುಟ್ scaling ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Communication Overhead in Gradient Averaging',
      titleKn: 'Gradient Averaging ನಲ್ಲಿ Communication Overhead ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate the communication cost that real (multi-machine or multi-GPU) data parallelism must pay to synchronize gradients across devices, which your CPU simulation from the previous exercise likely didn't have to model explicitly.\n\n1) Genuinely measure the actual SIZE (in bytes) of your model's full gradient set that would need to be communicated between devices at every single training step under data parallelism -- report this for your mini-GPT's actual parameter count.\n\n2) Genuinely research (or reason from first principles about) how this communication volume would scale as you increase the NUMBER of parallel devices K -- does the total data that must be communicated per step grow with K, stay constant, or scale in some other way, given that a standard 'all-reduce' gradient-averaging operation typically requires each device to both send and receive gradient data proportional to the model size, with total communication volume that scales with the number of participating devices?\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, based on your calculated gradient-set size and reasoning about communication scaling, why real large-scale data-parallel training becomes increasingly bottlenecked by inter-device COMMUNICATION bandwidth (rather than raw compute) as you add more devices and/or train larger models with correspondingly larger gradient sets -- connecting this genuine bottleneck to why real distributed training systems need specialized high-bandwidth interconnects (not just any collection of networked machines) and communication-efficient algorithms to scale effectively beyond a modest number of devices.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಜ (multi-machine ಅಥವಾ multi-GPU) data parallelism devices ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ gradients synchronize ಮಾಡಲು ಪಾವತಿಸಬೇಕಾದ communication cost ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ training step ನಲ್ಲಿ devices ನಡುವೆ ಸಂವಹಿಸಬೇಕಾದ ನಿಮ್ಮ model ya ಪೂರ್ಣ gradient set ya ನಿಜ SIZE ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n2) parallel devices ya ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ K ಹೆಚ್ಚಾದಂತೆ ಈ communication volume ಹೇಗೆ scale ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಸಂಶೋಧಿಸಿ ಅಥವಾ ಮೂಲಭೂತ ತತ್ವಗಳಿಂದ ತಾರ್ಕಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಜ ದೊಡ್ಡ-ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ data-parallel training ಹೆಚ್ಚು devices ಸೇರಿಸಿದಂತೆ inter-device COMMUNICATION bandwidth ಇಂದ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಬಾಟಲ್‌ನೆಕ್ ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m189, l189_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'FSDP/ZeRO: Sharding the Model Itself, Not Just the Batch',
      titleKn: 'FSDP/ZeRO: Batch ಅಲ್ಲ, Model ಅನ್ನೇ Shard ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Fully Sharded Data Parallel (FSDP, PyTorch's implementation of Microsoft's ZeRO -- Zero Redundancy Optimizer -- idea) fixes data parallelism's core limitation by SHARDING (splitting into non-overlapping pieces) the model's parameters, gradients, AND optimizer states across devices, so no single device ever holds a full copy of everything -- each device only permanently stores its OWN shard, and temporarily gathers the OTHER devices' shards (via communication) only when it actually needs the full parameter set to compute a forward or backward pass for a given layer, then immediately discards those gathered pieces afterward.\n\n1) Explain why this scheme lets you train models LARGER than a single device's memory, when plain data parallelism could not -- specifically, what is each device permanently storing under FSDP versus under plain data parallelism?\n\n2) Explain the fundamental tradeoff FSDP introduces: since parameters must be temporarily gathered via communication every time they're needed for compute, explain why FSDP trades some of data parallelism's low communication overhead for the ability to fit much larger models, and why this makes FSDP training more sensitive to interconnect bandwidth between devices than plain data parallelism.",
      problemKn: "Fully Sharded Data Parallel (FSDP, Microsoft ya ZeRO ಕಲ್ಪನೆಯ PyTorch ಜಾರಿ) model ya parameters, gradients, ಮತ್ತು optimizer states ಅನ್ನೂ devices ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ SHARD (ಅತಿಕ್ರಮಿಸದ ತುಣುಕುಗಳಾಗಿ ವಿಭಜನೆ) ಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ data parallelism ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಮಿತಿಯನ್ನೂ ಸರಿಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ ಯೋಜನೆ single device ya memory ಗಿಂತ ದೊಡ್ಡ models ಅನ್ನೂ train ಮಾಡಲು ಏಕೆ ಅನುಮತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) FSDP ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಭೂತ tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Parameter Sharding and On-Demand Gathering',
      titleKn: 'Parameter Sharding ಮತ್ತು On-Demand Gathering ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified simulation of FSDP-style parameter sharding on your mini-GPT (using multiple CPU processes or simply multiple in-memory 'shard' data structures to simulate devices, since genuinely verifying the sharding LOGIC does not require actual multi-GPU hardware).\n\n1) Genuinely implement a scheme that splits your model's full parameter tensor into K non-overlapping shards (one per simulated device), where each simulated device permanently stores ONLY its own shard (genuinely verify this by measuring the actual memory/array size each device holds, confirming it's roughly 1/K of the full model).\n\n2) Genuinely implement an 'all-gather' step that, when a forward pass needs the FULL parameters for a given layer, temporarily reconstructs the complete parameter tensor by combining all K shards, uses it to compute the forward pass, and then discards the gathered copy (retaining only the original shard) -- genuinely verify that after this gather-compute-discard cycle, each device is back to holding only its 1/K shard.\n\n3) Genuinely verify numerical correctness: confirm that a forward pass computed via this shard-gather-discard mechanic produces IDENTICAL outputs to a forward pass computed with the full, unsharded parameter tensor directly.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ FSDP-ಶೈಲಿಯ parameter sharding ya ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ simulation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ simulated device ಕೇವಲ ತನ್ನ ಸ್ವಂತ shard ಅನ್ನೂ ಶಾಶ್ವತವಾಗಿ ಇಡುವ, model ya ಪೂರ್ಣ parameter tensor ಅನ್ನೂ K ಅತಿಕ್ರಮಿಸದ shards ಆಗಿ ವಿಭಜಿಸುವ ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) forward pass ಗೆ ಪೂರ್ಣ parameters ಬೇಕಾದಾಗ, ಎಲ್ಲಾ K shards ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ ತಾತ್ಕಾಲಿಕವಾಗಿ ಪೂರ್ಣ tensor ಅನ್ನೂ ಮರುನಿರ್ಮಿಸುವ 'all-gather' step ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ನಂತರ ಅದನ್ನೂ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ shard-gather-discard mechanic ಮೂಲಕ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿದ forward pass, ಪೂರ್ಣ unsharded parameter tensor ಜೊತೆ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿದ forward pass ಗೆ IDENTICAL ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ನೀಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Memory-Communication Tradeoff Across ZeRO Stages',
      titleKn: 'ZeRO Stages ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Memory-Communication Tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "ZeRO is typically described in three progressive stages -- Stage 1 shards only the OPTIMIZER STATES (e.g. Adam's momentum and variance buffers, which for Adam are typically 2x the size of the parameters themselves) across devices while keeping full parameter and gradient copies on every device; Stage 2 additionally shards the GRADIENTS; and Stage 3 (full FSDP) additionally shards the PARAMETERS themselves, so at Stage 3 no device permanently holds more than its own 1/K shard of anything.\n\n1) Genuinely calculate, for your mini-GPT's actual parameter count, the total per-device memory footprint (in number of values, or bytes if you assign a dtype) under Stage 1, Stage 2, and Stage 3 for K=4 simulated devices, accounting for parameters, gradients, and Adam's two optimizer-state buffers separately at each stage -- report your calculated numbers for all three stages and confirm the expected trend of decreasing per-device memory as you progress from Stage 1 to Stage 3.\n\n2) Genuinely reason about (or, if feasible, simulate) why each additional stage of sharding, while saving more memory, requires MORE frequent or more complex communication between devices -- explain specifically what new communication step Stage 3 requires that Stage 1 does not, given that Stage 1 already has full parameters on every device (needing no parameter-gathering) while Stage 3 must gather parameters from other devices before every forward and backward computation.\n\n3) Genuinely report which ZeRO stage would be the right choice for a model that comfortably fits on a single device with room to spare, versus one that is right at the edge of a single device's memory capacity, versus one that is far too large to fit even its optimizer states on a single device -- justifying each choice using your calculated memory numbers and reasoning about communication cost.",
      problemKn: "ZeRO ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಶಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ಮೂರು ಪ್ರಗತಿಶೀಲ ಹಂತಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ವಿವರಿಸಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ -- Stage 1 ಕೇವಲ OPTIMIZER STATES ಅನ್ನೂ shard ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ; Stage 2 ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿಯಾಗಿ GRADIENTS ಅನ್ನೂ shard ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ; ಮತ್ತು Stage 3 (ಪೂರ್ಣ FSDP) ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿಯಾಗಿ PARAMETERS ಅನ್ನೂ shard ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya ನಿಜ parameter count ಗೆ, K=4 simulated devices ಗೆ ಪ್ರತಿ ಹಂತದಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರತಿ-device memory footprint ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ sharding ಹಂತ ಏಕೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಆಗಾಗ್ಗೆ ಅಥವಾ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಸಂಕೀರ್ಣ ಸಂವಹನ ಬಯಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತಾರ್ಕಿಸಿ ಅಥವಾ simulate ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಯಾವ ZeRO stage ಯಾವ ಪರಿಸ್ಥಿತಿಗೆ ಸರಿಯಾದ ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ ಲೆಕ್ಕಾಚಾರದ memory numbers ಬಳಸಿ ಸಮರ್ಥಿಸುತ್ತಾ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m189, l189_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'DeepSpeed: Offloading and Overlapping to Push Efficiency Further',
      titleKn: 'DeepSpeed: Efficiency ಅನ್ನೂ ಮತ್ತಷ್ಟು ಹೆಚ್ಚಿಸಲು Offloading ಮತ್ತು Overlapping',
      problem: "DeepSpeed (Microsoft's distributed training library, which implements ZeRO and adds further optimizations) adds techniques beyond plain ZeRO sharding, most notably: (a) CPU/NVMe OFFLOADING, which moves optimizer states or even parameters OFF the GPU entirely and onto (slower but much larger) CPU RAM or disk when they're not immediately needed for compute, trading speed for the ability to train even larger models than GPU memory alone (even with full sharding) would allow; and (b) COMMUNICATION-COMPUTE OVERLAP, which schedules the gathering of NEXT layer's parameters to happen DURING the current layer's compute, so the GPU is never idly waiting for communication to finish.\n\n1) Explain why offloading to CPU/NVMe is a genuine LAST-RESORT technique rather than a free win -- what is the fundamental cost being paid, given that CPU RAM and NVMe storage have dramatically lower bandwidth than GPU memory?\n\n2) Explain why overlapping communication with compute doesn't reduce the TOTAL amount of data that must be communicated, but can still substantially improve training THROUGHPUT -- what specific inefficiency does overlap eliminate?",
      problemKn: "DeepSpeed (Microsoft ya distributed training library, ZeRO ಅನ್ನೂ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ optimizations ಸೇರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ) plain ZeRO sharding ಮೀರಿದ techniques ಸೇರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: (a) CPU/NVMe OFFLOADING, ಇದು optimizer states ಅಥವಾ parameters ಅನ್ನೂ GPU ಇಂದ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣವಾಗಿ ತೆಗೆದು (ನಿಧಾನ ಆದರೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ದೊಡ್ಡ) CPU RAM ಅಥವಾ disk ಗೆ ಸ್ಥಳಾಂತರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ; ಮತ್ತು (b) COMMUNICATION-COMPUTE OVERLAP, ಇದು ಮುಂದಿನ layer ya parameters gather ಮಾಡುವುದನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ layer ya compute ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲೇ ನಡೆಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) CPU/NVMe ಗೆ offloading ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಕೊನೆಯ-ಆಯ್ಕೆ technique ಏಕೆ, ಉಚಿತ ಲಾಭ ಅಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) communication ಅನ್ನೂ compute ಜೊತೆ overlap ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಒಟ್ಟು ಸಂವಹಿಸಬೇಕಾದ data ಪ್ರಮಾಣವನ್ನೂ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುವುದಿಲ್ಲ, ಆದರೆ throughput ಅನ್ನೂ ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ಸುಧಾರಿಸಬಹುದು ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Measuring a CPU-Offload Simulation',
      titleKn: 'CPU-Offload Simulation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified simulation of DeepSpeed-style offloading on your mini-GPT training loop.\n\n1) Genuinely implement two versions of your optimizer-state storage: version A keeps optimizer states (e.g. Adam's momentum/variance buffers) in the same location as your active compute (simulating 'on-GPU' storage even if you're actually just using one CPU array), and version B genuinely moves those same buffers to a SEPARATE, deliberately slower-to-access location (e.g. writing them to and reading them from actual disk files via numpy's save/load or Python's pickle, to genuinely simulate the bandwidth penalty of CPU/NVMe offloading) between each optimizer step.\n\n2) Genuinely measure and report the actual wall-clock time per training step for version A versus version B -- confirm that version B (the offloaded simulation) is measurably slower, and calculate the actual slowdown factor you observed.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, based on your measured slowdown factor, under what circumstances (e.g. model size relative to available fast memory) this measured cost would nonetheless be worth paying.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT training loop ಮೇಲೆ DeepSpeed-ಶೈಲಿಯ offloading ya ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ simulation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) optimizer states ಅನ್ನೂ active compute ya ಅದೇ ಸ್ಥಳದಲ್ಲಿ ಇಡುವ version A ಮತ್ತು ಅದೇ buffers ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ optimizer step ನಡುವೆ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ, ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ ನಿಧಾನ ಸ್ಥಳಕ್ಕೆ (ನಿಜ disk files ಗೆ) ಸ್ಥಳಾಂತರಿಸುವ version B ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) version A ಮತ್ತು B ಗೆ ಪ್ರತಿ training step ya ನಿಜ wall-clock ಸಮಯವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ slowdown factor ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ, ಯಾವ ಸಂದರ್ಭಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಈ ವೆಚ್ಚ ಪಾವತಿಸುವುದೂ ಯೋಗ್ಯ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing End-to-End Throughput Across Parallelism Configurations',
      titleKn: 'Parallelism Configurations ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ End-to-End Throughput ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run a comparison study across the distributed-training techniques covered in this module, using your mini-GPT and CPU-based simulation.\n\n1) Genuinely implement and benchmark at least three configurations: (a) plain data parallelism (full model replicated per simulated device), (b) simulated FSDP-style sharding (parameters/gradients/optimizer states sharded, gathered on demand), and (c) simulated FSDP with CPU offloading of optimizer states added on top.\n\n2) For each configuration, genuinely measure and report: wall-clock time per training step, and peak per-device memory footprint (approximated by tracking the actual size of arrays each simulated device holds at any given time).\n\n3) Genuinely report the actual tradeoff curve you observe -- confirm whether your measurements show the expected pattern of decreasing per-device memory but increasing per-step time as you move from (a) to (c), and discuss, using your OWN genuinely measured numbers (not textbook claims), why real large-model training teams must choose a specific point on this memory-vs-speed tradeoff curve based on their actual available hardware, rather than always defaulting to the most memory-efficient configuration.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ನಲ್ಲಿ ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿರುವ distributed-training techniques ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಒಂದು ಹೋಲಿಕೆ ಅಧ್ಯಯನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ ಮೂರು configurations ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು benchmark ಮಾಡಿ: (a) plain data parallelism, (b) simulated FSDP-ಶೈಲಿಯ sharding, ಮತ್ತು (c) CPU offloading ಸೇರಿಸಿದ simulated FSDP.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ configuration ಗೆ, ಪ್ರತಿ training step ya wall-clock ಸಮಯ ಮತ್ತು peak per-device memory footprint ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಲಾದ numbers ಬಳಸಿ, real large-model training teams ತಮ್ಮ ನಿಜ ಲಭ್ಯ hardware ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ ಈ tradeoff curve ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಬಿಂದುವನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಮಾಡಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m190, l190_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'From Next-Token Predictor to Instruction Follower: What SFT Actually Changes',
      titleKn: 'Next-Token Predictor ಇಂದ Instruction Follower ಗೆ: SFT ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಏನನ್ನೂ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸುತ್ತದೆ',
      problem: "A base (pretrained) language model has only learned to predict the next token over raw internet-scale text -- it has NOT learned to behave like a helpful assistant that follows instructions, answers questions directly, or stops when a response is complete. Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) addresses this by continuing to train the SAME next-token-prediction objective, but on a curated dataset of (instruction, desired-response) pairs, typically formatted with special role markers (e.g. distinguishing a 'user' turn from an 'assistant' turn) so the model learns WHEN to speak and in WHAT style.\n\n1) Explain why SFT does not introduce any new training objective or loss function beyond what pretraining already used -- what specifically changes between pretraining and SFT, if not the fundamental objective?\n\n2) Explain why the QUALITY and FORMAT of the SFT dataset (not just its quantity) is disproportionately important compared to pretraining data -- given that a base model has already learned language and world knowledge from trillions of pretraining tokens, why can a comparatively tiny SFT dataset (sometimes just thousands of examples) so dramatically change the model's surface BEHAVIOR?",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು base (pretrained) language model ಕೇವಲ raw internet-scale text ಮೇಲೆ next token ಊಹಿಸಲು ಕಲಿತಿದೆ -- ಅದು ಸಹಾಯಕವಾಗಿ ಸೂಚನೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಅನುಸರಿಸುವ ಹಾಗೆ ವರ್ತಿಸಲು ಕಲಿತಿಲ್ಲ. Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) ಅದೇ next-token-prediction objective ಅನ್ನೂ ಮುಂದುವರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದರೆ (instruction, desired-response) ಜೋಡಿಗಳ ಕ್ಯುರೇಟೆಡ್ dataset ಮೇಲೆ.\n\n1) SFT ಪೂರ್ವತರಬೇತಿ ಮೀರಿ ಯಾವುದೇ ಹೊಸ training objective ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) SFT dataset ya ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ಮತ್ತು ಸ್ವರೂಪ (ಪ್ರಮಾಣ ಮಾತ್ರವಲ್ಲ) ಏಕೆ ಅಸಮಾನವಾಗಿ ಮುಖ್ಯ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing SFT Loss Masking on Instruction-Response Pairs',
      titleKn: 'Instruction-Response Pairs ಮೇಲೆ SFT Loss Masking ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "A critical, often-overlooked detail of SFT is that the training loss is typically computed ONLY over the response/assistant tokens, NOT over the instruction/prompt tokens -- the model is trained to PREDICT good responses, not to predict (and thus be penalized for failing to predict) the user's own instruction text, which it doesn't need to generate.\n\n1) Genuinely implement this loss-masking scheme on your mini-GPT: construct a small set of (instruction, response) training examples, tokenize each as a single concatenated sequence, and genuinely implement a loss mask that zeros out the cross-entropy loss contribution for every token position belonging to the instruction portion (and any formatting/role tokens), leaving only response-token positions contributing to the loss.\n\n2) Genuinely verify this is implemented correctly: run a forward pass, compute the masked loss, and confirm (e.g. by checking gradient magnitudes or by temporarily disabling the mask and comparing) that gradients ARE flowing to update predictions at response-token positions, while instruction-token positions contribute exactly zero to the loss.\n\n3) Genuinely train your mini-GPT on this masked-loss objective for a small number of steps on a toy instruction dataset, and report whether the model's generated completions after training show ANY qualitative shift toward following the instruction format, even at small scale.",
      problemKn: "SFT ya ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ಣಾಯಕ ವಿವರವೆಂದರೆ training loss ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ ಕೇವಲ response/assistant tokens ಮೇಲೆ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ, instruction/prompt tokens ಮೇಲೆ ಅಲ್ಲ.\n\n1) ಈ loss-masking ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: instruction ಭಾಗಕ್ಕೆ ಸೇರಿದ ಪ್ರತಿ token position ಗೆ cross-entropy loss ಕೊಡುಗೆಯನ್ನೂ zero ಮಾಡುವ loss mask ಅನ್ನೂ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) gradients response-token positions ಗೆ ಹರಿಯುತ್ತಿವೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು toy instruction dataset ಮೇಲೆ ಈ masked-loss objective ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Catastrophic Forgetting and Overfitting Risk in SFT',
      titleKn: 'SFT ನಲ್ಲಿ Catastrophic Forgetting ಮತ್ತು Overfitting ಅಪಾಯವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Because SFT datasets are typically orders of magnitude smaller than pretraining corpora, SFT runs a genuine risk of two distinct failure modes: (a) catastrophic forgetting, where fine-tuning on a narrow instruction dataset degrades the model's general pretrained capabilities (e.g. its broader knowledge or language fluency) because the small SFT dataset dominates gradient updates for too many steps or too high a learning rate; and (b) overfitting, where the model memorizes the specific surface patterns of the small SFT dataset (e.g. always starting responses with a particular phrase) rather than learning the general instruction-following behavior those examples were meant to teach.\n\n1) Genuinely design and run an experiment on your mini-GPT: fine-tune it on a small, narrow instruction dataset for a genuinely EXCESSIVE number of epochs, and measure (using a held-out set of general text, e.g. from your original pretraining corpus) whether the model's loss or perplexity on that general text genuinely WORSENS as SFT training progresses -- report your actual measured numbers, confirming or disconfirming catastrophic forgetting in your specific setup.\n\n2) Genuinely check for overfitting-driven memorization: after SFT training, prompt the model with an instruction genuinely NOT present in your SFT dataset (but similar in style) and report whether its response shows any genuine generalization versus simply regurgitating memorized surface patterns from training examples.\n\n3) Based on your two genuine measurements, discuss what specific hyperparameter choices (e.g. learning rate, number of epochs, mixing in some general pretraining data during SFT) a genuine implementer would need to tune to mitigate the risks you measured.",
      problemKn: "SFT datasets ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ pretraining corpora ಗಿಂತ ಬಹಳಷ್ಟು ಚಿಕ್ಕದಾಗಿರುವುದರಿಂದ, SFT ಎರಡು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ವೈಫಲ್ಯ ವಿಧಾನಗಳ ನಿಜ ಅಪಾಯ ಎದುರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: (a) catastrophic forgetting, (b) overfitting/memorization.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ instruction dataset ಮೇಲೆ ಅತಿಯಾದ epochs ಗಟ್ಟಲೆ fine-tune ಮಾಡಿ, ಮತ್ತು general text ಮೇಲೆ ಅದರ loss ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹದಗೆಡುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) SFT training ನಂತರ, dataset ನಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲದ ಒಂದು instruction ಜೊತೆ model ಅನ್ನೂ prompt ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ response ನಿಜ generalization ತೋರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಎರಡು ನಿಜ ಅಳತೆಗಳ ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ, ಈ ಅಪಾಯಗಳನ್ನೂ ತಗ್ಗಿಸಲು ಯಾವ hyperparameter ಆಯ್ಕೆಗಳು ಬೇಕಾಗುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m190, l190_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Chat Templates: Encoding Conversation Structure into Token Sequences',
      titleKn: 'Chat Templates: Conversation Structure ಅನ್ನೂ Token Sequences ಆಗಿ Encode ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Since a language model only ever sees a flat sequence of tokens (not an explicit data structure of 'turns' or 'roles'), every multi-turn conversation must be serialized into a single token sequence using a CHAT TEMPLATE -- a fixed textual format with special marker tokens (e.g. distinct markers for where a user turn starts, where an assistant turn starts, and where a turn ends) that let the model learn to distinguish who is 'speaking' at each position purely from these markers.\n\n1) Explain why a model trained with ONE chat template format at SFT time will generally perform poorly (or unpredictably) if given inputs formatted with a DIFFERENT template at inference time -- what exactly is the model's learned association tied to, if not the semantic content of the conversation?\n\n2) Explain why the specific marker used to signal 'end of assistant turn' is especially important operationally -- what specific inference-time behavior does the model rely on this marker to trigger, and what would go wrong if this marker were missing or corrupted in the training data?",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು language model ಕೇವಲ tokens ya ಒಂದು ಸಮತಟ್ಟಾದ ಅನುಕ್ರಮವನ್ನೂ ಮಾತ್ರ ನೋಡುವುದರಿಂದ, ಪ್ರತಿ multi-turn conversation ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು CHAT TEMPLATE ಬಳಸಿ ಒಂದೇ token sequence ಆಗಿ serialize ಮಾಡಬೇಕು.\n\n1) SFT ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದು chat template format ಜೊತೆ train ಮಾಡಿದ model, inference ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಬೇರೆ template ಜೊತೆ ಕಳಪೆಯಾಗಿ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) 'assistant turn ಅಂತ್ಯ' ಸೂಚಿಸುವ marker ಏಕೆ ವಿಶೇಷವಾಗಿ ಕಾರ್ಯಾಚರಣೆಯಲ್ಲಿ ಮುಖ್ಯ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Multi-Turn Chat Template with Correct Loss Masking',
      titleKn: 'ಸರಿಯಾದ Loss Masking ಜೊತೆ Multi-Turn Chat Template ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and implement your own chat template format for your mini-GPT's tokenizer, extending the single-turn loss masking you built in the previous lesson to genuinely handle MULTI-turn conversations.\n\n1) Genuinely define a small set of special marker tokens (e.g. distinct 'user-start', 'assistant-start', 'turn-end' tokens) and genuinely implement a serialization function that takes a list of (role, text) turns and produces a single flat token sequence with these markers correctly interleaved.\n\n2) Genuinely extend your loss mask so that in a MULTI-turn conversation, loss is computed over ALL assistant-turn tokens across the whole conversation (not just the final turn), while ALL user-turn tokens and marker tokens remain masked out at every position -- genuinely verify this on a constructed 3-turn (user, assistant, user, assistant) example by printing out, for every token position, whether it's included in the loss or not, and manually confirming the pattern is correct.\n\n3) Genuinely train your mini-GPT briefly on a small set of such multi-turn examples and report whether its generated completions (when given a new partial conversation) correctly stop generating at a turn-end marker rather than continuing indefinitely.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದಲ್ಲಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ single-turn loss masking ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya tokenizer ಗೆ ಸ್ವಂತ chat template format ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ special marker tokens ಸೆಟ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು (role, text) turns ಪಟ್ಟಿಯನ್ನೂ ಒಂದೇ flat token sequence ಆಗಿ ಸೀರಿಯಲೈಸ್ ಮಾಡುವ function ಅನ್ನೂ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) multi-turn conversation ನಲ್ಲಿ loss ಅನ್ನೂ ಎಲ್ಲಾ assistant-turn tokens ಮೇಲೆ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುವ ಹಾಗೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ loss mask ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಇದನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು 3-turn ಉದಾಹರಣೆ ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ multi-turn ಉದಾಹರಣೆಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಂಕ್ಷಿಪ್ತವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Template-Mismatch Degradation at Inference Time',
      titleKn: 'Inference ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ Template-Mismatch Degradation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment that directly measures the practical consequence of chat-template mismatch, which the beginner exercise in this lesson only asked you to reason about.\n\n1) Genuinely train your mini-GPT via SFT using YOUR chat template format from the intermediate exercise, on a small instruction dataset.\n\n2) Genuinely construct a set of test prompts formatted TWO ways: (a) using the exact template format the model was trained on, and (b) using a deliberately DIFFERENT template (e.g. swapped marker tokens, or plain-text role labels like 'User:' / 'Assistant:' instead of your special tokens) -- genuinely run both formats through your trained model and compare the generated completions.\n\n3) Genuinely report concrete, measured differences between the two conditions -- e.g. does the mismatched-template condition produce completions that fail to stop at the right point, ramble into unrelated text, or otherwise show measurably different generation behavior compared to the correctly-templated condition? Report your actual observed generations for both conditions as evidence, and discuss why this genuine finding explains why real deployed chat models are so strict about exact template conformance in their inference-serving code.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಪಾಠದ beginner exercise ಕೇವಲ ತಾರ್ಕಿಸಲು ಕೇಳಿದ chat-template mismatch ya ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಪರಿಣಾಮವನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ chat template format ಬಳಸಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ SFT ಮೂಲಕ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಸರಿಯಾದ template ಮತ್ತು ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ ಬೇರೆ template ಬಳಸಿ test prompts ಗಳ ಒಂದು ಸೆಟ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡು ಪರಿಸ್ಥಿತಿಗಳ ನಡುವೆ ನಿಜ, ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m190, l190_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Building a Quality SFT Dataset: Diversity, Correctness, and Style Consistency',
      titleKn: 'ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟದ SFT Dataset ನಿರ್ಮಾಣ: Diversity, Correctness, ಮತ್ತು Style Consistency',
      problem: "Real SFT datasets (whether human-written, model-generated-and-filtered, or a mix) are typically judged along three key dimensions: (a) DIVERSITY -- covering a wide range of task types, topics, and instruction phrasings so the model generalizes to instructions it hasn't seen verbatim, rather than narrowly overfitting to one task type; (b) CORRECTNESS -- responses must actually be accurate, safe, and well-reasoned, since the model will directly imitate whatever behavior the responses demonstrate, including any errors; and (c) STYLE CONSISTENCY -- responses should share a consistent tone, formatting, and level of detail, since the model will also learn (and reproduce) whatever surface-level stylistic patterns are present across the dataset, for better or worse.\n\n1) Explain why a dataset that is highly diverse in TOPIC but inconsistent in RESPONSE STYLE (e.g. some responses are terse, others are verbose and over-explain) can still produce a poorly-behaved model, even though topic diversity is usually considered a good thing.\n\n2) Explain why a single systematically WRONG or low-quality response type repeated many times in an SFT dataset (e.g. a particular question format always answered incorrectly) is more damaging to the trained model than the same error appearing just once -- connect this to how gradient-based learning aggregates signal across repeated examples.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಜ SFT datasets ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ ಮೂರು ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಆಯಾಮಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ ನಿರ್ಣಯಿಸಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ: (a) DIVERSITY, (b) CORRECTNESS, (c) STYLE CONSISTENCY.\n\n1) TOPIC ನಲ್ಲಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ವೈವಿಧ್ಯಮಯ ಆದರೆ RESPONSE STYLE ನಲ್ಲಿ ಅಸಮಂಜಸ dataset ಇನ್ನೂ ಕಳಪೆಯಾಗಿ ವರ್ತಿಸುವ model ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಬಹುದು ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) SFT dataset ನಲ್ಲಿ ಅನೇಕ ಬಾರಿ ಪುನರಾವರ್ತಿತವಾದ ಒಂದೇ ವ್ಯವಸ್ಥಿತ ತಪ್ಪು response type ಏಕೆ ಒಂದೇ ಬಾರಿ ಕಾಣಿಸಿಕೊಳ್ಳುವುದಕ್ಕಿಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಹಾನಿಕಾರಕ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Curating and Filtering a Small Instruction Dataset',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ Instruction Dataset ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Curate ಮತ್ತು Filter ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely build and clean a small instruction-tuning dataset from scratch (either by writing examples yourself, sourcing from a permissively-licensed public dataset, or generating candidates and manually reviewing them).\n\n1) Genuinely assemble at least 30-50 (instruction, response) pairs spanning several distinct task types (e.g. question-answering, summarization, simple reasoning, creative writing), genuinely tracking the task-type distribution to confirm actual diversity rather than assuming it.\n\n2) Genuinely implement and apply at least two concrete quality filters to your raw candidate set: a length filter (removing responses that are suspiciously too short to be genuinely helpful, or too long relative to the instruction's apparent scope) and a duplicate/near-duplicate filter (using a technique from the corpus-deduplication lessons earlier in this phase) to remove redundant examples.\n\n3) Genuinely report, with actual before/after counts, how many candidate examples your filters removed and why, and manually spot-check a sample of the surviving examples for style consistency, reporting any inconsistencies you genuinely find and how you resolved them.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ instruction-tuning dataset ಅನ್ನೂ ಶೂನ್ಯದಿಂದ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಶುಚಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ task types ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 30-50 (instruction, response) ಜೋಡಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜೋಡಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ raw candidate set ಗೆ ಕನಿಷ್ಠ ಎರಡು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ filters ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಿ: length filter ಮತ್ತು duplicate/near-duplicate filter.\n\n3) ನಿಜ before/after counts ಜೊತೆ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಎಷ್ಟು candidate examples ನಿಮ್ಮ filters ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕಿದವು ಮತ್ತು ಏಕೆ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Impact of Dataset Quality on Fine-Tuned Behavior',
      titleKn: 'Fine-Tuned Behavior ಮೇಲೆ Dataset Quality ya ಪ್ರಭಾವವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run a controlled comparison that directly tests the claims from the beginner exercise in this lesson, using the curated dataset you built in the intermediate exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely create a deliberately DEGRADED version of your curated dataset by reintroducing the problems your filters were designed to catch -- e.g. add back near-duplicate examples (over-representing one task type), inject a handful of genuinely incorrect responses to a specific question format, and/or introduce genuine style inconsistency (mixing extremely terse and extremely verbose responses for similar instructions).\n\n2) Genuinely fine-tune two SEPARATE copies of your mini-GPT: one on your clean, filtered dataset, and one on your deliberately degraded version, using identical hyperparameters and number of steps for both.\n\n3) Genuinely evaluate both fine-tuned models on a held-out set of test instructions (not seen during either training run), and report concrete, measured differences in their outputs -- e.g. does the model trained on degraded data visibly reproduce the specific errors or style inconsistencies you injected, or overrepresent the over-sampled task type in its response tendencies? Report actual generated examples from both models as evidence, and discuss what your genuine, measured result implies about how much of a fine-tuned model's behavior is directly attributable to specific, identifiable properties of its training data (versus being an unpredictable emergent property).",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಪಾಠದ beginner exercise ya ಹಕ್ಕುಗಳನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನಿಯಂತ್ರಿತ ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ filters ಹಿಡಿಯಲು ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿದ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಮರುಪರಿಚಯಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ ನಿಮ್ಮ curated dataset ya ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ ಹದಗೆಟ್ಟ ಆವೃತ್ತಿಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದೇ hyperparameters ಬಳಸಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya ಎರಡು ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ ಪ್ರತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ fine-tune ಮಾಡಿ: ಒಂದು ಶುದ್ಧ dataset ಮೇಲೆ, ಒಂದು ಹದಗೆಟ್ಟ ಆವೃತ್ತಿ ಮೇಲೆ.\n\n3) ಎರಡೂ ಮಾದರಿಗಳನ್ನೂ held-out test instructions ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ, ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m191, l191_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Reward Model: Learning Human Preference as a Scalar Score',
      titleKn: 'Reward Model: Human Preference ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು Scalar Score ಆಗಿ ಕಲಿಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) begins by training a REWARD MODEL: a separate neural network (typically initialized from the SFT model itself, with its final unembedding layer replaced by a single scalar output) that takes a (prompt, response) pair and outputs a single number representing how much a human would PREFER that response. Because collecting an absolute quality score for every response is unreliable and hard for humans to calibrate consistently, reward models are instead trained on PAIRWISE COMPARISONS -- humans are shown two candidate responses to the same prompt and simply indicate which one they prefer, and the model is trained (via a loss based on the difference between the two responses' predicted scores) to assign a HIGHER score to the preferred response than the rejected one.\n\n1) Explain why pairwise comparisons ('which of these two is better') are typically far easier and more consistent for human labelers to provide than absolute scores ('rate this response 1-10') -- what specific inconsistency problem does pairwise comparison sidestep?\n\n2) Explain why the reward model's output is only meaningful in a RELATIVE sense (comparing two scores against each other) rather than as an absolute, universally-interpretable quality measure -- what does this imply about trying to interpret a single reward score in isolation, without any other response to compare it against?",
      problemKn: "RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) ಒಂದು REWARD MODEL ಅನ್ನೂ train ಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಆರಂಭವಾಗುತ್ತದೆ: ಇದು ಒಂದು (prompt, response) ಜೋಡಿಯನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಂಡು ಒಂದು human ಎಷ್ಟು ಆ response ಅನ್ನೂ ಇಷ್ಟಪಡುತ್ತಾರೆ ಎಂದು ಪ್ರತಿನಿಧಿಸುವ ಒಂದೇ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಔಟ್‌ಪುಟ್ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ. reward models ಅನ್ನೂ PAIRWISE COMPARISONS ಮೇಲೆ train ಮಾಡಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) pairwise comparisons ಏಕೆ human labelers ಗೆ absolute scores ಗಿಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಸುಲಭ ಮತ್ತು ಸ್ಥಿರ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) reward model ya ಔಟ್‌ಪುಟ್ ಕೇವಲ ಒಂದು RELATIVE ಅರ್ಥದಲ್ಲಿ ಮಾತ್ರ ಅರ್ಥಪೂರ್ಣ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Training a Pairwise Reward Model',
      titleKn: 'Pairwise Reward Model ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Train ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement the Bradley-Terry pairwise preference loss used to train real reward models, and genuinely train a small reward model on your mini-GPT architecture.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a reward-model 'head': take your mini-GPT's transformer body and replace its final unembedding layer with a single linear layer producing one scalar score from the final token's hidden state, for a given (prompt, response) input.\n\n2) Genuinely implement the Bradley-Terry loss: for a pair of responses (chosen, rejected) to the same prompt, compute loss = -log(sigmoid(reward(chosen) - reward(rejected))), and genuinely verify this loss decreases (and the score GAP between chosen and rejected responses genuinely widens) as you train on a small, manually-labeled set of preference pairs (you can construct these yourself, e.g. pairing a clearly better response against a clearly worse one for the same prompt, to have a genuine, verifiable ground truth).\n\n3) Genuinely evaluate your trained reward model's ACCURACY on a held-out set of preference pairs (not seen during training) -- report what fraction of held-out pairs it correctly ranks (chosen score > rejected score), and discuss what this measured accuracy tells you about whether your reward model has learned a genuinely generalizable notion of preference versus memorizing the specific training pairs.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಜ reward models train ಮಾಡಲು ಬಳಸುವ Bradley-Terry pairwise preference loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT architecture ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ reward model ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) reward-model 'head' ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) Bradley-Terry loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು loss ಕಡಿಮೆಯಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ trained reward model ya ACCURACY ಅನ್ನೂ held-out preference pairs ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: "Genuinely Measuring Reward Model Overoptimization Risk (Goodhart's Law)",
      titleKn: 'Reward Model Overoptimization ಅಪಾಯವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ (Goodhart ya Law)',
      problem: "A genuine, well-documented failure mode of RLHF is REWARD HACKING (also called reward overoptimization): because the reward model is only an imperfect, learned PROXY for true human preference (not human preference itself), a policy optimized aggressively enough against the reward model can find responses that score highly on the reward model's proxy metric WITHOUT genuinely being preferred by actual humans -- exploiting quirks, blind spots, or biases specific to the reward model rather than genuinely improving response quality.\n\n1) Genuinely investigate this on your own trained reward model from the intermediate exercise: identify (through genuine experimentation, e.g. trying various response modifications like excessive length, specific repeated phrases, or particular formatting tricks) whether you can find any SPECIFIC, cheap surface-level modification to a response that genuinely increases your reward model's assigned score without genuinely improving the response's actual quality or correctness by your own honest judgment.\n\n2) If you genuinely find such an exploit, report it concretely (the specific modification and the measured score increase it produces), and discuss what this reveals about a blind spot or bias in your specific reward model's training data or architecture.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss why real RLHF pipelines cannot simply optimize the policy against the reward model indefinitely, and what practical safeguards (e.g. KL-divergence penalties keeping the policy close to the reference/SFT model, using multiple diverse reward models, or periodically re-collecting human feedback on the CURRENT policy's outputs to catch drift) address this genuine overoptimization risk you just demonstrated.",
      problemKn: "RLHF ya ಒಂದು ನಿಜ, ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿ ದಾಖಲಿಸಲಾದ ವೈಫಲ್ಯ ವಿಧಾನ REWARD HACKING (reward overoptimization ಎಂದೂ ಕರೆಯಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ).\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ trained reward model ಮೇಲೆ ಇದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ: ಯಾವುದೇ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ, ಅಗ್ಗದ ಮೇಲ್ಮೈ-ಮಟ್ಟದ ಮಾರ್ಪಾಡು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ reward model ya score ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಗುರುತಿಸಿ, ಆದರೆ response ya ನಿಜ ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ಸುಧಾರಿಸದೆ.\n\n2) ನೀವು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಂತಹ exploit ಕಂಡುಕೊಂಡರೆ, ಅದನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಜ RLHF pipelines ಏಕೆ policy ಅನ್ನೂ reward model ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಅನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ optimize ಮಾಡಲು ಸಾಧ್ಯವಿಲ್ಲ ಮತ್ತು ಯಾವ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಸುರಕ್ಷತೆಗಳು ಈ ಅಪಾಯವನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m191, l191_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'PPO for Language Models: Policy, Value Function, and the KL Penalty',
      titleKn: 'Language Models ಗೆ PPO: Policy, Value Function, ಮತ್ತು KL Penalty',
      problem: "Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) is the reinforcement learning algorithm most commonly used to actually update the language model (now called the 'policy') to produce higher-reward responses according to the trained reward model. Applied to language models, PPO involves: (a) the POLICY -- the language model itself, being updated to generate better responses; (b) a VALUE FUNCTION -- a separate learned estimate of expected future reward from a given partial sequence, used to reduce variance in the training signal; and (c) a KL-DIVERGENCE PENALTY -- a term subtracted from the reward that penalizes the policy for drifting too far (in output-distribution terms) from the original SFT reference model.\n\n1) Explain why PPO's core mechanism (clipping the policy update so it can't change TOO drastically in a single step, hence 'Proximal') is specifically important when the 'reward signal' is a learned reward model rather than a ground-truth reward -- what could go wrong if the policy were allowed to change drastically based on a single batch of (possibly reward-hacked) high-scoring responses?\n\n2) Explain concretely why the KL penalty against the reference SFT model is necessary in addition to (not instead of) PPO's own clipping -- what SPECIFIC failure mode does the KL penalty guard against that clipping alone does not, given that a policy could still, over MANY small clipped steps, gradually drift very far from reasonable, human-like language even while each individual step remains small?",
      problemKn: "Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) ಎಂಬುದೂ ಒಂದು reinforcement learning algorithm ಆಗಿದ್ದು, ಇದನ್ನೂ language model ('policy') ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ update ಮಾಡಲು ಬಳಸಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ. Language models ಗೆ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಿದಾಗ, PPO ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿದೆ: (a) POLICY, (b) VALUE FUNCTION, (c) KL-DIVERGENCE PENALTY.\n\n1) PPO ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆ (policy update ಅನ್ನೂ clip ಮಾಡುವುದೂ) ಏಕೆ ಮುಖ್ಯ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) reference SFT model ವಿರುದ್ಧ KL penalty ಏಕೆ ಅಗತ್ಯ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Simplified PPO Training Loop for Text Generation',
      titleKn: 'Text Generation ಗೆ ಸರಳೀಕೃತ PPO Training Loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified end-to-end PPO training loop connecting your mini-GPT policy to your reward model from the previous lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the rollout phase: sample a batch of prompts, generate completions from your current policy, and score each completed (prompt, response) pair using your trained reward model to get a scalar reward per sample.\n\n2) Genuinely implement the KL penalty: compute the token-level log-probability of the generated response under BOTH the current policy and a frozen copy of the reference (pre-PPO) model, and genuinely subtract a scaled KL-divergence estimate (the difference between these log-probabilities) from each sample's reward, so responses that drift too far from the reference model's distribution are penalized.\n\n3) Genuinely implement the clipped PPO policy-gradient update itself (using the standard clipped surrogate objective) and genuinely train for a small number of iterations, reporting whether the mean reward-model score of generated completions genuinely INCREASES over training iterations, and separately reporting whether the measured KL-divergence from the reference model stays bounded (rather than exploding) throughout training.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT policy ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ reward model ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುವ ಸರಳೀಕೃತ end-to-end PPO training loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) rollout phase ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: prompts ya ಒಂದು batch sample ಮಾಡಿ, completions generate ಮಾಡಿ, ಮತ್ತು reward model ಬಳಸಿ ಸ್ಕೋರ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) KL penalty ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) clipped PPO policy-gradient update ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಸಂಕ್ಷಿಪ್ತವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ, mean reward score ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚಾಗುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Reward-vs-KL Tradeoff Across Penalty Strengths',
      titleKn: 'ವಿಭಿನ್ನ Penalty Strengths ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Reward-vs-KL Tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an ablation study over the KL-penalty coefficient in your PPO implementation from the intermediate exercise, to genuinely characterize the reward-vs-divergence tradeoff that real RLHF practitioners must tune.\n\n1) Genuinely run your PPO training loop to completion (same number of iterations, same prompts) under at least three different KL-penalty coefficient values: one very small (weak KL penalty), one moderate, and one very large (strong KL penalty).\n\n2) For each run, genuinely measure and report both the FINAL mean reward-model score achieved and the FINAL measured KL-divergence from the reference model.\n\n3) Genuinely report the actual tradeoff curve you observe across your three runs -- confirm whether weaker KL penalties genuinely produce higher reward-model scores at the cost of larger KL-divergence (and, if you can, spot-check whether the weak-KL run's generated text has begun to look qualitatively stranger, more repetitive, or otherwise degraded compared to the strong-KL run's outputs), and discuss why this genuine, measured tradeoff is precisely why the KL coefficient is one of the most carefully-tuned hyperparameters in real production RLHF pipelines, rather than something that can be set once and forgotten.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ PPO implementation ನಲ್ಲಿ KL-penalty coefficient ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ablation ಅಧ್ಯಯನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ ಮೂರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ KL-penalty coefficient ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳ ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ PPO training loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪೂರ್ಣಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ run ಗೆ, final mean reward-model score ಮತ್ತು final KL-divergence ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂರು runs ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಜ tradeoff curve ಅನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದು ಏಕೆ KL coefficient ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜ production RLHF pipelines ನಲ್ಲಿ ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಎಚ್ಚರಿಕೆಯಿಂದ ಟ್ಯೂನ್ ಮಾಡಿದ hyperparameters ಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದಾಗಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m191, l191_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Full RLHF Pipeline: SFT, Reward Model, and PPO as Three Connected Stages',
      titleKn: 'ಪೂರ್ಣ RLHF Pipeline: SFT, Reward Model, ಮತ್ತು PPO ಮೂರು ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿತ Stages ಆಗಿ',
      problem: "The complete RLHF pipeline chains together the three components covered across this module's lessons into a specific ORDER, where each stage's output is the next stage's required input: Stage 1 (SFT) produces a model that follows instructions reasonably, which becomes BOTH the starting point for Stage 3's policy AND the frozen 'reference model' used for Stage 3's KL penalty; Stage 2 (Reward Modeling) uses human preference data (often collected by having the Stage-1 SFT model generate candidate responses for humans to compare) to produce a reward model; and Stage 3 (PPO) uses the Stage-2 reward model to further optimize the Stage-1 policy via reinforcement learning.\n\n1) Explain why this pipeline could NOT be reordered to run PPO (Stage\n\n3) BEFORE SFT (Stage\n\n1) -- what would the policy being optimized even look like at the start of RL training, if it hadn't already learned basic instruction-following, and why would this make the RL optimization signal far less useful?\n\n2) Explain why the Stage-1 SFT model plays TWO distinct roles in Stage 3 (both the initial policy AND the frozen KL-penalty reference) -- what would go wrong if, instead, Stage 3's KL penalty referenced some OTHER model (e.g. the base pretrained model, before any SFT) rather than the SFT model specifically?",
      problemKn: "ಪೂರ್ಣ RLHF pipeline ಈ module ya ಪಾಠಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿರುವ ಮೂರು ಘಟಕಗಳನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಕ್ರಮದಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: Stage 1 (SFT), Stage 2 (Reward Modeling), Stage 3 (PPO).\n\n1) ಈ pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ PPO ಅನ್ನೂ SFT ಗಿಂತ ಮೊದಲು ಚಲಾಯಿಸುವ ಹಾಗೆ ಏಕೆ ಮರುಕ್ರಮಗೊಳಿಸಲು ಸಾಧ್ಯವಿಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) Stage-1 SFT model Stage 3 ನಲ್ಲಿ ಎರಡು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ಪಾತ್ರಗಳನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ವಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Running the Complete SFT-to-Reward-Model-to-PPO Pipeline End-to-End',
      titleKn: 'ಪೂರ್ಣ SFT-ಇಂದ-Reward-Model-ಇಂದ-PPO Pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೊದಲಿಂದ ಕೊನೆಯವರೆಗೆ ಚಲಾಯಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely connect all three stages from this module's lessons into one complete, working pipeline on your mini-GPT, using your own implementations from the previous lessons.\n\n1) Genuinely run Stage 1: take your pretrained mini-GPT and SFT-tune it on your instruction dataset (from the earlier lesson in this phase), producing a starting policy.\n\n2) Genuinely run Stage 2: use this SFT model to generate multiple candidate responses per prompt for a small set of prompts, genuinely construct preference pairs yourself (by your own honest judgment of which response is better) for a handful of these, and train your reward model on these genuine pairs.\n\n3) Genuinely run Stage 3: use your Stage-1 SFT model as both the initial PPO policy and the frozen KL-penalty reference, and your Stage-2 reward model as the reward signal, running your PPO loop for a modest number of iterations.\n\n4) Genuinely evaluate the FINAL PPO-tuned policy against the ORIGINAL Stage-1 SFT policy by generating completions from both for a held-out set of prompts and comparing their reward-model scores -- report whether the full pipeline genuinely produced a measurable improvement, and discuss any genuine surprises or difficulties you encountered connecting the three stages together.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಪಾಠಗಳ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರು ಹಂತಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ implementations ಬಳಸಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ, ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ pipeline ಆಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸಿ.\n\n1) Stage 1 ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ: SFT ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) Stage 2 ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ: preference pairs ರಚಿಸಿ reward model train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) Stage 3 ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ: PPO loop ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n4) FINAL policy ಅನ್ನೂ ORIGINAL SFT policy ಜೊತೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Auditing the Full Pipeline for Compounding Failure Modes',
      titleKn: 'Compounding Failure Modes ಗಾಗಿ ಪೂರ್ಣ Pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Audit ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Because RLHF is a three-stage pipeline where each stage depends on the previous one's output, errors or biases introduced at an EARLIER stage can compound or become amplified by LATER stages in ways that are hard to detect by examining any single stage in isolation. Genuinely audit your complete pipeline from the intermediate exercise for this compounding effect.\n\n1) Genuinely trace a SPECIFIC bias or quirk through all three stages: identify some specific stylistic or content tendency present in your Stage-1 SFT dataset (e.g. a particular phrase, length tendency, or topic bias), then genuinely check whether this SAME tendency shows up amplified (i.e. appears MORE strongly/frequently) in your Stage-3 PPO-tuned model's outputs compared to the Stage-1 SFT model's outputs.\n\n2) Genuinely reason about (and where feasible, verify empirically) the specific mechanism by which such amplification could occur: if your Stage-2 preference data happened to (even inadvertently) favor responses exhibiting this tendency, explain how Stage-3's optimization against that reward model would systematically push the policy to exhibit the tendency even MORE strongly than the original SFT data did.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, based on your concrete finding, why real RLHF teams treat preference-data collection (Stage\n\n2) as one of the highest-leverage and highest-risk points in the entire pipeline for controlling a model's final behavior -- more so than either the SFT data or the PPO hyperparameters alone -- and what practical auditing practices (e.g. diverse labeler pools, regular spot-checks of PPO outputs against the original SFT outputs for concerning drift) would help catch the kind of compounding effect you just demonstrated.",
      problemKn: "RLHF ಒಂದು ಮೂರು-ಹಂತದ pipeline ಆಗಿರುವುದರಿಂದ, ಮೊದಲಿನ ಹಂತದಲ್ಲಿ ಪರಿಚಯಿಸಲಾದ ದೋಷಗಳು ಅಥವಾ biases ನಂತರದ ಹಂತಗಳಿಂದ compound ಆಗಬಹುದು ಅಥವಾ ವರ್ಧಿಸಬಹುದು. ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ compounding ಪರಿಣಾಮಕ್ಕಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ audit ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ bias ಅಥವಾ quirk ಅನ್ನೂ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರು ಹಂತಗಳ ಮೂಲಕ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಟ್ರೇಸ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ವರ್ಧನೆ ಸಂಭವಿಸುವ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯ ಬಗ್ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತಾರ್ಕಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಜ RLHF ತಂಡಗಳು ಏಕೆ preference-data collection ಅನ್ನೂ ಇಡೀ pipeline ನಲ್ಲಿ ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು-ಪ್ರಭಾವಶಾಲಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅಪಾಯಕಾರಿ ಬಿಂದುವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಗಣಿಸುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m192, l192_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "DPO: Skipping the Reward Model and RL Loop Entirely",
      titleKn: "DPO: Reward Model ಮತ್ತು RL Loop ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣವಾಗಿ ಬಿಟ್ಟುಬಿಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) is a mathematically-derived alternative to the full RLHF pipeline (SFT + reward model + PPO) that achieves the same goal -- a policy that prefers human-preferred responses -- using ONLY a single, simple supervised-learning-style loss computed directly on preference pairs, with NO separate reward model and NO reinforcement learning loop at all. DPO's key insight is that the OPTIMAL policy under the RLHF objective (maximize reward minus KL-penalty from reference) has a known closed-form relationship to the reward function, which lets you algebraically substitute this relationship into the Bradley-Terry preference loss and get a loss expressed PURELY in terms of the policy's own token probabilities (for both the current policy and a frozen reference model) -- eliminating the reward model as a separate object entirely.\n\n1) Explain what practical engineering complexity DPO eliminates compared to full RLHF -- specifically, list the distinct trained models and moving parts (reward model training, PPO rollouts, value function, KL-penalty bookkeeping) that a full RLHF pipeline requires which DPO's single loss function does not.\n\n2) Explain why DPO still needs a FROZEN REFERENCE model (just like PPO's KL penalty did) even though it has no explicit KL-penalty term written separately -- what role does comparing against the reference model's probabilities play in DPO's loss, if not an explicit, separately-weighted penalty?",
      problemKn: "Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) ಎಂಬುದೂ ಪೂರ್ಣ RLHF pipeline (SFT + reward model + PPO) ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಗಣಿತೀಯವಾಗಿ-ಪಡೆದ ಪರ್ಯಾಯವಾಗಿದ್ದು, ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ reward model ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ಮತ್ತು reinforcement learning loop ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ಒಂದೇ, ಸರಳ supervised-learning-ಶೈಲಿಯ loss ಬಳಸಿ ಅದೇ ಗುರಿಯನ್ನೂ ಸಾಧಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) DPO ಪೂರ್ಣ RLHF ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿದರೆ ಯಾವ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ engineering ಸಂಕೀರ್ಣತೆಯನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) DPO ಗೆ ಇನ್ನೂ FROZEN REFERENCE model ಏಕೆ ಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Training the DPO Loss on Preference Pairs',
      titleKn: 'Preference Pairs ಮೇಲೆ DPO Loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Train ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement the DPO loss function and genuinely train your mini-GPT policy directly on preference pairs, without any reward model or RL loop.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the DPO loss: for a (prompt, chosen, rejected) triple, compute the sum of token-level log-probabilities of the chosen response under the current policy and under a frozen reference copy, do the same for the rejected response, and combine these four quantities into the DPO loss formula (loss = -log(sigmoid(beta * ((logp_policy_chosen - logp_ref_chosen) - (logp_policy_rejected - logp_ref_rejected))))) -- genuinely verify your implementation's arithmetic by manually computing this loss by hand for one tiny example and confirming your code produces the same number.\n\n2) Genuinely train your mini-GPT policy using this DPO loss on the SAME set of preference pairs you used to train your reward model in the RLHF lesson's intermediate exercise, and genuinely verify the loss decreases over training.\n\n3) Genuinely evaluate the DPO-trained policy: generate completions for held-out prompts both before and after DPO training, and use your PREVIOUSLY-trained reward model (from the RLHF lesson) purely as an independent evaluation metric (not as part of DPO training itself) to check whether DPO training genuinely increased the reward-model score of generated completions, despite reward model scores never being directly used anywhere in the DPO training process.",
      problemKn: "DPO loss function ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು reward model ಅಥವಾ RL loop ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ನೇರವಾಗಿ preference pairs ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) DPO loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ ಉದಾಹರಣೆಗೆ ಕೈಯಾರೆ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n2) RLHF ಪಾಠದ ಅದೇ preference pairs ಮೇಲೆ ಈ DPO loss ಬಳಸಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) DPO-trained policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ ಹಿಂದಿನ reward model ಅನ್ನೂ ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಸಾಧನವಾಗಿ ಬಳಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing DPO and PPO on Identical Data, Reporting the Real Tradeoffs',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದೇ Data ಮೇಲೆ DPO ಮತ್ತು PPO ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ, ನಿಜ Tradeoffs ವರದಿ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run a head-to-head comparison between DPO (this lesson) and PPO (the previous lesson), using the EXACT same starting SFT model and the EXACT same preference-pair data for both, so any observed differences are attributable to the algorithm rather than data differences.\n\n1) Genuinely train one policy via your PPO+reward-model pipeline and a SEPARATE, identically-initialized policy via your DPO implementation, using the same preference pairs (recall PPO requires them indirectly, to first train the reward model; DPO uses them directly).\n\n2) Genuinely measure and report at least two concrete comparison points: (a) total wall-clock training TIME for each approach (accounting for DPO's simplicity of needing no separate reward-model training phase or rollout sampling, versus PPO's multi-stage process), and (b) final policy quality, evaluated by using your reward model to score both policies' generated completions on a held-out prompt set (using the reward model purely as an independent judge for both, even though DPO didn't use it during training).\n\n3) Genuinely report which approach achieved a better score-per-unit-training-time in YOUR specific experiment, and discuss, based on your own genuine measurements (not received wisdom), what specific circumstances might favor PPO over DPO despite DPO's simplicity (for example, situations where the reward model itself is expected to generalize to states unseen in the fixed preference dataset, which PPO's online rollout-sampling can exploit but DPO's fixed-dataset training cannot).",
      problemKn: "DPO (ಈ ಪಾಠ) ಮತ್ತು PPO (ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠ) ನಡುವೆ ಒಂದು ನೇರ ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ಒಂದೇ ಆರಂಭಿಕ SFT model ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದೇ preference-pair data ಬಳಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು policy ಅನ್ನೂ PPO+reward-model pipeline ಮೂಲಕ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ policy ಅನ್ನೂ DPO ಮೂಲಕ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಒಟ್ಟು wall-clock training ಸಮಯ ಮತ್ತು final policy ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪ್ರಯೋಗದಲ್ಲಿ ಯಾವ ವಿಧಾನ ಉತ್ತಮ score-per-unit-time ಸಾಧಿಸಿತು ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m192, l192_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "The Beta Parameter: Controlling How Sharply DPO Trusts Preferences",
      titleKn: "Beta Parameter: DPO ಆದ್ಯತೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಎಷ್ಟು ತೀಕ್ಷ್ಣವಾಗಿ ನಂಬುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಯಂತ್ರಿಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "DPO's loss formula includes a temperature-like hyperparameter, beta, that controls how strongly the loss penalizes the policy for NOT separating chosen and rejected responses' relative log-probabilities -- effectively playing the same practical role that the KL-penalty coefficient played in PPO, even though DPO has no explicit, separately-computed KL term.\n\n1) Explain what happens at the mathematical extremes of beta: as beta approaches zero, the loss's sigmoid argument shrinks toward zero regardless of how well-separated chosen and rejected responses are, making gradients vanish -- explain what this implies practically about how strongly the policy would be updated at very low beta. As beta grows very large, small differences in the policy-vs-reference log-probability gap get amplified enormously by the sigmoid -- explain what this implies about how aggressively (and how far from the reference model) the policy could be pushed at very high beta.\n\n2) Explain why, despite DPO having no explicit KL-penalty term, beta still controls essentially the same reward-vs-divergence tradeoff that the KL coefficient controlled in PPO -- connecting this back to DPO's derivation from the same underlying RLHF objective.",
      problemKn: "DPO ya loss formula ಒಂದು temperature-ರೀತಿಯ hyperparameter, beta, ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿದೆ, ಇದು PPO ಯಲ್ಲಿ KL-penalty coefficient ವಹಿಸಿದ ಅದೇ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಪಾತ್ರವನ್ನೂ ವಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) beta ya ಗಣಿತೀಯ ತೀವ್ರತೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಏನಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ: beta ಶೂನ್ಯಕ್ಕೆ ಹತ್ತಿರವಾದಾಗ, beta ಬಹಳ ದೊಡ್ಡದಾದಾಗ.\n\n2) DPO ಗೆ ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ KL-penalty term ಇಲ್ಲದಿದ್ದರೂ, beta ಅದೇ reward-vs-divergence tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ನಿಯಂತ್ರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Sweeping Beta and Measuring Its Effect on Policy Divergence',
      titleKn: 'Beta ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Sweep ಮಾಡಿ Policy Divergence ಮೇಲೆ ಅದರ ಪ್ರಭಾವವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely run your DPO implementation from the previous lesson's intermediate exercise at several different beta values, to genuinely characterize its practical effect.\n\n1) Genuinely train separate DPO policies at three beta values (one small, one moderate, one large), keeping every other hyperparameter and the preference-pair dataset identical across runs.\n\n2) For each resulting policy, genuinely measure the KL-divergence (or a simple proxy like average per-token log-probability difference) between the DPO-trained policy and the frozen reference model on a held-out set of prompts, and separately measure each policy's average reward-model score (using your previously-trained reward model purely as an evaluation tool) on the same held-out prompts.\n\n3) Genuinely report the actual relationship you observe between beta and (a) measured divergence from the reference model and (b) measured reward-model score -- confirm whether your results match the direction predicted by the beginner exercise's mathematical reasoning, and report any genuine surprises (e.g. a beta value that produces degenerate, repetitive, or otherwise clearly-broken generations) that a purely theoretical analysis wouldn't have revealed.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ DPO implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ beta ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಮೂರು beta ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ DPO policies ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ policy ಗೆ, reference model ಇಂದ KL-divergence ಮತ್ತು reward-model score ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) beta ಮತ್ತು ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ divergence/score ನಡುವಿನ ನಿಜ ಸಂಬಂಧವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ, ಯಾವುದೇ ನಿಜ ಆಶ್ಚರ್ಯಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸೇರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Investigating DPO’s Length Bias: Does It Reward Verbosity Alone?',
      titleKn: 'DPO ya Length Bias ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ: ಇದು ಕೇವಲ Verbosity ಗೆ ಬಹುಮಾನ ನೀಡುತ್ತದೆಯೇ?',
      problem: "A genuine, documented practical issue with DPO (and RLHF more broadly) is a tendency for trained policies to become more VERBOSE over training, sometimes because human preference data (or automated proxies for it) mildly favors longer, more elaborate-seeming responses independent of their actual correctness or helpfulness -- a specific, measurable instance of the reward-hacking-like concerns raised in the RLHF lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely investigate whether this occurs in your own DPO training: measure the average response LENGTH (in tokens) generated by your DPO-trained policy versus the original SFT reference model on the same set of held-out prompts, and report your actual measured length difference, if any.\n\n2) Genuinely check your OWN preference-pair dataset (from the RLHF lesson's exercises) for a length confound: for each preference pair, check whether the response YOU (or whoever labeled it) marked as 'chosen' was, on average, longer than the 'rejected' response, independent of actual quality -- report the measured average length difference between chosen and rejected responses in your specific dataset, and discuss whether this could explain any length increase you measured in step 1.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, based on your concrete findings, one practical mitigation (e.g. explicitly balancing preference-pair length between chosen/rejected during data collection, or adding an explicit length-normalization term to the loss) and, if feasible, genuinely test whether removing or rebalancing the length confound in your training data measurably reduces the length increase you originally observed.",
      problemKn: "DPO (ಮತ್ತು ವಿಶಾಲವಾಗಿ RLHF) ya ಒಂದು ನಿಜ, ದಾಖಲಿತ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ trained policies training ಮೇಲೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು VERBOSE ಆಗುವ ಪ್ರವೃತ್ತಿ.\n\n1) ಇದು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ DPO training ನಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಭವಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ: ಸರಾಸರಿ response LENGTH ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ preference-pair dataset ಅನ್ನೂ length confound ಗಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಪರಿಹಾರವನ್ನೂ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಸಾಧ್ಯವಾದರೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m192, l192_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Beyond Pairwise: KTO and Preference-Free Alignment Alternatives',
      titleKn: 'Pairwise ಮೀರಿ: KTO ಮತ್ತು Preference-Free Alignment ಪರ್ಯಾಯಗಳು',
      problem: "Both PPO and DPO require PAIRED preference data (a chosen response and a rejected response for the SAME prompt), which can be expensive and slow to collect at scale. Newer alignment methods such as KTO (Kahneman-Tversky Optimization) instead work with UNPAIRED binary feedback -- simply a single response per prompt labeled 'good' or 'bad' independently, without needing a matched counterpart -- drawing on prospect theory's finding that humans weigh losses and gains asymmetrically, and adapting this into a loss that only requires knowing whether EACH individual response was desirable, not how it compares to a specific alternative.\n\n1) Explain why unpaired binary feedback is typically much cheaper and faster to collect at scale than paired comparisons -- what specific data-collection step does pairing require that unpaired labeling does not?\n\n2) Explain a genuine limitation of unpaired feedback compared to paired comparisons: since a pairwise comparison directly tells you response A is better THAN response B for the same prompt, while an unpaired 'good'/'bad' label on a single response says nothing about how it would compare to some OTHER response to the same prompt, what specific calibration problem could arise if different labelers have different absolute standards for what counts as 'good'?",
      problemKn: "PPO ಮತ್ತು DPO ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ PAIRED preference data ಬೇಕು, ಇದನ್ನೂ ದೊಡ್ಡ ಪ್ರಮಾಣದಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುವುದೂ ದುಬಾರಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಧಾನವಾಗಬಹುದು. KTO ನಂತಹ ಹೊಸ alignment methods UNPAIRED binary feedback ಜೊತೆ ಕೆಲಸ ಮಾಡುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) unpaired binary feedback ಏಕೆ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ paired comparisons ಗಿಂತ ಅಗ್ಗ ಮತ್ತು ವೇಗ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) unpaired feedback ya ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಮಿತಿಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Training a Simplified Unpaired KTO-Style Loss',
      titleKn: 'ಸರಳೀಕೃತ Unpaired KTO-Style Loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Train ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified unpaired preference-alignment loss inspired by KTO, and genuinely compare its data requirements against your DPO implementation.\n\n1) Genuinely convert your existing PAIRED preference dataset (from the DPO exercises) into an UNPAIRED format: split each (chosen, rejected) pair into two independent, separately-labeled examples (chosen response labeled 'desirable', rejected response labeled 'undesirable'), discarding the pairing information, to genuinely simulate what an unpaired dataset would look like if collected from scratch.\n\n2) Genuinely implement a simplified unpaired loss that pushes up the policy's relative log-probability (versus the frozen reference) for 'desirable'-labeled responses and pushes it down for 'undesirable'-labeled responses, each independently (without referencing any specific paired counterpart).\n\n3) Genuinely train a policy with this unpaired loss on your reformatted data, and genuinely compare it against your DPO-trained policy from the earlier lesson (both starting from the same SFT model) by evaluating both on held-out prompts using your reward model as an independent judge -- report which approach achieved a higher measured score on YOUR specific data, and discuss whether discarding the pairing information (as your unpaired conversion did) measurably hurt performance compared to DPO's use of the full paired signal.",
      problemKn: "KTO ಇಂದ ಪ್ರೇರಿತ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ unpaired preference-alignment loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಸ್ತಿತ್ವದಲ್ಲಿರುವ PAIRED preference dataset ಅನ್ನೂ UNPAIRED format ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿವರ್ತಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ unpaired loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ unpaired loss ಜೊತೆ ಒಂದು policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ DPO-trained policy ಜೊತೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ, ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Auditing Label Noise Sensitivity: Paired vs Unpaired Alignment',
      titleKn: 'Label Noise Sensitivity ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Audit ಮಾಡುವುದೂ: Paired vs Unpaired Alignment',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment that stress-tests the calibration concern raised in this lesson's beginner exercise: that unpaired labels are more vulnerable to inconsistent labeler standards than paired comparisons.\n\n1) Genuinely inject controlled LABEL NOISE into both your paired DPO dataset and your unpaired KTO-style dataset from the intermediate exercise -- for a chosen fraction (e.g. 10-20%) of examples in each, genuinely flip the label (swap chosen/rejected in the paired case; flip desirable/undesirable in the unpaired case) to simulate inconsistent or low-quality labeling.\n\n2) Genuinely retrain both a DPO policy and an unpaired-loss policy on their respective noise-injected datasets (using the same noise fraction for both, for a fair comparison), and genuinely evaluate both against your reward model on held-out prompts.\n\n3) Genuinely report which approach's final performance degraded MORE, measured in absolute reward-model score drop, when trained on noisy labels versus clean labels -- and discuss, based on your concrete measured result, whether your finding supports or contradicts the theoretical concern that paired comparisons are inherently more robust to individual labeling errors (since a single flipped pair only corrupts one relative comparison, whereas a flipped unpaired label could conflict with the ABSOLUTE standard implied by every other label in the dataset).",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಪಾಠದ beginner exercise ಎತ್ತಿದ calibration ಕಾಳಜಿಯನ್ನೂ ಒತ್ತಡ-ಪರೀಕ್ಷೆ ಮಾಡುವ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ paired DPO dataset ಮತ್ತು unpaired KTO-style dataset ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ನಿಯಂತ್ರಿತ LABEL NOISE ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸೇರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಎರಡು policies ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ noise-injected datasets ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮರುtrain ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಯಾವ ವಿಧಾನ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಹದಗೆಟ್ಟಿತು ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m193, l193_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Constitutional AI: Replacing Human Labelers with a Written Set of Principles",
      titleKn: "Constitutional AI: Human Labelers ಬದಲಿಗೆ ಬರೆದ Principles ಸೆಟ್",
      problem: "Constitutional AI (CAI) reduces the amount of direct human labeling RLHF normally requires by having the MODEL ITSELF critique and revise its own responses according to a written 'constitution' -- a set of explicit natural-language principles (e.g. 'choose the response that is more helpful and honest' or 'choose the response that is less likely to encourage illegal behavior') -- and then using THESE self-generated, principle-guided revisions (or AI-generated preference judgments based on the constitution) as training signal, in place of some or all human-labeled preference data.\n\n1) Explain the core mechanism that makes this work: the SAME underlying language model that struggles to produce a good response directly on its first attempt can often successfully CRITIQUE a response against a stated principle and produce a better revision when explicitly prompted to do so -- why might critique-and-revise be an easier task for a model than getting the response right in one shot?\n\n2) Explain what specific practical problem CAI addresses that pure RLHF (with only human labelers) does not scale well to solve -- what happens to human-labeling cost and consistency as you want to train against a large, diverse, and evolving set of behavioral principles, versus a single, relatively narrow preference dimension?",
      problemKn: "Constitutional AI (CAI) RLHF ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ ಬೇಡುವ ನೇರ human labeling ಪ್ರಮಾಣವನ್ನೂ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ, model ತಾನೇ ಒಂದು ಬರೆದ 'constitution' ಪ್ರಕಾರ ತನ್ನ ಸ್ವಂತ responses ಅನ್ನೂ ಟೀಕಿಸಿ ಪರಿಷ್ಕರಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ.\n\n1) ಇದನ್ನೂ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವಂತೆ ಮಾಡುವ ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) CAI ಯಾವ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Critique-and-Revise Self-Improvement Loop',
      titleKn: 'Critique-and-Revise Self-Improvement Loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified Constitutional-AI-style critique-and-revise pipeline using your SFT mini-GPT (or, if your mini-GPT is too small to reliably follow critique instructions, use a larger accessible model via API purely for this exercise's self-improvement LOOP mechanics, while still genuinely implementing and running every step yourself).\n\n1) Genuinely write a small, explicit set of 3-5 written principles (your own 'constitution') covering concrete behavioral properties you want responses to have.\n\n2) Genuinely implement a three-step loop: (a) generate an initial response to a prompt, (b) generate a CRITIQUE of that response against one of your written principles by explicitly prompting the model to identify ways the response violates the principle, and (c) generate a REVISED response that addresses the critique, explicitly prompting the model to fix the identified issues.\n\n3) Genuinely run this loop on at least 10 diverse prompts and manually evaluate (by your own honest judgment) what fraction of the REVISED responses were genuinely better than the ORIGINAL responses according to your stated principles -- report your actual measured improvement rate, and report any cases where the revision was NOT genuinely better (or was worse), discussing what specifically went wrong in those cases.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ SFT mini-GPT ಬಳಸಿ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ Constitutional-AI-ಶೈಲಿಯ critique-and-revise pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) 3-5 ಬರೆದ principles ya ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ ಸೆಟ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬರೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಮೂರು-ಹಂತದ loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: initial response, critique, revised response.\n\n3) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 10 ವೈವಿಧ್ಯಮಯ prompts ಮೇಲೆ ಈ loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು revised responses ಎಷ್ಟು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಉತ್ತಮವಾಗಿದ್ದವು ಎಂದು ಹಸ್ತಚಾಲಿತವಾಗಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Using AI-Generated Preferences to Train a Reward Model (RLAIF)',
      titleKn: 'Reward Model Train ಮಾಡಲು AI-Generated Preferences ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬಳಸುವುದೂ (RLAIF)',
      problem: "Genuinely implement RLAIF (Reinforcement Learning from AI Feedback), the follow-on step to constitutional critique-and-revise, where an AI model's preference JUDGMENTS (not just its revisions) become training labels for a reward model, replacing some or all human preference labels.\n\n1) Genuinely generate a set of response pairs for a shared set of prompts (e.g. by sampling twice from your SFT model per prompt, or by pairing an original response against its constitutional revision from the intermediate exercise).\n\n2) Genuinely implement an AI-labeling step: explicitly prompt a model (your mini-GPT if capable, or a larger accessible model purely for this labeling step) with one of your written constitutional principles and both candidate responses, and have it output WHICH response better satisfies the principle -- genuinely collect these AI-generated preference labels for at least 20 pairs.\n\n3) Genuinely train a reward model on these AI-generated preference labels (reusing your Bradley-Terry implementation from the RLHF lesson), and genuinely spot-check the AI-labeler's judgments against your OWN honest judgment on a held-out sample of pairs it labeled -- report the measured agreement rate between the AI labeler and your own judgment, and discuss what this measured agreement rate implies about the trustworthiness of using RLAIF as a partial or full substitute for human labeling in your specific setup, including any systematic biases you noticed in the AI labeler's judgments.",
      problemKn: "RLAIF (Reinforcement Learning from AI Feedback) ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಇಲ್ಲಿ AI model ya preference ತೀರ್ಮಾನಗಳು reward model ಗೆ training labels ಆಗುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ prompts ಸೆಟ್ ಗೆ response pairs ಸೆಟ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರಚಿಸಿ.\n\n2) AI-labeling ಹಂತವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ಒಂದು constitutional principle ಮತ್ತು ಎರಡು candidate responses ಜೊತೆ model ಅನ್ನೂ prompt ಮಾಡಿ, ಯಾವುದೂ ಉತ್ತಮ ಎಂದು output ಮಾಡಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ AI-generated preference labels ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು reward model ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು AI-labeler ya ತೀರ್ಮಾನಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ತೀರ್ಮಾನದ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ, ಒಪ್ಪಂದ ದರವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m193, l193_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Iterated Self-Improvement: Bootstrapping a Policy Against Its Own Past Outputs",
      titleKn: "Iterated Self-Improvement: Policy ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದರ ಸ್ವಂತ ಹಿಂದಿನ Outputs ವಿರುದ್ಧ Bootstrap ಮಾಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Iterated self-improvement extends the single-round critique-and-revise idea into a repeated LOOP: train a policy on self-generated (critiqued and revised, or AI-preference-labeled) data, then use THIS newly-improved policy to generate an even better round of self-generated training data, and repeat -- each iteration's training data is produced by the PREVIOUS iteration's (hopefully improved) policy, rather than by a fixed external source.\n\n1) Explain the core assumption this bootstrapping process depends on -- specifically, why does the loop only produce genuine improvement if each iteration's critique-and-revise or preference-judgment step is GENUINELY more reliable at judging quality than the policy's OWN one-shot generation ability at that same iteration; what would happen across many iterations if the judging step were merely as unreliable as (or correlated with the same blind spots as) the generation step?\n\n2) Explain a specific risk unique to MULTI-iteration self-improvement that a single-round critique-and-revise process doesn't have: since each iteration's training data is generated by a model that was itself shaped by the PREVIOUS iteration's self-generated data, explain how a subtle, consistent bias or error could compound and amplify across iterations, similarly to (but potentially worse than) the compounding-bias concern raised in the RLHF pipeline lesson.",
      problemKn: "Iterated self-improvement ಒಂದು-ಸುತ್ತಿನ critique-and-revise ಕಲ್ಪನೆಯನ್ನೂ ಪುನರಾವರ್ತಿತ LOOP ಆಗಿ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: self-generated data ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು policy train ಮಾಡಿ, ನಂತರ ಈ ಸುಧಾರಿತ policy ಬಳಸಿ ಇನ್ನೂ ಉತ್ತಮ ಸುತ್ತಿನ self-generated training data ರಚಿಸಿ, ಪುನರಾವರ್ತಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಈ bootstrapping ಪ್ರಕ್ರಿಯೆ ಅವಲಂಬಿಸಿರುವ ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಊಹೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) multi-iteration self-improvement ಗೆ ವಿಶಿಷ್ಟವಾದ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಅಪಾಯವನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Running a Multi-Round Self-Improvement Loop and Tracking Quality',
      titleKn: 'Multi-Round Self-Improvement Loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟವನ್ನೂ ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely extend your critique-and-revise pipeline from the previous lesson into a multi-round iterated self-improvement experiment.\n\n1) Genuinely run at least 3 successive rounds: in each round, use the CURRENT policy to generate responses, critique-and-revise them against your written constitution, fine-tune (via SFT or DPO, reusing your earlier implementation) a NEW copy of the policy on these revised responses, and designate this newly fine-tuned copy as the 'current policy' for the next round.\n\n2) Genuinely track a consistent quality metric across all rounds (e.g. your reward model's average score on a FIXED held-out set of prompts, evaluated identically after each round) and report the actual measured score trajectory across your 3+ rounds.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether your measured trajectory shows genuine, continued improvement, a plateau, or (following the risk raised in the beginner exercise) any genuine DEGRADATION in later rounds -- and if you observe degradation or plateau, genuinely inspect a sample of the LATEST round's generated responses for any qualitative signs of compounding bias, repetitive patterns, or loss of diversity compared to the FIRST round's outputs, reporting concrete examples of what you find.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ critique-and-revise pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು multi-round iterated self-improvement ಪ್ರಯೋಗವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 3 ಸತತ ಸುತ್ತುಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಸುತ್ತುಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ metric ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ trajectory ನಿಜ ಸುಧಾರಣೆ, plateau, ಅಥವಾ ಹದಗೆಡುವಿಕೆ ತೋರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Diagnosing Mode Collapse and Diversity Loss Across Self-Improvement Rounds',
      titleKn: 'Self-Improvement Rounds ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Mode Collapse ಮತ್ತು Diversity Loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ರೋಗನಿರ್ಣಯ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "A genuine, well-documented risk of iterated self-improvement (and of repeatedly fine-tuning any model on its own generated data more broadly) is MODE COLLAPSE -- a measurable narrowing of the diversity of the policy's outputs over successive rounds, as the model increasingly reinforces its own most-confident patterns at the expense of variety, even if the average quality score doesn't obviously drop.\n\n1) Genuinely design and apply a concrete DIVERSITY metric to the outputs your policy produced at EACH round of the multi-round experiment from the intermediate exercise -- for example, generate multiple responses to the SAME held-out prompt at each round (using sampling, not greedy decoding) and measure how similar those multiple responses are to each other (e.g. via a simple n-gram overlap or edit-distance measure), or measure the diversity of vocabulary/phrasing used ACROSS your full set of held-out prompts' responses at each round.\n\n2) Genuinely report the actual measured diversity trend across your rounds -- does diversity measurably DECREASE as self-improvement rounds progress, even in cases where your quality metric from the intermediate exercise stayed flat or improved?\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, using your own concrete measurements, why average-quality metrics alone (like a single reward-model score) can fail to catch mode collapse, and why a genuine, responsible self-improvement pipeline needs to separately monitor DIVERSITY as its own tracked metric, not just quality -- connecting this to why real production systems built on iterated self-training incorporate explicit diversity-preservation techniques (e.g. maintaining some exploration/sampling temperature, or mixing in original non-self-generated data) rather than purely bootstrapping outputs from one round to the next.",
      problemKn: "Iterated self-improvement ya ಒಂದು ನಿಜ, ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿ ದಾಖಲಿಸಲಾದ ಅಪಾಯ MODE COLLAPSE -- policy ya outputs ya ವೈವಿಧ್ಯತೆ ಸತತ ಸುತ್ತುಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಅಳೆಯಬಹುದಾದ ಕಿರಿದಾಗುವಿಕೆ.\n\n1) intermediate exercise ya multi-round ಪ್ರಯೋಗದ ಪ್ರತಿ ಸುತ್ತಿನ outputs ಗೆ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ DIVERSITY metric ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸುತ್ತುಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಜ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ diversity ಪ್ರವೃತ್ತಿಯನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) average-quality metrics ಒಂಟಿಯಾಗಿ mode collapse ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಹಿಡಿಯಲು ವಿಫಲವಾಗಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಅಳತೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಬಳಸಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m193, l193_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'The Limits of Self-Improvement: What a Model Cannot Bootstrap Its Way Out Of',
      titleKn: 'Self-Improvement ya ಮಿತಿಗಳು: Model Bootstrap ಮಾಡಿ ಹೊರಬರಲಾಗದ್ದು',
      problem: "Despite its power, self-improvement (whether via constitutional critique-and-revise, RLAIF, or iterated bootstrapping) has fundamental limits rooted in a simple fact: a model can only critique, revise, or judge according to knowledge and capabilities it ALREADY possesses -- it cannot use self-critique to acquire genuinely NEW factual knowledge it was never exposed to, and it cannot reliably self-correct errors that stem from a genuine gap in its underlying capability (as opposed to errors that stem from a one-shot generation being careless in a way a slower, more deliberate critique pass can catch).\n\n1) Explain why self-improvement techniques are much better suited to improving STYLE, SAFETY, and CONSISTENCY of responses (dimensions where the model likely already 'knows better' but doesn't reliably apply that knowledge in one-shot generation) than to improving genuine FACTUAL ACCURACY on topics the model was never trained on or has a genuine knowledge gap about.\n\n2) Explain the specific failure mode where an AI-feedback labeler (as used in RLAIF) shares the SAME underlying knowledge gaps or biases as the policy it's judging, since both are typically derived from similar base models -- why would this shared-blind-spot problem make AI feedback systematically less useful for catching certain classes of errors than genuinely independent human feedback would be, even if the AI feedback is cheaper and faster to collect.",
      problemKn: "ಅದರ ಶಕ್ತಿಯ ಹೊರತಾಗಿಯೂ, self-improvement ಒಂದು ಸರಳ ಸತ್ಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಬೇರೂರಿದ ಮೂಲಭೂತ ಮಿತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೊಂದಿದೆ: ಒಂದು model ಕೇವಲ ಅದು ಈಗಾಗಲೇ ಹೊಂದಿರುವ ಜ್ಞಾನ ಮತ್ತು ಸಾಮರ್ಥ್ಯಗಳ ಪ್ರಕಾರ ಮಾತ್ರ ಟೀಕಿಸಬಹುದು, ಪರಿಷ್ಕರಿಸಬಹುದು, ಅಥವಾ ತೀರ್ಮಾನಿಸಬಹುದು.\n\n1) self-improvement techniques ಏಕೆ STYLE, SAFETY, ಮತ್ತು CONSISTENCY ಸುಧಾರಿಸಲು ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಸೂಕ್ತ ಆದರೆ ನಿಜ FACTUAL ACCURACY ಸುಧಾರಿಸಲು ಅಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) RLAIF ಯಲ್ಲಿ AI-feedback labeler ಅದೇ ಜ್ಞಾನ ಅಂತರಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹಂಚಿಕೊಳ್ಳಬಹುದಾದ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ವೈಫಲ್ಯ ವಿಧಾನವನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Testing Self-Improvement\\u2019s Limits on Factual vs Stylistic Errors',
      titleKn: 'Factual vs Stylistic Errors ಮೇಲೆ Self-Improvement ya ಮಿತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run a controlled experiment testing the beginner exercise's claim that self-improvement helps more with style/consistency than with genuine factual accuracy.\n\n1) Genuinely construct two distinct sets of test prompts: set A contains prompts with STYLE/FORMAT/SAFETY issues you can inject into initial responses (e.g. responses that are rude, poorly formatted, or overly verbose), and set B contains prompts asking about SPECIFIC FACTS your model genuinely does not know or gets genuinely wrong on first attempt (verify this by checking the model's initial one-shot answers against ground truth yourself).\n\n2) Genuinely run your critique-and-revise pipeline (from the earlier lesson) on both sets, using constitutional principles targeted at each respective issue type (a style/safety principle for set A, an accuracy/honesty principle for set B).\n\n3) Genuinely measure and report, separately for each set, what fraction of responses genuinely IMPROVED after critique-and-revision (using your own honest judgment against ground truth for set B, and against your stated principles for set A) -- report your actual measured improvement rates for both sets, and discuss whether your concrete results support or contradict the beginner exercise's claim, including any genuine surprises (e.g. cases where critique-and-revise on a factual question produced a confident-sounding but still WRONG revision, which would itself be a genuine, concrete demonstration of self-improvement's limits).",
      problemKn: "self-improvement ಶೈಲಿ/consistency ಗೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಸಹಾಯ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಆದರೆ ನಿಜ factual accuracy ಗೆ ಅಲ್ಲ ಎಂಬ beginner exercise ya ಹಕ್ಕನ್ನೂ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನಿಯಂತ್ರಿತ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಎರಡು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ test prompts ಸೆಟ್ ಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ: style/format ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಗಳ ಸೆಟ್ A, ಮತ್ತು model ge ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತಿಳಿದಿಲ್ಲದ facts ya ಸೆಟ್ B.\n\n2) ಎರಡು ಸೆಟ್ ಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ critique-and-revise pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಪ್ರತಿ ಸೆಟ್ ಗೆ ಎಷ್ಟು responses ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸುಧಾರಿಸಿದವು ಎಂದು ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Auditing Shared Blind Spots Between Policy and AI Labeler',
      titleKn: 'Policy ಮತ್ತು AI Labeler ನಡುವೆ Shared Blind Spots ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Audit ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment that directly tests the beginner exercise's shared-blind-spot concern about RLAIF.\n\n1) Using your factual test set (set B) from the intermediate exercise, genuinely identify a handful of SPECIFIC factual questions where your policy model confidently produces a WRONG answer (not just an uncertain one -- genuinely confirm the model states the wrong answer with no hedging).\n\n2) Genuinely use your AI-feedback labeler setup from the RLAIF exercise to have it judge between the model's actual (wrong) answer and the genuinely correct answer (which you supply, having verified it yourself against ground truth) for each of these questions -- report whether the AI labeler correctly identifies the genuinely correct answer as better, or whether it (sharing the same underlying knowledge gap as the policy, if using the same or a closely related base model) also confidently prefers the WRONG answer.\n\n3) Genuinely report your measured rate of AI-labeler agreement with ground truth specifically on these adversarially-selected 'known blind spot' questions (versus its general agreement rate on typical questions, which you can compare against your earlier RLAIF exercise's measurement), and discuss what a genuine gap between these two rates implies about the specific danger of using RLAIF (or any same-model-family self-improvement technique) to validate correctness on topics where the base model family has a shared, systematic knowledge gap -- and what practical mitigation (e.g. using a genuinely different/independent model family as the AI labeler, or reserving human review specifically for domains with known model weaknesses) this concrete finding would motivate.",
      problemKn: "RLAIF ಬಗ್ಗೆ beginner exercise ya shared-blind-spot ಕಾಳಜಿಯನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ factual test set (ಸೆಟ್ B) ಇಂದ, ನಿಮ್ಮ policy model ಆತ್ಮವಿಶ್ವಾಸದಿಂದ ತಪ್ಪು ಉತ್ತರ ನೀಡುವ ಕೆಲವು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಪ್ರಶ್ನೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಗುರುತಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ AI-feedback labeler ಅನ್ನೂ ಬಳಸಿ ಇದನ್ನೂ ಸರಿಯಾದ ಉತ್ತರ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ತೀರ್ಮಾನಿಸಲು ಹೇಳಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ 'known blind spot' ಪ್ರಶ್ನೆಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ AI-labeler ya ground truth ಜೊತೆ ಒಪ್ಪಂದ ದರವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m194, l194_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Benchmarks as Proxies: What a Standardized Test Score Can and Can't Tell You",
      titleKn: "Proxies ಆಗಿ Benchmarks: ಒಂದು Standardized Test Score ಏನನ್ನೂ ಹೇಳಬಹುದು ಮತ್ತು ಹೇಳಲಾಗದ್ದು",
      problem: "LLM benchmarks (e.g. multiple-choice academic-knowledge tests, coding-problem suites, or math-reasoning test sets) are standardized, automatically-scoreable tasks used to compare models numerically, but every benchmark score is fundamentally a PROXY measurement -- it tells you how well a model performs on THAT specific, fixed set of test items, and only tells you something useful about the model's real-world capability to the extent that the benchmark's items are genuinely representative of the broader skill you actually care about.\n\n1) Explain the specific risk of 'benchmark contamination' -- since LLMs are trained on massive scraped internet text, and popular benchmarks are often publicly available on the internet, explain how a model could achieve a high score on a benchmark not because it has the underlying capability the benchmark intends to measure, but because it (or text very similar to the exact test items) appeared in its training data.\n\n2) Explain why a single aggregate benchmark score (e.g. '72% on benchmark X') can obscure important information about a model's actual capability profile -- what would you want to know, beyond the single aggregate number, to properly understand a model's genuine strengths and weaknesses on the underlying skill the benchmark is trying to measure?",
      problemKn: "LLM benchmarks (ಉದಾ: academic-knowledge tests, coding-problem suites, ಅಥವಾ math-reasoning test sets) ಮಾದರಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸಂಖ್ಯಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಲು ಬಳಸುವ ಸ್ಟ್ಯಾಂಡರ್ಡೈಸ್ಡ್ ಕಾರ್ಯಗಳಾಗಿವೆ, ಆದರೆ ಪ್ರತಿ benchmark score ಮೂಲಭೂತವಾಗಿ ಒಂದು PROXY ಅಳತೆ.\n\n1) 'benchmark contamination' ya ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಅಪಾಯವನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದೇ aggregate benchmark score ಒಂದು model ya ನಿಜ ಸಾಮರ್ಥ್ಯ ಪ್ರೊಫೈಲ್ ಬಗ್ಗೆ ಮುಖ್ಯ ಮಾಹಿತಿಯನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಮರೆಮಾಡಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Running a Multiple-Choice Evaluation Harness',
      titleKn: 'Multiple-Choice Evaluation Harness ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a standard LLM evaluation harness pattern (the same core mechanic used by real benchmark-running tools) on your mini-GPT.\n\n1) Genuinely construct a small multiple-choice evaluation set (10-20 questions, each with 4 answer options and one genuinely correct answer, covering a topic your mini-GPT has plausibly seen during its training).\n\n2) Genuinely implement the standard 'log-likelihood scoring' evaluation method: rather than having the model generate free-form text, format each question with each of its 4 candidate answers appended in turn, compute your mini-GPT's log-probability of each complete (question+answer) sequence, and predict whichever answer option received the HIGHEST log-probability as the model's 'choice' -- genuinely implement this scoring loop and verify it runs correctly by manually checking a couple of individual examples' computed log-probabilities.\n\n3) Genuinely compute and report your mini-GPT's overall accuracy on your evaluation set using this method, and separately genuinely test whether the ORDER in which you list the 4 answer options (e.g. correct answer always first, versus randomized position) measurably changes the model's accuracy -- report your actual measured results and discuss what any order-sensitivity you find implies about the robustness of your evaluation methodology.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಮಾಣಿತ LLM evaluation harness ಮಾದರಿಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ multiple-choice evaluation ಸೆಟ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) 'log-likelihood scoring' evaluation ವಿಧಾನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya ಒಟ್ಟು accuracy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ, ಮತ್ತು answer options ya ಕ್ರಮ accuracy ಅನ್ನೂ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Your Own Training Data',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ Training Data ನಲ್ಲಿ Benchmark Contamination ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪತ್ತೆ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run a concrete experiment testing for the benchmark-contamination risk raised in the beginner exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely take your evaluation set from the intermediate exercise and deliberately INSERT a subset of its exact question-and-correct-answer text into your mini-GPT's pretraining corpus (simulating contamination), then retrain (or continue training) your mini-GPT on this now-contaminated corpus.\n\n2) Genuinely re-run your evaluation harness on the SAME evaluation set for both the contaminated and a genuinely clean (uncontaminated) version of your model, and report the measured accuracy difference between the two.\n\n3) Genuinely implement a simple contamination-DETECTION check inspired by real practice: for each evaluation question, search your training corpus for exact or near-exact (e.g. via a simple string-overlap or n-gram matching check) matches to the question text, and report what fraction of your evaluation set you can detect as contaminated using this check -- compare this detected-contamination rate against the actual (ground-truth, since you deliberately inserted it) contamination rate, and discuss any genuine gaps between what your detection method catches and what's actually contaminated, connecting this to why real benchmark contamination is often harder to detect than this controlled experiment made it appear (e.g. paraphrased or translated contamination that a simple string-match check would miss entirely).",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ಎತ್ತಿದ benchmark-contamination ಅಪಾಯವನ್ನೂ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ evaluation ಸೆಟ್ ya ಒಂದು ಉಪವಿಭಾಗವನ್ನೂ ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ pretraining corpus ಗೆ ಸೇರಿಸಿ (contamination ಅನ್ನೂ simulate ಮಾಡಿ), ನಂತರ ಮರುtrain ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) contaminated ಮತ್ತು clean ಮಾದರಿಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದೇ evaluation ಸೆಟ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ಮರುಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ accuracy ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಸರಳ contamination-DETECTION ಪರಿಶೀಲನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m194, l194_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "LLM-as-Judge: Using a Model to Evaluate Open-Ended Generation Quality",
      titleKn: "LLM-as-Judge: Open-Ended Generation ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಲು ಒಂದು Model ಬಳಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Multiple-choice benchmarks work well for tasks with a single objectively-correct answer, but many capabilities that matter (helpfulness, writing quality, coherent multi-step reasoning) produce OPEN-ENDED free-text outputs that have no single correct answer to match against, making them hard to score automatically. LLM-as-Judge addresses this by using a separate (often larger/more capable) language model, explicitly prompted with grading criteria, to read a generated response and output a quality score or a preference between two candidate responses -- essentially automating what would otherwise require human raters.\n\n1) Explain why LLM-as-Judge is generally considered a reasonable (if imperfect) substitute for human evaluation on open-ended tasks, given that both a human judge and an LLM judge are ultimately applying subjective judgment against stated criteria rather than checking against one objectively-correct reference answer.\n\n2) Explain a specific, well-documented bias of LLM judges: POSITION BIAS, where a judge model shown two candidate responses tends to systematically favor whichever response appears FIRST (or in some models, second) in the prompt, independent of the responses' actual quality -- why does this bias make raw, unmitigated pairwise LLM-judge comparisons unreliable, and what simple experimental control would let you detect whether your specific judge setup exhibits this bias?",
      problemKn: "Multiple-choice benchmarks ಒಂದೇ ವಸ್ತುನಿಷ್ಠ-ಸರಿಯಾದ ಉತ್ತರ ಇರುವ ಕಾರ್ಯಗಳಿಗೆ ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿ ಕೆಲಸ ಮಾಡುತ್ತವೆ, ಆದರೆ ಅನೇಕ ಮುಖ್ಯ ಸಾಮರ್ಥ್ಯಗಳು OPEN-ENDED free-text outputs ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತವೆ. LLM-as-Judge ಇದನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಒಂದು ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ model ಅನ್ನೂ ಗ್ರೇಡಿಂಗ್ ಮಾನದಂಡಗಳೊಂದಿಗೆ prompt ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) LLM-as-Judge ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ಸಮಂಜಸ ಪರ್ಯಾಯ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) POSITION BIAS ಎಂಬ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ, ದಾಖಲಿತ bias ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing an LLM-as-Judge Pipeline and Testing for Position Bias',
      titleKn: 'LLM-as-Judge Pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Position Bias ಗಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement an LLM-as-Judge pairwise comparison pipeline (using your mini-GPT if capable of following grading instructions, or a larger accessible model purely for the judging role, while you genuinely implement and run every pipeline step yourself).\n\n1) Genuinely write an explicit grading prompt template that presents a user instruction and two candidate responses (labeled 'Response A' and 'Response B'), states clear grading criteria (e.g. helpfulness and correctness), and asks the judge to output which response is better and why.\n\n2) Genuinely run this judge on at least 10 response pairs where you already know (from your own honest assessment) which response is genuinely better, and report the judge's measured AGREEMENT rate with your own ground-truth judgment.\n\n3) Genuinely test for position bias directly: for the SAME 10 pairs, run the judge TWICE per pair -- once with the better response as 'Response A' and once with the SAME better response relabeled as 'Response B' (i.e. swapping the two responses' positions) -- and report what fraction of pairs the judge's verdict FLIPPED when only the position changed (not the content), which would be direct, concrete evidence of the position bias the beginner exercise described.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು LLM-as-Judge pairwise comparison pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ ಗ್ರೇಡಿಂಗ್ ಮಾನದಂಡಗಳೊಂದಿಗೆ ಒಂದು grading prompt template ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬರೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 10 response pairs ಮೇಲೆ ಈ judge ಅನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ground-truth ಜೊತೆ ಒಪ್ಪಂದ ದರ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) position bias ಅನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ: ಪ್ರತಿ pair ಗೆ judge ಅನ್ನೂ ಎರಡು ಬಾರಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, positions ಅದಲುಬದಲು ಮಾಡಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಎಷ್ಟು verdicts flip ಆದವು ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Mitigating Judge Biases and Measuring the Improvement',
      titleKn: 'Judge Biases ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತಗ್ಗಿಸಿ ಸುಧಾರಣೆ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design, implement, and measure the effect of a concrete mitigation for the position bias you demonstrated in the intermediate exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a debiasing scheme: for each comparison, run the judge TWICE (once in each position order, as in the intermediate exercise) and combine the two verdicts using a simple rule (e.g. only count a response as the genuine winner if it wins in BOTH position orders; treat a disagreement between the two orders as a 'tie' rather than trusting either single verdict).\n\n2) Genuinely re-run this debiased scheme on your same 10 (or more) test pairs from the intermediate exercise, and report the new measured agreement rate with your own ground-truth judgment, comparing it directly against the single-position-order agreement rate you measured before.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, using your own concrete before/after numbers, whether this debiasing approach genuinely improved judge reliability, what it COST you to get that improvement (specifically, twice the judge inference calls per comparison, plus a fraction of pairs now falling into an unresolved 'tie' bucket that needs some other resolution strategy), and why real production LLM-evaluation pipelines (e.g. those used to benchmark and compare frontier models) build in exactly this kind of position-swapping and other bias-control machinery rather than trusting a single raw judge call.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ನಲ್ಲಿ ತೋರಿಸಿದ position bias ಗೆ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಪರಿಹಾರವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ ಪರಿಣಾಮವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು debiasing ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ಪ್ರತಿ ಹೋಲಿಕೆಗೆ judge ಅನ್ನೂ ಎರಡು ಬಾರಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ debiased ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ಮರುಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹೊಸ ಒಪ್ಪಂದ ದರ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಇದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸುಧಾರಿಸಿತೇ, ಏನು ವೆಚ್ಚವಾಯಿತು ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m194, l194_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Building a Custom Eval Suite: Task Selection, Held-Out Data, and Regression Tracking',
      titleKn: 'ಸ್ವಂತ Eval Suite ನಿರ್ಮಾಣ: Task Selection, Held-Out Data, ಮತ್ತು Regression Tracking',
      problem: "Real production LLM teams typically build CUSTOM evaluation suites tailored to their specific product's needs, in addition to (not instead of) running standard public benchmarks -- because public benchmarks, however useful for cross-model comparison, rarely test the SPECIFIC tasks, edge cases, and failure modes that matter most for a particular application.\n\n1) Explain why a custom eval suite needs its own genuinely HELD-OUT data, separate from any data used in fine-tuning or prompt-engineering iteration -- what specific failure mode (analogous to overfitting) would arise if a team iterated on their fine-tuning or prompts by repeatedly checking performance against the exact same eval set they use to report final quality numbers?\n\n2) Explain the purpose of REGRESSION TESTING in an eval suite -- specifically, why real teams keep a growing, permanent collection of specific past failure cases (each time a bug or bad output is found in production, it gets added as a new eval example) rather than only running eval sets that were designed once at the start of the project.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಜ production LLM ತಂಡಗಳು ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ ತಮ್ಮ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ product ಅಗತ್ಯಗಳಿಗೆ ಅನುಗುಣವಾಗಿ CUSTOM evaluation suites ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು custom eval suite ಗೆ ತನ್ನದೇ ಆದ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ HELD-OUT data ಏಕೆ ಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) eval suite ನಲ್ಲಿ REGRESSION TESTING ya ಉದ್ದೇಶವನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Building a Custom Eval Suite with Regression Cases for Your Mini-GPT',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Mini-GPT ಗೆ Regression Cases ಜೊತೆ ಸ್ವಂತ Eval Suite ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely build a small custom evaluation suite for your own mini-GPT (across the phases of this course), combining several task types you've cared about across earlier lessons.\n\n1) Genuinely assemble a held-out eval set (separate from any SFT/DPO/RLHF training data you've used) spanning at least 3 distinct categories relevant to your model (e.g. instruction-following, factual recall, and a specific failure mode you've personally observed while testing your model in earlier lessons).\n\n2) Genuinely implement a 'regression case' mechanism: actively try to find at least 3 GENUINE current failures of your model (prompts where it produces a clearly wrong, incoherent, or undesirable output right now) through your own testing, and add each as a permanent, named eval case with its expected/desired behavior documented.\n\n3) Genuinely run your full custom eval suite against your CURRENT model checkpoint and report the results category-by-category, then genuinely re-run the SAME suite against an EARLIER checkpoint of your model (e.g. before your most recent fine-tuning step) to verify your regression cases actually function as regression tests -- report whether the regression cases you added correctly show the earlier checkpoint failing them (confirming they're genuine, discriminating tests) rather than passing trivially regardless of model quality.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ mini-GPT ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ custom evaluation suite ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 3 ವಿಭಿನ್ನ categories ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಒಂದು held-out eval ಸೆಟ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜೋಡಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು 'regression case' ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ನಿಮ್ಮ model ya ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 3 ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ ವೈಫಲ್ಯಗಳನ್ನೂ ಕಂಡುಹಿಡಿಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ custom eval suite ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ ಮತ್ತು ಹಿಂದಿನ checkpoint ಎರಡರ ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Auditing Your Own Eval Suite for Coverage Gaps and False Confidence',
      titleKn: 'Coverage Gaps ಮತ್ತು False Confidence ಗಾಗಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ Eval Suite ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Audit ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely stress-test your own custom eval suite from the intermediate exercise for a subtle but genuine danger: an eval suite that reports reassuringly high scores while still missing significant categories of real failure, giving FALSE CONFIDENCE in the model's quality.\n\n1) Genuinely conduct an adversarial self-audit: spend focused effort trying to find NEW failure modes in your CURRENT model checkpoint that are NOT covered by any existing case in your eval suite -- explore categories genuinely different from your existing eval categories (e.g. if your suite tests instruction-following and factual recall, try testing something you haven't yet covered, such as consistency across a multi-turn conversation, or handling of ambiguous/underspecified prompts).\n\n2) Genuinely report how many NEW, genuinely uncovered failure modes you found through this adversarial search, and add the most significant ones as new permanent regression cases in your suite (following the same process as the intermediate exercise).\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, based on how easily (or how hard) it was for you to find new uncovered failures, what this implies about the relationship between a 'passing' eval suite score and genuine confidence in a model's real-world quality -- specifically, discuss why the ABSENCE of a failure case for some behavior in an eval suite is fundamentally different from genuine EVIDENCE that the model handles that behavior correctly, and what ongoing practice (e.g. continuous adversarial probing, incorporating real user-reported issues) would be needed to keep a custom eval suite's coverage genuinely representative over time as a model and its usage evolve.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ custom eval suite ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಸೂಕ್ಷ್ಮ ಆದರೆ ನಿಜ ಅಪಾಯಕ್ಕಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಒತ್ತಡ-ಪರೀಕ್ಷೆ ಮಾಡಿ: high scores ವರದಿ ಮಾಡುತ್ತಿರುವ ಒಂದು eval suite ಇನ್ನೂ ನಿಜ ವೈಫಲ್ಯದ ಮಹತ್ವದ ವರ್ಗಗಳನ್ನೂ ತಪ್ಪಿಸಿಕೊಳ್ಳಬಹುದು.\n\n1) ಒಂದು adversarial self-audit ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಡೆಸಿ: ನಿಮ್ಮ eval suite ಒಳಗೊಳ್ಳದ ಹೊಸ ವೈಫಲ್ಯ ವಿಧಾನಗಳನ್ನೂ ಕಂಡುಹಿಡಿಯಲು ಪ್ರಯತ್ನಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಎಷ್ಟು ಹೊಸ, ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಒಳಗೊಳ್ಳದ ವೈಫಲ್ಯಗಳನ್ನೂ ಕಂಡುಕೊಂಡಿರಿ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅವನ್ನೂ suite ಗೆ ಸೇರಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಇದು 'passing' eval score ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟದ ವಿಶ್ವಾಸದ ನಡುವಿನ ಸಂಬಂಧದ ಬಗ್ಗೆ ಏನನ್ನೂ ಸೂಚಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m195, l195_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Quantization Basics: Trading Numeric Precision for Memory and Speed",
      titleKn: "Quantization ಮೂಲಭೂತಗಳು: Memory ಮತ್ತು Speed ಗಾಗಿ Numeric Precision ವಿನಿಮಯ",
      problem: "Quantization reduces the numeric precision used to store a model's weights (and sometimes its activations) -- for example, converting weights from 16-bit floating point down to 8-bit integers (INT8) or even lower -- which directly shrinks the model's memory footprint (an INT8 weight takes half the storage of an FP16 weight) and can speed up computation on hardware with fast low-precision arithmetic, at the cost of some numeric approximation error introduced into every weight value.\n\n1) Explain concretely how INT8 quantization works at a basic level: given a range of floating-point weight values, explain how a SCALE factor (and possibly a zero-point offset) lets you map that continuous range onto the 256 discrete integer values INT8 can represent, and explain why this mapping necessarily introduces some rounding error for most original weight values.\n\n2) Explain why quantization error doesn't affect all weights or all layers equally -- specifically, why might a layer whose weights have a large DYNAMIC RANGE (values spanning from very small to very large magnitudes) suffer more quantization error than a layer whose weights are more tightly clustered, given that the same fixed number of discrete integer levels must cover the full range in both cases?",
      problemKn: "Quantization ಒಂದು model ya weights (ಮತ್ತು ಕೆಲವೊಮ್ಮೆ ಅದರ activations) ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸಲು ಬಳಸುವ numeric precision ಅನ್ನೂ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ. ಇದು model ya memory footprint ಅನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಕುಗ್ಗಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) INT8 quantization ಒಂದು ಮೂಲಭೂತ ಮಟ್ಟದಲ್ಲಿ ಹೇಗೆ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) quantization error ಎಲ್ಲಾ weights ಅಥವಾ layers ಗೆ ಸಮಾನವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Measuring INT8 Weight Quantization',
      titleKn: 'INT8 Weight Quantization ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement basic INT8 weight quantization on your mini-GPT and measure its real effect.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a quantization function that, for a given weight matrix, computes an appropriate per-tensor scale factor (e.g. based on the maximum absolute weight value), converts the floating-point weights to INT8 integers using that scale, and implement a matching DEQUANTIZATION function that converts back to floating point for use in computation.\n\n2) Genuinely apply this quantize-dequantize round-trip to every weight matrix in your mini-GPT, and genuinely measure the actual reconstruction error introduced (e.g. mean absolute difference between original and dequantized weights) for at least three different layers/matrices, reporting whether some layers show measurably more error than others (connecting to the beginner exercise's dynamic-range reasoning).\n\n3) Genuinely run your quantized (quantize-dequantize round-tripped) mini-GPT on a held-out evaluation set (reusing your evaluation harness from the earlier lesson in this phase) and compare its measured accuracy/perplexity against the original, unquantized model -- report your actual measured quality degradation, if any, from this basic quantization scheme.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ಮೂಲಭೂತ INT8 weight quantization ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ ನಿಜ ಪರಿಣಾಮವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು quantization function ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹೊಂದಾಣಿಕೆಯ dequantization function ಅನ್ನೂ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ round-trip ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ weight matrix ಗೆ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ reconstruction error ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಕ್ವಾಂಟೈಸ್ಡ್ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು held-out evaluation ಸೆಟ್ ಮೇಲೆ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ಹದಗೆಡುವಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing Per-Tensor vs Per-Channel Quantization Granularity',
      titleKn: 'Per-Tensor vs Per-Channel Quantization Granularity ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run a comparison between two quantization GRANULARITIES on your mini-GPT: PER-TENSOR quantization (a single scale factor for an entire weight matrix, as you implemented in the intermediate exercise) versus PER-CHANNEL quantization (a separate scale factor computed independently for each output channel/row of a weight matrix, allowing channels with different dynamic ranges to each get their own well-fitted scale).\n\n1) Genuinely implement per-channel quantization: for each weight matrix, compute a separate scale factor per output-channel (row), quantize each channel's values using its own scale, and implement the corresponding per-channel dequantization.\n\n2) Genuinely measure and compare the reconstruction error (as in the intermediate exercise) between per-tensor and per-channel quantization on the SAME weight matrices, reporting whether per-channel genuinely reduces error, and by how much, for the specific layers you tested.\n\n3) Genuinely run both quantized versions of your mini-GPT through your evaluation harness and report the measured accuracy/perplexity difference between per-tensor and per-channel quantization -- discuss, using your own concrete measured numbers, why real quantization libraries (e.g. those implementing GPTQ or AWQ) generally default to per-channel (or even finer-grained per-group) quantization despite the extra bookkeeping of storing multiple scale factors, connecting your finding back to the dynamic-range variation you observed across different weight matrices/channels.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ಎರಡು quantization GRANULARITIES ನಡುವೆ ಒಂದು ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ: PER-TENSOR ಮತ್ತು PER-CHANNEL quantization.\n\n1) per-channel quantization ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) reconstruction error ಅನ್ನೂ ಎರಡರ ನಡುವೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡೂ ಕ್ವಾಂಟೈಸ್ಡ್ ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m195, l195_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "GPTQ and AWQ: Calibration-Aware Quantization That Corrects for Error",
      titleKn: "GPTQ ಮತ್ತು AWQ: Error ಗಾಗಿ ಸರಿಪಡಿಸುವ Calibration-Aware Quantization",
      problem: "The naive quantization from the previous lesson (compute a scale, round every weight independently) treats every weight as equally important, but real post-training quantization methods like GPTQ and AWQ instead use a small CALIBRATION dataset (a handful of representative input examples run through the model) to make smarter decisions. GPTQ quantizes weights one at a time (or in small groups) and, after quantizing each, adjusts the REMAINING not-yet-quantized weights in that layer to compensate for the error just introduced, using calibration-data activation statistics to guide this correction. AWQ instead observes (via calibration data) which weight CHANNELS receive activations with unusually large magnitude, and protects those specific 'salient' channels by scaling them to be quantized more precisely, since errors on high-activation channels affect the output disproportionately.\n\n1) Explain why GPTQ's sequential compensate-as-you-go approach can achieve lower overall error than independently rounding every weight (as in naive quantization), even though both ultimately produce integer-quantized weights -- what information does GPTQ exploit that naive quantization ignores?\n\n2) Explain AWQ's core insight in your own words: why would protecting the precision of weight channels that receive large-magnitude activations matter MORE for final output quality than protecting channels whose activations are typically small, even if the RAW weight values themselves aren't obviously different in magnitude?",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ಸರಳ quantization ಪ್ರತಿ weight ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಮಾನವಾಗಿ ಮುಖ್ಯ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಗಣಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದರೆ GPTQ ಮತ್ತು AWQ ನಂತಹ ನಿಜ ವಿಧಾನಗಳು ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ CALIBRATION dataset ಬಳಸಿ ಬುದ್ಧಿವಂತ ನಿರ್ಧಾರಗಳನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತವೆ. GPTQ ಪ್ರತಿ weight ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದೊಂದಾಗಿ ಕ್ವಾಂಟೈಸ್ ಮಾಡಿ ಉಳಿದ weights ಅನ್ನೂ ಸರಿಹೊಂದಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. AWQ ದೊಡ್ಡ activations ಪಡೆಯುವ channels ಅನ್ನೂ ರಕ್ಷಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) GPTQ ya sequential ವಿಧಾನ ಏಕೆ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಒಟ್ಟು error ಸಾಧಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) AWQ ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಒಳನೋಟವನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪದಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Simplified Calibration-Based Channel Protection Scheme',
      titleKn: 'ಸರಳೀಕೃತ Calibration-Based Channel Protection ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified version of AWQ's salient-channel-protection idea on your mini-GPT.\n\n1) Genuinely run a small calibration set (e.g. 20-50 example sequences) through your mini-GPT and genuinely record, for one chosen weight matrix, the actual activation magnitudes flowing into each input channel across the calibration set -- identify which channels genuinely receive the largest-magnitude activations on average.\n\n2) Genuinely implement a protection scheme: apply your per-channel quantization from the previous lesson normally to most channels, but for the top-K salient channels you identified (by activation magnitude), genuinely apply a scaling correction (e.g. temporarily scale up those channels' weights before quantizing, and scale back down after dequantizing, so their effective quantization step size is finer) to reduce their quantization error specifically.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and report the output-level error (e.g. difference in the layer's output activations on calibration data, comparing your salient-channel-protected quantization against uniform per-channel quantization with no special protection) -- report whether protecting salient channels genuinely reduced output error more than an equal quantization 'budget' spent uniformly, confirming or disconfirming AWQ's core claim in your own concrete experiment.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ AWQ ya salient-channel-protection ಕಲ್ಪನೆಯ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ ಆವೃತ್ತಿಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ calibration ಸೆಟ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು activation magnitudes ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ದಾಖಲಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು protection ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, top-K salient channels ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಕ್ವಾಂಟೈಸ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) output-level error ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Benchmarking Quantization Methods Across the Bits-vs-Quality Curve',
      titleKn: 'Bits-vs-Quality Curve ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Quantization Methods ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Benchmark ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run a comprehensive comparison across all the quantization approaches you've built in this module (naive per-tensor, per-channel, and your salient-channel-protected scheme), at multiple bit-widths if feasible (e.g. INT8 and a lower bit-width like INT4, computing the scale/rounding math for 4-bit integers analogously to your INT8 implementation).\n\n1) Genuinely quantize your mini-GPT using each method/bit-width combination you can feasibly implement, and genuinely evaluate each resulting model on your evaluation harness from the earlier evaluation lesson, recording accuracy/perplexity for every combination.\n\n2) Genuinely plot or tabulate your own measured results as a 'bits vs quality' comparison -- report, using your own concrete numbers, whether lower bit-widths consistently produce worse quality (as expected) and whether your more sophisticated methods (per-channel, salient-protection) genuinely 'buy back' quality at the same bit-width compared to naive per-tensor quantization, or at a LOWER bit-width compared to what naive quantization would need to hit the same quality bar.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, based on your own measured curve, how a real practitioner should choose a specific quantization method and bit-width for a specific deployment (e.g. a memory-constrained edge device needing maximum compression, versus a server deployment where a modest memory saving with near-zero quality loss is preferred) -- grounding your recommendation in your own concrete measured tradeoff data rather than general claims from the literature.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ನಲ್ಲಿ ನೀವು ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಎಲ್ಲಾ quantization ವಿಧಾನಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಒಂದು ಸಮಗ್ರ ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, ಸಾಧ್ಯವಾದಷ್ಟು ಬಹು bit-widths ಗಳಲ್ಲಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ ವಿಧಾನ/bit-width ಸಂಯೋಜನೆ ಬಳಸಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಕ್ವಾಂಟೈಸ್ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ 'bits vs quality' ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ನಿಜ practitioner ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ deployment ಗೆ ಯಾವ ವಿಧಾನ ಮತ್ತು bit-width ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಮಾಡಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ ಡೇಟಾ ಆಧರಿಸಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m195, l195_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'GGUF and Deployment Formats: Packaging a Quantized Model for Real Use',
      titleKn: 'GGUF ಮತ್ತು Deployment Formats: ಕ್ವಾಂಟೈಸ್ಡ್ Model ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜ ಬಳಕೆಗೆ Package ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "GGUF (used by the llama.cpp ecosystem) is a single-file model format specifically designed for efficient LOCAL inference of quantized LLMs -- it bundles the model's quantized weights, the tokenizer, and metadata (architecture hyperparameters, quantization scheme used) into one self-contained file that an inference program can load and run without needing the original training framework or separate config files.\n\n1) Explain why bundling the tokenizer and architecture metadata INSIDE the same file as the weights (rather than as separate files, which is common in training-framework-native formats) is specifically valuable for a format meant for wide redistribution and local deployment -- what practical failure mode does this bundling prevent, especially when a quantized model file gets shared and used across many different machines or contexts?\n\n2) Explain why GGUF files are typically named with the specific quantization scheme encoded in the filename (e.g. distinguishing different bit-widths and per-channel-vs-per-group quantization variants) -- given everything you learned in the previous lessons about how different quantization methods and bit-widths produce measurably different quality/size tradeoffs, why would a user downloading a pre-quantized model need this information visible up front, before even loading the file?",
      problemKn: "GGUF (llama.cpp ecosystem ಬಳಸುತ್ತದೆ) ಎಂಬುದೂ ಕ್ವಾಂಟೈಸ್ಡ್ LLMs ya ಸಮರ್ಥ LOCAL inference ಗಾಗಿ ವಿಶೇಷವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿದ ಒಂದೇ-ಫೈಲ್ model format ಆಗಿದೆ.\n\n1) tokenizer ಮತ್ತು architecture metadata ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ ಫೈಲ್ ಒಳಗೆ ಬಂಡಲ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಮೌಲ್ಯಯುತ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) GGUF ಫೈಲ್‌ಗಳು ಫೈಲ್‌ಹೆಸರಿನಲ್ಲಿ ಕ್ವಾಂಟೈಸೇಶನ್ ಯೋಜನೆ ಎನ್ಕೋಡ್ ಮಾಡಿ ಏಕೆ ಹೆಸರಿಸಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Building a Minimal Self-Contained Model Package for Your Mini-GPT',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Mini-GPT ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಕನಿಷ್ಠ Self-Contained Model Package ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and build your own minimal single-file packaging format for your quantized mini-GPT, inspired by GGUF's design goals (though far simpler).\n\n1) Genuinely implement a serialization function that writes, into a SINGLE file, your mini-GPT's quantized weights (from one of your quantization schemes in this module), the per-tensor or per-channel scale factors needed to dequantize them, your tokenizer's vocabulary/merge rules, and the architecture hyperparameters (layer count, hidden size, number of heads, etc.) needed to reconstruct the model class -- choose a concrete serialization approach (e.g. a simple binary format with a header specifying byte offsets, or a structured format like a single JSON+binary-blob combination) and genuinely implement both the writer and a matching reader.\n\n2) Genuinely verify your package format works by writing your quantized model to this single file, then LOADING it back using ONLY your reader function and the file itself (not any of your original in-memory objects), reconstructing a working model, and confirming its generated outputs for a test prompt EXACTLY match the outputs of your original (pre-serialization) quantized model.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and report the actual file size of your packaged model, comparing it against the size of your original unquantized model's weights, and discuss what additional metadata (that you may have skipped for simplicity) real formats like GGUF include that your minimal version does not.",
      problemKn: "GGUF ya ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ಗುರಿಗಳಿಂದ ಪ್ರೇರಿತ, ನಿಮ್ಮ ಕ್ವಾಂಟೈಸ್ಡ್ mini-GPT ಗೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಕನಿಷ್ಠ single-file packaging format ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಕ್ವಾಂಟೈಸ್ಡ್ weights, scale factors, tokenizer, ಮತ್ತು architecture hyperparameters ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದೇ ಫೈಲ್ ಗೆ ಬರೆಯುವ serialization function ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಫೈಲ್ ಇಂದ ಮಾತ್ರ ಮಾದರಿಯನ್ನೂ ಮರುನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು outputs ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಹೊಂದಿಕೆಯಾಗುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಫೈಲ್ size ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring End-to-End Load Time and Memory Across Format Choices',
      titleKn: 'Format Choices ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ End-to-End Load Time ಮತ್ತು Memory ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment measuring the practical deployment benefit of your packaged format from the intermediate exercise, versus a naive 'separate files' alternative.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a naive alternative loading path that reconstructs your mini-GPT from SEPARATE files (e.g. weights in one file via a standard tensor-serialization method, tokenizer data in another, architecture config in a third, as many training-framework-native formats do), and genuinely measure the actual wall-clock time to fully load a working, ready-to-generate model from this separate-files approach versus from your single-file packaged format.\n\n2) Genuinely measure peak memory usage during the loading process for both approaches, reporting any genuine difference you observe.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, using your own concrete measured numbers, whether your single-file format provided any genuine loading-speed or memory benefit over the separate-files approach in your specific implementation, or whether (as may genuinely be the case for a simple implementation) the format itself made little measurable difference and the REAL benefit of formats like GGUF is more about deployment SIMPLICITY and portability (one file to distribute and verify, no risk of mismatched component versions) than raw loading performance -- ground your conclusion in what you actually measured, not in what the literature claims.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ packaged format ya ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ deployment ಪ್ರಯೋಜನವನ್ನೂ, ಒಂದು ಸರಳ 'separate files' ಪರ್ಯಾಯದ ವಿರುದ್ಧ, ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಸರಳ ಪರ್ಯಾಯ loading path ಅನ್ನೂ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಎರಡರ ನಡುವೆ ಲೋಡ್ ಸಮಯವನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n2) peak memory usage ಅನ್ನೂ ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ single-file format ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ ನೀಡಿತೇ ಅಥವಾ ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ deployment ಸರಳತೆಯಲ್ಲಿ ಇದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಅಳತೆಗಳ ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m196, l196_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "The KV Cache: Avoiding Redundant Recomputation During Autoregressive Generation",
      titleKn: "KV Cache: Autoregressive Generation ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಪುನರಾವರ್ತಿತ ಲೆಕ್ಕಾಚಾರ ತಪ್ಪಿಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Autoregressive text generation produces one token at a time, feeding each new token back in to produce the next -- naively, this would mean recomputing the attention Key and Value projections for EVERY previous token in the sequence at EVERY single generation step, even though those previous tokens' Key/Value vectors never change once computed. The KV CACHE optimization avoids this waste: it stores each token's Key and Value vectors (for every layer) the FIRST time they're computed, and on every subsequent generation step, only computes Key/Value for the ONE new token, reusing the cached values for all previous tokens directly.\n\n1) Explain precisely why previous tokens' Key and Value vectors never change as generation proceeds -- given how self-attention computes Keys and Values purely from a token's own embedding and position (not from later tokens), why is caching them safe and correct, rather than an approximation?\n\n2) Explain why the KV cache does NOT eliminate the need to recompute the Query vector and the actual attention computation at every step -- what specifically about the query and the final attention-weighted output still depends on the newly-generated token, even though the past Keys/Values themselves are reused unchanged?",
      problemKn: "Autoregressive text generation ಒಂದು ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದು token ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. ಸರಳವಾಗಿ, ಇದು ಪ್ರತಿ generation step ನಲ್ಲಿ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಹಿಂದಿನ tokens ಗೆ Key ಮತ್ತು Value projections ಅನ್ನೂ ಮರುಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುವುದೂ ಅರ್ಥ, ಆದರೂ ಆ ವೆಕ್ಟರ್ಗಳು ಎಂದಿಗೂ ಬದಲಾಗುವುದಿಲ್ಲ. KV CACHE optimization ಈ ವ್ಯರ್ಥವನ್ನೂ ತಪ್ಪಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ tokens ya Key ಮತ್ತು Value ವೆಕ್ಟರ್ಗಳು ಏಕೆ ಎಂದಿಗೂ ಬದಲಾಗುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) KV cache ಏಕೆ ಪ್ರತಿ step ನಲ್ಲಿ Query ವೆಕ್ಟರ್ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ attention ಲೆಕ್ಕಾಚಾರವನ್ನೂ ಮರುಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುವ ಅಗತ್ಯವನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಹಾಕುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a KV Cache and Measuring Its Speedup',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು KV Cache ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ Speedup ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement KV caching in your mini-GPT's generation loop and genuinely measure its real speed impact.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a cache data structure that stores, per attention layer, the Key and Value tensors computed so far, and modify your generation loop so that at each new step, only the new token's Query/Key/Value are computed, the new Key/Value are appended to the cache, and attention is computed using the cached Keys/Values for all past tokens plus the new one.\n\n2) Genuinely verify correctness: run generation with your KV-cache implementation and, separately, run the SAME prompt through your original naive (full-recomputation-every-step) generation code, and confirm both produce IDENTICAL generated tokens (since KV caching is a pure optimization, not an approximation) -- report this exact-match verification result.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and report actual wall-clock generation time (for generating a reasonably long sequence, e.g. 200+ tokens) with versus without your KV cache, reporting your measured speedup factor, and discuss how you'd expect this speedup factor to change as the generated sequence gets LONGER, based on how the naive approach's redundant recomputation cost scales with sequence length.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya generation loop ನಲ್ಲಿ KV caching ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ ನಿಜ speed ಪ್ರಭಾವವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ attention layer ಗೆ ಈಗಾಗಲೇ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿದ Key/Value tensors ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸುವ ಒಂದು cache data structure ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ KV-cache implementation ಮತ್ತು ಮೂಲ naive implementation ಎರಡೂ ಒಂದೇ tokens ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) actual wall-clock generation time ಅನ್ನೂ ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು speedup factor ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring KV Cache Memory Cost and the Length-Scaling Tradeoff',
      titleKn: 'KV Cache Memory ವೆಚ್ಚ ಮತ್ತು Length-Scaling Tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "The KV cache's speed benefit comes with a genuine memory cost that grows as generation proceeds -- unlike model weights (a fixed size), the KV cache grows with EVERY new generated token, since each token's Key/Value vectors must be stored for every layer for the entire remaining generation. Genuinely investigate this cost concretely.\n\n1) Genuinely instrument your KV-cache implementation from the intermediate exercise to measure the ACTUAL memory (in number of stored values, or bytes if you track a dtype) consumed by the cache at several points during a long generation (e.g. at 50, 100, 200, and 400 generated tokens), and report how cache memory genuinely scales as a function of sequence length -- confirm whether it grows linearly, and calculate the actual per-token memory cost for your mini-GPT's specific architecture (number of layers, heads, head dimension).\n\n2) Genuinely calculate, by extrapolating your measured per-token cache cost, what sequence length would be needed for the KV cache to reach a specific memory budget you choose (e.g. equal to the size of the model's weights themselves) -- report this concrete calculated length.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss why this measured, linearly-growing memory cost is precisely why real long-context LLM serving is a genuinely hard systems problem distinct from short-context serving, and why it directly motivates techniques covered elsewhere in this phase (e.g. multi-query/grouped-query attention reducing KV cache size per token, or paged/chunked KV cache memory management) -- ground your discussion in your own concrete measured numbers rather than general claims.",
      problemKn: "KV cache ya speed ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ generation ಮುಂದುವರೆದಂತೆ ಬೆಳೆಯುವ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ memory ವೆಚ್ಚದೊಂದಿಗೆ ಬರುತ್ತದೆ. ಇದನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ KV-cache implementation ಅನ್ನೂ instrument ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು cache ya ನಿಜ memory ಬಳಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ಹಲವಾರು ಬಿಂದುಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ, sequence length ya ಒಂದು function ಆಗಿ ಹೇಗೆ scale ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ memory budget ತಲುಪಲು ಬೇಕಾದ sequence length ಅನ್ನೂ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ, ರೇಖೀಯವಾಗಿ ಬೆಳೆಯುವ memory ವೆಚ್ಚ ಏಕೆ ನಿಜ long-context LLM serving ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಕಷ್ಟದ systems ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯಾಗಿ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m196, l196_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Continuous Batching: Keeping the GPU Busy Across Requests of Different Lengths",
      titleKn: "Continuous Batching: ವಿಭಿನ್ನ Lengths ya Requests ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ GPU ಅನ್ನೂ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರತವಾಗಿಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "A naive LLM-serving approach batches requests statically: wait for a fixed number of requests to arrive, run them all through generation together, and only start a new batch once every request in the current batch has finished generating -- but since different requests generate different numbers of tokens before finishing (e.g. one request might need a one-sentence answer while another needs a full essay), static batching wastes GPU capacity waiting for the SLOWEST request in each batch to finish before any new request can be admitted. CONTINUOUS BATCHING (also called dynamic or in-flight batching) instead allows individual requests to be added to and removed from an in-progress batch at every generation step -- as soon as one request finishes, a new waiting request can immediately take its place in the very next step, without waiting for the whole batch to complete.\n\n1) Explain concretely why static batching's GPU utilization degrades as the VARIANCE in requests' output lengths increases -- what happens to overall throughput in a static batch containing one very-short-output request and one very-long-output request, compared to a batch where all requests happen to need similar output lengths?\n\n2) Explain why continuous batching is significantly more complex to implement than static batching -- what specific bookkeeping challenge does it introduce, given that different requests in the same in-progress batch may now be at COMPLETELY different generation steps (and thus need different amounts of KV cache) at any given moment?",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಸರಳ LLM-serving ವಿಧಾನ requests ಅನ್ನೂ ಸ್ಥಿರವಾಗಿ batch ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ: ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯ requests ಬರುವವರೆಗೆ ಕಾಯಿರಿ, ಎಲ್ಲವನ್ನೂ ಒಟ್ಟಿಗೆ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ. ಆದರೆ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ requests ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯ tokens ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುವುದರಿಂದ, static batching GPU ಸಾಮರ್ಥ್ಯವನ್ನೂ ವ್ಯರ್ಥ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ. CONTINUOUS BATCHING ಬದಲಿಗೆ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ requests ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ step ನಲ್ಲಿ ಸೇರಿಸಲು/ತೆಗೆಯಲು ಅನುಮತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) static batching ya GPU utilization variance ಹೆಚ್ಚಾದಂತೆ ಏಕೆ ಹದಗೆಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) continuous batching ಏಕೆ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಲು ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಸಂಕೀರ್ಣ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Benchmarking a Continuous Batching Scheduler',
      titleKn: 'Continuous Batching Scheduler ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Benchmark ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified continuous-batching scheduler for your mini-GPT (using your KV-cache implementation from the previous lesson) and genuinely measure its throughput benefit over static batching.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a static-batching baseline: given a queue of incoming requests (prompts) with varying desired output lengths, form fixed-size batches, run each batch to completion (until every request in it has generated its full desired output), and only then start the next batch -- genuinely measure total wall-clock time to process a queue of, say, 20 requests with deliberately varied output lengths.\n\n2) Genuinely implement a continuous-batching scheduler: maintain an active batch slot set, and at every generation step, check for any FINISHED requests (whose desired length has been reached) and immediately replace them with the NEXT waiting request from the queue (which starts its own KV cache from scratch while other slots continue their in-progress generation) -- genuinely handle the bookkeeping of tracking each active slot's own independent KV cache and remaining-length state.\n\n3) Genuinely measure total wall-clock time to process the SAME queue of 20 requests using your continuous-batching scheduler, and report the actual measured speedup (or lack thereof) versus your static-batching baseline, discussing whether your measured result matches the theoretical benefit described in the beginner exercise.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ continuous-batching scheduler ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು static-batching baseline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಟ್ಟು wall-clock ಸಮಯವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು continuous-batching scheduler ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಪ್ರತಿ active slot ya ಸ್ವಂತ KV cache ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಅದೇ queue ಮೇಲೆ ಒಟ್ಟು ಸಮಯ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು speedup ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Continuous Batching Gains Across Different Length-Variance Regimes',
      titleKn: 'ವಿಭಿನ್ನ Length-Variance Regimes ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Continuous Batching Gains ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment that directly tests the beginner exercise's claim that continuous batching's advantage GROWS as request-length variance increases.\n\n1) Genuinely construct at least three different test request queues (same total number of requests each time) with deliberately different output-length distributions: queue A with LOW variance (all requests need very similar output lengths), queue B with MODERATE variance, and queue C with HIGH variance (a mix of very short and very long requests).\n\n2) Genuinely run BOTH your static-batching and continuous-batching implementations from the intermediate exercise on all three queues, and genuinely measure total wall-clock completion time for each of the 6 resulting runs (2 schedulers x 3 queues).\n\n3) Genuinely report the measured speedup of continuous-batching over static-batching SEPARATELY for each queue's variance level, and confirm or disconfirm whether your own concrete measurements show the speedup genuinely growing as variance increases (as the beginner exercise's reasoning predicted) -- discuss any genuine discrepancies between your measured results and the theoretical prediction, and what specific real-world request-pattern characteristics (e.g. a chat application with mostly short exchanges but occasional long document-summarization requests) would make continuous batching's benefit most pronounced in an actual production deployment.",
      problemKn: "continuous batching ya ಅನುಕೂಲ request-length variance ಹೆಚ್ಚಾದಂತೆ ಬೆಳೆಯುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂಬ beginner exercise ya ಹಕ್ಕನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ ಮೂರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ variance ಮಟ್ಟಗಳ test queues ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಎರಡೂ schedulers ಅನ್ನೂ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರು queues ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಪ್ರತಿ variance ಮಟ್ಟಕ್ಕೆ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ speedup ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಸೈದ್ಧಾಂತಿಕ ಭವಿಷ್ಯವಾಣಿಯ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m196, l196_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Speculative Decoding and Fused Kernels: Two More Ways to Cut Inference Latency',
      titleKn: 'Speculative Decoding ಮತ್ತು Fused Kernels: Inference Latency ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡಲು ಇನ್ನೂ ಎರಡು ಮಾರ್ಗಗಳು',
      problem: "Two further inference-optimization techniques address latency from different angles. SPECULATIVE DECODING uses a small, fast 'draft' model to quickly guess several upcoming tokens at once, then has the large, accurate 'target' model verify all those guessed tokens in a SINGLE forward pass (rather than one pass per token) -- accepting the guesses that match what the target model would have generated anyway, and only falling back to normal one-token-at-a-time generation when a guess is rejected. FUSED KERNELS combine multiple separate low-level GPU operations (e.g. a matrix multiply immediately followed by an activation function) into a SINGLE compiled operation, avoiding the overhead of writing intermediate results to GPU memory and reading them back for the next operation.\n\n1) Explain why speculative decoding's speedup depends heavily on the draft model's guesses being frequently CORRECT (matching what the target model would generate) -- what happens to the technique's benefit if the draft model's guesses are almost always wrong, requiring the target model to reject them and fall back to normal generation almost every time?\n\n2) Explain why fused kernels provide a genuine speed benefit even though they don't reduce the total amount of ARITHMETIC being performed -- what specific overhead (unrelated to arithmetic operation count) does avoiding intermediate memory writes/reads eliminate?",
      problemKn: "ಎರಡು ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ inference-optimization techniques ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ಕೋನಗಳಿಂದ latency ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತವೆ. SPECULATIVE DECODING ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ, ವೇಗದ 'draft' model ಬಳಸಿ ಹಲವಾರು ಮುಂಬರುವ tokens ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಟ್ಟಿಗೆ ಊಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ನಂತರ ದೊಡ್ಡ 'target' model ಒಂದೇ forward pass ನಲ್ಲಿ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಊಹೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆ. FUSED KERNELS ಅನೇಕ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ low-level GPU operations ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದೇ compiled operation ಆಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) speculative decoding ya speedup ಏಕೆ draft model ya ಊಹೆಗಳು ಆಗಾಗ್ಗೆ ಸರಿಯಾಗಿರುವುದರ ಮೇಲೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಅವಲಂಬಿತವಾಗಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) fused kernels ಏಕೆ ನಿಜ speed ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ ನೀಡುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಒಟ್ಟು arithmetic ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡದಿದ್ದರೂ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Basic Speculative Decoding Loop',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಮೂಲಭೂತ Speculative Decoding Loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a basic speculative decoding loop using two versions of your mini-GPT as draft and target (e.g. a smaller/fewer-layer version as the draft model and your full mini-GPT as the target, or simply your mini-GPT itself run with a shortened context as a cheap proxy draft if you don't have two distinctly-sized models available).\n\n1) Genuinely implement the draft-then-verify loop: at each step, use the draft model to autoregressively generate K candidate tokens (a small lookahead window), then run the target model ONCE on the full sequence (original context plus all K draft tokens) to get the target model's own probability distribution at each of those K positions, and genuinely implement the accept/reject logic that compares the draft tokens against what the target model would have chosen, accepting a prefix of matching tokens and rejecting (and replacing with the target's own token) at the first mismatch.\n\n2) Genuinely verify correctness: confirm that the FINAL sequence of accepted tokens your speculative loop produces is IDENTICAL to what plain greedy generation from the target model alone would have produced for the same prompt (since correctly-implemented speculative decoding with greedy verification should be exact, not approximate).\n\n3) Genuinely measure the actual number of target-model forward passes needed to generate a fixed-length sequence using your speculative loop, versus the number needed by plain one-token-at-a-time target-only generation, and report your measured reduction in target-model forward-pass count (a proxy for actual latency savings, since the target model is presumed to be the expensive one).",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya ಎರಡು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ draft ಮತ್ತು target ಆಗಿ ಬಳಸಿ ಒಂದು ಮೂಲಭೂತ speculative decoding loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) draft-then-verify loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: draft model K candidate tokens ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, target model ಒಮ್ಮೆ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, accept/reject logic ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) FINAL ಅನುಕ್ರಮ plain target-only generation ಗೆ ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಹೊಂದಿಕೆಯಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) target-model forward passes ya ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಕಡಿತವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Speculative Decoding\\u2019s Sensitivity to Draft-Target Agreement Rate',
      titleKn: 'Speculative Decoding ya Draft-Target Agreement Rate ಗೆ Sensitivity ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment testing the beginner exercise's claim about speculative decoding's dependence on draft-model accuracy.\n\n1) Genuinely construct (or select) at least two different draft models with genuinely different expected agreement rates with your target model -- for example, a draft model trained/configured to be reasonably capable (higher expected agreement) versus a deliberately weaker or less-trained draft model (e.g. fewer training steps, or a much smaller architecture) expected to agree with the target less often.\n\n2) Genuinely measure the actual empirical agreement rate for each draft model (what fraction of individual draft-proposed tokens the target model actually accepts, across a representative sample of generation steps) and genuinely measure the resulting number of target-model forward passes needed per generated token for each draft model, using your speculative decoding implementation from the intermediate exercise.\n\n3) Genuinely report the measured relationship between agreement rate and speedup across your two (or more) draft models -- confirm whether a lower agreement rate genuinely produces less speedup (or even a slowdown, if the draft model's own computation cost plus wasted verification effort exceeds the benefit of any accepted tokens), and discuss, using your own concrete numbers, why real production speculative decoding systems invest specifically in making the draft model's output distribution closely track the target model's (e.g. by training the draft model on the target model's own outputs) rather than using an arbitrary small model as the draft.",
      problemKn: "speculative decoding ya draft-model accuracy ಮೇಲಿನ ಅವಲಂಬನೆಯ ಬಗ್ಗೆ beginner exercise ya ಹಕ್ಕನ್ನೂ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ನಿರೀಕ್ಷಿತ agreement rates ಹೊಂದಿರುವ ಕನಿಷ್ಠ ಎರಡು draft models ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ draft model ಗೆ ನಿಜ agreement rate ಮತ್ತು target-model forward passes ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) agreement rate ಮತ್ತು speedup ನಡುವಿನ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ ಸಂಬಂಧವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m197, l197_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Wiring the Pipeline: From Raw Text to a Deployed, Aligned, Quantized Model",
      titleKn: "Pipeline ಜೋಡಣೆ: Raw Text ಇಂದ Deployed, Aligned, Quantized Model ವರೆಗೆ",
      problem: "This phase has covered every individual stage of a complete LLM pipeline as separate lessons -- tokenization, data cleaning/deduplication, pretraining with checkpointing, SFT, RLHF/DPO alignment, evaluation, and quantization for deployment -- but building a genuinely working system requires chaining ALL of these stages together in the correct order, with each stage's OUTPUT format matching the next stage's REQUIRED input format.\n\n1) Explain why the order of these stages is not arbitrary: specifically, why must tokenizer training happen before pretraining even begins (rather than, say, being decided afterward), and why must pretraining (producing a base model with general language capability) complete before SFT is meaningful, given what you learned about SFT needing a capable base to fine-tune.\n\n2) Explain a genuine practical failure mode of pipeline integration that has nothing to do with any single stage being 'wrong' in isolation: describe a plausible mismatch (e.g. a tokenizer vocabulary size that doesn't match the model's embedding table size, or an SFT chat-template format that doesn't match what the evaluation harness expects) that could cause a fully-functional-in-isolation set of components to fail when connected together, and why this kind of integration bug is often harder to debug than a bug within a single stage.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ LLM pipeline ya ಪ್ರತಿ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ ಹಂತವನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ ಪಾಠಗಳಾಗಿ ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿದೆ, ಆದರೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ ವ್ಯವಸ್ಥೆ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಲು ಈ ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಹಂತಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸರಿಯಾದ ಕ್ರಮದಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸಬೇಕು.\n\n1) ಈ ಹಂತಗಳ ಕ್ರಮ ಅನಿಯಂತ್ರಿತವಲ್ಲ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಯಾವುದೇ ಒಂಟಿ ಹಂತ 'ತಪ್ಪು' ಇಲ್ಲದಿದ್ದರೂ pipeline integration ya ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ವೈಫಲ್ಯ ವಿಧಾನವನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Assembling Your Own End-to-End Pipeline Across Every Stage',
      titleKn: 'ಪ್ರತಿ ಹಂತದಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ End-to-End Pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜೋಡಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely connect every stage you've built across this phase's lessons into one working, end-to-end pipeline, run on your mini-GPT.\n\n1) Genuinely run, in order, using your own previous implementations: (a) tokenizer training/loading, (b) a small pretraining run (or reuse an already-pretrained checkpoint from earlier in the course), (c) SFT on your instruction dataset, (d) DPO or RLHF alignment on your preference data, (e) evaluation via your custom eval suite, and (f) quantization for a final deployable artifact -- genuinely execute each stage's actual code (not simulated placeholders) and confirm each stage's output is successfully consumed by the next.\n\n2) Genuinely document, for each stage transition, the EXACT data format/interface being passed (e.g. what file format or in-memory object the SFT stage hands to the alignment stage) so you have a concrete, verified record of your pipeline's actual interfaces.\n\n3) Genuinely run your custom eval suite (from the evaluation lesson) on the FINAL, fully-pipelined, quantized model, and report its measured scores, comparing them against the scores of the base (pre-SFT) model on the same eval suite -- report the total measured improvement your end-to-end pipeline achieved, and report how long the full pipeline took to run end-to-end on your hardware.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ya ಪಾಠಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನೀವು ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಪ್ರತಿ ಹಂತವನ್ನೂ ಒಂದೇ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ end-to-end pipeline ಆಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಪರ್ಕಿಸಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಕ್ರಮದಲ್ಲಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಹಿಂದಿನ implementations ಬಳಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ: tokenizer, pretraining, SFT, DPO/RLHF, evaluation, quantization.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ ಹಂತ ಪರಿವರ್ತನೆಗೆ ನಿಖರ data format ಅನ್ನೂ ದಾಖಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) FINAL, quantized model ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ custom eval suite ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು base model ಜೊತೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Auditing Your Pipeline for Silent Data Leakage Across Stages',
      titleKn: 'ಹಂತಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Silent Data Leakage ಗಾಗಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ Pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Audit ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely audit your complete pipeline from the intermediate exercise for a specific, genuine risk that spans multiple stages: DATA LEAKAGE, where evaluation data (or data very similar to it) inadvertently ends up in an earlier stage's training data, inflating your final eval scores in a way that doesn't reflect genuine capability -- connecting back to the benchmark-contamination concern from the evaluation lesson, but now checking for it across YOUR ENTIRE pipeline rather than just the pretraining corpus.\n\n1) Genuinely check each stage's training data (pretraining corpus, SFT dataset, preference-pair dataset) against your custom eval suite's held-out questions/prompts, using a concrete method (e.g. exact string matching or n-gram overlap, as in the evaluation lesson's contamination-detection exercise) to detect any genuine overlap or near-duplication.\n\n2) Genuinely report what you find -- if you discover any actual leakage, report exactly which stage introduced it and how, and re-measure your final eval scores after removing the leaked examples and re-running the affected stage(s), reporting the actual score change this produces.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, based on your concrete finding (whether or not you found leakage), why auditing for cross-stage data leakage needs to happen at the WHOLE-PIPELINE level rather than checking each stage's data against only that stage's own immediate concerns, and what practical process (e.g. a single shared 'eval set' held out and excluded from every single stage's data sourcing, checked at every stage boundary) would make this kind of leakage structurally harder to introduce by accident in a real production pipeline.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ಅನೇಕ ಹಂತಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಹರಡುವ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ, ನಿಜ ಅಪಾಯಕ್ಕಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ audit ಮಾಡಿ: DATA LEAKAGE.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ ಹಂತದ training data ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ custom eval suite ya held-out ಪ್ರಶ್ನೆಗಳ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನೀವು ಕಂಡುಕೊಂಡದ್ದನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು leaked ಉದಾಹರಣೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದ ನಂತರ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಮರುಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) cross-stage data leakage ಗಾಗಿ ಆಡಿಟ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಇಡೀ-pipeline ಮಟ್ಟದಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಭವಿಸಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m197, l197_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Serving the Pipeline's Output: APIs, Latency Budgets, and Graceful Degradation",
      titleKn: "Pipeline ya Output Serve ಮಾಡುವುದೂ: APIs, Latency Budgets, ಮತ್ತು Graceful Degradation",
      problem: "Once a model has been trained, aligned, evaluated, and quantized, making it actually USABLE requires a serving layer -- typically an API that accepts requests, manages the inference-optimization techniques from the earlier lesson (KV caching, continuous batching), and returns responses within an acceptable LATENCY BUDGET (a maximum acceptable response time for the application's use case, e.g. a chat interface needing the first token to appear within a second or two, versus a batch-processing job that can tolerate minutes).\n\n1) Explain why a serving layer needs explicit GRACEFUL DEGRADATION behavior for when incoming request volume exceeds available capacity -- what specifically should happen to NEW requests when the server is already at its continuous-batching capacity limit, and why is silently dropping requests or letting response times grow unboundedly both worse choices than an explicit, visible strategy (e.g. queueing with a maximum wait time, or explicitly rejecting with a clear retry-later signal)?\n\n2) Explain why the SAME underlying model might need different serving configurations (e.g. different batch sizes, different KV-cache memory allocations) for different deployment contexts -- specifically, contrast what you'd optimize for in a real-time interactive chat deployment (latency-sensitive, often single request per user) versus a bulk offline-processing deployment (throughput-sensitive, many requests processed without a human waiting).",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು model ಅನ್ನೂ train, align, evaluate, ಮತ್ತು quantize ಮಾಡಿದ ನಂತರ, ಅದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬಳಸಬಹುದಾಗಿಸಲು ಒಂದು serving layer ಬೇಕು -- ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ requests ಸ್ವೀಕರಿಸುವ, inference-optimization techniques ನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ, ಮತ್ತು ಒಪ್ಪಿಗೆಯಾದ LATENCY BUDGET ಒಳಗೆ responses ಹಿಂತಿರುಗಿಸುವ API.\n\n1) capacity ಮೀರಿದಾಗ serving layer ಗೆ ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ GRACEFUL DEGRADATION ನಡವಳಿಕೆ ಏಕೆ ಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದೇ underlying model ಗೆ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ deployment ಸಂದರ್ಭಗಳಿಗೆ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ serving configurations ಏಕೆ ಬೇಕಾಗಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Building a Serving Layer with a Latency Budget and Load Shedding',
      titleKn: 'Latency Budget ಮತ್ತು Load Shedding ಜೊತೆ Serving Layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely build a minimal serving layer around your pipelined mini-GPT from the previous lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a simple request-handling server (e.g. a lightweight local HTTP server, or an in-process request queue simulating one if a real network server is impractical in your environment) that accepts text-generation requests, manages them through your continuous-batching scheduler (from the inference-optimization lesson), and returns generated text.\n\n2) Genuinely implement and enforce an explicit LATENCY BUDGET: measure actual time-to-first-token and total generation time for each request, and genuinely implement a load-shedding policy that explicitly REJECTS (with a clear error/retry signal, not a silent failure or unbounded queueing) new requests once your simulated capacity is exceeded, rather than letting queue depth and latency grow without bound.\n\n3) Genuinely load-test your serving layer by sending a burst of requests (deliberately exceeding your configured capacity) and report concrete measured results: how many requests were served within your latency budget, how many were explicitly rejected by your load-shedding logic, and confirm that NO request was silently dropped or left hanging indefinitely -- report your actual measured numbers as evidence your graceful-degradation logic works as designed.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ pipelined mini-GPT ಸುತ್ತಲೂ ಒಂದು ಕನಿಷ್ಠ serving layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n1) text-generation requests ಸ್ವೀಕರಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ಸರಳ request-handling server ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟ LATENCY BUDGET ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, capacity ಮೀರಿದಾಗ ಹೊಸ requests ಅನ್ನೂ ಸ್ಪಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸುವ load-shedding policy ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ serving layer ಅನ್ನೂ load-test ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing Chat vs Batch Serving Configurations Under Realistic Load',
      titleKn: 'ನೈಜ Load ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ Chat vs Batch Serving Configurations ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design, implement, and measure two DIFFERENT serving configurations of your same pipelined model, optimized for the two contrasting use cases raised in the beginner exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely configure and run a 'chat-optimized' serving setup (e.g. smaller max-batch-size prioritizing low per-request latency, aggressive request admission so individual users get fast responses) and genuinely measure its time-to-first-token and total-completion latency under a simulated realistic chat workload (many small, independent, latency-sensitive requests arriving somewhat unpredictably).\n\n2) Genuinely configure and run a 'batch-optimized' serving setup (e.g. larger max-batch-size prioritizing total throughput over any individual request's latency) and genuinely measure its total throughput (total tokens generated across all requests per unit wall-clock time) under a simulated realistic batch workload (many requests submitted all at once, with no individual user waiting in real time).\n\n3) Genuinely run the WRONG configuration against each workload as well (chat-optimized config under batch workload, and vice versa) and report the measured performance degradation this mismatch causes -- using your own concrete numbers, discuss why a real production system serving both interactive and batch use cases might need entirely separate serving deployments (rather than one 'one-size-fits-all' configuration) of the same underlying model.",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ಎತ್ತಿದ ಎರಡು ವ್ಯತಿರಿಕ್ತ ಬಳಕೆ ಪ್ರಕರಣಗಳಿಗೆ ಆಪ್ಟಿಮೈಸ್ ಮಾಡಿದ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅದೇ pipelined model ya ಎರಡು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ serving configurations ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) 'chat-optimized' serving setup ಅನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು latency ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) 'batch-optimized' serving setup ಅನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು throughput ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ತಪ್ಪಾದ configuration ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ workload ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹದಗೆಡುವಿಕೆ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m197, l197_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: 'Closing the Loop: Monitoring Production Behavior and Feeding It Back Into the Pipeline',
      titleKn: 'Loop ಮುಚ್ಚುವುದೂ: Production Behavior ಮಾನಿಟರ್ ಮಾಡಿ Pipeline ಗೆ ಹಿಂತಿರುಗಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "A genuinely complete LLM pipeline doesn't end at deployment -- real production systems MONITOR live model behavior (e.g. logging a sample of real requests and responses, tracking user feedback signals like thumbs-up/down or regeneration requests, watching for unusual output patterns) and feed genuine findings back into EARLIER pipeline stages, closing the loop from the evaluation lesson's regression-testing idea into an ongoing, continuous process rather than a one-time check.\n\n1) Explain why production monitoring can catch categories of failure that even a thorough custom eval suite (built before deployment) cannot -- specifically, what does REAL user behavior and REAL request distribution reveal that a fixed, pre-deployment eval suite (necessarily built from the team's best guesses about what users will ask) cannot fully anticipate?\n\n2) Explain the concrete mechanism by which a genuine production issue should flow back through the pipeline -- if monitoring reveals a specific category of real user request that the model handles poorly, trace which earlier stage(s) (new eval regression case, new SFT/preference-pair examples, or something else) would need to be updated to address it, and why simply patching the model's behavior for that one specific observed case (rather than feeding it back as new training/eval data) would be a fragile, non-scalable fix.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪೂರ್ಣ LLM pipeline deployment ನಲ್ಲಿ ಕೊನೆಗೊಳ್ಳುವುದಿಲ್ಲ -- ನಿಜ production systems live model behavior ಅನ್ನೂ ಮಾನಿಟರ್ ಮಾಡುತ್ತವೆ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ ಸಂಶೋಧನೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ pipeline ಹಂತಗಳಿಗೆ ಹಿಂತಿರುಗಿಸುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) production monitoring ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ custom eval suite ಸಹ ಹಿಡಿಯಲಾಗದ ವೈಫಲ್ಯ ವರ್ಗಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹಿಡಿಯಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ನಿಜ production ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ pipeline ಮೂಲಕ ಹಿಂತಿರುಗಿ ಹರಿಯಬೇಕಾದ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Monitoring-to-Retraining Feedback Loop',
      titleKn: 'Monitoring-ಇಂದ-Retraining Feedback Loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a small but complete monitoring-to-improvement feedback loop around your serving layer from the previous lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely implement request/response logging: capture a sample of actual (prompt, generated response) pairs your serving layer produces during a simulated usage session, along with a simple automated 'quality flag' heuristic (e.g. flagging unusually short responses, responses containing certain error-indicating patterns, or responses your reward model scores unusually low) to surface POTENTIALLY problematic cases without requiring manual review of every single logged interaction.\n\n2) Genuinely review the flagged cases from a simulated usage session (you can generate this session yourself by deliberately including some prompts your model is known to handle poorly, alongside normal prompts, to have genuine flagged cases to work with) and, for the genuinely problematic ones, genuinely add them as new regression cases to your custom eval suite (from the evaluation lesson) AND as new training examples in your SFT or preference-pair dataset (from the appropriate earlier lesson).\n\n3) Genuinely re-run the affected earlier pipeline stage(s) (e.g. re-run SFT or DPO with the augmented dataset) and re-evaluate using your now-expanded eval suite, reporting whether your model's behavior on the specific previously-flagged cases has genuinely improved, and whether your monitoring-driven update caused any measurable regression on your ORIGINAL (pre-expansion) eval cases.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ serving layer ಸುತ್ತಲೂ ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ ಆದರೆ ಪೂರ್ಣ monitoring-ಇಂದ-improvement feedback loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) request/response logging ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಒಂದು ಸ್ವಯಂಚಾಲಿತ 'quality flag' heuristic ಜೊತೆ.\n\n2) flagged cases ಅನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು regression cases ಮತ್ತು training examples ಆಗಿ ಸೇರಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಪೀಡಿತ ಹಂತಗಳನ್ನೂ ಮರುಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಸುಧಾರಣೆ ಹಾಗೂ regressions ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Running the Full Pipeline Twice to Verify the Loop Is Truly Closed',
      titleKn: 'Loop ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮುಚ್ಚಿದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಲು ಪೂರ್ಣ Pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ಎರಡು ಬಾರಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely conduct the ultimate end-to-end verification of this entire phase's work: prove, with concrete measurements across TWO full pipeline generations, that your monitoring-to-retraining loop genuinely improves the deployed system over time rather than merely appearing to on paper.\n\n1) Genuinely treat your CURRENT pipelined-and-served model (from the previous lessons) as 'Generation 1', and record its complete eval-suite score profile as a fixed baseline.\n\n2) Genuinely run one full cycle of the monitoring-to-retraining loop from the intermediate exercise (collect flagged production cases, augment training/eval data, re-run the affected pipeline stages) to produce 'Generation 2', and genuinely re-run your FULL custom eval suite (all categories, all regression cases, both original and newly-added) on Generation 2.\n\n3) Genuinely report a complete, category-by-category comparison between Generation 1 and Generation 2's eval scores -- confirm whether Generation 2 genuinely improved on the specific cases that motivated the update, discuss whether it introduced any measurable regressions elsewhere, and reflect honestly on what your own concrete two-generation experiment reveals about the real difficulty of genuinely closing the loop in a production ML system: specifically, why continuous monitoring, re-evaluation, and disciplined regression-tracking (rather than a single one-time pipeline build) are what actually separates a genuinely maintainable production LLM system from a one-off research prototype, grounding every claim in what you personally measured across your two generations rather than in received wisdom.",
      problemKn: "ಈ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ phase ya ಕೆಲಸದ ಅಂತಿಮ end-to-end ಪರಿಶೀಲನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಡೆಸಿ: ಎರಡು ಪೂರ್ಣ pipeline generations ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಅಳತೆಗಳೊಂದಿಗೆ ಸಾಬೀತುಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ pipelined model ಅನ್ನೂ 'Generation 1' ಆಗಿ ಪರಿಗಣಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ eval-suite score profile ಅನ್ನೂ ದಾಖಲಿಸಿ.\n\n2) monitoring-ಇಂದ-retraining loop ya ಒಂದು ಸುತ್ತನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ 'Generation 2' ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪೂರ್ಣ eval suite ಮರುಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n3) Generation 1 ಮತ್ತು 2 ನಡುವೆ ಒಂದು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ, category-by-category ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರತಿಬಿಂಬಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m198, l198_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Reading a Real Model Card: What Architecture Choices Actually Vary Across Open Models",
      titleKn: "ಒಂದು ನಿಜ Model Card ಓದುವುದೂ: Open Models ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಯಾವ Architecture Choices ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬದಲಾಗುತ್ತವೆ",
      problem: "Open-weight model families (e.g. Llama, Mistral, Qwen, Gemma) share the same fundamental transformer decoder skeleton you've studied throughout this course, but differ in specific architectural choices published in their technical reports and config files -- attention variant (standard multi-head vs grouped-query attention), normalization placement and type, activation function choice, positional encoding scheme, and vocabulary size, among others.\n\n1) Explain why comparing open models purely by parameter COUNT (e.g. '7B' vs '8B') is insufficient to predict their relative capability or resource requirements -- what architectural choice you've studied elsewhere in this course (e.g. grouped-query attention's effect on KV-cache size) means two models with the SAME parameter count could have meaningfully different inference memory footprints or serving costs?\n\n2) Explain why reading a model's actual published config (hidden size, number of layers, number of attention heads, vocabulary size) rather than relying on marketing-level descriptions is necessary to genuinely understand what you're deploying -- what specific detail relevant to deployment (e.g. context length limit, tokenizer vocabulary size affecting embedding table size) would marketing materials typically omit that the actual config file would reveal?",
      problemKn: "Open-weight model families (ಉದಾ: Llama, Mistral, Qwen, Gemma) ಈ course ನಲ್ಲಿ ನೀವು ಅಧ್ಯಯನ ಮಾಡಿದ ಅದೇ ಮೂಲಭೂತ transformer decoder ಅಸ್ಥಿಪಂಜರವನ್ನೂ ಹಂಚಿಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತವೆ, ಆದರೆ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ architectural ಆಯ್ಕೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಭಿನ್ನವಾಗಿವೆ.\n\n1) open models ಅನ್ನೂ ಕೇವಲ parameter COUNT ಮೂಲಕ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಸಾಕಾಗುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು model ya ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಕಟಿತ config ಓದುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಅಗತ್ಯ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Diffing Two Real Open-Model Configs Against Your Own Architecture Knowledge',
      titleKn: 'ಎರಡು ನಿಜ Open-Model Configs ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ Architecture ಜ್ಞಾನದ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Diff ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely obtain (e.g. via a publicly-available config.json or published technical report) the architectural specifications of two different real open-weight model families of similar parameter scale, and genuinely analyze their differences using the concepts from this course.\n\n1) Genuinely tabulate, side by side, each model's actual published values for: number of layers, hidden size, number of attention heads, attention variant (standard/grouped-query/multi-query), normalization type and placement, activation function, positional encoding scheme, vocabulary size, and maximum context length.\n\n2) Genuinely calculate, using the formulas and reasoning you've built throughout this course (e.g. from the distributed-training and inference-optimization lessons), each model's approximate KV-cache memory cost PER TOKEN at their respective published configs, and report which of the two models has a smaller per-token KV-cache footprint and by what factor, connecting this concretely to their attention-variant choice.\n\n3) Genuinely identify at least one architectural choice where the two real models genuinely DIFFER, and explain, using your own understanding from earlier lessons in this phase and course, what concrete tradeoff that specific difference represents (e.g. training/inference speed, quality, memory) rather than simply restating the difference.",
      problemKn: "ಇದೇ ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ ಎರಡು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ನಿಜ open-weight model families ya architectural specifications ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪಡೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳಿ ಮತ್ತು ಈ course ya ಕಲ್ಪನೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಬಳಸಿ ಅವುಗಳ ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಶ್ಲೇಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ model ya ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಕಟಿತ ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳನ್ನೂ ಪಕ್ಕದಲ್ಲಿ ಪಟ್ಟಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ model ya ಪ್ರತಿ-token KV-cache memory ವೆಚ್ಚವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡು models ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಭಿನ್ನವಾಗಿರುವ ಕನಿಷ್ಠ ಒಂದು architectural ಆಯ್ಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ಗುರುತಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ tradeoff ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Reimplementing One Real Open Model\\u2019s Specific Attention Variant on Your Mini-GPT',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ನಿಜ Open Model ya ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ Attention Variant ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ Mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮರುಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely take the specific attention variant used by ONE of the two real models you analyzed in the intermediate exercise (e.g. grouped-query attention with its specific number of KV-heads, if that's what the model uses), and genuinely reimplement it as a modification to your mini-GPT's attention layer, matching the real model's actual published ratio of query-heads to KV-heads as closely as your mini-GPT's own head count allows.\n\n1) Genuinely modify your mini-GPT's attention mechanism to use this specific configuration (rather than your original standard multi-head attention), ensuring the implementation is a genuine, working modification (queries project to the full head count, keys/values project to the reduced KV-head count, with appropriate broadcasting/grouping in the attention computation) rather than a cosmetic renaming.\n\n2) Genuinely verify your modified attention layer runs correctly: confirm the output shapes are correct, run a forward and backward pass without errors, and genuinely train for a small number of steps to confirm the modified model can still learn (loss decreases).\n\n3) Genuinely measure and report the actual KV-cache memory reduction your modification achieves (using your KV-cache implementation from the inference-optimization lesson) compared to your original standard-multi-head-attention mini-GPT, and compare your OWN measured reduction factor against the theoretical reduction factor implied by the real model's published query-head-to-KV-head ratio, discussing any discrepancy.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ನಲ್ಲಿ ವಿಶ್ಲೇಷಿಸಿದ ಎರಡು ನಿಜ models ಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದರ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ attention variant ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಂಡು ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya attention layer ಗೆ ಮಾರ್ಪಾಡಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮರುಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya attention mechanism ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ configuration ಬಳಸಲು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿದ attention layer ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ ಚಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಜ KV-cache memory ಕಡಿತವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ model ya ಸೈದ್ಧಾಂತಿಕ ಕಡಿತದ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m198, l198_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Mixture-of-Experts: Growing Parameter Count Without Growing Compute Per Token",
      titleKn: "Mixture-of-Experts: ಪ್ರತಿ Token ಗೆ Compute ಬೆಳೆಸದೆ Parameter Count ಬೆಳೆಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Several real open models (e.g. Mixtral, DeepSeek-MoE, and others) replace the standard dense feed-forward network in some or all transformer layers with a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer: instead of ONE feed-forward network processing every token, there are MULTIPLE parallel feed-forward 'expert' networks, and a small learned ROUTER network looks at each token and decides which small subset (e.g. 2 out of 8) of experts should process that specific token -- only the SELECTED experts' computation counts toward that token's forward pass cost, even though ALL experts' parameters exist in the model.\n\n1) Explain concretely why this design lets a model have a much larger TOTAL parameter count than a comparably-fast dense model -- given that only a fixed small number of experts process any given token, why does adding MORE experts (increasing total parameters) NOT proportionally increase the compute cost of processing each token, unlike simply making a dense feed-forward layer wider?\n\n2) Explain the specific new problem this design introduces that a dense model doesn't have: LOAD BALANCING -- why would a router that (without any special encouragement) always sends tokens to the SAME one or two 'favorite' experts be a genuine problem, given that unused experts' parameters would then be contributing nothing to the model's effective capacity despite occupying memory.",
      problemKn: "ಹಲವಾರು ನಿಜ open models (ಉದಾ: Mixtral, DeepSeek-MoE) transformer layers ನಲ್ಲಿ standard dense feed-forward network ಅನ್ನೂ Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer ಜೊತೆ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸುತ್ತವೆ: ಒಂದೇ feed-forward network ಬದಲಿಗೆ, ಅನೇಕ parallel 'expert' networks ಇವೆ, ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ ROUTER network ಪ್ರತಿ token ಗೆ ಯಾವ ಚಿಕ್ಕ ಉಪವಿಭಾಗದ experts ಸಂಸ್ಕರಿಸಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ನಿರ್ಧರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಇದು ಒಂದು model ಗೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು TOTAL parameter count ಹೊಂದಲು ಏಕೆ ಅನುಮತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) LOAD BALANCING ಎಂಬ ಹೊಸ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Mixture-of-Experts Layer with a Learned Router',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Learned Router ಜೊತೆ Mixture-of-Experts Layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a basic MoE layer and substitute it for the dense feed-forward layer in one or more blocks of your mini-GPT.\n\n1) Genuinely implement N (e.g. 4-8) separate small feed-forward 'expert' networks (each with the same architecture as your original dense FFN, but as independent parameter sets) and a router: a small linear layer that takes each token's hidden state and outputs a score per expert, from which you select the TOP-K (e.g. top-2) highest-scoring experts for that specific token.\n\n2) Genuinely implement the routing and combination logic: route each token to its selected top-K experts, run it through ONLY those experts' computation, and combine their outputs (e.g. weighted by the router's softmax-normalized scores for the selected experts) into the layer's final output -- genuinely verify this runs correctly with proper shapes for a batch of tokens where DIFFERENT tokens in the same batch may route to different experts.\n\n3) Genuinely train your MoE-modified mini-GPT for a small number of steps and confirm the loss decreases, then genuinely instrument and report which experts each token in a test batch actually got routed to, confirming your routing logic behaves as designed (different tokens genuinely receiving different expert assignments, not all collapsing onto one expert).",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು ಮೂಲಭೂತ MoE layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya ಒಂದು ಅಥವಾ ಹೆಚ್ಚು blocks ನಲ್ಲಿ dense feed-forward layer ಬದಲಿಗೆ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) N ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ ಚಿಕ್ಕ 'expert' networks ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು router ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) routing ಮತ್ತು combination logic ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ MoE-modified mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಂಕ್ಷಿಪ್ತವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಯಾವ tokens ಯಾವ experts ಗೆ ರೂಟ್ ಆದವು ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Measuring a Load-Balancing Auxiliary Loss',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Load-Balancing Auxiliary Loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate and address the load-balancing problem raised in the beginner exercise, using your working MoE implementation from the intermediate exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely measure the actual expert-utilization DISTRIBUTION your MoE layer produces (without any special load-balancing mechanism) over a reasonably large batch of diverse tokens -- report what fraction of tokens got routed to each expert, and confirm (or disconfirm) whether some experts are genuinely receiving disproportionately more tokens than others in your specific setup.\n\n2) Genuinely implement a standard load-balancing auxiliary loss (e.g. an additional loss term that penalizes the router for producing an uneven distribution of tokens across experts, computed from the actual per-expert token-assignment fractions and per-expert average routing-score, added to your main training loss with a small weighting coefficient) and genuinely retrain your MoE mini-GPT with this auxiliary loss included.\n\n3) Genuinely re-measure the expert-utilization distribution after training with the auxiliary loss, and report the measured before/after difference in load balance (e.g. using a concrete metric like the standard deviation of per-expert token counts) -- confirm whether your auxiliary loss genuinely produced a more balanced utilization, and discuss, based on your own concrete measurement, why real MoE model training (as used in models like Mixtral) treats this auxiliary loss's weighting coefficient as a carefully-tuned hyperparameter, balancing genuine load-balance against not distracting the router with a penalty so strong it overrides genuinely useful token-to-expert specialization.",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ಎತ್ತಿದ load-balancing ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ MoE implementation ಬಳಸಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ ಪರಿಹರಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಯಾವುದೇ ವಿಶೇಷ load-balancing ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆ ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ನಿಜ expert-utilization distribution ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಮಾಣಿತ load-balancing auxiliary loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಮರುtrain ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) auxiliary loss ನಂತರ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ ಬದಲಾವಣೆಯನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m198, l198_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Sliding Window Attention: Trading Full Context for Bounded Compute",
      titleKn: "Sliding Window Attention: Bounded Compute ಗಾಗಿ Full Context ವಿನಿಮಯ",
      problem: "Some real open models (e.g. Mistral's early architecture) use SLIDING WINDOW ATTENTION instead of full attention: rather than every token attending to every previous token in the sequence, each token only attends to a fixed-size WINDOW of the most recent W previous tokens, bounding the attention computation's cost per token to a constant regardless of total sequence length, rather than growing with the full sequence length as standard full attention does.\n\n1) Explain concretely why sliding window attention bounds per-token compute and KV-cache memory to a constant (roughly proportional to window size W) rather than growing with total sequence length -- what specifically does a token at position 10,000 in a long sequence attend to under this scheme, compared to what it would attend to under standard full attention?\n\n2) Explain the clever mechanism by which STACKING multiple sliding-window-attention layers can still let information flow across distances GREATER than the window size W, despite each individual layer only directly attending within its own window -- specifically, why can a token's representation at layer 2 already contain influence from tokens up to 2*W away, even though layer 2's OWN attention only directly looks back W positions?",
      problemKn: "ಕೆಲವು ನಿಜ open models (ಉದಾ: Mistral ya ಆರಂಭಿಕ architecture) full attention ಬದಲಿಗೆ SLIDING WINDOW ATTENTION ಬಳಸುತ್ತವೆ: ಪ್ರತಿ token ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಹಿಂದಿನ tokens ಗೆ ಗಮನ ನೀಡುವ ಬದಲು, ಪ್ರತಿ token ಕೇವಲ ಇತ್ತೀಚಿನ W tokens ya ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ-ಗಾತ್ರದ WINDOW ಗೆ ಮಾತ್ರ ಗಮನ ನೀಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) sliding window attention ಏಕೆ ಪ್ರತಿ-token compute ಮತ್ತು KV-cache memory ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರಕ್ಕೆ ಬಂಧಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಅನೇಕ sliding-window-attention layers ಅನ್ನೂ ಜೋಡಿಸುವುದೂ ಹೇಗೆ W ಗಿಂತ ದೊಡ್ಡ ದೂರಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಮಾಹಿತಿ ಹರಿಯಲು ಅನುಮತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Sliding Window Attention and Verifying Its Receptive Field',
      titleKn: 'Sliding Window Attention ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ Receptive Field ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement sliding window attention as a modification to your mini-GPT's attention layer, and genuinely verify its expanding-receptive-field property across stacked layers.\n\n1) Genuinely modify your attention mask/computation so that, in addition to the standard causal (no-attending-to-future) restriction, each token is also prevented from attending to any token further than W positions in the past -- genuinely implement this as an explicit additional masking rule and verify with a small test case that a token at position P only attends to positions [P-W, P] and no earlier.\n\n2) Genuinely train your sliding-window-modified mini-GPT for a small number of steps and confirm it still learns (loss decreases), then genuinely design a concrete experiment to verify the multi-layer expanding-receptive-field claim from the beginner exercise: construct a sequence where a SPECIFIC token's correct next-token prediction genuinely depends on information from a position MORE than W tokens back, and check whether your (multi-layer) sliding-window model can still solve this task, versus a genuinely single-layer version of the same model (where the beginner exercise's stacking argument would predict failure since there's no second layer to extend the reach).\n\n3) Genuinely report your concrete result and discuss whether it supports the theoretical claim that stacking recovers long-range information flow.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya attention layer ಗೆ ಮಾರ್ಪಾಡಾಗಿ sliding window attention ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಜೋಡಿಸಿದ layers ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಅದರ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸುವ receptive-field ಗುಣವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ attention mask ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ, ಪ್ರತಿ token ಅನ್ನೂ W position ಗಳಿಗಿಂತ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಯಾವುದೇ token ಗೆ ಗಮನ ನೀಡುವುದನ್ನೂ ತಡೆಯಿರಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ test case ಜೊತೆ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ sliding-window-modified mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಂಕ್ಷಿಪ್ತವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇನ್ನೂ ಕಲಿಯುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ, ನಂತರ multi-layer expanding-receptive-field ಹಕ್ಕನ್ನೂ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದು ಸೈದ್ಧಾಂತಿಕ ಹಕ್ಕನ್ನೂ ಬೆಂಬಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Long-Range Task Performance Gap of Bounded-Window Attention',
      titleKn: 'Bounded-Window Attention ya Long-Range Task Performance Gap ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run a controlled comparison quantifying exactly when sliding window attention's bounded context genuinely hurts task performance versus when it's genuinely harmless, extending the intermediate exercise's single anecdotal test into a systematic study.\n\n1) Genuinely construct a suite of synthetic tasks (e.g. 'copy a token seen exactly D positions back' or 'answer based on information present D positions back') at SEVERAL different distances D, some smaller than your window size W and some larger, spanning a range that includes distances requiring 1, 2, and 3+ stacked layers to reach under the beginner exercise's stacking argument.\n\n2) Genuinely train and evaluate both your sliding-window mini-GPT and a standard full-attention mini-GPT (same layer count, same W where applicable) on this full suite, and genuinely report each model's measured accuracy as a function of distance D -- confirm whether the sliding-window model's accuracy genuinely degrades specifically around and beyond distances requiring MORE stacked layers than your model actually has to reach them, while the full-attention model's accuracy stays flat across all tested distances.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, using your own concrete measured accuracy-vs-distance curves, what this implies about a fundamental limitation of sliding window attention that is NOT fully solved by stacking layers in practice -- specifically, why a model with a FIXED number of layers L and window size W has a genuine effective maximum reach of roughly L*W, and why real deployments choosing sliding window attention (e.g. for its bounded-memory serving benefits) must therefore deliberately size W and L to cover their application's genuinely required context distances, rather than assuming stacking makes the window size irrelevant.",
      problemKn: "sliding window attention ya bounded context ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಯಾವಾಗ task performance ಹಾನಿ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಮಾಣೀಕರಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನಿಯಂತ್ರಿತ ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ದೂರಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ synthetic tasks ya ಒಂದು ಸೂಟ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ sliding-window ಮತ್ತು standard full-attention mini-GPT ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಈ ಪೂರ್ಣ ಸೂಟ್ ಮೇಲೆ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು accuracy-vs-distance ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಇದು sliding window attention ya ಒಂದು ಮೂಲಭೂತ ಮಿತಿಯ ಬಗ್ಗೆ ಏನನ್ನೂ ಸೂಚಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಅಳತೆಗಳ ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m199, l199_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "EAGLE's Key Idea: Drafting from Hidden States, Not Just Token IDs",
      titleKn: "EAGLE ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಕಲ್ಪನೆ: Token IDs ಮಾತ್ರವಲ್ಲ, Hidden States ಇಂದ Draft ಮಾಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "The basic speculative decoding you implemented earlier in this phase uses a completely SEPARATE small draft model that only sees token IDs, just like the target model does. EAGLE (and its successors like EAGLE-3) improve on this by having the draft mechanism operate on the target model's own internal HIDDEN STATES (specifically, feature vectors from one of the target model's later layers) rather than starting from scratch on raw tokens -- a lightweight draft head predicts the target model's likely next FEATURE representations autoregressively, which tends to align much more closely with what the large target model would actually produce than an independent small model's token-level guesses do.\n\n1) Explain why drafting from the target model's own hidden-state features (which already encode rich, target-model-specific context) would plausibly produce candidate tokens that agree with the target model MORE often than a fully independent small draft model's guesses -- what information does the feature-based draft head have access to that a separate, independently-trained small model does not?\n\n2) Explain why a higher draft-target agreement rate (as reasoned about in the earlier speculative decoding lesson) directly translates into EAGLE achieving a better speedup than basic speculative decoding at a similar draft-model computational cost -- connect this back to the accept/reject mechanics you already implemented.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದಲ್ಲಿ ನೀವು ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿದ ಮೂಲಭೂತ speculative decoding ಒಂದು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ ಚಿಕ್ಕ draft model ಬಳಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಇದು ಕೇವಲ token IDs ಅನ್ನೂ ಮಾತ್ರ ನೋಡುತ್ತದೆ. EAGLE (ಮತ್ತು EAGLE-3 ನಂತಹ ಅದರ ಉತ್ತರಾಧಿಕಾರಿಗಳು) ಈ draft mechanism ಅನ್ನೂ target model ya ಸ್ವಂತ internal HIDDEN STATES ಮೇಲೆ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವಂತೆ ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) target model ya ಸ್ವಂತ hidden-state features ಇಂದ draft ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ target model ಜೊತೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಒಪ್ಪುವ candidate tokens ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಹೆಚ್ಚಿನ draft-target agreement rate ಏಕೆ EAGLE ಗೆ ಉತ್ತಮ speedup ನೀಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Feature-Level Draft Head on Your Mini-GPT',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು Feature-Level Draft Head ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified EAGLE-style feature-level draft mechanism on your mini-GPT.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a lightweight draft head: a small module (e.g. a single transformer layer or even a simple feed-forward+attention block, much smaller than your full mini-GPT) that takes the target model's hidden-state features at the current position (extracted from one of your mini-GPT's later layers) concatenated with the actual next token's embedding, and predicts the NEXT position's hidden-state feature, which you then autoregressively feed back in to predict several steps ahead.\n\n2) Genuinely train this draft head (keeping your main mini-GPT's weights frozen) to predict the target model's own future hidden states, using a regression loss (e.g. mean-squared error between predicted and actual target-model hidden states) on a training corpus, and verify the loss decreases.\n\n3) Genuinely integrate your trained feature-level draft head into the speculative-decoding accept/reject loop you built earlier in this phase (using your draft head's predicted features run through the target model's own output head to get draft token IDs), and genuinely measure the empirical draft-target agreement rate this produces, comparing it directly against the agreement rate your independent, token-ID-based draft model achieved in the earlier lesson -- report whether the feature-level approach genuinely achieved higher agreement in your specific experiment.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ EAGLE-ಶೈಲಿಯ feature-level draft ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಹಗುರ draft head ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಇದು target model ya hidden-state features ಬಳಸಿ ಮುಂದಿನ feature ಅನ್ನೂ ಊಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n2) ಈ draft head ಅನ್ನೂ ಟ್ರೈನ್ ಮಾಡಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮುಖ್ಯ mini-GPT weights ಅನ್ನೂ frozen ಇಟ್ಟುಕೊಂಡು.\n\n3) ಈ trained feature-level draft head ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ speculative-decoding loop ಗೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು agreement rate ಅನ್ನೂ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Tree-Based Draft Verification for Multi-Path Speculation',
      titleKn: 'Multi-Path Speculation ಗಾಗಿ Tree-Based Draft Verification ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "EAGLE-3 and related methods further improve speedup by having the draft mechanism propose a TREE of multiple candidate continuations (not just one single linear sequence of guesses) at each speculation round, and having the target model verify ALL branches of this tree in a single batched forward pass using a specialized tree-attention mask -- since some branches will diverge from what the target model would generate, but different branches may still substantially overlap in their EARLY tokens, allowing more total candidate tokens to be verified per target-model call than a single linear draft sequence could offer.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a simplified tree-based draft: have your draft head (from the intermediate exercise) propose, at each step, the TOP-2 most likely next features/tokens rather than just one, and expand this into a small tree of depth 2-3 (e.g. 2 first-level branches, each further expanded into 2 second-level branches, for up to 4 total candidate sequences sharing a common prefix).\n\n2) Genuinely implement a tree-attention mask for your target model's verification pass: construct an attention mask that ensures each node in the tree only attends to its own ancestors along the tree (not to sibling branches it doesn't share ancestry with), and genuinely run your target model ONCE on the flattened tree structure with this custom mask to verify all branches simultaneously.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and report the total number of ACCEPTED tokens per target-model forward pass using your tree-based approach, compared to the single-linear-sequence approach from the intermediate exercise, on the same test prompts -- report whether tree-based verification genuinely accepted more tokens per target-model call in your specific experiment, and discuss the concrete computational cost tradeoff (larger batched forward pass per call, but potentially far fewer total calls needed) that determines whether tree-based speculation is worth its added implementation complexity for a given draft-model agreement rate.",
      problemKn: "EAGLE-3 ಮತ್ತು ಸಂಬಂಧಿತ ವಿಧಾನಗಳು draft mechanism ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ speculation ಸುತ್ತಿನಲ್ಲಿ ಅನೇಕ candidate continuations ya ಒಂದು TREE ಪ್ರಸ್ತಾಪಿಸುವಂತೆ ಮಾಡಿ speedup ಅನ್ನೂ ಮತ್ತಷ್ಟು ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ tree-based draft ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ draft head top-2 ಸಾಧ್ಯತೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಸ್ತಾಪಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n2) target model ya verification pass ಗೆ ಒಂದು tree-attention mask ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಪ್ರತಿ target-model forward pass ಗೆ ACCEPTED tokens ya ಒಟ್ಟು ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು single-linear-sequence ವಿಧಾನದ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m199, l199_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "EAGLE-3's Multi-Layer Feature Fusion: Why One Layer's Features Aren't Enough",
      titleKn: "EAGLE-3 ya Multi-Layer Feature Fusion: ಒಂದೇ Layer ya Features ಸಾಕಾಗದಿರುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ",
      problem: "The original EAGLE drafted from a single chosen hidden-state layer of the target model, but EAGLE-3 improves further by FUSING features from MULTIPLE different layers of the target model (e.g. combining an early layer's more token-level/local features with a later layer's more abstract/contextual features) before feeding them into the draft head, rather than relying on any single layer's representation alone.\n\n1) Explain why a single layer's hidden-state features might be an incomplete signal for predicting the target model's eventual output -- given that different layers in a transformer tend to represent progressively more abstract information as depth increases (as you learned in earlier phases on transformer internals), what could a draft head miss if it only sees one specific layer's features, versus getting a fused view spanning multiple depths?\n\n2) Explain why simply using the VERY LAST layer's features (right before the model's output projection) alone might not be optimal either, even though those features are 'closest' to the actual output -- what information from EARLIER layers might still be genuinely useful to the draft head that gets lost or transformed away by the time processing reaches the final layer?",
      problemKn: "ಮೂಲ EAGLE target model ya ಒಂದೇ ಆಯ್ಕೆಮಾಡಿದ hidden-state layer ಇಂದ draft ಮಾಡಿತು, ಆದರೆ EAGLE-3 target model ya ಅನೇಕ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ layers ಇಂದ features ಅನ್ನೂ FUSE ಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಮತ್ತಷ್ಟು ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದೇ layer ya hidden-state features ಒಂದು ಅಪೂರ್ಣ ಸಿಗ್ನಲ್ ಆಗಿರಬಹುದು ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಕೇವಲ ಕೊನೆಯ layer ya features ಮಾತ್ರ ಬಳಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಸೂಕ್ತವಲ್ಲದಿರಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Comparing Multi-Layer Feature Fusion in Your Draft Head',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Draft Head ನಲ್ಲಿ Multi-Layer Feature Fusion ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely extend your feature-level draft head from the previous lesson to fuse features from multiple layers, and genuinely measure whether this improves agreement rate.\n\n1) Genuinely modify your draft head's input to concatenate (or otherwise combine, e.g. via a small learned weighted-sum) hidden-state features from at least 3 different layers of your mini-GPT (e.g. an early, middle, and late layer) instead of just the single layer you used previously.\n\n2) Genuinely retrain this multi-layer-fusion draft head (keeping your main mini-GPT frozen, as before) on the same training setup you used for your single-layer draft head, and confirm the regression loss decreases.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and directly compare the empirical draft-target agreement rate of your NEW multi-layer-fusion draft head against your ORIGINAL single-layer draft head from the previous lesson, using the same test prompts for both -- report your actual measured agreement-rate difference, and discuss whether your concrete result supports or complicates the beginner exercise's claim that multiple layers provide additional useful signal (including honestly reporting if you find little or no measurable difference in your specific small-scale setup).",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ feature-level draft head ಅನ್ನೂ ಅನೇಕ layers ಇಂದ features ಅನ್ನೂ fuse ಮಾಡಲು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದು agreement rate ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 3 ವಿಭಿನ್ನ layers ಇಂದ hidden-state features ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಲು ನಿಮ್ಮ draft head ya input ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ multi-layer-fusion draft head ಅನ್ನೂ ಮರುtrain ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಹೊಸ ಮತ್ತು ಮೂಲ draft heads ya agreement rate ಅನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring End-to-End Speedup of Your Full EAGLE-Style Pipeline vs Basic Speculative Decoding',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ EAGLE-Style Pipeline ya End-to-End Speedup ಅನ್ನೂ Basic Speculative Decoding ವಿರುದ್ಧ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely conduct the definitive end-to-end comparison of everything built across this module: your basic (token-ID, single independent draft model) speculative decoding from the earlier inference-optimization lesson, versus your full EAGLE-style pipeline (feature-level drafting, multi-layer fusion, and tree-based verification) built across this module's lessons.\n\n1) Genuinely run BOTH complete pipelines on the SAME set of test prompts, and genuinely measure, for each: total wall-clock generation time for a fixed-length output, total number of target-model forward passes required, and the empirical draft-target agreement/acceptance rate achieved.\n\n2) Genuinely report a complete, honest comparison table of your measured results across both approaches, confirming (or disconfirming, if your specific small-scale implementation doesn't show it) whether the EAGLE-style approach achieved a genuinely better speedup-per-draft-compute-cost than basic speculative decoding.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, grounded in your own concrete measurements (not the published EAGLE papers' claims), what specific factor in your implementation contributed MOST to any speedup difference you observed (e.g. was it primarily the feature-level drafting's higher agreement rate, or primarily the tree-based verification's higher tokens-accepted-per-call, or did they contribute comparably) -- and honestly report any ways your small-scale mini-GPT experiment's results might NOT generalize to the much larger real models EAGLE was originally designed for, such as differences in how pronounced the effects are at vastly larger hidden-state dimensions and model depths.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಎಲ್ಲದರ ಅಂತಿಮ end-to-end ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಡೆಸಿ: ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲಭೂತ speculative decoding ವಿರುದ್ಧ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ EAGLE-style pipeline.\n\n1) ಎರಡೂ ಪೂರ್ಣ pipelines ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ test prompts ಮೇಲೆ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಟ್ಟು wall-clock ಸಮಯ, target-model forward passes ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ, ಮತ್ತು agreement rate ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ, ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕ ಹೋಲಿಕೆ ಕೋಷ್ಟಕವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಯಾವ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಅಂಶ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಕೊಡುಗೆ ನೀಡಿತು ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಚಿಕ್ಕ-ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳು ದೊಡ್ಡ ನಿಜ models ಗೆ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯೀಕರಿಸದಿರಬಹುದಾದ ವಿಧಾನಗಳನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m199, l199_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Where Speculative Decoding Helps Least: Batch Size, Task Type, and Hardware Limits",
      titleKn: "Speculative Decoding ಕಡಿಮೆ ಸಹಾಯ ಮಾಡುವ ಸ್ಥಳ: Batch Size, Task Type, ಮತ್ತು Hardware Limits",
      problem: "Speculative decoding's benefit is not universal -- it specifically helps most when generation is MEMORY-BANDWIDTH-BOUND (the GPU spends more time moving weights from memory than doing actual arithmetic, common at SMALL batch sizes, since a single small batch doesn't use all available compute anyway) rather than COMPUTE-BOUND (common at LARGE batch sizes, where the GPU's arithmetic units are already fully saturated processing many requests simultaneously).\n\n1) Explain why speculative decoding's benefit specifically shrinks (and can even become a net slowdown) at large batch sizes -- given that verifying K draft tokens for MANY simultaneous requests all at once requires substantially more total arithmetic per target-model call than verifying 1 token per request would, why might this extra arithmetic cost outweigh the benefit of needing fewer total target-model calls, once the GPU's compute (rather than memory bandwidth) becomes the bottleneck?\n\n2) Explain why tasks where the draft model's guesses are systematically LESS reliable (e.g. highly creative or unpredictable generation, versus more constrained/predictable tasks like code completion with a fixed template) would see less speculative-decoding benefit -- connecting this back to the draft-target agreement rate concept from the earlier lesson in this phase.",
      problemKn: "Speculative decoding ya ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ ಸಾರ್ವತ್ರಿಕವಲ್ಲ -- ಇದು specifically ಚಿಕ್ಕ batch sizes ನಲ್ಲಿ memory-bandwidth-bound ಪರಿಸ್ಥಿತಿಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಸಹಾಯ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) large batch sizes ನಲ್ಲಿ speculative decoding ya ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ ಏಕೆ ಕುಗ್ಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) draft model ya ಊಹೆಗಳು ಕಡಿಮೆ ವಿಶ್ವಾಸಾರ್ಹ ಇರುವ tasks ಏಕೆ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ ಕಾಣುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Speculative Decoding Speedup Across Different Batch Sizes',
      titleKn: 'ವಿಭಿನ್ನ Batch Sizes ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Speculative Decoding Speedup ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment testing the beginner exercise's batch-size claim directly on your own EAGLE-style implementation from this module.\n\n1) Genuinely run your speculative decoding pipeline at several different batch sizes (e.g. batch size 1, a moderate size, and the largest batch size your hardware/implementation can handle), measuring at each batch size: total wall-clock generation time WITH speculative decoding versus WITHOUT it (plain target-only autoregressive generation), for the same total amount of generated text.\n\n2) Genuinely report the measured speedup factor (with-speculative-decoding time versus without) at EACH batch size you tested, and confirm or disconfirm whether the speedup genuinely shrinks as batch size increases, as the beginner exercise's reasoning predicted.\n\n3) Genuinely identify, from your own measured data, the approximate batch size at which speculative decoding's benefit becomes negligible or reverses into a net slowdown in your specific setup, and discuss what this concrete finding implies about deployment decisions -- specifically, why a real serving system might need to dynamically enable or disable speculative decoding depending on CURRENT batch load (using your continuous-batching scheduler from the earlier inference-optimization lesson) rather than using it unconditionally for every request.",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ya batch-size ಹಕ್ಕನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ EAGLE-style implementation ಮೇಲೆ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ speculative decoding pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ batch sizes ನಲ್ಲಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ batch size ಗೆ speedup ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) speedup ಬ್ಯಾಚ್ ಗಾತ್ರ ಹೆಚ್ಚಾದಂತೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಕುಗ್ಗುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) speculative decoding ya ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ ಅತ್ಯಲ್ಪವಾಗುವ batch size ಅನ್ನೂ ಗುರುತಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು deployment ನಿರ್ಧಾರಗಳ ಬಗ್ಗೆ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Dynamic Speculative-Decoding Toggling Based on Live Batch Load',
      titleKn: 'Live Batch Load ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ Dynamic Speculative-Decoding Toggling ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design, implement, and measure a dynamic policy that automatically enables or disables speculative decoding based on current serving load, directly acting on the concrete finding from the intermediate exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a policy function that, given the CURRENT number of active requests in your continuous-batching scheduler (from the inference-optimization lesson), decides whether to use speculative decoding or plain generation for the current batch -- set the threshold using the specific batch size you identified in the intermediate exercise as the crossover point.\n\n2) Genuinely integrate this dynamic policy into your full serving pipeline (combining your continuous-batching scheduler and your speculative decoding implementation) and genuinely simulate a realistic workload where batch load VARIES over time (e.g. starting with few requests, ramping up to many, then dropping back down), logging which mode (speculative or plain) your policy chose at each point.\n\n3) Genuinely measure total wall-clock time to process this full variable-load workload using your DYNAMIC policy, compared against two static baselines: always using speculative decoding, and never using it -- report whether your dynamic policy genuinely achieved the best (or close to best) result across the full variable-load scenario, discussing any genuine overhead or misjudgment your policy introduced (e.g. switching modes too frequently, or reacting too slowly to load changes) that a real production system would need to handle more carefully.",
      problemKn: "ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ serving load ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ speculative decoding ಅನ್ನೂ ಸ್ವಯಂಚಾಲಿತವಾಗಿ ಸಕ್ರಿಯಗೊಳಿಸುವ ಅಥವಾ ನಿಷ್ಕ್ರಿಯಗೊಳಿಸುವ ಒಂದು dynamic policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ active requests ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ speculative decoding ಬಳಸಬೇಕೇ ಎಂದು ನಿರ್ಧರಿಸುವ ಒಂದು policy function ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, intermediate exercise ya crossover batch size ಬಳಸಿ threshold ಹೊಂದಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ dynamic policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ serving pipeline ಗೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು batch load ಸಮಯದೊಂದಿಗೆ ಬದಲಾಗುವ ಒಂದು ನೈಜ workload ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ simulate ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಪೂರ್ಣ variable-load workload ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಕ್ರಿಯೆಗೊಳಿಸಲು ಒಟ್ಟು ಸಮಯವನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ DYNAMIC policy ಬಳಸಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ, ಎರಡು ಸ್ಥಿರ baselines ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ policy ya ಯಾವುದೇ ನಿಜ overhead ಅಥವಾ ತಪ್ಪು ತೀರ್ಮಾನವನ್ನೂ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m200, l200_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Differential Attention: Cancelling Out Attention Noise with a Subtracted Second Map",
      titleKn: "Differential Attention: ಒಂದು Subtracted ಎರಡನೇ Map ಜೊತೆ Attention Noise ಅನ್ನೂ ರದ್ದುಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Standard attention computes a single softmax-normalized attention map per head, but this single map can allocate non-trivial attention weight to irrelevant tokens purely as a side effect of the softmax always needing to sum to 1 across all positions -- effectively spreading some 'attention noise' onto tokens that shouldn't matter. Differential Attention addresses this by computing TWO separate attention maps per head (using two independent sets of query/key projections) and taking their WEIGHTED DIFFERENCE (map1 minus lambda times map2, for a learned or scheduled scalar lambda) as the effective attention pattern, with the idea that noise common to both maps cancels out in the subtraction while the genuine, task-relevant signal (which differs between the two maps) survives.\n\n1) Explain, using an analogy to differential signal processing (e.g. noise-cancelling headphones subtracting a noise-reference signal from the main signal), why subtracting a SECOND, independently-computed attention map from the first can cancel out noise that's common to both, while preserving genuine attention patterns that differ between them -- what would need to be true about the noise for this cancellation to actually work?\n\n2) Explain why differential attention needs the lambda coefficient to be a genuinely LEARNED (or carefully scheduled) parameter rather than an arbitrary fixed constant like 1.0 -- what could go wrong (in terms of destroying genuinely useful signal, not just noise) if lambda were set too large or too small relative to how much genuine differing signal exists between the two maps?",
      problemKn: "Standard attention ಪ್ರತಿ head ಗೆ ಒಂದೇ softmax-normalized attention map ಅನ್ನೂ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದರೆ ಈ ಒಂಟಿ map softmax ಯಾವಾಗಲೂ 1 ಗೆ ಮೊತ್ತವಾಗಬೇಕಾದ ಕಾರಣ ಅಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ tokens ಗೆ ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ ಗಮನ ಹಂಚಬಹುದು. Differential Attention ಪ್ರತಿ head ಗೆ ಎರಡು ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ attention maps ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ ಅವುಗಳ WEIGHTED DIFFERENCE ಅನ್ನೂ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳುವ ಮೂಲಕ ಇದನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) noise-cancelling headphones ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಸಾದೃಶ್ಯ ಬಳಸಿ, ಎರಡನೇ, ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿದ attention map ಅನ್ನೂ ಕಳೆಯುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ noise ಅನ್ನೂ ರದ್ದುಗೊಳಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) lambda coefficient ಏಕೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಕಲಿತ ಪ್ಯಾರಾಮೀಟರ್ ಆಗಿರಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Differential Attention on Your Mini-GPT',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ Differential Attention ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement differential attention as a modification to your mini-GPT's attention layer.\n\n1) Genuinely implement two independent sets of query and key projections per attention head (doubling the Q/K projection parameters for each modified head, while keeping a shared or separately-projected value), compute two separate softmax attention maps from these two Q/K pairs, and combine them as map1 - lambda * map2, using a learned scalar lambda parameter (initialized to a small value, as real differential attention implementations do) that participates in training via backpropagation.\n\n2) Genuinely verify your implementation produces correctly-shaped outputs and trains without errors, running a small number of training steps and confirming the loss decreases, including checking that your learned lambda parameter's value actually CHANGES during training (confirming it's genuinely learned, not stuck at its initialization).\n\n3) Genuinely design a concrete diagnostic to check whether differential attention is actually behaving as intended in your implementation: construct a synthetic input where you know which tokens SHOULD receive high attention (e.g. a copy-task where a specific marker token should attend strongly back to an earlier matching token) and some deliberately irrelevant distractor tokens, then genuinely compare the attention weight your differential-attention layer assigns to the distractor tokens against what a standard (non-differential) attention layer in an otherwise-identical model assigns -- report whether your differential implementation genuinely assigns LESS weight to the irrelevant distractors, providing concrete evidence of the noise-cancellation claim.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya attention layer ಗೆ ಮಾರ್ಪಾಡಾಗಿ differential attention ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ attention head ಗೆ ಎರಡು ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರ query ಮತ್ತು key projections ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಎರಡು ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ softmax attention maps ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಅವುಗಳನ್ನೂ map1 - lambda*map2 ಆಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ, ಒಂದು learned lambda parameter ಬಳಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ implementation ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ train ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಮತ್ತು lambda ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬದಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು synthetic input ಬಳಸಿ noise-cancellation ಹಕ್ಕನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ, standard attention ಜೊತೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Whether Differential Attention Improves Long-Context Retrieval Accuracy',
      titleKn: 'Differential Attention Long-Context Retrieval Accuracy ಅನ್ನೂ ಸುಧಾರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment testing the practical, downstream benefit that differential attention's authors claim: improved accuracy on tasks requiring precise retrieval of specific information from a long context, precisely because less attention 'noise' should mean less chance of the wrong (irrelevant) token accidentally dominating the attention-weighted average.\n\n1) Genuinely construct a synthetic 'needle in a haystack' style retrieval task: embed a specific, unique piece of information (the 'needle') at a random position within a long sequence of otherwise-irrelevant filler text (the 'haystack'), and require the model to correctly report the needle's content when queried at the end of the sequence -- construct at least 20 such test instances with the needle at varying positions.\n\n2) Genuinely train two otherwise-identical mini-GPT models (same layer count, same hidden size) -- one using your differential attention implementation from the intermediate exercise, and one using standard attention -- on a training set of similar synthetic retrieval tasks, and genuinely evaluate both on your held-out test instances, measuring exact-match retrieval accuracy for each model.\n\n3) Genuinely report your measured accuracy comparison between the two models, and honestly discuss whether your concrete small-scale experiment supports the claimed benefit of differential attention for long-context retrieval -- including honestly reporting a null or mixed result if that's what you genuinely measured, and discussing what aspects of your small-scale setup (context length, model size, training data volume) might differ enough from the original paper's much larger-scale experiments to explain any discrepancy.",
      problemKn: "differential attention ya ಲೇಖಕರು ಹೇಳುವ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಪ್ರಯೋಜನವನ್ನೂ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ: ದೀರ್ಘ context ಇಂದ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಮಾಹಿತಿಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಹಿಂಪಡೆಯುವ tasks ನಲ್ಲಿ ಸುಧಾರಿತ accuracy.\n\n1) ಒಂದು synthetic 'needle in a haystack' retrieval task ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು differential attention ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು standard attention model ಅನ್ನೂ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು exact-match retrieval accuracy ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು null ಅಥವಾ mixed ಫಲಿತಾಂಶವನ್ನೂ ಸಹ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m200, l200_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "GroupNorm on Attention Heads: Stabilizing the Differential Signal Across Heads",
      titleKn: "Attention Heads ಮೇಲೆ GroupNorm: Heads ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Differential Signal ಸ್ಥಿರಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Differential Attention V2 adds an important refinement beyond the basic subtracted-map idea from the previous lesson: applying GROUP NORMALIZATION independently to EACH head's output BEFORE combining heads together, rather than normalizing only once after all heads are concatenated (as standard multi-head attention typically does downstream). This matters because after subtracting lambda times the second attention map from the first, different heads can end up with substantially different output SCALES (variance) depending on how much cancellation happened in each head, and combining heads with wildly different scales without per-head normalization can let a few high-variance heads dominate the combined representation.\n\n1) Explain concretely why the subtraction step in differential attention could produce different output variances across different heads -- given that each head learns its own independent lambda and its own independent pair of attention maps, why might one head's map1-lambda*map2 result end up with much smaller or larger magnitude than another head's, purely due to how much genuine cancellation occurred in each?\n\n2) Explain why applying normalization per-head BEFORE combining (rather than one normalization AFTER combining all heads) specifically addresses the domination problem -- what does per-head normalization guarantee about each head's contribution to the combined output that a single post-combination normalization does not?",
      problemKn: "Differential Attention V2 ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ಮೂಲಭೂತ subtracted-map ಕಲ್ಪನೆ ಮೀರಿ ಒಂದು ಮುಖ್ಯ ಸುಧಾರಣೆಯನ್ನೂ ಸೇರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: ಪ್ರತಿ head ya output ಗೆ heads ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವ ಮೊದಲು ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ GROUP NORMALIZATION ಅನ್ವಯಿಸುವುದೂ.\n\n1) differential attention ನಲ್ಲಿ subtraction ಹಂತ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ heads ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ output variances ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) heads ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪ್ರತಿ-head normalization ಅನ್ವಯಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ domination ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Per-Head GroupNorm and Measuring Its Stabilizing Effect',
      titleKn: 'Per-Head GroupNorm ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ Stabilizing ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely add per-head group normalization to your differential attention implementation from the previous lesson and genuinely measure its concrete effect.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a normalization step applied independently to each head's output (after the map1-lambda*map2 combination but before concatenating heads together), using a standard normalization technique (e.g. LayerNorm or GroupNorm applied per-head) with its own learned scale/shift parameters per head.\n\n2) Genuinely instrument your model to measure the actual output VARIANCE of each individual head, both WITHOUT this per-head normalization (your intermediate exercise's implementation from the previous lesson) and WITH it, across a batch of real training data -- report whether you observe genuinely different variances across heads in the unnormalized case, and whether the per-head normalization genuinely equalizes these variances as intended.\n\n3) Genuinely train both the normalized and unnormalized versions of your differential-attention mini-GPT for the same number of steps and compare their training loss curves -- report whether per-head normalization genuinely produced more stable (e.g. smoother, less spiky) training, and discuss any concrete evidence you found (or didn't find) for this stabilization benefit in your specific small-scale setup.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ differential attention implementation ಗೆ per-head group normalization ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸೇರಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಪರಿಣಾಮವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ head ya output ಗೆ ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸುವ ಒಂದು normalization ಹಂತವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ head ya ನಿಜ output variance ಅನ್ನೂ, normalization ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ಮತ್ತು ಇದ್ದು ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಎರಡೂ ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು training loss curves ಹೋಲಿಸಿ, ಸ್ಥಿರತೆ ಬಗ್ಗೆ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing Differential Attention V2 Against Standard Attention on Noise-Robustness',
      titleKn: 'Differential Attention V2 ಅನ್ನೂ Standard Attention ವಿರುದ್ಧ Noise-Robustness ಗಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run a comprehensive comparison between your complete Differential Attention V2 implementation (subtracted maps plus per-head normalization from this module's two lessons) and standard attention, specifically targeting the 'attention noise' claim from the very first lesson in this module.\n\n1) Genuinely construct a controlled synthetic task where you can precisely quantify attention 'noise': e.g. a sequence with one genuinely relevant token and many deliberately irrelevant filler tokens, where you can directly measure (by inspecting the attention weights themselves, which you have full access to in your own implementation) exactly how much total attention weight gets allocated to the irrelevant filler tokens versus the one relevant token.\n\n2) Genuinely measure this 'noise allocation' metric for both your Differential Attention V2 model and a standard-attention model of otherwise identical architecture, trained identically on the same task -- report your measured noise-allocation numbers for both, and confirm or disconfirm whether Differential Attention V2 genuinely allocates less attention weight to irrelevant tokens.\n\n3) Genuinely report the DOWNSTREAM task-accuracy difference (not just the raw attention-weight noise metric) between the two models on this task, and discuss, using your own concrete measured numbers across this full module's experiments, whether the added architectural complexity of differential attention (double the Q/K parameters, a learned lambda, per-head normalization) was genuinely justified by measurable gains in your specific small-scale setup, being honest about any results that don't clearly support the technique's claimed benefits.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಎರಡು ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ Differential Attention V2 implementation ಅನ್ನೂ standard attention ವಿರುದ್ಧ, ಈ module ya ಮೊದಲ ಪಾಠದ 'attention noise' ಹಕ್ಕನ್ನೂ ಗುರಿಯಾಗಿಸಿ, ಸಮಗ್ರವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n1) attention 'noise' ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಮಾಣೀಕರಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನಿಯಂತ್ರಿತ synthetic task ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ 'noise allocation' metric ಅನ್ನೂ ಎರಡೂ models ಗೆ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) DOWNSTREAM task-accuracy ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ architectural ಸಂಕೀರ್ಣತೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಮರ್ಥನೀಯವಾಗಿತ್ತೇ ಎಂದು ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕವಾಗಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m200, l200_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "The Cost Side of Differential Attention: Double Parameters, Double Compute",
      titleKn: "Differential Attention ya ವೆಚ್ಚ ಬದಿ: Double Parameters, Double Compute",
      problem: "Everything covered so far in this module has focused on differential attention's quality benefits, but a genuinely complete understanding requires weighing these against its concrete COSTS: since each head now needs TWO independent query/key projections instead of one, differential attention roughly DOUBLES the parameter count and computation specifically devoted to the query/key projections and the attention-score computation (though not necessarily the value projection or the rest of the transformer block), compared to standard attention at the same head count and hidden size.\n\n1) Explain precisely which parts of the attention layer's parameter count and compute genuinely double under differential attention, and which parts (if any) stay the same -- distinguish between the query/key projection matrices, the value projection matrix, and the output projection matrix.\n\n2) Explain why a genuinely fair comparison between differential attention and standard attention (e.g. the kind you ran in the advanced exercise of the previous lesson) needs to control for this cost difference -- what would be wrong with concluding 'differential attention is better' from an experiment that gave the differential-attention model MORE total parameters than the standard-attention baseline, without adjusting for it?",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ನಲ್ಲಿ ಈವರೆಗೆ ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿರುವುದೂ differential attention ya ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ಪ್ರಯೋಜನಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ ಗಮನಹರಿಸಿದೆ, ಆದರೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪೂರ್ಣ ತಿಳುವಳಿಕೆಗೆ ಇವುಗಳನ್ನೂ ಅದರ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ವೆಚ್ಚಗಳ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ತೂಗಬೇಕು: ಪ್ರತಿ head ಗೆ ಈಗ ಎರಡು ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರ query/key projections ಬೇಕಾಗಿರುವುದರಿಂದ, differential attention ಸರಿಸುಮಾರು parameter count ಮತ್ತು compute ಅನ್ನೂ ಡಬಲ್ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) attention layer ya ಯಾವ ಭಾಗಗಳು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಡಬಲ್ ಆಗುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನ್ಯಾಯಯುತ ಹೋಲಿಕೆಗೆ ಈ ವೆಚ್ಚ ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸವನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ನಿಯಂತ್ರಿಸಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Running a Parameter-Matched Comparison Between Differential and Standard Attention',
      titleKn: 'Differential ಮತ್ತು Standard Attention ನಡುವೆ Parameter-Matched ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run a fair, parameter-matched comparison addressing the cost concern from the beginner exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely calculate the exact extra parameter count your differential attention implementation (from the earlier lessons in this module) adds compared to a standard-attention model of the same head count and hidden size, and genuinely construct a MODIFIED standard-attention baseline model that has been widened (e.g. more heads, or a larger hidden size in its feed-forward layers) so its TOTAL parameter count genuinely matches your differential-attention model's total parameter count as closely as you can achieve.\n\n2) Genuinely train both your differential-attention model and this parameter-MATCHED standard-attention baseline (not the original, smaller standard baseline) on the same synthetic retrieval task from the earlier lesson's advanced exercise, for the same number of steps.\n\n3) Genuinely report the accuracy comparison between these two now-parameter-matched models, and discuss whether differential attention's benefit (if any) survives once you've given the standard-attention baseline an equal parameter budget to spend however it wants -- report honestly whether your parameter-matched comparison still favors differential attention, favors the wider standard baseline, or shows no clear difference in your specific small-scale experiment.",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ya ವೆಚ್ಚ ಕಾಳಜಿಯನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನ್ಯಾಯಯುತ, parameter-matched ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ differential attention implementation ಸೇರಿಸುವ ನಿಖರ ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ parameter count ಅನ್ನೂ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದೇ ಒಟ್ಟು parameter count ಹೊಂದಿರುವ ಒಂದು ಅಗಲಗೊಳಿಸಿದ standard-attention baseline ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಎರಡೂ models ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ synthetic retrieval task ಮೇಲೆ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ parameter-matched models ನಡುವಿನ accuracy ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕವಾಗಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Wall-Clock Training and Inference Cost of Differential Attention',
      titleKn: 'Differential Attention ya Wall-Clock Training ಮತ್ತು Inference ವೆಚ್ಚವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely measure the actual wall-clock cost of differential attention, completing the cost-side analysis this module has built toward.\n\n1) Genuinely measure actual wall-clock time per training step for your differential-attention mini-GPT versus your original standard-attention mini-GPT (same head count/hidden size, NOT the parameter-matched wider baseline from the intermediate exercise), reporting the measured slowdown factor, and separately measure actual generation latency per token for both models using your KV-cache implementation from the inference-optimization lesson (noting that differential attention's KV cache must now store two sets of keys per head, affecting cache memory too -- genuinely measure this memory difference as well).\n\n2) Genuinely combine ALL your findings from across this entire module into a single honest cost-benefit summary: the measured quality/noise-robustness benefit (from the earlier lessons), the measured parameter-matched accuracy result (from the intermediate exercise), and the measured wall-clock/memory costs (from this exercise).\n\n3) Genuinely give your own concrete, evidence-based recommendation for when (if ever) differential attention's measured benefits in your specific experiments were worth its measured costs, explicitly citing your own numbers rather than the original paper's claims, and discuss what additional experiments (e.g. at larger model scale, or on more diverse task types) would be needed before generalizing your small-scale conclusion to real production model design decisions.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ವೆಚ್ಚ-ಬದಿ ವಿಶ್ಲೇಷಣೆಯನ್ನೂ ಪೂರ್ಣಗೊಳಿಸಿ, differential attention ya ನಿಜ wall-clock ವೆಚ್ಚವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ differential-attention mini-GPT ಮತ್ತು ಮೂಲ standard-attention mini-GPT ಗೆ ಪ್ರತಿ training step wall-clock ಸಮಯ ಮತ್ತು generation latency ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ, KV-cache memory ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸವನ್ನೂ ಸೇರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ module ya ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕ cost-benefit ಸಾರಾಂಶವಾಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪುರಾವೆ-ಆಧಾರಿತ ಶಿಫಾರಸನ್ನೂ ನೀಡಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ numbers ಉಲ್ಲೇಖಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m201, l201_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Native Sparse Attention: Learning WHERE to Attend Instead of Attending Everywhere",
      titleKn: "Native Sparse Attention: ಎಲ್ಲೆಡೆ ಗಮನ ನೀಡುವ ಬದಲು ಎಲ್ಲಿ ಗಮನ ನೀಡಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ಕಲಿಯುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Standard attention computes a score between every query and every key, an approach whose compute cost grows QUADRATICALLY with sequence length -- expensive for very long contexts, most of which is often 'wasted' since most tokens in a long sequence are irrelevant to any given query. Native Sparse Attention (NSA, from DeepSeek) addresses this NATIVELY (built into the architecture and trained from scratch, rather than a training-free approximation applied to an already-trained dense model) by combining multiple parallel attention 'branches' operating at different granularities -- for example, a coarse-grained branch that compresses blocks of tokens into summary representations to cheaply identify which broad regions of the sequence are likely relevant, alongside a fine-grained branch that only computes detailed, full-resolution attention within the specific blocks the coarse branch identified as worth examining closely.\n\n1) Explain why doing sparsity NATIVELY (training the model from the start with this sparse pattern) can preserve more quality than taking an already-trained DENSE model and applying sparsity as a post-hoc approximation at inference time -- what does the model get to do during training that a post-hoc approximation applied only at inference cannot?\n\n2) Explain why a purely coarse-grained (block-summary-only) approach alone would lose important fine-grained information -- what specific detail would summarizing a whole block of tokens into one representation necessarily discard, motivating NSA's need for a SEPARATE fine-grained branch operating on the selected blocks.",
      problemKn: "Standard attention ಪ್ರತಿ query ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ key ನಡುವೆ ಒಂದು score ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುತ್ತದೆ, ಇದರ compute ವೆಚ್ಚ sequence length ಜೊತೆ QUADRATIC ಆಗಿ ಬೆಳೆಯುತ್ತದೆ. Native Sparse Attention (NSA, DeepSeek ಇಂದ) ಇದನ್ನೂ NATIVELY ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ವಿಭಿನ್ನ granularities ನಲ್ಲಿ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ ಅನೇಕ parallel attention 'branches' ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ.\n\n1) sparsity ಅನ್ನೂ NATIVELY ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ಸಂರಕ್ಷಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಕೇವಲ coarse-grained ವಿಧಾನ ಒಂಟಿಯಾಗಿ ಏಕೆ ಮುಖ್ಯ ವಿವರಗಳನ್ನೂ ಕಳೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Two-Branch Coarse-Then-Fine Sparse Attention Layer',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Two-Branch Coarse-Then-Fine Sparse Attention Layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified two-branch NSA-style sparse attention layer on your mini-GPT.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the coarse-grained branch: partition the key/value sequence into fixed-size blocks (e.g. blocks of 16-32 tokens), compute a compressed summary representation for each block (e.g. a mean-pooled or learned-weighted-average of the keys within the block), and compute attention scores between each query and these block-level SUMMARY keys (a much smaller set than the full token-level keys) to identify the top-K most relevant blocks for that query.\n\n2) Genuinely implement the fine-grained branch: for each query, gather the FULL, un-summarized keys/values ONLY from the top-K blocks identified by the coarse branch, and compute standard full-resolution attention restricted to just those tokens.\n\n3) Genuinely verify your implementation runs correctly (correct output shapes, successful forward/backward pass, loss decreases over a small number of training steps), and genuinely measure the actual number of key/value positions each query ends up attending to under your sparse scheme versus the total sequence length, reporting the real sparsity ratio (fraction of positions skipped) your implementation achieves for a chosen sequence length and block/top-K configuration.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ two-branch NSA-ಶೈಲಿಯ sparse attention layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) coarse-grained branch ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: key/value ಅನುಕ್ರಮವನ್ನೂ ಸ್ಥಿರ-ಗಾತ್ರದ blocks ಆಗಿ ವಿಭಜಿಸಿ, ಪ್ರತಿ block ಗೆ ಒಂದು compressed summary ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ, top-K blocks ಗುರುತಿಸಿ.\n\n2) fine-grained branch ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: top-K blocks ಇಂದ ಮಾತ್ರ ಪೂರ್ಣ keys/values ಸಂಗ್ರಹಿಸಿ, standard attention ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ implementation ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ ಚಲಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ sparsity ratio ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Quality-Speed Tradeoff of Your Sparse Attention Across Block Sizes',
      titleKn: 'Block Sizes ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಮ್ಮ Sparse Attention ya Quality-Speed Tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an ablation study over your NSA-style implementation's key hyperparameters (block size and top-K), to genuinely characterize the tradeoff real sparse-attention system designers must navigate.\n\n1) Genuinely train your sparse-attention mini-GPT at several different (block size, top-K) configurations -- e.g. small blocks with a larger top-K (finer-grained selection) versus large blocks with a smaller top-K (coarser-grained selection) -- while keeping the EFFECTIVE sparsity ratio (roughly, total tokens actually attended to divided by total sequence length) as similar as possible across configurations, so you're comparing granularity choices at a fixed compute budget rather than comparing different total amounts of compute.\n\n2) Genuinely evaluate each configuration on both a standard held-out perplexity/accuracy measure (reusing your evaluation harness) AND the synthetic long-range retrieval task from the differential-attention module's advanced exercise (adapting it as needed), reporting measured quality for each.\n\n3) Genuinely measure actual wall-clock speedup each sparse configuration achieves over full dense attention at the same sequence length, and report your complete quality-vs-speed tradeoff table across configurations -- discuss, using your own concrete numbers, whether finer-grained block/top-K choices (more precise selection, but more routing overhead) or coarser-grained choices (less precise, but simpler/faster routing) won out in your specific small-scale experiments, and what this suggests about the genuine engineering tradeoffs real NSA-style systems must tune for their specific target sequence lengths and hardware.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಜ sparse-attention system designers ನ್ಯಾವಿಗೇಟ್ ಮಾಡಬೇಕಾದ tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಮಾಣೀಕರಿಸಲು ನಿಮ್ಮ NSA-ಶೈಲಿಯ implementation ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ hyperparameters (block size ಮತ್ತು top-K) ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ablation ಅಧ್ಯಯನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ (block size, top-K) configurations ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ sparse-attention mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ, ಪರಿಣಾಮಕಾರಿ sparsity ratio ಅನ್ನೂ ಸ್ಥಿರವಾಗಿಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ configuration ಅನ್ನೂ standard perplexity/accuracy ಮತ್ತು synthetic long-range retrieval task ಎರಡರ ಮೇಲೆ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಪ್ರತಿ sparse configuration ya ನಿಜ wall-clock speedup ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪೂರ್ಣ quality-vs-speed tradeoff ಕೋಷ್ಟಕವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m201, l201_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "The Third Branch: Adding a Local Sliding Window for Nearby-Token Guarantees",
      titleKn: "ಮೂರನೇ Branch: Nearby-Token ಖಾತರಿಗಳಿಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು Local Sliding Window ಸೇರಿಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Full NSA adds a THIRD parallel branch beyond the coarse-summary and fine-grained-selection branches from the previous lesson: a LOCAL SLIDING WINDOW branch (similar to the sliding window attention you studied in an earlier module) that unconditionally guarantees every query attends to its immediate nearby tokens, regardless of what the coarse-branch's block-selection decides.\n\n1) Explain why relying purely on the learned block-selection mechanism from the previous lesson (without this guaranteed local branch) risks a genuine failure mode -- what could go wrong if the coarse branch's router, for some query, mistakenly failed to select the block containing that query's own IMMEDIATELY preceding tokens (which are almost always relevant for coherent local language generation, e.g. maintaining grammatical agreement with the last few words)?\n\n2) Explain why combining all three branches (coarse-selection, fine-grained-on-selected-blocks, and guaranteed-local-window) and learning how to WEIGHT their combined contribution (rather than simply picking one branch's output) lets the model adaptively rely more on whichever branch is genuinely most useful for a given query, rather than being locked into a single fixed sparsity pattern for every token.",
      problemKn: "ಪೂರ್ಣ NSA ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ coarse-summary ಮತ್ತು fine-grained-selection branches ಮೀರಿ ಒಂದು ಮೂರನೇ parallel branch ಸೇರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: ಒಂದು LOCAL SLIDING WINDOW branch, ಇದು ಪ್ರತಿ query ಅದರ ತಕ್ಷಣದ ಹತ್ತಿರದ tokens ಗೆ ಬೇಷರತ್ತಾಗಿ ಗಮನ ನೀಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಖಾತರಿಪಡಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಕೇವಲ ಕಲಿತ block-selection ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯ ಮೇಲೆ ಅವಲಂಬಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ವೈಫಲ್ಯ ವಿಧಾನದ ಅಪಾಯ ಹೊಂದಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಮೂರು branches ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವುದೂ ಮತ್ತು ಅವುಗಳ ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ ಕೊಡುಗೆಯನ್ನೂ WEIGHT ಮಾಡಲು ಕಲಿಯುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ model ಗೆ ಹೊಂದಾಣಿಕೆಯಾಗಲು ಅನುಮತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing the Full Three-Branch NSA Layer with Learned Gating',
      titleKn: 'Learned Gating ಜೊತೆ ಪೂರ್ಣ Three-Branch NSA Layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely extend your two-branch sparse attention implementation from the previous lesson into the complete three-branch NSA design.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the third, local-sliding-window branch: for each query, unconditionally compute standard full-resolution attention restricted to a fixed-size window of the most recent W tokens (reusing your sliding-window attention implementation from the earlier relevant module in this phase if you built one).\n\n2) Genuinely implement a learned gating mechanism: a small module (e.g. a linear layer taking the query's hidden state as input) that outputs a weight for each of the three branches' outputs (coarse-selected fine-grained attention, and local-window attention -- treat the coarse branch's role as feeding the selection for the fine-grained branch, so the combination is over the fine-grained and local-window outputs, or include all three if your design computes standalone outputs for each), and combine the branches' outputs via this learned weighting rather than a fixed, hand-picked combination rule.\n\n3) Genuinely verify your full three-branch implementation trains correctly (loss decreases) and genuinely instrument it to report, across a batch of real training data, the actual learned gate weights assigned to each branch -- report whether different tokens/positions genuinely receive different gate-weight patterns (confirming the gating mechanism is doing genuine, position-dependent work) or whether it collapses to always favoring one branch uniformly (which would suggest the gating isn't learning anything useful in your specific small-scale setup).",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ two-branch sparse attention implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಪೂರ್ಣ three-branch NSA ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಕ್ಕೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಮೂರನೇ, local-sliding-window branch ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು learned gating ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಇದು ಪ್ರತಿ branch ya output ಗೆ ಒಂದು weight ಔಟ್‌ಪುಟ್ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ three-branch implementation ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ train ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು learned gate weights ಅನ್ನೂ instrument ಮಾಡಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Ablating Each NSA Branch to Measure Its Individual Contribution',
      titleKn: 'ಪ್ರತಿ NSA Branch ya ವೈಯಕ್ತಿಕ ಕೊಡುಗೆಯನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಲು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Ablate ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an ablation study that isolates the individual contribution of each of the three NSA branches to overall model quality, directly testing the beginner exercise's claims about why each branch is necessary.\n\n1) Genuinely train four versions of your mini-GPT: the full three-branch NSA model from the intermediate exercise, and three ABLATED versions each missing exactly one branch (e.g. coarse+fine-grained only with no local window; local-window+coarse-selection only with no fine-grained detail; fine-grained+local-window only with no coarse block-selection -- adapt based on which ablations are meaningful given your specific implementation).\n\n2) Genuinely evaluate all four model versions on both your standard evaluation harness (perplexity/accuracy) and a set of test cases SPECIFICALLY designed to stress each branch's claimed purpose (e.g. a task requiring precise local grammatical coherence to stress-test the local-window branch's necessity, and a task requiring identifying one relevant long-range block among many to stress-test the coarse-selection branch's necessity).\n\n3) Genuinely report which ablated version showed the LARGEST measured quality drop on which specific stress-test task, and discuss whether your concrete results confirm the beginner exercise's reasoning about each branch's distinct purpose -- report honestly if any branch's removal had surprisingly little measured impact in your specific small-scale setup, and discuss what that would suggest about whether that branch's function was being substantially covered by one of the other branches instead.",
      problemKn: "ಮೂರು NSA branches ಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರತಿಯೊಂದರ ವೈಯಕ್ತಿಕ ಕೊಡುಗೆಯನ್ನೂ ಒಟ್ಟು model ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟಕ್ಕೆ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ablation ಅಧ್ಯಯನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಾಲ್ಕು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ: ಪೂರ್ಣ three-branch model ಮತ್ತು ಮೂರು ablated ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳು, ಪ್ರತಿಯೊಂದೂ ಒಂದು branch ಕಳೆದುಕೊಂಡಿದೆ.\n\n2) ಎಲ್ಲಾ ನಾಲ್ಕು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ standard evaluation ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ branch ya ಹಕ್ಕಿನ ಉದ್ದೇಶವನ್ನೂ ಒತ್ತಡ-ಪರೀಕ್ಷೆ ಮಾಡುವ test cases ಮೇಲೆ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಯಾವ ablated ಆವೃತ್ತಿ ಯಾವ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ stress-test task ಮೇಲೆ ಅತಿ ದೊಡ್ಡ ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ಕುಸಿತ ತೋರಿಸಿತು ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m201, l201_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Hardware-Aligned Sparsity: Why Theoretical FLOP Reduction Isn't Enough",
      titleKn: "Hardware-Aligned Sparsity: Theoretical FLOP Reduction ಸಾಕಾಗದಿರುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ",
      problem: "A genuinely important, often-overlooked design goal behind NSA is that its sparsity pattern is deliberately BLOCK-STRUCTURED (operating on contiguous chunks of tokens) rather than scattered/arbitrary (individual, non-contiguous tokens selected one at a time) -- specifically because real GPU hardware achieves its best throughput on large, contiguous, regularly-shaped memory accesses and matrix operations, and a sparsity pattern that theoretically skips the same NUMBER of computations but does so in a scattered, irregular pattern often fails to translate that theoretical FLOP savings into actual wall-clock speedup.\n\n1) Explain concretely why a sparse attention pattern that skips individual, scattered token positions (rather than contiguous blocks) might achieve LESS real speedup than its theoretical FLOP reduction would suggest -- what does GPU hardware need in terms of memory access patterns to run efficiently, and why does irregular/scattered sparsity fail to provide this even though it does less total arithmetic?\n\n2) Explain why this consideration means a sparse attention mechanism's DESIGN (block size, selection granularity) cannot be chosen purely by asking 'what pattern would a human expert or a learned router consider most semantically relevant' -- what additional, hardware-specific constraint must genuinely also shape these design choices for the mechanism to deliver real speedup rather than just a smaller theoretical FLOP count.",
      problemKn: "NSA ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮುಖ್ಯ, ಆಗಾಗ್ಗೆ ಕಡೆಗಣಿಸಲಾದ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ಗುರಿಯೆಂದರೆ ಅದರ sparsity pattern ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ BLOCK-STRUCTURED ಆಗಿದೆ, ಚದುರಿದ/ಅನಿಯಂತ್ರಿತವಲ್ಲ. ಇದಕ್ಕೆ ಕಾರಣ ನಿಜ GPU hardware ದೊಡ್ಡ, contiguous, ನಿಯಮಿತ-ಆಕಾರದ memory accesses ಮೇಲೆ ಉತ್ತಮ throughput ಸಾಧಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಚದುರಿದ token positions ಬಿಡುವ ಒಂದು sparse attention pattern ಏಕೆ ಅದರ ಸೈದ್ಧಾಂತಿಕ FLOP ಕಡಿತಕ್ಕಿಂತ ಕಡಿಮೆ ನಿಜ speedup ಸಾಧಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಪರಿಗಣನೆ ಒಂದು sparse attention mechanism ya ವಿನ್ಯಾಸವನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ಕೇವಲ 'ಶಬ್ದಾರ್ಥದ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತತೆ' ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಮಾಡಲಾಗುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Gap Between Theoretical FLOP Savings and Real Speedup',
      titleKn: 'Theoretical FLOP Savings ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ Speedup ನಡುವಿನ ಅಂತರವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment that directly measures the gap the beginner exercise describes, comparing your block-structured NSA implementation against a deliberately scattered/irregular sparsity pattern with the SAME theoretical FLOP count.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a scattered-sparsity variant: instead of your NSA implementation's contiguous block-based selection, implement an attention mechanism where each query attends to the SAME total NUMBER of key positions as your block-based version, but those positions are chosen individually/scattered (e.g. via a fixed random or round-robin pattern unrelated to block structure) rather than as contiguous blocks.\n\n2) Genuinely calculate the theoretical FLOP count for both your block-structured NSA and your scattered-sparsity variant, confirming they are indeed equal (or very close) as designed, then genuinely measure ACTUAL wall-clock inference time for both implementations at the same sequence length on your available hardware.\n\n3) Genuinely report the measured wall-clock difference between the two theoretically-equal-FLOP-count implementations -- confirm whether your block-structured version genuinely runs faster in practice despite doing the 'same amount' of theoretical work, and discuss what this concrete, measured gap reveals about why real sparse-attention system papers report both theoretical FLOP reduction AND separately-measured wall-clock speedup, rather than treating the two as interchangeable.",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ವಿವರಿಸುವ ಅಂತರವನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ block-structured NSA implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ ಸೈದ್ಧಾಂತಿಕ FLOP count ಹೊಂದಿರುವ ಒಂದು ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ ಚದುರಿದ sparsity pattern ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು scattered-sparsity variant ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ಸೈದ್ಧಾಂತಿಕ FLOP count ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ ಸಮಾನ ಎಂದು ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ, ನಂತರ ನಿಜ wall-clock inference time ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ wall-clock ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Tuning Block Size for Your Specific Hardware\\u2019s Real Measured Sweet Spot',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ Hardware ya ನಿಜ Measured Sweet Spot ಗಾಗಿ Block Size ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Tune ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely conduct a hardware-grounded tuning study that finds the actual best-performing block size for YOUR specific available hardware, rather than assuming any block size choice from the literature transfers directly.\n\n1) Genuinely measure actual wall-clock inference time for your block-structured NSA implementation (from the earlier lesson) across a range of different block sizes (e.g. very small blocks, moderate blocks, and large blocks), holding the overall sparsity ratio approximately constant across all tested block sizes by adjusting top-K accordingly.\n\n2) Genuinely plot or tabulate your measured wall-clock time against block size, and identify the empirical 'sweet spot' block size that minimizes measured latency on YOUR specific hardware -- report whether very small blocks (approaching the scattered-sparsity extreme from the intermediate exercise) genuinely perform worse, and whether very large blocks (approaching full dense attention, since a large enough block covers most of the sequence) also perform worse, confirming or disconfirming a genuine U-shaped or otherwise non-trivial relationship between block size and measured speed.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, grounded in your own concrete measurements, why the 'optimal' block size for sparse attention is fundamentally a HARDWARE-DEPENDENT empirical question rather than a fixed universal constant -- and what this implies about why real production teams deploying sparse-attention models must re-benchmark and potentially re-tune these hyperparameters when moving to different GPU generations or hardware configurations, rather than reusing a fixed configuration from a published paper unconditionally.",
      problemKn: "ಸಾಹಿತ್ಯದ ಯಾವುದೇ block size ಆಯ್ಕೆ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ವರ್ಗಾವಣೆಗೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಊಹಿಸುವ ಬದಲು, ನಿಮ್ಮ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಲಭ್ಯ hardware ಗೆ ನಿಜ ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ-ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸುವ block size ಅನ್ನೂ ಕಂಡುಹಿಡಿಯುವ ಒಂದು hardware-grounded tuning ಅಧ್ಯಯನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಡೆಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ block sizes ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಜ wall-clock inference time ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ, ಒಟ್ಟು sparsity ratio ಅನ್ನೂ ಸ್ಥಿರವಾಗಿಡಿ.\n\n2) block size ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ wall-clock ಸಮಯವನ್ನೂ ಪ್ಲಾಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ 'sweet spot' ಗುರುತಿಸಿ.\n\n3) 'optimal' block size ಏಕೆ ಮೂಲಭೂತವಾಗಿ ಒಂದು HARDWARE-DEPENDENT ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಪ್ರಶ್ನೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಅಳತೆಗಳ ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m202, l202_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Multi-Token Prediction: Training the Model to Predict Several Tokens Ahead at Once",
      titleKn: "Multi-Token Prediction: ಒಂದೇ ಬಾರಿಗೆ ಹಲವಾರು Tokens ಮುಂದೆ ಊಹಿಸಲು Model ಟ್ರೈನ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Standard language model pretraining trains the model to predict only the SINGLE immediate next token at each position. Multi-Token Prediction (MTP, used in models like DeepSeek-V3) adds additional prediction heads that, from the SAME shared hidden representation at a given position, also predict tokens 2, 3, or more steps ahead -- each additional head contributing its own auxiliary training loss during pretraining, even though only the standard single-next-token head is typically used for actual autoregressive generation at inference time.\n\n1) Explain why forcing the model's shared hidden representation to be useful for predicting MULTIPLE future tokens (not just the immediate next one) could produce a genuinely richer, more informative internal representation than training on next-token prediction alone -- what kind of information must the representation capture to succeed at predicting 3 tokens ahead that it might not strictly need to capture to succeed at predicting just 1 token ahead?\n\n2) Explain why MTP's EXTRA prediction heads are typically discarded (or repurposed, e.g. for speculative-decoding-style drafting) at actual inference/deployment time, rather than being used to directly generate multiple tokens per forward pass during normal generation -- what would go wrong if you naively tried to use the 2-steps-ahead head's prediction as your ACTUAL second generated token, skipping the normal one-token-at-a-time autoregressive process?",
      problemKn: "Standard language model pretraining model ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ position ನಲ್ಲಿ ಕೇವಲ ಒಂಟಿ ತಕ್ಷಣದ next token ಊಹಿಸಲು train ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ. Multi-Token Prediction (MTP, DeepSeek-V3 ನಂತಹ models ನಲ್ಲಿ ಬಳಸಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ) ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ prediction heads ಸೇರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಇವು ಅದೇ ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ hidden representation ಇಂದ 2, 3, ಅಥವಾ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಹಂತಗಳ ಮುಂದೆ tokens ಅನ್ನೂ ಊಹಿಸುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) model ya ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ hidden representation ಅನ್ನೂ ಅನೇಕ ಭವಿಷ್ಯದ tokens ಊಹಿಸಲು ಉಪಯುಕ್ತವಾಗಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಶ್ರೀಮಂತ ಆಂತರಿಕ representation ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) MTP ya ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ prediction heads ಏಕೆ ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ ನಿಜ inference ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Multi-Token Prediction Heads and Their Combined Training Loss',
      titleKn: 'Multi-Token Prediction Heads ಮತ್ತು ಅವುಗಳ Combined Training Loss ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement multi-token prediction on your mini-GPT and genuinely measure its effect on the shared representation.\n\n1) Genuinely add N (e.g. 3-4) additional prediction heads to your mini-GPT, each taking the SAME final hidden representation at a given position as input, where head k (for k=1 being the standard next-token head) is trained to predict the token k positions ahead -- genuinely implement the combined loss as a weighted sum of all N heads' individual cross-entropy losses against their respective future-token targets.\n\n2) Genuinely train your MTP-augmented mini-GPT on this combined multi-head loss and confirm all N heads' individual losses decrease over training (verify this by logging each head's loss separately, not just the combined total).\n\n3) Genuinely measure whether adding these auxiliary MTP heads measurably changed your model's performance on the STANDARD single-next-token generation task (the one actually used at inference) -- compare perplexity/accuracy (using your evaluation harness) between your MTP-trained model (using only its head-1/standard next-token output at inference) and an otherwise-identical model trained WITHOUT the auxiliary MTP heads, reporting whether the auxiliary training signal genuinely helped, hurt, or had no clear measurable effect on standard next-token quality in your specific small-scale setup.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ multi-token prediction ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ representation ಮೇಲೆ ಅದರ ಪರಿಣಾಮವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) N ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ prediction heads ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸೇರಿಸಿ, ಪ್ರತಿ head k ಅನ್ನೂ k positions ಮುಂದೆ token ಊಹಿಸಲು train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ MTP-augmented mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ combined loss ಮೇಲೆ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಎಲ್ಲಾ N heads ya losses ಕಡಿಮೆಯಾಗುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ MTP heads standard next-token task ಮೇಲೆ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಬಹುದಾದ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಬೀರಿದವೇ ಎಂದು ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Repurposing MTP Heads for Speculative-Decoding-Style Draft Generation',
      titleKn: 'Speculative-Decoding-Style Draft Generation ಗಾಗಿ MTP Heads ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮರುಬಳಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate the connection between MTP and speculative decoding hinted at in the beginner exercise: real MTP-trained models can repurpose their extra prediction heads as a built-in, essentially-free draft mechanism for speculative decoding, since the heads already predict several tokens ahead from a single forward pass.\n\n1) Genuinely adapt your MTP-trained mini-GPT from the intermediate exercise into a speculative-decoding draft-and-verify loop (reusing your speculative decoding implementation from the earlier inference-optimization lesson): use the MTP heads' predictions (for positions 2, 3, ... ahead) as your DRAFT tokens directly from a single forward pass (rather than needing a separate draft model or separate forward passes to generate them), then verify these draft tokens using a standard full forward pass of the SAME model (checking whether the model's own standard next-token predictions, computed fresh at each position, agree with what the MTP heads guessed).\n\n2) Genuinely measure the empirical acceptance rate of these MTP-head-based draft tokens (what fraction genuinely match what the model's standard generation would have produced), and genuinely measure the resulting number of full forward passes needed to generate a fixed-length sequence using this MTP-based speculation, compared to plain one-token-at-a-time generation with the same model.\n\n3) Genuinely compare this MTP-based speculative approach against your EARLIER separate-draft-model speculative decoding approach (from the inference-optimization module) on the same test prompts -- report which approach achieved better measured speedup in your specific experiments, and discuss the genuine engineering advantage MTP-based drafting has (no separate draft model to train, host, or keep in sync with the target model) even if its raw acceptance rate doesn't exceed a well-tuned separate draft model's.",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ಸೂಚಿಸಿದ MTP ಮತ್ತು speculative decoding ನಡುವಿನ ಸಂಪರ್ಕವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n1) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ MTP-trained mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು speculative-decoding draft-and-verify loop ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳವಡಿಸಿ, MTP heads ya ಊಹೆಗಳನ್ನೂ draft tokens ಆಗಿ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಬಳಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ MTP-head-based draft tokens ya ನಿಜ acceptance rate ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಈ MTP-based ವಿಧಾನವನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ-draft-model ವಿಧಾನದ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m202, l202_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Sequential vs Parallel MTP Heads: Do Future-Token Predictions Depend on Each Other?",
      titleKn: "Sequential vs Parallel MTP Heads: Future-Token Predictions ಪರಸ್ಪರ ಅವಲಂಬಿಸುತ್ತವೆಯೇ?",
      problem: "There are two genuinely different ways to architect multiple future-token prediction heads: PARALLEL heads, where every head predicts its respective future position independently and simultaneously from the SAME shared hidden state (as you implemented in the previous lesson), versus SEQUENTIAL heads, where each head's prediction is conditioned on the PREVIOUS head's predicted token (e.g. head 2's prediction incorporates head 1's predicted token as additional input), more closely mimicking the true sequential dependency of real autoregressive generation.\n\n1) Explain why sequential heads might produce genuinely more ACCURATE far-future predictions than parallel heads -- given that in true autoregressive generation, what token comes 3 steps ahead genuinely does depend on what token comes 2 steps ahead (which itself depends on what comes 1 step ahead), what information does a parallel head lack access to that a sequential head has?\n\n2) Explain the genuine practical cost sequential heads introduce compared to parallel heads -- specifically, why can parallel heads all be computed in a single batched operation from one shared hidden state, while sequential heads require an inherently step-by-step computation, and what does this imply about which approach is more amenable to fast, parallelized training on a GPU.",
      problemKn: "ಅನೇಕ future-token prediction heads ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಲು ಎರಡು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ಮಾರ್ಗಗಳಿವೆ: PARALLEL heads, ಪ್ರತಿ head ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ ಮತ್ತು ಏಕಕಾಲದಲ್ಲಿ ಅದೇ ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ hidden state ಇಂದ ಊಹಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಮತ್ತು SEQUENTIAL heads, ಪ್ರತಿ head ya ಊಹೆ ಹಿಂದಿನ head ya ಊಹಿಸಿದ token ಮೇಲೆ ಅವಲಂಬಿತವಾಗಿದೆ.\n\n1) sequential heads ಏಕೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ನಿಖರ far-future ಊಹೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) sequential heads ಪ್ಯಾರಲಲ್ heads ಗೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿದರೆ ಯಾವ ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ವೆಚ್ಚ ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Comparing Sequential MTP Heads Against Your Parallel Version',
      titleKn: 'Sequential MTP Heads ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ Parallel ಆವೃತ್ತಿ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement the sequential MTP variant and genuinely compare its predictive accuracy against your parallel implementation from the previous lesson.\n\n1) Genuinely modify your MTP heads so that head k's prediction is computed from the shared hidden state COMBINED with an embedding of head (k-1)'s predicted (or, during training, ground-truth teacher-forced) token, creating a genuine sequential dependency chain across your N heads.\n\n2) Genuinely train this sequential-MTP mini-GPT using the same combined multi-head loss setup as your parallel version, and genuinely measure each head's individual prediction accuracy (e.g. top-1 accuracy on its respective future position) -- report whether the FAR-future heads (e.g. head 3 or 4) show genuinely HIGHER accuracy under the sequential design compared to the parallel design from the previous lesson, as the beginner exercise predicted.\n\n3) Genuinely measure actual wall-clock TRAINING time per step for both your sequential and parallel MTP implementations (same hardware, same batch size), and report the measured training-speed cost of the sequential design's inherently step-by-step computation -- discuss, using your own concrete accuracy and speed measurements, whether the sequential design's accuracy improvement (if any) was worth its measured training-speed cost in your specific setup.",
      problemKn: "sequential MTP variant ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ parallel implementation ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಅದರ predictive accuracy ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n1) head k ya ಊಹೆಯನ್ನೂ ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ hidden state ಮತ್ತು head (k-1) ya ಊಹಿಸಿದ token ya embedding ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಲು ನಿಮ್ಮ MTP heads ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ sequential-MTP mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಟ್ರೈನ್ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ head ya accuracy ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಸೀಕ್ವೆನ್ಷಿಯಲ್ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ಯಾರಲಲ್ ಎರಡಕ್ಕೂ ನಿಜ training ಸಮಯ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು tradeoff ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Which MTP Design Produces a More Useful Shared Representation',
      titleKn: 'ಯಾವ MTP ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಉಪಯುಕ್ತ Shared Representation ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment testing which MTP design (parallel from the earlier lesson, or sequential from this lesson) produces a genuinely more useful SHARED underlying representation, going beyond each head's own prediction accuracy to test the representation quality itself -- directly connecting to the beginner exercise's original claim in the first lesson of this module that MTP's real value lies in improving the shared representation, not just in the extra heads themselves.\n\n1) Genuinely take the shared hidden representations (BEFORE the final prediction heads) produced by your parallel-MTP model, your sequential-MTP model, and a baseline model trained WITHOUT any MTP (standard single-next-token training only), all trained on the same data for the same number of steps.\n\n2) Genuinely evaluate the DOWNSTREAM usefulness of each model's shared representation using a probe task unrelated to raw next-token prediction (e.g. reusing one of your custom eval suite's tasks from the evaluation lesson, or a simple linear-probe classification task trained on top of the frozen shared representations) -- report which model's representation performs best on this downstream probe.\n\n3) Genuinely report your complete three-way comparison (parallel-MTP, sequential-MTP, no-MTP) across standard next-token accuracy, downstream probe performance, and training cost, and give your own honest, evidence-based conclusion about whether MTP (in either design) is genuinely worth its added training complexity for your specific mini-GPT scale, being explicit about which specific measured benefit (if any) justifies the added complexity and which measured costs you'd want a real practitioner to weigh before adopting MTP in a real, much larger-scale training run.",
      problemKn: "ಯಾವ MTP ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ (parallel ಅಥವಾ sequential) ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಉಪಯುಕ್ತ ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ underlying representation ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) parallel-MTP, sequential-MTP, ಮತ್ತು no-MTP ಮಾದರಿಗಳ ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ hidden representations ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ model ya representation ya downstream ಉಪಯುಕ್ತತೆಯನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು probe task ಬಳಸಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಮೂರು-ಮಾರ್ಗದ ಹೋಲಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು MTP ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಯೋಗ್ಯವೇ ಎಂದು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕ ತೀರ್ಮಾನ ನೀಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m202, l202_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Scaling the MTP Loss Weight: Balancing Auxiliary Signal Against the Main Objective",
      titleKn: "MTP Loss Weight Scale ಮಾಡುವುದೂ: Auxiliary Signal ಅನ್ನೂ Main Objective ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಸಮತೋಲನಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Because MTP's auxiliary future-token losses are added to the model's main (standard next-token) training loss, the WEIGHT given to these auxiliary losses relative to the main loss is a genuinely important hyperparameter -- too small a weight and the auxiliary heads barely influence the shared representation (defeating MTP's purpose), too large a weight and the model may over-prioritize the harder, less-reliable far-future prediction task at the expense of its primary, most important objective (accurate immediate next-token prediction, which is what's actually used at inference).\n\n1) Explain why far-future token prediction (e.g. predicting 4 steps ahead) is INHERENTLY a harder, higher-uncertainty task than immediate next-token prediction -- given that many possible continuations could plausibly follow from the same context the further ahead you try to predict, why would this head's loss naturally tend to be numerically larger (even for a well-trained model) than the standard next-token head's loss?\n\n2) Explain why this inherent loss-scale difference between heads means you cannot simply combine all heads' losses with EQUAL weighting and expect a sensible result -- what would likely happen to the model's training dynamics if a numerically much-larger auxiliary loss were allowed to dominate gradient updates over the smaller main-objective loss?",
      problemKn: "MTP ya auxiliary future-token losses ಮಾಡೆಲ್ ya ಮುಖ್ಯ (standard next-token) training loss ಗೆ ಸೇರಿಸಲಾಗುವುದರಿಂದ, ಈ auxiliary losses ಗೆ ಕೊಟ್ಟ WEIGHT ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಮುಖ್ಯ hyperparameter ಆಗಿದೆ.\n\n1) far-future token prediction ಏಕೆ ಮೂಲಭೂತವಾಗಿ ತಕ್ಷಣದ next-token prediction ಗಿಂತ ಕಷ್ಟದ, ಹೆಚ್ಚು-ಅನಿಶ್ಚಿತ task ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) heads ನಡುವಿನ ಈ ಸಹಜ loss-scale ವ್ಯತ್ಯಾಸ ಏಕೆ ಎಲ್ಲಾ heads ya losses ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಮಾನ weighting ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಲಾಗುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ಅರ್ಥ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Sweeping the MTP Auxiliary Loss Weight and Measuring Its Effect',
      titleKn: 'MTP Auxiliary Loss Weight ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Sweep ಮಾಡಿ ಅದರ ಪರಿಣಾಮ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely run your MTP implementation (either parallel or sequential design from the earlier lessons) at several different auxiliary-loss weight values, to genuinely characterize the tradeoff raised in the beginner exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely train separate MTP-augmented mini-GPT models at three auxiliary-loss weight settings: a very small weight, a moderate weight, and a very large weight (relative to the main next-token loss's implicit weight of 1.0), keeping every other hyperparameter identical across runs.\n\n2) Genuinely measure, for each weight setting, BOTH the standard next-token evaluation performance (perplexity/accuracy on your evaluation harness, using only the standard head at inference) AND the auxiliary heads' own prediction accuracy at their respective future positions.\n\n3) Genuinely report the actual measured relationship you observe between auxiliary-loss weight and (a) standard next-token quality and (b) auxiliary-head accuracy -- confirm whether an overly large auxiliary weight genuinely degrades standard next-token quality (as the beginner exercise predicted), identify the approximate weight value that gave you the best standard next-token quality in your specific experiments, and discuss whether this 'best' weight also happened to give reasonably good auxiliary-head accuracy or whether there was a genuine tradeoff you had to navigate.",
      problemKn: "ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ auxiliary-loss weight ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ MTP implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಮೂರು weight settings ನಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ MTP-augmented models ಅನ್ನೂ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ setting ಗೆ standard next-token ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ಮತ್ತು auxiliary-head accuracy ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) weight ಮತ್ತು ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ನಡುವಿನ ನಿಜ ಸಂಬಂಧವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ weight ಮೌಲ್ಯವನ್ನೂ ಗುರುತಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Loss-Weight Annealing Schedule and Measuring Its Benefit',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Loss-Weight Annealing Schedule ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design, implement, and measure a more sophisticated alternative to a single FIXED auxiliary-loss weight: an ANNEALING schedule that changes the MTP auxiliary loss weight over the course of training (e.g. starting with a relatively HIGH auxiliary weight early in training, when the shared representation is still unrefined and could benefit most from the additional multi-token training signal, then GRADUALLY reducing the auxiliary weight as training progresses, letting the model increasingly prioritize the main next-token objective as it approaches convergence).\n\n1) Genuinely implement this annealing schedule (a simple linear or exponential decay of the auxiliary weight from a chosen starting value down to a chosen ending value over the course of training) and genuinely train an MTP-augmented mini-GPT with it.\n\n2) Genuinely compare this ANNEALED-weight model against your best FIXED-weight model from the intermediate exercise (the weight value that gave the best standard next-token quality), training both for the same total number of steps, and measure standard next-token evaluation performance for both.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether the annealed schedule genuinely outperformed the best fixed weight in your specific experiment, and discuss, using your own concrete measured numbers, whether the added implementation complexity of an annealing schedule (versus simply sweeping and picking one fixed weight, as in the intermediate exercise) was genuinely justified by the measured improvement -- being honest if your small-scale experiment showed the annealed schedule provided little to no measurable benefit over a well-tuned fixed weight.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದೇ FIXED auxiliary-loss weight ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಅತ್ಯಾಧುನಿಕ ಪರ್ಯಾಯವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ: training ಅವಧಿಯಲ್ಲಿ MTP auxiliary loss weight ಬದಲಾಯಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ANNEALING schedule.\n\n1) ಈ annealing schedule ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು MTP-augmented mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದರೊಂದಿಗೆ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಈ annealed-weight model ಅನ್ನೂ intermediate exercise ya ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ fixed-weight model ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) annealed schedule ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಉತ್ತಮವಾಗಿ ಕಾರ್ಯನಿರ್ವಹಿಸಿತೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ ಸಂಕೀರ್ಣತೆ ಸಮರ್ಥನೀಯವಾಗಿತ್ತೇ ಎಂದು ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕವಾಗಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m203, l203_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Pipeline Parallelism and Its Bubble Problem: Idle GPUs Waiting Their Turn",
      titleKn: "Pipeline Parallelism ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ Bubble ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ: ತಮ್ಮ ಸರದಿಗಾಗಿ ಕಾಯುತ್ತಿರುವ Idle GPUs",
      problem: "Pipeline parallelism splits a model's LAYERS across different devices (device 1 holds layers 1-10, device 2 holds layers 11-20, etc.), so a full forward pass requires data to flow sequentially through each device in order -- but this creates a genuine efficiency problem called the 'PIPELINE BUBBLE': while device 2 is waiting to receive device 1's output for the FIRST micro-batch, device 2 sits completely idle, and similarly device 1 sits idle waiting for the backward-pass gradient to flow back from later devices, wasting compute capacity especially at the very start and end of processing each batch.\n\n1) Explain concretely why naively splitting layers across devices and processing ONE micro-batch at a time through the whole pipeline would leave most devices idle most of the time -- trace through what device 3 (in a 4-device pipeline) is doing while device 1 processes the very first micro-batch's forward pass, and while device 1 processes the very LAST micro-batch's backward pass.\n\n2) Explain the standard mitigation of splitting a batch into many small MICRO-batches and pipelining them through the devices (so device 1 starts processing micro-batch 2 while device 2 is still working on micro-batch 1's output) -- why does using MORE, smaller micro-batches reduce the FRACTION of total time wasted in the bubble, even though it doesn't eliminate the bubble at the very start and end entirely?",
      problemKn: "Pipeline parallelism ಒಂದು model ya LAYERS ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ devices ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ವಿಭಜಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದ್ದರಿಂದ ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ forward pass ಗೆ data ಕ್ರಮದಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರತಿ device ಮೂಲಕ ಅನುಕ್ರಮವಾಗಿ ಹರಿಯಬೇಕು. ಆದರೆ ಇದು 'PIPELINE BUBBLE' ಎಂಬ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ದಕ್ಷತೆ ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಸೃಷ್ಟಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) layers ಅನ್ನೂ ಸರಳವಾಗಿ ವಿಭಜಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿನ devices ಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿನ ಸಮಯ idle ಆಗಿ ಬಿಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು batch ಅನ್ನೂ ಅನೇಕ ಚಿಕ್ಕ micro-batches ಆಗಿ ವಿಭಜಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ bubble ನಲ್ಲಿ ವ್ಯರ್ಥವಾದ ಸಮಯದ ಅಂಶವನ್ನೂ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Measuring the Pipeline Bubble on a Simulated Multi-Stage Pipeline',
      titleKn: 'Simulated Multi-Stage Pipeline ಮೇಲೆ Pipeline Bubble ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simulated pipeline-parallel training loop for your mini-GPT (using CPU-based multi-stage simulation if you don't have multiple GPUs, splitting your model's layers into 3-4 sequential 'stages' assigned to simulated separate devices) and genuinely measure its bubble overhead.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the basic pipeline mechanic: split your mini-GPT's layers into N sequential stages, process a batch by splitting it into M micro-batches, and genuinely implement the scheduling logic where each stage processes micro-batches in sequence, passing its output to the next stage, with explicit bookkeeping for which stage is doing what at each simulated timestep.\n\n2) Genuinely measure, for a FIXED number of micro-batches M, the fraction of total simulated device-time that is spent idle (in the bubble) versus actively computing, and genuinely verify this by running with DIFFERENT numbers of micro-batches M (small M, e.g. equal to N stages, versus large M, e.g. 8-10x the number of stages) and reporting how the measured idle fraction changes.\n\n3) Genuinely confirm whether your measured results match the theoretical prediction that idle fraction roughly scales as (N-1)/(N-1+M) for N stages and M micro-batches -- report your own calculated theoretical values alongside your measured simulation results and discuss any discrepancy.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಗೆ ಒಂದು simulated pipeline-parallel training loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ bubble overhead ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ಮೂಲಭೂತ pipeline ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya layers ಅನ್ನೂ N ಅನುಕ್ರಮ stages ಆಗಿ ವಿಭಜಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಸ್ಥಿರ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯ micro-batches M ಗೆ, idle ಆಗಿ ಕಳೆದ ಸಮಯದ ಅಂಶವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ M ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳು ಸೈದ್ಧಾಂತಿಕ ಭವಿಷ್ಯವಾಣಿಗೆ ಹೊಂದಿಕೆಯಾಗುತ್ತವೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Implementing 1F1B Scheduling and Measuring Its Bubble Reduction',
      titleKn: '1F1B Scheduling ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಅದರ Bubble ಕಡಿತ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement the standard '1F1B' (one-forward-one-backward) pipeline scheduling improvement over the naive all-forwards-then-all-backwards schedule from the intermediate exercise, and genuinely measure its concrete benefit.\n\n1) Genuinely implement 1F1B scheduling: instead of having each stage complete ALL its forward passes for every micro-batch before starting ANY backward passes (which requires holding activations for every in-flight micro-batch in memory simultaneously), interleave forward and backward passes so each stage alternates between forward-processing a new micro-batch and backward-processing an already-forward-processed one, reducing the PEAK number of activations that must be held in memory at once.\n\n2) Genuinely measure, for the same (N stages, M micro-batches) configuration you tested in the intermediate exercise, the peak memory usage (approximated by tracking the maximum number of simultaneously-held activation sets) under your naive all-forward-then-all-backward schedule versus your new 1F1B schedule, reporting the measured memory reduction.\n\n3) Genuinely measure whether 1F1B scheduling changes the total bubble-idle-time fraction compared to the naive schedule (it's designed primarily to reduce memory, not necessarily bubble time) -- report your measured comparison of both TIME-efficiency and MEMORY-efficiency between the two schedules, and discuss, using your own concrete numbers, why real large-scale pipeline-parallel training systems adopted 1F1B specifically for its memory benefit, motivating the further improvements (like DualPipe, covered in the next lesson) that additionally target the bubble-time problem.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ya naive all-forwards-then-all-backwards schedule ಮೀರಿ standard '1F1B' (one-forward-one-backward) pipeline scheduling ಸುಧಾರಣೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) 1F1B scheduling ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, forward ಮತ್ತು backward passes ಅನ್ನೂ interleave ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಅದೇ configuration ಗೆ peak memory usage ಅನ್ನೂ ಎರಡೂ schedules ಗೆ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) 1F1B scheduling bubble-idle-time ಅಂಶವನ್ನೂ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m203, l203_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "DualPipe's Key Idea: Feeding the Pipeline from Both Ends at Once",
      titleKn: "DualPipe ya ಪ್ರಮುಖ ಕಲ್ಪನೆ: Pipeline ಅನ್ನೂ ಎರಡೂ ತುದಿಗಳಿಂದ ಒಟ್ಟಿಗೆ ಫೀಡ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "DualPipe (used in DeepSeek-V3's training) further reduces the pipeline bubble beyond 1F1B by processing micro-batches from BOTH ends of the pipeline simultaneously -- one stream of micro-batches flows forward through the stages in the normal order (stage 1 to stage N), while a SECOND stream flows in the REVERSE direction (effectively treating the pipeline symmetrically from both directions), so that while one direction's micro-batch is in its early, bubble-prone stages, the other direction's micro-batch can be filling in the compute that would otherwise sit idle.\n\n1) Explain conceptually why processing micro-batches from both directions simultaneously could fill in MORE of the bubble than 1F1B's single-direction interleaving alone -- specifically, during the very first few timesteps of a single-direction pipeline (where only the first stage has any work while later stages sit idle waiting for data to arrive), what work could a REVERSE-direction micro-batch stream provide to occupy those otherwise-idle later stages?\n\n2) Explain the added complexity DualPipe's bidirectional scheme introduces that a single-direction pipeline does not have -- specifically, why must the SAME physical devices now handle both the 'stage k' role for the forward-direction stream AND effectively a different role for the reverse-direction stream, and what does this imply about the scheduling logic needing to track twice as many distinct micro-batch streams through the same set of devices.",
      problemKn: "DualPipe (DeepSeek-V3 ya training ನಲ್ಲಿ ಬಳಸಲಾಗಿದೆ) 1F1B ಮೀರಿ pipeline bubble ಅನ್ನೂ ಮತ್ತಷ್ಟು ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ, micro-batches ಅನ್ನೂ pipeline ya ಎರಡೂ ತುದಿಗಳಿಂದ ಏಕಕಾಲದಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರಕ್ರಿಯೆಗೊಳಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಕ.\n\n1) ಎರಡೂ ದಿಕ್ಕುಗಳಿಂದ ಏಕಕಾಲದಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರಕ್ರಿಯೆಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ 1F1B ya single-direction interleaving ಗಿಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು bubble ತುಂಬಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) DualPipe ya bidirectional ಯೋಜನೆ ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುವ ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ ಸಂಕೀರ್ಣತೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Simplified Bidirectional DualPipe Schedule',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ Bidirectional DualPipe Schedule ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely extend your pipeline simulation from the previous lesson into a simplified bidirectional DualPipe-style schedule.\n\n1) Genuinely implement two independent micro-batch streams through your N-stage simulated pipeline: a FORWARD stream (processed stage 1 to stage N, as in your previous implementation) and a REVERSE stream (processed stage N to stage 1, treating the model's layers in reverse order for this stream), and genuinely implement the scheduling logic so each simulated device alternates between servicing its role in the forward stream and its role in the reverse stream at each timestep, based on which has work ready.\n\n2) Genuinely measure the total bubble-idle-time fraction of your bidirectional schedule (using the same N stages and same total number of micro-batches split between the two directions) and directly compare it against your single-direction 1F1B schedule's measured idle fraction from the previous lesson's advanced exercise.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether your bidirectional implementation achieved a genuinely lower measured idle fraction, and by how much, discussing any implementation challenges you encountered in correctly interleaving two simultaneous streams through the same set of simulated devices without them interfering with each other's scheduling.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ pipeline simulation ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ bidirectional DualPipe-ಶೈಲಿಯ schedule ಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ N-stage simulated pipeline ಮೂಲಕ ಎರಡು ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರ micro-batch streams ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ಒಂದು FORWARD stream ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು REVERSE stream.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ bidirectional schedule ya ಒಟ್ಟು bubble-idle-time ಅಂಶವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ single-direction 1F1B schedule ವಿರುದ್ಧ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ bidirectional implementation ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಕಡಿಮೆ idle ಅಂಶ ಸಾಧಿಸಿತೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring DualPipe\\u2019s Memory Cost Tradeoff Against Its Bubble Reduction',
      titleKn: 'DualPipe ya Bubble ಕಡಿತದ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಅದರ Memory ವೆಚ್ಚ Tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate a genuine cost of DualPipe's bidirectional scheme that the beginner exercise only hinted at: since each device must now hold activations and gradient state for BOTH the forward-stream and reverse-stream micro-batches passing through it, DualPipe likely requires more peak memory per device than a single-direction 1F1B schedule at the same micro-batch count.\n\n1) Genuinely measure the actual peak memory usage (approximated as in the previous lesson's advanced exercise, by tracking maximum simultaneously-held activation sets) of your bidirectional DualPipe implementation from the intermediate exercise, and directly compare it against your single-direction 1F1B implementation's peak memory from the previous lesson, using the same total micro-batch count for both.\n\n2) Genuinely report the measured memory INCREASE (if any) DualPipe's bidirectional scheme required to achieve its measured bubble-reduction benefit, presenting both numbers (bubble-time reduction percentage, and memory-increase percentage) side by side as a genuine tradeoff table.\n\n3) Genuinely discuss, using your own concrete measured tradeoff, why a real large-scale training system (like DeepSeek-V3's actual deployment) would need to weigh this specific memory-vs-speed tradeoff against their available per-device memory budget when deciding whether DualPipe's added complexity and memory cost is worth its bubble-reduction benefit for their specific model size and hardware configuration -- grounding your discussion in your own measured numbers rather than assuming DualPipe is unconditionally better just because it's a newer, more sophisticated technique.",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ಕೇವಲ ಸೂಚಿಸಿದ DualPipe ya bidirectional ಯೋಜನೆ ya ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ವೆಚ್ಚವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ: ಪ್ರತಿ device ಈಗ forward-stream ಮತ್ತು reverse-stream ಎರಡರ activations ಮತ್ತು gradient state ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಿಡಿದಿಡಬೇಕು.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ bidirectional DualPipe implementation ya ನಿಜ peak memory usage ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ single-direction 1F1B implementation ya peak memory ವಿರುದ್ಧ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n2) DualPipe ya ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ bubble-reduction ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ ಸಾಧಿಸಲು ಬೇಕಾದ memory ಹೆಚ್ಚಳವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ, ಎರಡೂ numbers ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ tradeoff ಕೋಷ್ಟಕವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ tradeoff ಬಳಸಿ, ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ದೊಡ್ಡ-ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ training system ಈ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ memory-vs-speed tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ಏಕೆ ತೂಗಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m203, l203_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Composing Pipeline Parallelism with Data and Tensor Parallelism: A Multi-Dimensional Strategy",
      titleKn: "Pipeline Parallelism ಅನ್ನೂ Data ಮತ್ತು Tensor Parallelism ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವುದೂ: ಒಂದು Multi-Dimensional ತಂತ್ರ",
      problem: "Real large-scale training of models too big for any single parallelism strategy alone typically COMBINES pipeline parallelism (splitting layers across devices, covered in this module) with data parallelism/FSDP (splitting batches and sharding parameters, covered in the distributed-training module earlier in this phase) and sometimes tensor parallelism (splitting individual weight matrices within a layer across devices) SIMULTANEOUSLY, arranging devices into a multi-dimensional grid where each dimension of the grid corresponds to a different parallelism strategy.\n\n1) Explain why a real frontier-scale model training run typically cannot rely on just ONE of these parallelism strategies alone -- specifically, connect back to what you learned about FSDP's communication-bandwidth limits at very high device counts, and pipeline parallelism's bubble overhead at very deep pipelines, to explain why combining strategies (e.g. using pipeline parallelism ACROSS a smaller number of large device groups, and FSDP/data-parallelism WITHIN each group) lets a training system scale to far more total devices than either strategy could efficiently support alone.\n\n2) Explain why the specific GROUPING of devices for each parallelism dimension matters for real hardware -- given that tensor-parallelism (splitting individual matrix multiplications) requires the MOST frequent, lowest-latency communication between its devices, why would real systems always place tensor-parallel device groups on the physically closest, highest-bandwidth-interconnected hardware (e.g. GPUs within the same physical server), reserving the SLOWER inter-server network connections for the pipeline-parallel and data-parallel dimensions which need to communicate less frequently.",
      problemKn: "ಯಾವುದೇ ಒಂಟಿ parallelism ತಂತ್ರಕ್ಕೆ ಬಹಳ ದೊಡ್ಡದಾದ models ya ನಿಜ ದೊಡ್ಡ-ಪ್ರಮಾಣದ training ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ pipeline parallelism ಅನ್ನೂ data parallelism/FSDP ಜೊತೆ ಮತ್ತು ಕೆಲವೊಮ್ಮೆ tensor parallelism ಜೊತೆ ಏಕಕಾಲದಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ನಿಜ frontier-scale model training run ಏಕೆ ಈ parallelism ತಂತ್ರಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಕೇವಲ ಒಂದನ್ನೂ ಮಾತ್ರ ಅವಲಂಬಿಸಲಾಗುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ parallelism ಆಯಾಮಕ್ಕೆ devices ya ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಗುಂಪೀಕರಣ ಏಕೆ ನಿಜ hardware ಗೆ ಮುಖ್ಯ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Composing Your Pipeline-Parallel Simulation with Your Earlier FSDP Simulation',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Pipeline-Parallel Simulation ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಹಿಂದಿನ FSDP Simulation ಜೊತೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely combine your pipeline-parallel simulation (from the earlier lessons in this module) with your FSDP/data-parallel simulation (from the distributed-training module earlier in this phase) into a single, two-dimensional parallelism strategy on your mini-GPT.\n\n1) Genuinely design a device grid: arrange your simulated devices into a 2D grid where one dimension represents PIPELINE stages (each column of the grid holds one pipeline stage's layers) and the other dimension represents DATA-PARALLEL/FSDP replicas (each row processes a different micro-batch/data shard, with FSDP-style parameter sharding WITHIN each row's set of pipeline stages).\n\n2) Genuinely implement the combined training step: each row independently runs its own pipeline-parallel forward/backward pass (using your 1F1B or DualPipe scheduling from the earlier lessons) on its own data shard, and after all rows complete their backward passes, genuinely implement a data-parallel gradient-averaging step ACROSS rows (reusing your all-reduce logic from the distributed-training module) to synchronize the model replica's parameters across the data-parallel dimension.\n\n3) Genuinely verify your combined 2D implementation trains correctly (loss decreases) and genuinely measure its total per-step wall-clock time and peak per-device memory, comparing these against running pipeline-parallelism alone (no data-parallel replication) at the same total device count -- report whether the combined strategy let you process a genuinely larger effective batch size (or fit a genuinely larger model) than pipeline-parallelism alone could, within the same total device budget.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ pipeline-parallel simulation ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ phase ya ಹಿಂದಿನ distributed-training module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ FSDP/data-parallel simulation ಜೊತೆ ಒಂದು ಏಕ, ಎರಡು-ಆಯಾಮದ parallelism ತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು 2D device grid ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ training step ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ 2D implementation ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ train ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ ವೆಚ್ಚ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರಯೋಜನವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Designing and Justifying a Full Device-Grid Layout for a Target Model Size',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಗುರಿ Model Size ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ Device-Grid Layout ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಸಮರ್ಥಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design a complete, justified multi-dimensional parallelism strategy for a hypothetical target model size significantly larger than your mini-GPT, using everything you've measured across this entire phase's distributed-training and pipeline-parallelism lessons as evidence for your design choices.\n\n1) Genuinely choose a specific hypothetical target model size (e.g. 10x or 100x your mini-GPT's actual parameter count) and a specific hypothetical available device count and per-device memory budget, then genuinely calculate, using your own measured per-device memory formulas from the FSDP lesson's exercises, how many devices' worth of FSDP sharding would be needed just to fit this model's parameters/gradients/optimizer-states within your assumed per-device memory budget.\n\n2) Genuinely calculate how many ADDITIONAL pipeline stages (beyond pure FSDP sharding) would be needed if the model's activation memory (not just parameters) also exceeds your per-device budget at your chosen micro-batch size, using reasoning grounded in your own pipeline-parallel memory measurements from this module.\n\n3) Genuinely present a complete, justified device-grid layout (specifying how many devices go to each parallelism dimension, and why) for your hypothetical target model and hardware budget, explicitly citing which of your OWN measured experiments and formulas (from across this entire phase) justify each specific number in your design -- and honestly discuss which parts of your design are grounded in genuine measurement versus which parts required additional assumptions or extrapolations beyond what you've actually tested at your mini-GPT's small scale.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಗಿಂತ ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ದೊಡ್ಡದಾದ ಒಂದು ಕಾಲ್ಪನಿಕ ಗುರಿ model size ಗಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ, ಸಮರ್ಥಿತ multi-dimensional parallelism ತಂತ್ರವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಈ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ phase ya distributed-training ಮತ್ತು pipeline-parallelism ಪಾಠಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನೀವು ಅಳೆದ ಎಲ್ಲವನ್ನೂ ಪುರಾವೆಯಾಗಿ ಬಳಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಕಾಲ್ಪನಿಕ ಗುರಿ model size ಮತ್ತು device count ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು FSDP sharding ಗೆ ಎಷ್ಟು devices ಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n2) activation memory ಸಹ ಮೀರಿದರೆ ಎಷ್ಟು ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ pipeline stages ಬೇಕಾಗಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ, ಸಮರ್ಥಿತ device-grid layout ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತಪಡಿಸಿ, ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ ಪ್ರಯೋಗಗಳನ್ನೂ ಉಲ್ಲೇಖಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಯಾವ ಭಾಗಗಳು ನಿಜ ಅಳತೆಯಲ್ಲಿ ಆಧಾರಿತವಾಗಿವೆ ಮತ್ತು ಯಾವುದು ಹೆಚ್ಚುವರಿ ಊಹೆಗಳ ಅಗತ್ಯವಿದೆ ಎಂದು ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕವಾಗಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m204, l204_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "DeepSeek-V3 as a Synthesis: Recognizing the Techniques You've Already Built",
      titleKn: "ಒಂದು ಸಂಶ್ಲೇಷಣೆಯಾಗಿ DeepSeek-V3: ನೀವು ಈಗಾಗಲೇ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ Techniques ಗುರುತಿಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "DeepSeek-V3 is a genuinely useful capstone case study because it combines, in a single real, publicly-documented production model, MANY of the specific techniques covered across this phase's individual lessons: Mixture-of-Experts layers (from the open-models module), Multi-Token Prediction (from its own dedicated module), DualPipe pipeline parallelism (from the previous module), FP8 mixed-precision training (connecting to the quantization module's numeric-precision concepts, applied during TRAINING rather than post-hoc), and Multi-head Latent Attention (a KV-cache-compression technique related to the grouped-query-attention concepts from the open-models module).\n\n1) Before looking up DeepSeek-V3's actual published architecture details, genuinely predict, based purely on what you've learned about EACH of these techniques' individual benefits across this phase, what KIND of overall system goal combining all of them would serve -- what common thread (e.g. maximizing total capability while minimizing training/inference compute cost) connects choosing MoE, MTP, DualPipe, and precision-reduction techniques together, rather than these being an arbitrary grab-bag of unrelated tricks?\n\n2) Explain why studying a real, complete production architecture (rather than only studying techniques in isolation, as this phase's earlier modules did) is valuable for genuinely understanding how these techniques' individual tradeoffs interact -- what could you learn about, e.g., how MoE's memory-vs-compute tradeoff interacts with DualPipe's pipeline-stage division, that studying MoE and DualPipe in separate isolated lessons wouldn't directly reveal?",
      problemKn: "DeepSeek-V3 ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಉಪಯುಕ್ತ capstone case study ಆಗಿದೆ ಏಕೆಂದರೆ ಇದು ಒಂದೇ ನಿಜ, ಸಾರ್ವಜನಿಕವಾಗಿ ದಾಖಲಿತ production model ನಲ್ಲಿ ಈ phase ya ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ ಪಾಠಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿರುವ ಅನೇಕ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ techniques ಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: MoE layers, Multi-Token Prediction, DualPipe pipeline parallelism, FP8 mixed-precision training, ಮತ್ತು Multi-head Latent Attention.\n\n1) DeepSeek-V3 ya ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಕಟಿತ architecture ವಿವರಗಳನ್ನೂ ನೋಡುವ ಮೊದಲು, ಈ ಪ್ರತಿ technique ya ವೈಯಕ್ತಿಕ ಪ್ರಯೋಜನಗಳ ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ, ಇವೆಲ್ಲವನ್ನೂ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವುದೂ ಯಾವ ರೀತಿಯ ಒಟ್ಟು system ಗುರಿಯನ್ನೂ ಪೂರೈಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಊಹಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು ನಿಜ, ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ production architecture ಅಧ್ಯಯನ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಮೌಲ್ಯಯುತ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Assembling a Miniature DeepSeek-V3-Style Model from Your Own Built Components',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ Components ಇಂದ ಒಂದು ಕಿರು DeepSeek-V3-Style Model ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜೋಡಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely assemble a single mini-GPT variant that combines several of the specific techniques you've already independently implemented across this phase's modules, mirroring DeepSeek-V3's actual design philosophy of combining multiple efficiency techniques together.\n\n1) Genuinely combine, in a single model, your MoE layer implementation (from the open-models module), your Multi-Token Prediction heads (from the MTP module), and your KV-cache implementation, replacing your mini-GPT's standard dense feed-forward layers with MoE layers and adding the MTP auxiliary heads on top -- genuinely verify this combined model trains correctly (loss decreases) and that both the MoE routing and the MTP heads function as expected together (e.g. checking the MoE load-balance metric and MTP head accuracy simultaneously).\n\n2) Genuinely measure the combined model's actual parameter count (total, including all unused MoE experts) versus its actual per-token active-compute cost (accounting for MoE's sparse activation), and separately measure its training step time compared to a dense (non-MoE, non-MTP) baseline mini-GPT of comparable ACTIVE parameter count.\n\n3) Genuinely report any concrete implementation interactions you discovered between combining these techniques (e.g. did the MTP auxiliary heads' loss computation need any adjustment to work correctly with the MoE-routed final layer's output, or did any component's assumptions about the model's structure need updating to accommodate the other) -- discuss what this concrete integration experience reveals about why combining many techniques into one production model (as DeepSeek-V3 does) is significantly more engineering work than each technique in isolation.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ya modules ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನೀವು ಈಗಾಗಲೇ ಸ್ವತಂತ್ರವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿದ ಹಲವಾರು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ techniques ಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವ ಒಂದೇ mini-GPT variant ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜೋಡಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ MoE layer implementation ಮತ್ತು Multi-Token Prediction heads ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದೇ model ನಲ್ಲಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ, ಈ ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ model ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ train ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ model ya ಒಟ್ಟು parameter count ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಅದರ ನಿಜ ಪ್ರತಿ-token active-compute ವೆಚ್ಚವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಈ techniques ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವಾಗ ಕಂಡುಕೊಂಡ ಯಾವುದೇ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ implementation ಪರಸ್ಪರ ಕ್ರಿಯೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Comparing Your Assembled Model Against Published DeepSeek-V3 Design Ratios',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ ಜೋಡಿಸಿದ Model ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಕಟಿತ DeepSeek-V3 Design Ratios ವಿರುದ್ಧ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹೋಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely obtain (via DeepSeek-V3's publicly available technical report) the actual published architecture ratios and design choices DeepSeek-V3 uses (e.g. its ratio of total-to-active parameters under MoE, its number of MTP prediction heads, its choice of FP8 for specific parts of training versus higher precision for others), and genuinely compare these real published choices against the design decisions you made in your own combined model from the intermediate exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely tabulate DeepSeek-V3's actual published values for at least 4 specific design choices relevant to techniques you've implemented in this phase (e.g. number of experts and experts-per-token in its MoE layers, number of MTP heads, its specific attention mechanism's KV-cache-reduction ratio) alongside your own model's corresponding choices.\n\n2) Genuinely reason, using your own concrete experimental measurements from across this phase (e.g. your own measured MoE load-balancing behavior, your own measured MTP accuracy-vs-head-count tradeoff), about WHY DeepSeek-V3's actual published ratios might have been chosen at their specific values rather than other plausible values -- connect at least 2 of DeepSeek-V3's specific published choices to specific tradeoffs you personally measured in your own smaller-scale experiments earlier in this phase.\n\n3) Genuinely reflect on and report which of your own design decisions in the intermediate exercise you would now REVISE, having seen DeepSeek-V3's actual published choices and reasoning through why they might have chosen differently than you did -- and honestly discuss which aspects of DeepSeek-V3's design remain genuinely unclear to you even after this comparison, identifying at least one open question you'd need further research (beyond this course) to resolve.",
      problemKn: "DeepSeek-V3 ya ಸಾರ್ವಜನಿಕವಾಗಿ ಲಭ್ಯ technical report ಮೂಲಕ ಅದರ ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಕಟಿತ architecture ratios ಮತ್ತು design choices ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪಡೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳಿ ಮತ್ತು intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ model ya ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ನಿರ್ಧಾರಗಳ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 4 ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ design choices ಗೆ DeepSeek-V3 ya ನಿಜ ಪ್ರಕಟಿತ ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳನ್ನೂ ಪಟ್ಟಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಅಳತೆಗಳನ್ನೂ ಬಳಸಿ, DeepSeek-V3 ya ಆಯ್ಕೆಗಳು ಏಕೆ ಆ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಆಯ್ಕೆಯಾಗಿರಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ತಾರ್ಕಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ನಿರ್ಧಾರಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಯಾವುದನ್ನೂ ಈಗ ಪರಿಷ್ಕರಿಸುತ್ತೀರಿ ಎಂದು ಪ್ರತಿಬಿಂಬಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಯಾವ ಅಂಶಗಳು ಇನ್ನೂ ಅಸ್ಪಷ್ಟವಾಗಿವೆ ಎಂದು ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕವಾಗಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m204, l204_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Multi-Head Latent Attention: Compressing KV Cache into a Shared Low-Rank Space",
      titleKn: "Multi-Head Latent Attention: KV Cache ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು Shared Low-Rank Space ಆಗಿ Compress ಮಾಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Multi-head Latent Attention (MLA), a key DeepSeek-V3 innovation, addresses the same KV-cache memory problem that grouped-query attention (studied in the open-models module) addresses, but via a genuinely different mechanism: instead of simply sharing Key/Value projections across GROUPS of query heads, MLA compresses the Keys and Values into a much lower-dimensional shared LATENT vector per token (via a learned down-projection), caching ONLY this small latent vector rather than full-sized per-head Keys/Values, and then up-projecting back to full per-head dimensionality only when actually needed for the attention computation.\n\n1) Explain concretely why caching a low-dimensional latent vector (rather than full per-head Keys/Values, or even the grouped/shared Keys/Values of GQA) can achieve a GREATER KV-cache memory reduction than GQA alone -- what is fundamentally different about compressing INTO a smaller shared representation versus merely sharing existing full-sized representations across a group of heads?\n\n2) Explain why MLA needs to genuinely learn its down-projection (compression) and up-projection (decompression) matrices during training, rather than these being some fixed, hand-designed transformation -- what would likely happen to attention quality if you tried to compress and reconstruct Keys/Values using an arbitrary, untrained linear projection instead of ones the model has learned specifically to preserve the information most useful for attention.",
      problemKn: "Multi-head Latent Attention (MLA), DeepSeek-V3 ya ಒಂದು ಪ್ರಮುಖ ನಾವೀನ್ಯತೆ, grouped-query attention ಪರಿಹರಿಸುವ ಅದೇ KV-cache memory ಸಮಸ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಆದರೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆ ಮೂಲಕ: Keys ಮತ್ತು Values ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿ token ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಬಹಳ ಕಡಿಮೆ-ಆಯಾಮದ ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ LATENT vector ಆಗಿ compress ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ಕಡಿಮೆ-ಆಯಾಮದ latent vector ಅನ್ನೂ cache ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ GQA ಗಿಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು KV-cache memory ಕಡಿತ ಸಾಧಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) MLA ಗೆ ಅದರ down-projection ಮತ್ತು up-projection matrices ಅನ್ನೂ training ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಕಲಿಯಬೇಕಾಗಿರುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Multi-Head Latent Attention on Your Mini-GPT',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ Multi-Head Latent Attention ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified version of Multi-head Latent Attention as a modification to your mini-GPT's attention layer.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a down-projection layer that compresses each token's Key/Value-relevant hidden state into a low-dimensional latent vector (e.g. compressing to 1/4 or 1/8 of your original per-head Key/Value dimensionality), and implement matching up-projection layers that reconstruct full-sized, per-head Keys and Values from this shared latent vector when needed for attention computation -- genuinely make both projections LEARNED (trainable parameters), not fixed.\n\n2) Genuinely modify your KV-cache implementation (from the inference-optimization lesson) to cache ONLY the compressed latent vectors rather than full per-head Keys/Values, and genuinely verify your implementation produces correctly-shaped outputs and trains without errors (loss decreases over a small number of steps).\n\n3) Genuinely measure and report the actual KV-cache memory reduction your MLA implementation achieves (in stored values per token) compared to your original standard-attention mini-GPT's KV cache, and separately compare it against the KV-cache size your grouped-query-attention implementation (from the open-models module) achieved at a similar configuration -- report which technique achieved a greater measured memory reduction in your specific implementations.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya attention layer ಗೆ ಮಾರ್ಪಾಡಾಗಿ Multi-head Latent Attention ya ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ ಆವೃತ್ತಿಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ token ya Key/Value-ಸಂಬಂಧಿತ hidden state ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಕಡಿಮೆ-ಆಯಾಮದ latent vector ಆಗಿ compress ಮಾಡುವ down-projection layer ಮತ್ತು ಹೊಂದಾಣಿಕೆಯ up-projection layers ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ KV-cache implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಕೇವಲ compressed latent vectors ಅನ್ನೂ cache ಮಾಡಲು ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಸರಿಯಾಗಿ train ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಜ KV-cache memory ಕಡಿತವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು GQA ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Quality Cost of Latent Compression at Different Compression Ratios',
      titleKn: 'ವಿಭಿನ್ನ Compression Ratios ನಲ್ಲಿ Latent Compression ya ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ವೆಚ್ಚವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment characterizing the fundamental quality-vs-compression tradeoff that MLA's low-rank latent compression introduces, since compressing into a smaller latent space necessarily discards SOME information, even with a well-trained learned projection.\n\n1) Genuinely train several versions of your MLA-modified mini-GPT from the intermediate exercise at different LATENT DIMENSIONALITIES (e.g. a very aggressive compression ratio, a moderate one, and a mild one close to the original uncompressed size), keeping all other hyperparameters identical.\n\n2) Genuinely evaluate each compression-ratio version on your standard evaluation harness (perplexity/accuracy) and genuinely measure each version's actual KV-cache memory footprint, reporting both numbers for every tested ratio.\n\n3) Genuinely report your measured quality-vs-compression-ratio curve, confirming whether more aggressive compression genuinely costs measurable quality (and roughly how much), and identify, based on YOUR concrete measured tradeoff curve, what compression ratio you would recommend for a genuinely quality-sensitive application versus a genuinely memory-constrained application -- discuss whether your measured curve shows a clear 'knee' (a ratio beyond which quality degrades sharply) or a more gradual tradeoff, and what this implies about how much headroom exists for aggressive KV-cache compression before quality genuinely suffers in your specific small-scale setup.",
      problemKn: "MLA ya low-rank latent compression ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುವ ಮೂಲಭೂತ quality-vs-compression tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಮಾಣೀಕರಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ MLA-modified mini-GPT ya ಹಲವಾರು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ LATENT DIMENSIONALITIES ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ compression-ratio ಆವೃತ್ತಿಯನ್ನೂ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ KV-cache memory footprint ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ quality-vs-compression-ratio ಕರ್ವ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಶಿಫಾರಸು ನೀಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m204, l204_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "FP8 Training: Pushing Reduced Precision from Inference into Training Itself",
      titleKn: "FP8 Training: ಕಡಿಮೆ Precision ಅನ್ನೂ Inference ಇಂದ Training ಗೇ ತಳ್ಳುವುದೂ",
      problem: "The quantization module earlier in this phase covered reducing precision AFTER training (post-training quantization, for deployment). DeepSeek-V3 goes further by using FP8 (8-bit floating point) precision DURING training itself for much of the computation, not just at deployment -- a genuinely harder problem, since training requires accumulating many small gradient updates over potentially millions of steps, and FP8's very limited numeric range/precision makes it much easier for training to become numerically unstable (e.g. gradients underflowing to zero, or activations overflowing) than a one-time, static post-training quantization of already-converged weights.\n\n1) Explain why using low precision DURING training is a genuinely harder engineering problem than using it only for a final, already-trained model's weights at inference time -- what specific numeric hazard (relating to how gradients accumulate and how small updates compound over many steps) does training introduce that a static, already-converged set of inference weights does not face?\n\n2) Explain why DeepSeek-V3's approach doesn't use FP8 UNIFORMLY for absolutely everything, but instead keeps certain specific parts of training (e.g. the master copy of weights used for the actual optimizer update, or certain especially precision-sensitive computations) at higher precision (e.g. FP32 or BF16) -- what does this selective/mixed approach suggest about which specific operations in training are most vulnerable to FP8's reduced precision, versus which can tolerate it safely?",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ya ಹಿಂದಿನ quantization module ಪೂರ್ವತರಬೇತಿ ನಂತರ precision ಕಡಿಮೆ ಮಾಡುವುದನ್ನೂ ಒಳಗೊಂಡಿತ್ತು. DeepSeek-V3 ಇನ್ನೂ ಮುಂದೆ ಹೋಗಿ FP8 precision ಅನ್ನೂ training ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲೇ ಬಳಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಕೇವಲ deployment ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಅಲ್ಲ.\n\n1) training ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಕಡಿಮೆ precision ಬಳಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಕಷ್ಟದ engineering ಸಮಸ್ಯೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) DeepSeek-V3 ya ವಿಧಾನ ಏಕೆ FP8 ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಮವಾಗಿ ಬಳಸುವುದಿಲ್ಲ, ಬದಲಿಗೆ ಕೆಲವು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಭಾಗಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿನ precision ನಲ್ಲಿ ಇಡುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Mixed-Precision Training with Selectively Reduced Precision',
      titleKn: 'ಆಯ್ದ ಕಡಿಮೆಗೊಳಿಸಿದ Precision ಜೊತೆ Mixed-Precision Training ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified mixed-precision training scheme on your mini-GPT, applying reduced precision selectively rather than uniformly, mirroring DeepSeek-V3's selective approach.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a training loop where the FORWARD pass's matrix multiplications are computed using your INT8 (or a simulated 8-bit) quantization scheme from the quantization module's exercises (quantizing activations/weights on the fly during the forward computation), while the master copy of weights used for the actual optimizer UPDATE step, and the gradient accumulation itself, remain in full (FP32) precision -- genuinely implement this selective mixed-precision scheme rather than reducing precision everywhere uniformly.\n\n2) Genuinely train your mixed-precision mini-GPT and compare its training loss curve against a full-FP32-precision baseline over the same number of steps, reporting whether your mixed-precision scheme trains stably (no NaN losses, no divergence) and produces a genuinely comparable final loss.\n\n3) Genuinely construct a deliberately UNSAFE variant where you ALSO reduce the master weight copy and gradient accumulation to low precision (removing the 'selective' protection), and report whether this uniformly-low-precision variant genuinely becomes numerically unstable (e.g. producing NaN losses, or diverging) more readily than your selective mixed-precision scheme did -- report concrete evidence (specific step numbers where instability appeared, if it did) supporting or complicating the beginner exercise's claim about why selective precision matters.",
      problemKn: "DeepSeek-V3 ya ಆಯ್ದ ವಿಧಾನವನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರತಿಬಿಂಬಿಸುತ್ತಾ, ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ mixed-precision training ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) forward pass ya matrix multiplications ಅನ್ನೂ INT8 quantization ಬಳಸಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುವ, ಆದರೆ master weights ಮತ್ತು gradient accumulation ಅನ್ನೂ ಪೂರ್ಣ FP32 precision ನಲ್ಲಿ ಇಡುವ ಒಂದು training loop ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ mixed-precision mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಪೂರ್ಣ-FP32 baseline ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) master weights ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಹ ಕಡಿಮೆ precision ಗೆ ಇಳಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ ಅಸುರಕ್ಷಿತ variant ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದು ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಅಸ್ಥಿರವಾಗುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring FP8 Mixed-Precision Training\\u2019s Speed Benefit and Its Genuine Limits',
      titleKn: 'FP8 Mixed-Precision Training ya Speed ಪ್ರಯೋಜನ ಮತ್ತು ಅದರ ನಿಜ ಮಿತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely complete the cost-benefit picture for mixed-precision training by measuring its actual speed benefit, not just its stability characteristics from the intermediate exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely measure actual wall-clock time per training step for your selective mixed-precision implementation (from the intermediate exercise) versus your full-FP32 baseline, reporting the measured speedup factor your reduced-precision forward computation achieved.\n\n2) Genuinely design and run a stress test that pushes your mixed-precision scheme toward its genuine limits: train for a substantially LONGER number of steps than your intermediate exercise did (to check whether instability that doesn't appear early in training might still emerge later, as small numeric errors potentially compound over a longer run), and separately test your scheme at a DEEPER or WIDER model configuration than your original mini-GPT (if computationally feasible) to check whether stability holds as model scale increases.\n\n3) Genuinely report your complete findings: the measured speed benefit, whether stability held under your longer-training and larger-scale stress tests, and any genuine instability you newly discovered -- discuss, using your own concrete evidence, why real production teams training frontier-scale models with FP8 (like DeepSeek-V3) invest heavily in extensive stability testing and careful selective-precision engineering specifically BECAUSE the failure modes (numeric instability appearing only after many steps, or only at larger scale) can be genuinely difficult to catch in small, short-duration experiments like the ones you've run in this exercise.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ya ಸ್ಥಿರತೆ ಗುಣಲಕ್ಷಣಗಳನ್ನೂ ಮೀರಿ, mixed-precision training ya ನಿಜ speed ಪ್ರಯೋಜನವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆದು cost-benefit ಚಿತ್ರವನ್ನೂ ಪೂರ್ಣಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ selective mixed-precision implementation ಮತ್ತು ಪೂರ್ಣ-FP32 baseline ಗೆ ಪ್ರತಿ training step ya ನಿಜ wall-clock ಸಮಯವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ಅದರ ನಿಜ ಮಿತಿಗಳ ಕಡೆಗೆ ತಳ್ಳುವ ಒಂದು ಒತ್ತಡ-ಪರೀಕ್ಷೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ಹೆಚ್ಚು steps ಗಟ್ಟಲೆ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ production ತಂಡಗಳು ಏಕೆ ವ್ಯಾಪಕ ಸ್ಥಿರತೆ ಪರೀಕ್ಷೆಯಲ್ಲಿ ಹೂಡಿಕೆ ಮಾಡುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m205, l205_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "State Space Models: Constant-Memory Sequence Processing Without Attention",
      titleKn: "State Space Models: Attention ಇಲ್ಲದೆ Constant-Memory Sequence Processing",
      problem: "State Space Models (SSMs, e.g. Mamba, which Jamba builds on) offer a fundamentally different approach to sequence modeling than attention: rather than computing pairwise interactions between every pair of tokens (which is what makes attention's KV cache grow linearly with sequence length, as you measured in the inference-optimization module), an SSM maintains a single, FIXED-SIZE hidden 'state' vector that gets updated recurrently as each new token is processed -- summarizing all relevant information from the entire sequence so far into this one constant-size state, similar in spirit to an RNN but with specific mathematical structure (derived from continuous-time state-space equations) that makes it trainable efficiently in parallel.\n\n1) Explain concretely why an SSM's memory footprint for processing a sequence stays CONSTANT regardless of sequence length, in contrast to attention's KV cache which you measured growing linearly with sequence length in the inference-optimization module -- what is fundamentally different about maintaining one fixed-size summary state versus caching a growing set of per-token Key/Value vectors?\n\n2) Explain the genuine tradeoff this constant-memory property implies: since an SSM must compress ALL relevant historical information into one fixed-size state vector (rather than keeping every past token's information separately retrievable, as attention effectively does via its KV cache), what specific capability might an SSM struggle with that full attention handles more naturally -- connect this to the needle-in-a-haystack precise-retrieval tasks from the differential-attention module's exercises.",
      problemKn: "State Space Models (SSMs, ಉದಾ: Mamba, Jamba ಇದರ ಮೇಲೆ ನಿರ್ಮಿತವಾಗಿದೆ) sequence modeling ಗೆ attention ಗಿಂತ ಮೂಲಭೂತವಾಗಿ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ವಿಧಾನ ನೀಡುತ್ತವೆ: ಪ್ರತಿ token ಜೋಡಿ ನಡುವೆ pairwise ಪರಸ್ಪರ ಕ್ರಿಯೆ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುವ ಬದಲು, ಒಂದು SSM ಒಂದೇ, ಸ್ಥಿರ-ಗಾತ್ರದ hidden 'state' vector ಅನ್ನೂ ಇಡುತ್ತದೆ, ಇದು ಪ್ರತಿ ಹೊಸ token ಪ್ರಕ್ರಿಯೆಗೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತಿದ್ದಂತೆ ಪುನರಾವರ್ತಿತವಾಗಿ update ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) SSM ya memory footprint ಏಕೆ sequence length ಲೆಕ್ಕಿಸದೆ ಸ್ಥಿರವಾಗಿರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಸ್ಥಿರ-memory ಗುಣ ಸೂಚಿಸುವ ನಿಜ tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing a Basic Recurrent State Space Layer',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಮೂಲಭೂತ Recurrent State Space Layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a simplified state-space sequence layer as an alternative to attention, and integrate it as an optional layer type in your mini-GPT.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the core recurrence: a fixed-size state vector that, at each sequence position, is updated as a function of the PREVIOUS state and the CURRENT input token's embedding (using learned transition and input matrices, following the basic linear-recurrence structure of an SSM), and a learned output projection that reads the current state to produce that position's output representation -- genuinely implement both the sequential (step-by-step, RNN-style) computation of this recurrence AND verify you understand why it's mathematically equivalent to certain parallelizable formulations (you do not need to implement the parallel-scan training optimization itself, just the correct sequential recurrence).\n\n2) Genuinely substitute this state-space layer for the attention layer in one or more blocks of your mini-GPT, and genuinely train this SSM-modified model, confirming the loss decreases over a small number of steps.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and report the actual memory footprint (size of the recurrent state) your SSM layer maintains during generation at several different sequence lengths, confirming it genuinely stays constant (unlike your measured KV-cache growth from the inference-optimization module) -- report your concrete measured numbers as direct evidence of the constant-memory property described in the beginner exercise.",
      problemKn: "attention ಗೆ ಪರ್ಯಾಯವಾಗಿ ಒಂದು ಸರಳೀಕೃತ state-space sequence layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಐಚ್ಛಿಕ layer type ಆಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಮೂಲಭೂತ recurrence ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: ಪ್ರತಿ position ನಲ್ಲಿ ಹಿಂದಿನ state ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರಸ್ತುತ input token embedding ya function ಆಗಿ update ಆಗುವ ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ-ಗಾತ್ರದ state vector.\n\n2) ಈ state-space layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya ಒಂದು ಅಥವಾ ಹೆಚ್ಚು blocks ನಲ್ಲಿ attention layer ಬದಲಿಗೆ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ sequence lengths ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜ memory footprint ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದು ಸ್ಥಿರವಾಗಿರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the SSM\\u2019s Retrieval Weakness Predicted in the Beginner Exercise',
      titleKn: 'Beginner Exercise ಊಹಿಸಿದ SSM ya Retrieval Weakness ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment directly testing the beginner exercise's prediction that SSMs would struggle with precise long-range retrieval tasks compared to full attention.\n\n1) Genuinely train your SSM-modified mini-GPT from the intermediate exercise and a standard full-attention mini-GPT of comparable size on the SAME synthetic needle-in-a-haystack retrieval task you built in the differential-attention module's advanced exercise (or rebuild an equivalent task if needed), using the same training data and step count for both.\n\n2) Genuinely evaluate both models' exact-match retrieval accuracy on held-out test instances, testing at SEVERAL different needle positions/distances within the sequence, and report the measured accuracy comparison between the SSM and full-attention models at each tested distance.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether your concrete measured results confirm the beginner exercise's prediction (SSM accuracy degrading more than attention's, especially at longer distances) or complicate it -- discuss, using your own measured numbers, what this implies about why real hybrid architectures like Jamba (covered in the next lesson) combine SSM layers with SOME attention layers rather than using either purely, and connect your own finding back to the fundamental fixed-size-state constraint from the beginner exercise.",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ya ಭವಿಷ್ಯವಾಣಿಯನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ SSM-modified mini-GPT ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು standard full-attention mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ synthetic needle-in-a-haystack task ಮೇಲೆ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಎರಡೂ ಮಾದರಿಗಳ exact-match retrieval accuracy ಅನ್ನೂ ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ needle positions ನಲ್ಲಿ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳು beginner exercise ya ಭವಿಷ್ಯವಾಣಿಯನ್ನೂ ದೃಢಪಡಿಸುತ್ತವೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು hybrid architectures ya ಅಗತ್ಯದ ಬಗ್ಗೆ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m205, l205_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Jamba's Hybrid Recipe: Interleaving SSM and Attention Layers in One Model",
      titleKn: "Jamba ya Hybrid Recipe: SSM ಮತ್ತು Attention Layers ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದೇ Model ನಲ್ಲಿ Interleave ಮಾಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Jamba directly addresses the retrieval-weakness-vs-memory-efficiency tradeoff you measured in the previous lesson by INTERLEAVING SSM layers and standard attention layers within the same model, following a specific ratio (e.g. roughly 1 attention layer for every several SSM layers) -- getting most of a pure-SSM model's memory efficiency (since most layers are cheap, constant-memory SSM layers) while still providing the FULL model with genuine access to precise long-range retrieval via the smaller number of attention layers that remain.\n\n1) Explain conceptually why even a SMALL fraction of full-attention layers mixed into an otherwise-SSM model might be enough to substantially recover attention's precise-retrieval capability -- given that information can flow between layers (as you learned about receptive fields in the sliding-window-attention lesson), why might the model be able to route retrieval-critical information THROUGH the SSM layers to reach an attention layer, even if the SSM layers themselves cannot perform precise retrieval directly?\n\n2) Explain why the overall model's KV-cache memory footprint under this hybrid design would be much closer to a pure-SSM model's footprint than to a pure-attention model's -- given that only a SMALL FRACTION of layers are attention layers needing a growing KV cache, while the majority are constant-memory SSM layers, explain how this changes the OVERALL per-token memory-growth rate compared to a model where every layer is an attention layer.",
      problemKn: "Jamba ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದಲ್ಲಿ ನೀವು ಅಳೆದ retrieval-weakness-vs-memory-efficiency tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ SSM layers ಮತ್ತು standard attention layers ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದೇ model ನಲ್ಲಿ INTERLEAVE ಮಾಡುವ ಮೂಲಕ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಒಟ್ಟಾರೆ SSM model ಗೆ ಬೆರೆಸಿದ ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ ಭಾಗದ full-attention layers ಸಹ attention ya ನಿಖರ-retrieval ಸಾಮರ್ಥ್ಯವನ್ನೂ ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ಮರುಪಡೆಯಲು ಸಾಕಾಗಬಹುದು ಏಕೆ ಎಂದು ಪರಿಕಲ್ಪನಾತ್ಮಕವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ hybrid ವಿನ್ಯಾಸದ ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಒಟ್ಟು model ya KV-cache memory footprint ಏಕೆ pure-SSM model ya footprint ಗೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಹತ್ತಿರವಾಗಿರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Building a Jamba-Style Hybrid Mini-GPT and Measuring Its Retrieval Recovery',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Jamba-Style Hybrid Mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಅದರ Retrieval Recovery ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely build a hybrid mini-GPT that interleaves your SSM layer implementation and your standard attention layer implementation, and genuinely test whether it recovers the retrieval capability your pure-SSM model lacked.\n\n1) Genuinely construct a hybrid mini-GPT architecture that replaces MOST (but not all) of its blocks' attention layers with your SSM layer from the previous lesson, keeping a small fraction (e.g. 1 out of every 4-6 layers) as standard full-attention layers, following Jamba's interleaving philosophy.\n\n2) Genuinely train this hybrid model on the same needle-in-a-haystack retrieval task from the previous lesson's advanced exercise, using the same data and step count as your pure-SSM and pure-attention baselines from that exercise.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and report your hybrid model's retrieval accuracy across the same tested needle distances, comparing it directly against your PREVIOUSLY measured pure-SSM accuracy and pure-attention accuracy -- report whether the hybrid model's accuracy is genuinely closer to the pure-attention model's (confirming the beginner exercise's claim that a small fraction of attention layers can recover most of the retrieval capability) or closer to the pure-SSM model's, and separately report the hybrid model's KV-cache memory footprint compared to both pure baselines, confirming whether it's genuinely much closer to the pure-SSM model's memory footprint as the beginner exercise predicted.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ SSM layer ಮತ್ತು standard attention layer implementations ಅನ್ನೂ interleave ಮಾಡುವ ಒಂದು hybrid mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಇದು ನಿಮ್ಮ pure-SSM model ಕಳೆದುಕೊಂಡ retrieval ಸಾಮರ್ಥ್ಯವನ್ನೂ ಮರುಪಡೆಯುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) Jamba ya interleaving ತತ್ವಶಾಸ್ತ್ರವನ್ನೂ ಅನುಸರಿಸಿ, ಹೆಚ್ಚಿನ blocks ya attention layers ಅನ್ನೂ SSM layer ಜೊತೆ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸುವ ಒಂದು hybrid architecture ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ hybrid model ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದೇ needle-in-a-haystack task ಮೇಲೆ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) hybrid model ya retrieval accuracy ಮತ್ತು KV-cache memory footprint ಅನ್ನೂ ಎರಡೂ pure baselines ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Tuning the SSM-to-Attention Ratio to Find Your Own Measured Sweet Spot',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ Measured Sweet Spot ಕಂಡುಹಿಡಿಯಲು SSM-to-Attention Ratio ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Tune ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an ablation study over the specific SSM-to-attention layer ratio in your hybrid architecture, to genuinely characterize the tradeoff curve Jamba's designers had to navigate when choosing their specific ratio.\n\n1) Genuinely train several versions of your hybrid mini-GPT from the intermediate exercise at DIFFERENT SSM-to-attention ratios (e.g. mostly-SSM with very few attention layers, a moderate mix, and mostly-attention with only a few SSM layers), keeping total layer count constant across versions.\n\n2) Genuinely measure, for each ratio, both the retrieval-task accuracy (as in the intermediate exercise) AND the KV-cache memory footprint, reporting both numbers for every tested ratio.\n\n3) Genuinely report your measured accuracy-vs-memory tradeoff curve across the different ratios, and identify, based on YOUR concrete measurements, the ratio that seems to offer the best balance for a hypothetical application that needs BOTH reasonably strong retrieval accuracy AND significant memory savings -- discuss whether your measured curve shows diminishing returns (e.g. going from 1-in-6 to 1-in-4 attention layers helps accuracy much more than going from 1-in-4 to 1-in-2 does) and what this implies about why Jamba's actual published ratio might represent a deliberately chosen 'sweet spot' rather than an arbitrary choice, connecting your own small-scale findings to the real design tradeoff.",
      problemKn: "Jamba ya ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಕಾರರು ತಮ್ಮ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ratio ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಮಾಡುವಾಗ ನ್ಯಾವಿಗೇಟ್ ಮಾಡಬೇಕಾದ tradeoff curve ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಮಾಣೀಕರಿಸಲು ನಿಮ್ಮ hybrid architecture ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ SSM-to-attention layer ratio ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ablation ಅಧ್ಯಯನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ hybrid mini-GPT ya ಹಲವಾರು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ SSM-to-attention ratios ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ ratio ಗೆ retrieval accuracy ಮತ್ತು KV-cache memory footprint ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ accuracy-vs-memory tradeoff ಕರ್ವ್ ಅನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ ratio ಗುರುತಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m205, l205_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Adding MoE to the Hybrid: Jamba's Full Three-Way Architectural Combination",
      titleKn: "Hybrid ಗೆ MoE ಸೇರಿಸುವುದೂ: Jamba ya ಪೂರ್ಣ Three-Way Architectural ಸಂಯೋಜನೆ",
      problem: "Jamba's actual full architecture goes one step further than the SSM/attention interleaving from the previous lesson: it ALSO applies Mixture-of-Experts (from the open-models module) to many of its feed-forward layers, combining THREE distinct efficiency techniques (SSM layers for cheap constant-memory sequence mixing, sparse attention layers for precise retrieval, and MoE for scaling total parameter count without proportionally scaling active compute) into one cohesive design.\n\n1) Explain why combining MoE with the SSM/attention hybrid design is a genuinely complementary choice rather than a redundant one -- specifically, what DIFFERENT axis of efficiency does MoE target (total capacity vs. active compute, primarily in the feed-forward sublayers) compared to what the SSM/attention ratio targets (sequence-length memory scaling, primarily in the sequence-mixing sublayers), such that combining both lets the model be efficient along BOTH axes simultaneously rather than one technique substituting for the other?\n\n2) Explain a genuine engineering complexity this three-way combination introduces that any single technique alone would not have -- specifically, why might tuning training stability (e.g. the load-balancing concerns from the MoE lesson, and the numeric considerations from any reduced-precision training) become genuinely harder when MoE's routing dynamics, SSM's recurrent state dynamics, and attention's dynamics must all interact and be jointly stable during the same training run.",
      problemKn: "Jamba ya ನಿಜ ಪೂರ್ಣ architecture ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ SSM/attention interleaving ಮೀರಿ ಒಂದು ಹೆಜ್ಜೆ ಮುಂದೆ ಹೋಗುತ್ತದೆ: ಇದು ಅದರ ಅನೇಕ feed-forward layers ಗೆ ಸಹ Mixture-of-Experts ಅನ್ವಯಿಸುತ್ತದೆ, ಮೂರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ efficiency techniques ಗಳನ್ನೂ ಒಂದೇ ಸುಸಂಬದ್ಧ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸವಾಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) MoE ಅನ್ನೂ SSM/attention hybrid ವಿನ್ಯಾಸ ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪೂರಕ ಆಯ್ಕೆ, ಪುನರಾವರ್ತಿತ ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಅಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಮೂರು-ಮಾರ್ಗದ ಸಂಯೋಜನೆ ಪರಿಚಯಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ engineering ಸಂಕೀರ್ಣತೆಯನ್ನೂ ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Building the Full Three-Technique Hybrid and Verifying Joint Stability',
      titleKn: 'ಪೂರ್ಣ Three-Technique Hybrid ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ Joint Stability ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely extend your SSM/attention hybrid mini-GPT from the previous lesson to also incorporate MoE feed-forward layers, building the complete three-technique combination this lesson describes.\n\n1) Genuinely modify your hybrid architecture so that the feed-forward sublayers (in both your SSM-layer blocks and your attention-layer blocks) use your MoE implementation from the open-models module instead of a standard dense feed-forward network, while keeping your chosen SSM-to-attention layer ratio from the previous lesson's advanced exercise.\n\n2) Genuinely train this full three-technique hybrid model and monitor training stability closely: track the main training loss, the MoE load-balancing auxiliary loss, and (if using any reduced-precision techniques from earlier lessons) any numeric stability indicators, reporting whether all three genuinely remain stable together (no NaN losses, no divergence) over your training run.\n\n3) Genuinely measure this full hybrid model's total parameter count (including all unused MoE experts) versus its active per-token compute cost, and its KV-cache memory footprint, comparing all three measurements against your SSM/attention-only hybrid from the previous lesson (without MoE) -- report whether adding MoE genuinely let you increase total model capacity without proportionally increasing active compute or memory cost, as the beginner exercise predicted.",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ SSM/attention hybrid mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ MoE feed-forward layers ಸಹ ಸೇರಿಸಲು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ, ಈ ಪಾಠ ವಿವರಿಸುವ ಪೂರ್ಣ ಮೂರು-technique ಸಂಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ hybrid architecture ya feed-forward sublayers ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ MoE implementation ಬಳಸಲು ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಪೂರ್ಣ ಮೂರು-technique hybrid model ಅನ್ನೂ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು training ಸ್ಥಿರತೆಯನ್ನೂ ಹತ್ತಿರದಿಂದ ಮಾನಿಟರ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಪೂರ್ಣ hybrid model ya ಒಟ್ಟು parameter count, active compute ವೆಚ್ಚ, ಮತ್ತು KV-cache memory footprint ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ SSM/attention-only hybrid ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Ablating Each of the Three Techniques to Attribute Overall Model Quality',
      titleKn: 'ಒಟ್ಟು Model ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟವನ್ನೂ ಆರೋಪಿಸಲು ಮೂರು Techniques ಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರತಿಯೊಂದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Ablate ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely conduct the definitive attribution study for this module: which of the three combined techniques (SSM layers, sparse attention layers, MoE feed-forward layers) contributes MOST to your full hybrid model's overall quality and efficiency, and which contributes least, in your own concrete experiments.\n\n1) Genuinely train four model versions: your full three-technique hybrid from the intermediate exercise, and three ablated versions each reverting exactly ONE technique to its 'plain' baseline (all-attention instead of SSM/attention mix; dense feed-forward instead of MoE; keeping the other two techniques as in the full hybrid) -- ensure all four versions have comparable total training compute budgets for a fair comparison.\n\n2) Genuinely evaluate all four versions on your standard evaluation harness, your needle-in-a-haystack retrieval task, and measure each version's total parameter count, active compute, and KV-cache memory footprint.\n\n3) Genuinely report a complete attribution table across all four versions and all measured metrics, and give your own honest, evidence-based conclusion about which technique contributed most to which specific benefit (quality, retrieval accuracy, memory efficiency, or active-compute efficiency) in your own concrete small-scale experiments -- explicitly discussing which of your conclusions you'd expect to generalize to real frontier-scale models like the actual Jamba, and which might be artifacts specific to your mini-GPT's much smaller scale.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ಗೆ ಅಂತಿಮ attribution ಅಧ್ಯಯನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಡೆಸಿ: ಮೂರು ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ techniques ಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಯಾವುದೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪೂರ್ಣ hybrid model ya ಒಟ್ಟು ಗುಣಮಟ್ಟ ಮತ್ತು ದಕ್ಷತೆಗೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಕೊಡುಗೆ ನೀಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ನಾಲ್ಕು model ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ: ಪೂರ್ಣ hybrid ಮತ್ತು ಮೂರು ablated ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳು, ಪ್ರತಿಯೊಂದೂ ಒಂದು technique ಅನ್ನೂ ಅದರ 'plain' baseline ಗೆ ಹಿಂತಿರುಗಿಸಿದೆ.\n\n2) ಎಲ್ಲಾ ನಾಲ್ಕು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ standard evaluation, retrieval task, ಮತ್ತು ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಅಳತೆಗಳ ಮೇಲೆ ಮೌಲ್ಯಮಾಪನ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ attribution ಕೋಷ್ಟಕವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಯಾವ technique ಯಾವ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಪ್ರಯೋಜನಕ್ಕೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು ಕೊಡುಗೆ ನೀಡಿತು ಎಂದು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕ ತೀರ್ಮಾನ ನೀಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m206, l206_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Hogwild!: Concurrent Updates Without Locks, Betting on Sparse Gradient Collisions",
      titleKn: "Hogwild!: Locks ಇಲ್ಲದೆ Concurrent Updates, Sparse Gradient Collisions ಮೇಲೆ Bet ಮಾಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Standard multi-worker training (like the data-parallel gradient-averaging you implemented earlier in this phase) synchronizes workers at every step, waiting for ALL workers to finish computing before applying a combined update -- Hogwild! takes a genuinely different, asynchronous approach: multiple workers read and write to the SAME shared model parameters completely without locks or synchronization, each independently computing a gradient and applying its OWN update immediately, potentially overwriting or interfering with another worker's concurrent update, betting that such collisions are rare enough (particularly when gradients are naturally SPARSE, touching only a small subset of parameters at a time) that the aggregate training process still converges despite the lack of coordination.\n\n1) Explain precisely what a genuine 'collision' looks like in this setting -- if worker A reads parameter W, computes a gradient, and is about to apply its update, but worker B reads and updates that SAME parameter W in between, what specifically happens to worker A's eventual update, and why is this technically a race condition rather than a clean, well-defined operation?\n\n2) Explain why gradient SPARSITY (most gradients being exactly zero for most parameters at any given step, common when different training examples primarily activate different parts of a large model) makes this uncoordinated approach more likely to work in practice than it would for dense gradients -- if two workers' gradients rarely touch the SAME specific parameters at the same time, how does this reduce the practical impact of the race condition you identified.",
      problemKn: "Standard multi-worker training (ಈ phase ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನೀವು ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿದ data-parallel gradient-averaging ನಂತೆ) ಪ್ರತಿ step ನಲ್ಲಿ workers ಅನ್ನೂ synchronize ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ. Hogwild! ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ, asynchronous ವಿಧಾನ ತೆಗೆದುಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆ: ಅನೇಕ workers ಅದೇ ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ model parameters ಅನ್ನೂ locks ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ಓದುತ್ತವೆ ಮತ್ತು ಬರೆಯುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) ಈ ಸಂದರ್ಭದಲ್ಲಿ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ 'collision' ಹೇಗೆ ಕಾಣುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) gradient SPARSITY ಏಕೆ ಈ ಸಮನ್ವಯಗೊಳಿಸದ ವಿಧಾನ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ ಕೆಲಸ ಮಾಡುವ ಸಾಧ್ಯತೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Hogwild!-Style Lock-Free Concurrent Training',
      titleKn: 'Hogwild!-Style Lock-Free Concurrent Training ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a Hogwild!-style lock-free concurrent training scheme on your mini-GPT using real multi-process or multi-thread concurrency (not merely a simulation), to genuinely observe race conditions and convergence behavior.\n\n1) Genuinely set up multiple concurrent workers (processes or threads, using your language's actual concurrency primitives) that all read from and write to the SAME shared model parameters (e.g. via shared memory or a shared in-memory object, depending on your language/framework's concurrency model) WITHOUT any locks or synchronization barriers between their gradient-compute-and-apply steps.\n\n2) Genuinely run this concurrent training setup and instrument it to detect actual collisions: log instances where you can verify (e.g. via timestamps or explicit before/after parameter value checks) that one worker's update was partially or fully overwritten by another worker's concurrent update, reporting the actual observed collision rate in your specific setup.\n\n3) Genuinely train your mini-GPT with this lock-free concurrent scheme for a meaningful number of steps and compare its final training loss against a standard synchronized (non-Hogwild) multi-worker baseline using the same total number of gradient computations -- report whether your lock-free approach genuinely converged despite the collisions you measured, and if so, how its final loss compares to the synchronized baseline's.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಜ multi-process ಅಥವಾ multi-thread concurrency ಬಳಸಿ ಒಂದು Hogwild!-style lock-free concurrent training ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಅದೇ ಹಂಚಿಕೊಂಡ model parameters ಅನ್ನೂ locks ಇಲ್ಲದೆ ಓದುವ ಮತ್ತು ಬರೆಯುವ ಅನೇಕ concurrent workers ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸ್ಥಾಪಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ concurrent training setup ಅನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ collisions ಪತ್ತೆ ಮಾಡಲು instrument ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ lock-free ಯೋಜನೆ ಜೊತೆ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಒಂದು standard synchronized baseline ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Sparsity-Convergence Relationship by Deliberately Varying Gradient Density',
      titleKn: 'Gradient Density ಅನ್ನೂ ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸಿ Sparsity-Convergence ಸಂಬಂಧವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment directly testing the beginner exercise's claim that gradient sparsity is what makes Hogwild! practically viable.\n\n1) Genuinely construct (or select) at least two training setups with genuinely different gradient sparsity characteristics on your mini-GPT -- e.g. a setup using a genuinely sparse computation pattern (such as training only specific embedding rows per batch, where different batches' gradients naturally touch mostly disjoint parameters) versus a setup using dense gradients (e.g. training dense feed-forward layers, where most parameters receive a nonzero gradient on every step).\n\n2) Genuinely run your Hogwild!-style lock-free implementation from the intermediate exercise on BOTH setups (same number of concurrent workers, same total training steps), and genuinely measure both the observed collision rate and the final training loss/convergence quality for each setup.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether your sparse-gradient setup showed genuinely fewer collisions and/or better convergence than your dense-gradient setup, confirming or complicating the beginner exercise's sparsity claim with your own concrete measured evidence -- discuss what this implies about when Hogwild!-style lock-free training is a genuinely appropriate choice for a real training workload versus when the dense-gradient collision rate would likely make it impractical, grounding your conclusion in your own measured collision rates and convergence outcomes rather than the original Hogwild! paper's claims alone.",
      problemKn: "gradient sparsity ಎಂಬುದೂ Hogwild! ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ ಕಾರ್ಯಸಾಧ್ಯಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂಬ beginner exercise ya ಹಕ್ಕನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ gradient sparsity ಗುಣಲಕ್ಷಣಗಳ ಕನಿಷ್ಠ ಎರಡು training setups ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಎರಡೂ setups ಮೇಲೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ Hogwild!-style lock-free implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು collision rate ಮತ್ತು convergence ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ sparse-gradient setup ಕಡಿಮೆ collisions ತೋರಿಸಿತೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m206, l206_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Asynchronous Inference Serving: Decoupling Request Arrival from Processing Order",
      titleKn: "Asynchronous Inference Serving: Request Arrival ಅನ್ನೂ Processing Order ಇಂದ ಬೇರ್ಪಡಿಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Building on your continuous-batching scheduler from the inference-optimization module, real async inference serving systems decouple WHEN a request arrives from WHEN and in what order it gets processed -- an async serving architecture accepts incoming requests into a queue immediately (never blocking the caller), and separately, one or more worker processes pull from this queue and run generation asynchronously, allowing the system to handle bursts of incoming requests smoothly and to reorder or prioritize requests without the request-submitting caller needing to know or care about the underlying scheduling details.\n\n1) Explain why decoupling request ACCEPTANCE from request PROCESSING (via an intermediate queue) is genuinely more robust to bursty traffic than a synchronous system that processes each request immediately as it arrives -- what specifically would go wrong in a purely synchronous system if 100 requests arrived within the same second, compared to how an async queue-based system would handle the same burst?\n\n2) Explain why async serving genuinely enables PRIORITIZATION strategies (e.g. processing a paying customer's request before a free-tier request, or processing a short request before a long one to reduce average wait time) that would be much harder to implement in a purely synchronous, first-come-first-served processing model -- what does having requests sit in an explicit, inspectable queue enable that immediate synchronous processing does not?",
      problemKn: "inference-optimization module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ continuous-batching scheduler ಮೇಲೆ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸುತ್ತಾ, ನಿಜ async inference serving systems ಒಂದು request ಯಾವಾಗ ಬರುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂಬುದನ್ನೂ ಅದು ಯಾವಾಗ ಮತ್ತು ಯಾವ ಕ್ರಮದಲ್ಲಿ ಪ್ರಕ್ರಿಯೆಗೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂಬುದರಿಂದ ಬೇರ್ಪಡಿಸುತ್ತವೆ.\n\n1) request ACCEPTANCE ಅನ್ನೂ request PROCESSING ಇಂದ ಬೇರ್ಪಡಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ bursty traffic ಗೆ ಹೆಚ್ಚು robust ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) async serving ಏಕೆ PRIORITIZATION ತಂತ್ರಗಳನ್ನೂ ಸಕ್ರಿಯಗೊಳಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing an Async Request Queue with Priority Scheduling',
      titleKn: 'Priority Scheduling ಜೊತೆ ಒಂದು Async Request Queue ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement an async serving layer with priority-based scheduling, extending your serving layer from the earlier building-a-complete-LLM-pipeline module.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a request queue that accepts new generation requests immediately (returning a request ID or a future/promise to the caller right away, without blocking), each tagged with a priority level (e.g. 'high' and 'normal'), and genuinely implement a background worker loop that pulls from this queue according to priority (always preferring high-priority requests when both are waiting) rather than pure first-in-first-out order.\n\n2) Genuinely integrate this priority queue with your continuous-batching scheduler (from the inference-optimization module) so that when forming each batch, the scheduler genuinely prefers admitting higher-priority waiting requests first.\n\n3) Genuinely load-test your priority-aware async system by submitting a mixed burst of high- and normal-priority requests simultaneously, and measure and report the actual average completion latency for high-priority requests versus normal-priority requests -- confirm that high-priority requests genuinely receive measurably lower average latency than normal-priority ones under load, providing concrete evidence that your prioritization logic is functioning as designed.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ya ಹಿಂದಿನ building-a-complete-LLM-pipeline module ya ನಿಮ್ಮ serving layer ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಸ್ತರಿಸಿ, priority-based scheduling ಜೊತೆ ಒಂದು async serving layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹೊಸ generation requests ಅನ್ನೂ ತಕ್ಷಣ ಸ್ವೀಕರಿಸುವ ಒಂದು request queue ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಪ್ರತಿಯೊಂದೂ ಒಂದು priority level ಜೊತೆ ಟ್ಯಾಗ್ ಆಗಿದೆ.\n\n2) ಈ priority queue ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ continuous-batching scheduler ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಮಿಶ್ರ ಬರ್ಸ್ಟ್ ಜೊತೆ ನಿಮ್ಮ priority-aware async system ಅನ್ನೂ load-test ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು latency ಹೋಲಿಕೆ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Fairness and Starvation Risk Under Sustained High-Priority Load',
      titleKn: 'ಸತತ High-Priority Load ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ Fairness ಮತ್ತು Starvation ಅಪಾಯವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely investigate a genuine risk your priority scheduler from the intermediate exercise could introduce: STARVATION, where normal-priority requests could theoretically wait indefinitely if high-priority requests keep arriving fast enough to always fill available capacity first.\n\n1) Genuinely design and run a stress test that submits a SUSTAINED, continuous stream of high-priority requests (arriving fast enough to keep your serving capacity constantly busy) while ALSO submitting a smaller number of normal-priority requests during this same period, and genuinely measure the actual wait time experienced by the normal-priority requests.\n\n2) Genuinely confirm whether your naive strict-priority implementation from the intermediate exercise does indeed show measurable starvation (normal-priority requests waiting substantially longer, or in the extreme, never getting served during your test window) under this sustained high-priority load.\n\n3) Genuinely implement and test one concrete anti-starvation mitigation (e.g. 'aging' -- gradually increasing a waiting request's effective priority the longer it waits, so an old normal-priority request eventually outranks new high-priority arrivals) and genuinely re-run your stress test with this mitigation in place, reporting the measured before/after difference in normal-priority request wait times -- discuss, using your own concrete measured numbers, why real production priority-scheduling systems cannot use naive strict priority alone and must genuinely implement some fairness/anti-starvation mechanism to provide acceptable service to all traffic classes.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ priority scheduler ಪರಿಚಯಿಸಬಹುದಾದ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಅಪಾಯವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ತನಿಖೆ ಮಾಡಿ: STARVATION.\n\n1) ಸತತ high-priority requests ya ಸ್ಟ್ರೀಮ್ ಜೊತೆ normal-priority requests ಸಲ್ಲಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ಒತ್ತಡ-ಪರೀಕ್ಷೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸರಳ strict-priority implementation ಅಳೆಯಬಹುದಾದ starvation ತೋರಿಸುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ anti-starvation ಪರಿಹಾರವನ್ನೂ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಮರುಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ, ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m206, l206_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Staleness in Asynchronous Systems: When 'Fast' Means 'Slightly Out of Date'",
      titleKn: "Asynchronous Systems ನಲ್ಲಿ Staleness: 'Fast' ಎಂದರೆ 'ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ Out of Date' ಆಗಿರುವಾಗ",
      problem: "Both the Hogwild!-style asynchronous TRAINING from earlier in this module and asynchronous INFERENCE serving from the previous lesson share a genuine underlying tradeoff: asynchrony trades STALENESS (working with slightly outdated information) for speed and throughput. In Hogwild! training, a worker might compute its gradient based on a slightly-outdated read of the shared parameters (since another worker may have updated them microseconds ago, before this worker's read completed) -- this is a form of staleness.\n\n1) Explain concretely why tolerating staleness is what allows async systems to avoid the synchronization overhead that a fully consistent (always up-to-date) system would require -- what specific waiting/coordination cost does a system avoid by allowing workers to proceed with slightly-stale information rather than always blocking until they have the absolute latest state?\n\n2) Explain why staleness tolerance has genuine LIMITS -- if the shared state becomes TOO stale (e.g. a worker's gradient is based on parameter values from many steps ago, not just microseconds ago), what could go wrong with the resulting update's usefulness, connecting this to why Hogwild!'s convergence guarantees typically come with explicit bounds on how stale updates are allowed to be before correctness/convergence breaks down.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳ Hogwild!-style asynchronous TRAINING ಮತ್ತು asynchronous INFERENCE serving ಎರಡೂ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ ಮೂಲಭೂತ tradeoff ಹಂಚಿಕೊಳ್ಳುತ್ತವೆ: asynchrony STALENESS ಅನ್ನೂ speed ಮತ್ತು throughput ಗೆ ವಿನಿಮಯ ಮಾಡುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) staleness ಸಹಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ async systems ಗೆ synchronization overhead ತಪ್ಪಿಸಲು ಅನುಮತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) staleness tolerance ಏಕೆ ನಿಜ ಮಿತಿಗಳನ್ನೂ ಹೊಂದಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Staleness Bounds in Your Hogwild! and Async Serving Implementations',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Hogwild! ಮತ್ತು Async Serving Implementations ನಲ್ಲಿ Staleness Bounds ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely measure actual staleness in both systems you built earlier in this module, quantifying the abstract concept from the beginner exercise with concrete numbers.\n\n1) Genuinely instrument your Hogwild!-style training implementation (from the first lesson) to measure, for each worker's gradient computation, how many OTHER workers' updates were applied to the shared parameters between when this worker READ the parameters and when it APPLIED its own update -- report the distribution of this measured 'staleness count' across many training steps in your specific setup.\n\n2) Genuinely instrument your async serving implementation (from the previous lesson) to measure a different but related form of staleness: the actual delay between when a request enters the queue and when it begins processing, under varying load conditions (light load versus heavy load), reporting how queue-wait 'staleness' changes with load.\n\n3) Genuinely correlate your Hogwild! staleness measurements with training outcomes: does your training run show any measurable convergence problems (e.g. loss spikes or slower convergence) specifically at moments where staleness counts were unusually high -- report whatever correlation (or lack thereof) you genuinely observe, and discuss what this implies about whether your specific implementation's staleness levels stayed within a range where correctness/convergence was preserved.",
      problemKn: "ಈ module ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನೀವು ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಎರಡೂ systems ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜ staleness ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ Hogwild!-style training implementation ಅನ್ನೂ instrument ಮಾಡಿ, ಪ್ರತಿ worker ya gradient computation ಗೆ ಎಷ್ಟು ಇತರ workers ya updates ಅನ್ವಯಿಸಲ್ಪಟ್ಟವು ಎಂದು ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ async serving implementation ಅನ್ನೂ instrument ಮಾಡಿ, queue-wait staleness ಅನ್ನೂ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ load ಪರಿಸ್ಥಿತಿಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ Hogwild! staleness ಅಳತೆಗಳನ್ನೂ training ಫಲಿತಾಂಶಗಳ ಜೊತೆ ಸಂಬಂಧಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Implementing and Testing a Staleness-Bounded Hybrid Async Scheme',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು Staleness-Bounded Hybrid Async Scheme ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design, implement, and measure a middle-ground scheme between fully-synchronous and fully-unbounded-async training, directly motivated by the staleness-limit concern from the beginner exercise: BOUNDED staleness, where workers are allowed to proceed asynchronously (avoiding full synchronization overhead) but are forced to synchronize/wait if their staleness would otherwise exceed some chosen maximum threshold.\n\n1) Genuinely modify your Hogwild!-style implementation to enforce a maximum staleness bound: track (as in the intermediate exercise) each worker's staleness count, and if a worker's staleness would exceed your chosen threshold (e.g. more than K other workers' updates having been applied since its last read), force that worker to wait/re-read fresh parameters before proceeding, rather than applying its now-too-stale gradient.\n\n2) Genuinely train your mini-GPT with this bounded-staleness scheme at several different threshold values (e.g. a very tight bound close to fully synchronous, a loose bound close to unbounded Hogwild!, and a moderate bound), measuring both training throughput (steps per second, accounting for any forced waiting) and final convergence quality (training loss) at each threshold.\n\n3) Genuinely report your measured throughput-vs-convergence-quality tradeoff across the different staleness bounds, identifying which threshold in your specific experiments offered the best balance -- discuss, using your own concrete measured numbers, why real production distributed-training systems that use asynchronous or semi-asynchronous updates (rather than either extreme of fully-synchronous or fully-unbounded-async) typically implement exactly this kind of bounded-staleness mechanism, grounding your discussion in the specific tradeoff curve you personally measured rather than general claims about async training.",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ya staleness-limit ಕಾಳಜಿಯಿಂದ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರೇರಿತವಾದ, fully-synchronous ಮತ್ತು fully-unbounded-async training ನಡುವಿನ ಒಂದು ಮಧ್ಯಮ-ಮಾರ್ಗ ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ: BOUNDED staleness.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ Hogwild!-style implementation ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದು ಗರಿಷ್ಠ staleness bound ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಲು ಮಾರ್ಪಡಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ threshold ಮೌಲ್ಯಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ bounded-staleness ಯೋಜನೆ ಜೊತೆ train ಮಾಡಿ, throughput ಮತ್ತು convergence ಎರಡನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ವಿಭಿನ್ನ staleness bounds ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ threshold ಗುರುತಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m207, l207_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Training a Draft Model via Self-Distillation from the Target Model",
      titleKn: "Target Model ಇಂದ Self-Distillation ಮೂಲಕ Draft Model ಟ್ರೈನ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Earlier speculative-decoding exercises in this phase assumed you already had a suitable draft model, but real deployments must genuinely TRAIN one specifically to agree with a specific target model as often as possible -- typically via SELF-DISTILLATION, where a small draft model is trained not on raw ground-truth text, but on the TARGET model's own output distribution (having the draft model learn to predict the SAME probability distribution the target model would produce for the same input, rather than just the single correct next token), since matching the target's actual predictive behavior (including its mistakes and stylistic quirks) is more valuable for draft-target agreement than matching ground truth alone.\n\n1) Explain why training a draft model on the target model's output DISTRIBUTION (e.g. via a KL-divergence loss between draft and target output distributions, or by training on target-model-GENERATED text rather than original human text) produces a draft model with genuinely higher agreement with the target than training the same small architecture on ground-truth text alone -- what specific target-model-specific behaviors (including its particular errors or stylistic tendencies) does distillation let the draft model learn that ground-truth training cannot?\n\n2) Explain why this distillation-based draft training approach means a draft model trained for ONE specific target model would likely NOT transfer well to speeding up a DIFFERENT target model, even one of similar size and general capability -- what is the draft model's learned behavior actually specialized to match?",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ya ಹಿಂದಿನ speculative-decoding exercises ನೀವು ಈಗಾಗಲೇ ಸೂಕ್ತ draft model ಹೊಂದಿದ್ದೀರಿ ಎಂದು ಊಹಿಸಿದವು, ಆದರೆ ನಿಜ deployments ಒಂದನ್ನೂ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟವಾಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಬೇಕು, ಸಾಮಾನ್ಯವಾಗಿ SELF-DISTILLATION ಮೂಲಕ.\n\n1) target model ya output DISTRIBUTION ಮೇಲೆ draft model ಟ್ರೈನ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ground-truth text ಮೇಲೆ ಮಾತ್ರ ಟ್ರೈನ್ ಮಾಡುವುದಕ್ಕಿಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು agreement ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ distillation-based draft training ವಿಧಾನ ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ target model ಗೆ train ಮಾಡಿದ draft model ಬೇರೆ target model ಗೆ ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿ transfer ಆಗುವುದಿಲ್ಲ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Distillation-Based Draft Model Training and Measuring Agreement Gains',
      titleKn: 'Distillation-Based Draft Model Training ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ Agreement Gains ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement self-distillation training for a small draft model targeting your mini-GPT, and genuinely measure whether it improves draft-target agreement over ground-truth training.\n\n1) Genuinely train two separate small draft models of IDENTICAL architecture: draft model A trained the standard way (next-token cross-entropy loss against ground-truth text), and draft model B trained via distillation (a KL-divergence loss between draft model B's output distribution and your target mini-GPT's own output distribution, computed by running your target model on the same training text and using ITS output probabilities as the training signal for the draft model, rather than the ground-truth one-hot labels).\n\n2) Genuinely measure the empirical draft-target agreement rate (reusing your methodology from the earlier speculative-decoding lessons) for BOTH draft model A and draft model B against your target mini-GPT, on the same held-out test prompts.\n\n3) Genuinely report which draft model achieved higher measured agreement with the target, and by how much -- confirm or disconfirm the beginner exercise's claim that distillation-based training produces better draft-target agreement than ground-truth training alone, using your own concrete measured numbers.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಗುರಿಯಾಗಿಸಿ ಒಂದು ಚಿಕ್ಕ draft model ಗೆ self-distillation training ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದೇ architecture ya ಎರಡು ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇಕ ಚಿಕ್ಕ draft models ಅನ್ನೂ ಟ್ರೈನ್ ಮಾಡಿ: draft A standard ground-truth ವಿಧಾನ ಮೂಲಕ, draft B distillation ಮೂಲಕ (target model ya output distribution ವಿರುದ್ಧ KL-divergence loss).\n\n2) ಎರಡೂ draft models ya empirical agreement rate ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ target mini-GPT ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಯಾವ draft model ಹೆಚ್ಚು agreement ಸಾಧಿಸಿತು ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Testing Draft-Model Transferability Across Different Target Models',
      titleKn: 'ವಿಭಿನ್ನ Target Models ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ Draft-Model Transferability ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an experiment directly testing the beginner exercise's claim about draft-model specialization and transfer failure.\n\n1) Genuinely train a second target mini-GPT model that is genuinely DIFFERENT from your original target (e.g. trained on a different data mixture, or with a meaningfully different random initialization and training run, while keeping the architecture comparable), and genuinely distill a SECOND draft model specifically against this new target, following your intermediate exercise's distillation methodology.\n\n2) Genuinely measure your ORIGINAL distilled draft model's (trained to match your FIRST target) agreement rate against this NEW second target model, and separately measure your NEW draft model's (trained to match the second target) agreement rate against the second target -- report the measured GAP between these two numbers.\n\n3) Genuinely report whether your original draft model's agreement rate genuinely dropped substantially when tested against a different target than it was distilled for, confirming or complicating the beginner exercise's transferability claim with concrete evidence -- discuss what this implies about the practical operational cost real deployments face when they update or replace their target model (e.g. a new model version), specifically regarding whether they can keep reusing an existing draft model or must genuinely retrain/redistill a new one for each target model version.",
      problemKn: "draft-model specialization ಮತ್ತು transfer ವೈಫಲ್ಯದ ಬಗ್ಗೆ beginner exercise ya ಹಕ್ಕನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸುವ ಪ್ರಯೋಗವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲ target ಇಂದ ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ಭಿನ್ನವಾದ ಒಂದು ಎರಡನೇ target mini-GPT model ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ train ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಅದಕ್ಕೆ ಒಂದು ಎರಡನೇ draft model ಅನ್ನೂ distill ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲ distilled draft model ya agreement rate ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ ಹೊಸ ಎರಡನೇ target ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು ಹೊಸ draft model ya agreement rate ಜೊತೆ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲ draft model ya agreement rate ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ಕುಸಿಯಿತೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕ ಪರಿಣಾಮಗಳನ್ನೂ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m207, l207_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Adaptive Draft Length: Guessing More When Confident, Less When Uncertain",
      titleKn: "Adaptive Draft Length: ಆತ್ಮವಿಶ್ವಾಸ ಇರುವಾಗ ಹೆಚ್ಚು, ಅನಿಶ್ಚಿತ ಇರುವಾಗ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಊಹಿಸುವುದೂ",
      problem: "Every speculative-decoding implementation you've built so far in this phase used a FIXED draft length (a constant number of tokens K speculated ahead each round), but a genuinely smarter approach adapts K dynamically based on the draft model's own CONFIDENCE at each step -- speculating MANY tokens ahead when the draft model's predicted probability distribution is sharply peaked (high confidence, likely to match the target), and speculating FEWER tokens (or even falling back to normal one-at-a-time generation) when the draft model's distribution is flat/uncertain (low confidence, likely to be rejected anyway), since continuing to draft low-confidence tokens wastes compute on guesses likely to be rejected.\n\n1) Explain why speculating a long sequence of tokens when the draft model is genuinely UNCERTAIN about even the first of them is likely to be wasteful -- given how the accept/reject mechanism works (rejecting at the FIRST mismatch and discarding everything after it), what happens to all the compute spent drafting tokens 2 through K if the draft model's very first guess is likely already wrong?\n\n2) Explain what specific, measurable signal from the draft model's own output (something you already have direct access to in your implementation, without needing the target model at all) could serve as a genuine confidence proxy to decide how far ahead to speculate at each step.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ನಲ್ಲಿ ಈವರೆಗೆ ನೀವು ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಪ್ರತಿ speculative-decoding implementation ಒಂದು FIXED draft length ಬಳಸಿತು, ಆದರೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಬುದ್ಧಿವಂತ ವಿಧಾನ K ಅನ್ನೂ draft model ya ಸ್ವಂತ CONFIDENCE ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ ಡೈನಾಮಿಕ್ ಆಗಿ ಹೊಂದಿಸುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) draft model ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅನಿಶ್ಚಿತವಾಗಿರುವಾಗ ಒಂದು ದೀರ್ಘ tokens ಅನುಕ್ರಮವನ್ನೂ ಊಹಿಸುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ವ್ಯರ್ಥಕಾರಿಯಾಗಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) draft model ya ಸ್ವಂತ output ಇಂದ ಯಾವ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ, ಅಳೆಯಬಹುದಾದ ಸಿಗ್ನಲ್ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ confidence proxy ಆಗಿ ಸೇವೆ ಸಲ್ಲಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Confidence-Based Adaptive Draft Length',
      titleKn: 'Confidence-Based Adaptive Draft Length ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement adaptive draft-length speculative decoding on top of your existing implementation from earlier in this phase.\n\n1) Genuinely implement a confidence metric computed directly from your draft model's output at each speculation step (e.g. the maximum softmax probability of its predicted next token, or the negative entropy of its full output distribution -- pick one and justify your choice), and genuinely implement a policy that stops speculating further tokens within the current round as soon as this confidence metric drops below a chosen threshold, rather than always drafting a fixed K tokens.\n\n2) Genuinely run this adaptive-length scheme on a set of test prompts and instrument it to log the ACTUAL draft length used at each speculation round, reporting the distribution of draft lengths your adaptive policy chose across a real generation run (confirming it genuinely varies, rather than collapsing to always the same length).\n\n3) Genuinely measure and compare the overall speedup (total target-model forward passes needed for a fixed-length generation) of your adaptive-length scheme against your ORIGINAL fixed-length scheme from earlier in this phase, using the SAME draft and target models for both -- report whether adaptive length genuinely improved speedup in your specific experiments, and by how much.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ya ಹಿಂದಿನ ಭಾಗದ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಸ್ತಿತ್ವದಲ್ಲಿರುವ implementation ಮೇಲೆ adaptive draft-length speculative decoding ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ draft model ya output ಇಂದ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿದ ಒಂದು confidence metric ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ adaptive-length ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ speculation ಸುತ್ತಿನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜ draft length ಅನ್ನೂ ಲಾಗ್ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಈ adaptive-length ಯೋಜನೆ ya ಒಟ್ಟು speedup ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲ fixed-length ಯೋಜನೆ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Tuning the Confidence Threshold to Find Your Own Measured Optimum',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ Measured Optimum ಕಂಡುಹಿಡಿಯಲು Confidence Threshold ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ Tune ಮಾಡುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an ablation study over the confidence-threshold hyperparameter in your adaptive-length implementation from the intermediate exercise, to genuinely characterize this design's own internal tradeoff.\n\n1) Genuinely run your adaptive-length speculative decoding at several different confidence thresholds (e.g. a very lenient threshold allowing long speculation even at moderate confidence, a strict threshold cutting off speculation quickly at any uncertainty, and a moderate middle threshold), measuring for each: overall speedup (total target-model forward passes for a fixed generation length) and the average draft length actually used.\n\n2) Genuinely report your measured speedup-vs-threshold relationship across the tested values, identifying whether there's a genuine 'sweet spot' threshold that outperforms both the very lenient and very strict extremes, or whether speedup monotonically improves/worsens as you move in one direction.\n\n3) Genuinely compare your BEST-performing adaptive threshold's speedup against your original fixed-K scheme's speedup (from the intermediate exercise) and discuss, using your own concrete measured numbers, how much of an improvement (if any) genuinely adaptive draft length provided over a well-chosen FIXED draft length in your specific small-scale experiments -- being honest if the improvement was marginal, and discussing what specific properties of your test prompts (e.g. how much genuine variability in draft-model confidence they exhibited) might make adaptive length matter more or less in different real-world deployment scenarios than in your own test setup.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ adaptive-length implementation ನಲ್ಲಿ confidence-threshold hyperparameter ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ablation ಅಧ್ಯಯನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ confidence thresholds ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ adaptive-length speculative decoding ಅನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು speedup ಹಾಗೂ ಸರಾಸರಿ draft length ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ವಿವಿಧ thresholds ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ speedup ಸಂಬಂಧವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ, ಒಂದು ನಿಜ 'sweet spot' ಇದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಗುರುತಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅತ್ಯುತ್ತಮ adaptive threshold ya speedup ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಮೂಲ fixed-K ಯೋಜನೆ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕವಾಗಿ ಚರ್ಚಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m207, l207_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Monitoring a Live Speculative-Decoding System: What Can Silently Degrade Over Time",
      titleKn: "ಒಂದು Live Speculative-Decoding System ಮಾನಿಟರ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ: ಸಮಯದೊಂದಿಗೆ ಏನು ಮೌನವಾಗಿ ಹದಗೆಡಬಹುದು",
      problem: "A speculative-decoding system that measures well in an initial benchmark (as you've done throughout this phase) can still genuinely degrade in production over time in ways that require ongoing monitoring to catch, connecting back to the production-monitoring principles from the building-a-complete-LLM-pipeline module. Specifically, if the TARGET model is ever updated (e.g. re-fine-tuned on new data, or a new checkpoint deployed) without also updating the draft model, the draft-target agreement rate you carefully measured and tuned could silently drop, since your distilled draft model (from the first lesson in this module) was specifically trained to match the OLD target's specific behavior.\n\n1) Explain concretely why updating a target model without also re-distilling or re-validating the draft model creates a genuine, silent risk -- what specifically would happen to your speculative-decoding system's SPEEDUP (not its correctness -- recall speculative decoding with proper accept/reject logic is always exactly correct) if draft-target agreement quietly dropped after a target model update, and why might this degradation go UNNOTICED without dedicated monitoring, given that the system would still produce correct outputs, just more slowly?\n\n2) Explain what specific metric a production system should continuously track to catch this kind of silent degradation -- connect back to the draft-target agreement rate concept from earlier in this phase and explain why monitoring THIS metric (rather than just overall system latency, which has many other confounding causes) would most directly and specifically reveal a draft-target mismatch problem.",
      problemKn: "ಒಂದು speculative-decoding system ಇದು ಆರಂಭಿಕ benchmark ನಲ್ಲಿ ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುತ್ತದೆ ಆದರೆ production ನಲ್ಲಿ ಸಮಯದೊಂದಿಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಹದಗೆಡಬಹುದು, ನಿರಂತರ ಮಾನಿಟರಿಂಗ್ ಅಗತ್ಯವಿದೆ. target model ಅಪ್ಡೇಟ್ ಆದರೆ draft model ಅಪ್ಡೇಟ್ ಆಗದಿದ್ದರೆ, draft-target agreement rate ಮೌನವಾಗಿ ಕುಸಿಯಬಹುದು.\n\n1) target model ಅಪ್ಡೇಟ್ ಮಾಡುವುದೂ draft model ಅನ್ನೂ ಮರುtrain ಮಾಡದೆ ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ, ಮೌನ ಅಪಾಯ ಸೃಷ್ಟಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ರೀತಿಯ ಮೌನ ಹದಗೆಡುವಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ಹಿಡಿಯಲು ಒಂದು production system ಯಾವ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ metric ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿರಂತರವಾಗಿ ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡಬೇಕು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Live Draft-Target Agreement Monitoring',
      titleKn: 'Live Draft-Target Agreement Monitoring ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement a continuous monitoring mechanism for your speculative-decoding system that tracks draft-target agreement rate over time, and genuinely simulate the target-model-update scenario from the beginner exercise.\n\n1) Genuinely instrument your speculative-decoding implementation (from earlier in this phase) to log the draft-target agreement rate (fraction of draft tokens accepted) for every generation request, maintaining a running/windowed average over recent requests (e.g. the last 100 requests) rather than a single lifetime average, so genuinely recent shifts are visible.\n\n2) Genuinely simulate a target-model update: after establishing a stable baseline agreement rate with your ORIGINAL target model, swap in a genuinely DIFFERENT target model (e.g. your second target model from the earlier draft-transferability exercise, or a freshly fine-tuned version of your original target) WITHOUT updating your draft model, and continue logging agreement rate through this swap.\n\n3) Genuinely report your logged agreement-rate time series showing the transition, confirming whether your monitoring genuinely detected a visible drop in the windowed average agreement rate at the point of the target swap -- report the actual magnitude and speed of the detected drop, providing concrete evidence that your monitoring mechanism would have caught this silent degradation in a real production setting.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ speculative-decoding system ಗೆ ಸಮಯದೊಂದಿಗೆ draft-target agreement rate ಟ್ರ್ಯಾಕ್ ಮಾಡುವ ಒಂದು ನಿರಂತರ monitoring ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರತಿ generation request ಗೆ draft-target agreement rate ಅನ್ನೂ ಲಾಗ್ ಮಾಡಿ, ಒಂದು running/windowed ಸರಾಸರಿಯನ್ನೂ ಇಟ್ಟುಕೊಳ್ಳಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು target-model update ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ simulate ಮಾಡಿ: ಸ್ಥಿರ baseline ಸ್ಥಾಪಿಸಿದ ನಂತರ, draft model ಅಪ್ಡೇಟ್ ಮಾಡದೆ ಗಣನೀಯವಾಗಿ ಭಿನ್ನ target model ಅನ್ನೂ ಬದಲಾಯಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ monitoring ಈ ಪರಿವರ್ತನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪತ್ತೆ ಮಾಡಿತೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Implementing an Automated Alert-and-Fallback Policy for Agreement Degradation',
      titleKn: 'Agreement Degradation ಗಾಗಿ ಸ್ವಯಂಚಾಲಿತ Alert-and-Fallback Policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design, implement, and test a complete automated response to the silent degradation you detected and measured in the intermediate exercise, closing the monitoring-to-action loop for your speculative-decoding system.\n\n1) Genuinely implement an alerting policy: define a concrete threshold drop in the windowed agreement rate (relative to its established baseline) that should trigger an automated alert, and genuinely implement the detection logic that fires this alert based on your logged time-series data from the intermediate exercise's target-swap simulation.\n\n2) Genuinely implement an automated FALLBACK policy that activates when agreement rate drops below a more severe threshold: temporarily disable speculative decoding entirely (falling back to plain one-token-at-a-time target-only generation) rather than continuing to run an increasingly-inefficient speculative scheme, since below some agreement rate, speculative decoding's overhead (running the draft model at all) may no longer be worth its shrinking benefit.\n\n3) Genuinely re-run your target-swap simulation from the intermediate exercise with both your alerting and fallback policies active, and report the complete before/during/after behavior: confirm your alert fired at the expected point, confirm your fallback activated and that the system's overall latency did not continue to silently degrade further once agreement dropped below your fallback threshold, and discuss, using your own concrete measured timeline, why real production ML-serving teams treat this kind of automated detect-alert-fallback loop as a required operational safeguard rather than an optional nice-to-have, especially for systems (like speculative decoding) whose FAILURE MODE is a silent performance regression rather than an obvious crash or error.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ನಲ್ಲಿ ಪತ್ತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ ಮೌನ ಹದಗೆಡುವಿಕೆಗೆ ಒಂದು ಪೂರ್ಣ ಸ್ವಯಂಚಾಲಿತ ಪ್ರತಿಕ್ರಿಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು alerting policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, agreement rate ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ threshold ಮೀರಿ ಕುಸಿದಾಗ ಒಂದು alert ಪ್ರಚೋದಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಹೆಚ್ಚು ತೀವ್ರ threshold ಕೆಳಗೆ agreement rate ಕುಸಿದಾಗ speculative decoding ಅನ್ನೂ ತಾತ್ಕಾಲಿಕವಾಗಿ ನಿಷ್ಕ್ರಿಯಗೊಳಿಸುವ ಒಂದು ಸ್ವಯಂಚಾಲಿತ FALLBACK policy ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) intermediate exercise ya target-swap simulation ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ ಎರಡೂ policies ಸಕ್ರಿಯವಾಗಿ ಮರುಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ before/during/after ನಡವಳಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m208, l208_1, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Why Backpropagation Needs Activations: The Memory Cost of Remembering Every Layer",
      titleKn: "Backpropagation ಗೆ Activations ಏಕೆ ಬೇಕು: ಪ್ರತಿ Layer ಅನ್ನೂ ನೆನಪಿಡುವ Memory ವೆಚ್ಚ",
      problem: "Standard backpropagation requires the FORWARD pass's intermediate activations (the output of every layer, not just the final output) to remain in memory until the BACKWARD pass reaches that layer, because computing a layer's gradient requires that layer's specific input/output values from the forward pass -- this means a naive implementation's PEAK memory usage grows roughly proportionally to the NUMBER OF LAYERS in the network, since every single layer's activations must be held simultaneously until backpropagation has worked its way back through the entire network.\n\n1) Explain concretely, using the chain rule of calculus you'd have learned about gradients, why computing the gradient with respect to a specific layer's weights genuinely requires that layer's specific forward-pass input (not just the final loss value) -- what information would be missing if you tried to compute a middle layer's gradient using ONLY the network's final output and loss, without retaining that layer's own forward-pass activations?\n\n2) Explain why this activation-memory cost becomes a genuine bottleneck specifically for very DEEP networks (many layers) -- given that this memory cost scales with layer COUNT (not just total parameter count), why might two networks with the SAME total parameter count but very different depths (one very deep and narrow, one shallow and wide) have very different activation-memory requirements during training.",
      problemKn: "Standard backpropagation ಗೆ forward pass ya intermediate activations (ಕೇವಲ ಅಂತಿಮ output ಅಲ್ಲ, ಪ್ರತಿ layer ya output) backward pass ಆ layer ಗೆ ತಲುಪುವವರೆಗೆ memory ನಲ್ಲಿ ಉಳಿಯಬೇಕು. ಇದರರ್ಥ ಒಂದು ಸರಳ implementation ya peak memory usage layers ya ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಗೆ ಅನುಗುಣವಾಗಿ ಬೆಳೆಯುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) chain rule ಬಳಸಿ, ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ layer ya weights ya gradient ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ ಆ layer ya ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ forward-pass input ಅಗತ್ಯವಿದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ activation-memory ವೆಚ್ಚ ಏಕೆ ವಿಶೇಷವಾಗಿ ಆಳವಾದ networks ಗೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ bottleneck ಆಗುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Measuring Activation Memory Growth with Network Depth',
      titleKn: 'Network Depth ಜೊತೆ Activation Memory Growth ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely measure the activation-memory scaling behavior described in the beginner exercise, using several versions of your mini-GPT at different depths.\n\n1) Genuinely construct at least 3 versions of your mini-GPT with different numbers of transformer blocks/layers (e.g. a shallow version, a moderate version, and a deep version), keeping other hyperparameters (hidden size, number of heads) as similar as possible across versions so depth is the primary variable being tested.\n\n2) Genuinely instrument a standard (non-checkpointed) forward-and-backward training step for each depth version to measure the ACTUAL peak memory consumed by stored activations (e.g. by tracking the size of every intermediate tensor your forward pass retains for the backward pass), reporting your measured activation-memory footprint for all 3 depths.\n\n3) Genuinely plot or tabulate your measured activation memory against layer count, and confirm whether the growth is genuinely proportional (linear) to depth, as the beginner exercise predicted -- report your concrete measured numbers and calculate the approximate per-layer activation-memory cost implied by your data, which will be useful for later exercises in this module.",
      problemKn: "beginner exercise ವಿವರಿಸಿದ activation-memory scaling ನಡವಳಿಕೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya ಹಲವಾರು ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳ ಬಳಸಿ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ depths ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) ವಿಭಿನ್ನ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯ layers ya ಕನಿಷ್ಠ 3 ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗಳ ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಪ್ರತಿ depth ಆವೃತ್ತಿಗೆ ಒಂದು standard forward-and-backward training step ಅನ್ನೂ instrument ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ peak activation memory ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n3) ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ activation memory ಅನ್ನೂ layer count ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಪ್ಲಾಟ್ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಬೆಳವಣಿಗೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅನುಪಾತಿಕವಾಗಿದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Identifying the Maximum Trainable Depth Under a Fixed Memory Budget',
      titleKn: 'ಒಂದು ಸ್ಥಿರ Memory Budget ಅಡಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಗರಿಷ್ಠ Trainable Depth ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಗುರುತಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely use your measured per-layer activation-memory cost from the intermediate exercise to make a concrete, testable prediction, then genuinely verify it.\n\n1) Genuinely choose a specific, deliberately constrained memory budget (e.g. a fraction of your actual available system memory, or an artificial cap you enforce in your code), and using your measured per-layer activation-memory cost from the intermediate exercise, genuinely CALCULATE the predicted maximum number of layers your mini-GPT architecture could train under standard (non-checkpointed) backpropagation before exceeding this budget.\n\n2) Genuinely test your prediction empirically: construct mini-GPT versions at layer counts around your calculated maximum (a few below, at, and a few above), and genuinely attempt a standard training step for each, recording whether it succeeds within your memory budget or fails (e.g. an out-of-memory error, or your own instrumented memory tracking exceeding the budget).\n\n3) Genuinely report whether your empirically-observed maximum trainable depth matched your calculated prediction, discussing any discrepancy (e.g. from other memory consumers like model weights and optimizer states that you may not have accounted for in your simple per-layer activation calculation) -- and explicitly motivate the need for the gradient-checkpointing technique covered in the next lesson by reporting how much DEEPER you'd like to be able to train than your measured/predicted current limit allows, given a real-world scenario where deeper models are known to perform meaningfully better.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ per-layer activation-memory ವೆಚ್ಚವನ್ನೂ ಬಳಸಿ ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ, ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಬಹುದಾದ ಭವಿಷ್ಯವಾಣಿ ಮಾಡಿ, ನಂತರ ಅದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಒಂದು ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ, ಉದ್ದೇಶಪೂರ್ವಕವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಬಂಧಿತ memory budget ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಗರಿಷ್ಠ trainable layers ಸಂಖ್ಯೆಯನ್ನೂ ಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಭವಿಷ್ಯವಾಣಿಯನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ ಪ್ರಾಯೋಗಿಕವಾಗಿ ಗಮನಿಸಿದ ಗರಿಷ್ಠ depth ನಿಮ್ಮ ಲೆಕ್ಕಾಚಾರಕ್ಕೆ ಹೊಂದಿಕೆಯಾಗುತ್ತದೆಯೇ ಎಂದು ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಮುಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ gradient-checkpointing technique ya ಅಗತ್ಯವನ್ನೂ ಸಮರ್ಥಿಸಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m208, l208_2, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Gradient Checkpointing: Trading Compute for Memory by Recomputing Instead of Storing",
      titleKn: "Gradient Checkpointing: Store ಮಾಡುವ ಬದಲು Recompute ಮಾಡಿ Compute ಗಾಗಿ Memory ವಿನಿಮಯ",
      problem: "Gradient checkpointing (also called activation recomputation) directly addresses the memory bottleneck from the previous lesson: instead of storing EVERY layer's activations from the forward pass, it stores only a SUBSET of 'checkpoint' layers' activations, and during the backward pass, whenever a NON-checkpointed layer's activation is needed for gradient computation, it is genuinely RECOMPUTED on the fly (by re-running a small piece of the forward pass starting from the nearest stored checkpoint) rather than having been kept in memory the whole time.\n\n1) Explain concretely why this technique is a genuine COMPUTE-for-MEMORY tradeoff rather than a free improvement -- what specific additional computational work does the backward pass now have to do that it didn't have to do under standard (fully-stored) backpropagation, and why does this genuinely increase total training TIME even though it decreases memory?\n\n2) Explain why checkpointing only a SUBSET of layers (rather than either storing everything, or storing nothing) lets you tune a genuine dial between memory savings and recompute cost -- if you checkpoint every OTHER layer versus only every FOURTH layer, explain how this choice affects both how much memory you save and how much extra recomputation work the backward pass must do.",
      problemKn: "Gradient checkpointing (activation recomputation ಎಂದೂ ಕರೆಯಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ memory bottleneck ಅನ್ನೂ ನೇರವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಹರಿಸುತ್ತದೆ: ಪ್ರತಿ layer ya activations ಇಡುವ ಬದಲು, ಕೇವಲ ಒಂದು ಉಪವಿಭಾಗದ 'checkpoint' layers ya activations ಅನ್ನೂ ಇಡುತ್ತದೆ, ಮತ್ತು backward pass ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ, ಇತರ layers ya activation ಬೇಕಾದಾಗ ಅದನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ RECOMPUTE ಮಾಡಲಾಗುತ್ತದೆ.\n\n1) ಈ technique ಏಕೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ COMPUTE-for-MEMORY tradeoff ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ, ಉಚಿತ ಸುಧಾರಣೆ ಅಲ್ಲ.\n\n2) ಕೇವಲ ಒಂದು ಉಪವಿಭಾಗದ layers ಅನ್ನೂ checkpoint ಮಾಡುವುದೂ ಏಕೆ memory savings ಮತ್ತು recompute ವೆಚ್ಚ ನಡುವೆ ಒಂದು ನಿಜ dial ಟ್ಯೂನ್ ಮಾಡಲು ಅನುಮತಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Gradient Checkpointing on Your Mini-GPT',
      titleKn: 'ನಿಮ್ಮ Mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ Gradient Checkpointing ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement gradient checkpointing on your mini-GPT's training loop.\n\n1) Genuinely implement the core checkpointing mechanic: during the forward pass, store activations ONLY at chosen 'checkpoint' layers (e.g. every K-th layer, for a K you choose), discarding intermediate layers' activations immediately after computing them; during the backward pass, when a discarded layer's activation is needed, genuinely re-run the forward computation from the nearest preceding stored checkpoint to regenerate it, then use it for the gradient computation before discarding it again.\n\n2) Genuinely verify correctness: confirm that the gradients your checkpointed implementation produces are NUMERICALLY IDENTICAL (or extremely close, accounting for floating-point nondeterminism) to the gradients produced by standard, fully-stored backpropagation on the same input and model weights -- report this exact-match verification as concrete evidence your recomputation logic is correct.\n\n3) Genuinely measure, using your checkpointed implementation, the actual peak activation memory used during training at the SAME model depth you tested in the previous lesson's advanced exercise (near your previously-measured maximum trainable depth under a fixed memory budget) -- report the measured memory reduction, and confirm whether checkpointing genuinely let you train at depths beyond your previously-calculated maximum.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya training loop ಮೇಲೆ gradient checkpointing ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಪ್ರಮುಖ checkpointing ಯಾಂತ್ರಿಕತೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ: forward pass ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಕೇವಲ ಆಯ್ಕೆಮಾಡಿದ checkpoint layers ya activations ಅನ್ನೂ ಇಡಿ, ಇತರ layers ya activations ಅನ್ನೂ ತಕ್ಷಣ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸಿ; backward pass ಸಮಯದಲ್ಲಿ ಬೇಕಾದಾಗ ಮರುಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಿ.\n\n2) ನಿಮ್ಮ checkpointed implementation ಉತ್ಪಾದಿಸುವ gradients standard backpropagation ya gradients ಗೆ ನಿಖರವಾಗಿ ಹೊಂದಿಕೆಯಾಗುತ್ತವೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಗರಿಷ್ಠ depth ಬಳಸಿ ನಿಜ peak activation memory ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ ಮತ್ತು checkpointing ಈ ಮಿತಿ ಮೀರಿ train ಮಾಡಲು ಅನುಮತಿಸಿತೇ ಎಂದು ಖಚಿತಪಡಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Measuring the Checkpoint-Interval Tradeoff Between Memory and Recompute Time',
      titleKn: 'Memory ಮತ್ತು Recompute Time ನಡುವೆ Checkpoint-Interval Tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely design and run an ablation study over the checkpoint interval K (checkpointing every K-th layer) in your implementation from the intermediate exercise, to genuinely characterize the memory-vs-recompute-time tradeoff the beginner exercise described.\n\n1) Genuinely run your gradient-checkpointed mini-GPT at several different checkpoint intervals (e.g. checkpointing every layer -- equivalent to no savings, every 2nd layer, every 4th layer, and every 8th layer, or as many variations as your model depth allows), measuring for each: actual peak activation memory used, and actual wall-clock TIME per training step (accounting for the genuine extra recomputation work).\n\n2) Genuinely report your measured memory-vs-time tradeoff table across all tested intervals, confirming whether SPARSER checkpointing (larger K, fewer stored checkpoints) genuinely reduces memory further but at the cost of genuinely increased recompute time, as the beginner exercise predicted.\n\n3) Genuinely identify, from your own concrete measured tradeoff curve, which checkpoint interval would be the right choice for a scenario where memory is the binding constraint (e.g. training the deepest possible model within a fixed memory budget) versus a scenario where training TIME is the binding constraint (e.g. needing to finish training within a fixed time budget, with memory to spare) -- ground your recommendation in your own measured numbers, and discuss why real large-scale training systems typically expose the checkpoint interval as a user-configurable hyperparameter precisely because the right choice depends on which resource (memory or time) is scarcer for a given specific training run.",
      problemKn: "intermediate exercise ya ನಿಮ್ಮ implementation ನಲ್ಲಿ checkpoint interval K ಮೇಲೆ ಒಂದು ablation ಅಧ್ಯಯನವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿನ್ಯಾಸಗೊಳಿಸಿ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, beginner exercise ವಿವರಿಸಿದ memory-vs-recompute-time tradeoff ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಪ್ರಮಾಣೀಕರಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಹಲವಾರು ವಿಭಿನ್ನ checkpoint intervals ನಲ್ಲಿ ನಿಮ್ಮ gradient-checkpointed mini-GPT ಅನ್ನೂ ಚಲಾಯಿಸಿ, ಪ್ರತಿಯೊಂದಕ್ಕೂ ನಿಜ peak activation memory ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ wall-clock ಸಮಯವನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ಎಲ್ಲಾ ಪರೀಕ್ಷಿಸಿದ intervals ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ನಿಮ್ಮ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ memory-vs-time tradeoff ಕೋಷ್ಟಕವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ.\n\n3) ಮೆಮೊರಿ ನಿರ್ಬಂಧಕ ಪರಿಸ್ಥಿತಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಮತ್ತು ಸಮಯ ನಿರ್ಬಂಧಕ ಪರಿಸ್ಥಿತಿಯಲ್ಲಿ ಯಾವ checkpoint interval ಸರಿಯಾದ ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಎಂದು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಅಳತೆ ಮಾಡಿದ numbers ಆಧಾರದ ಮೇಲೆ ಗುರುತಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಶಿಫಾರಸು ಮಾಡಿ." },
  ]),
  ...set(m208, l208_3, [
    { difficulty: 'beginner', title: "Selective Checkpointing: Recomputing Cheap Operations, Storing Expensive Ones",
      titleKn: "Selective Checkpointing: ಅಗ್ಗದ Operations ಮರುಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುವುದೂ, ದುಬಾರಿ Operations ಇಡುವುದೂ",
      problem: "A refinement beyond the uniform every-K-th-layer checkpointing from the previous lesson is SELECTIVE activation checkpointing: rather than treating every operation equally, genuinely analyze which specific operations within a layer are CHEAP to recompute (e.g. simple elementwise operations like activation functions, which take negligible time to redo) versus EXPENSIVE to recompute (e.g. large matrix multiplications, which take substantial time) -- and selectively discard-and-recompute only the CHEAP operations' outputs, while keeping the EXPENSIVE operations' outputs stored, since recomputing something expensive defeats much of the purpose of saving time, while recomputing something cheap costs almost nothing extra.\n\n1) Explain concretely why this selective approach can achieve a genuinely BETTER memory-vs-time tradeoff than the uniform (every-K-th-layer) checkpointing from the previous lesson -- given that uniform checkpointing treats ALL layers as equally expensive to recompute, while a real network's operations have genuinely different recomputation costs, what specific opportunity does selective checkpointing exploit that uniform checkpointing misses?\n\n2) Explain what specific property of an operation makes it a GOOD candidate for being recomputed rather than stored -- connecting to the actual compute cost (FLOPs) versus memory footprint (size of its output tensor) of different operation types you've learned about throughout this phase, what ratio of (compute cost) to (memory footprint) would make an operation an attractive recomputation target?",
      problemKn: "ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ uniform every-K-th-layer checkpointing ಮೀರಿದ ಒಂದು ಸುಧಾರಣೆ SELECTIVE activation checkpointing: ಪ್ರತಿ operation ಅನ್ನೂ ಸಮಾನವಾಗಿ ಪರಿಗಣಿಸುವ ಬದಲು, ಒಂದು layer ಒಳಗಿನ ಯಾವ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ operations ಮರುಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕಲು ಅಗ್ಗ ಮತ್ತು ಯಾವುದೂ ದುಬಾರಿ ಎಂದು ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ವಿಶ್ಲೇಷಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ಈ ಆಯ್ದ ವಿಧಾನ ಏಕೆ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ uniform checkpointing ಗಿಂತ ಉತ್ತಮ memory-vs-time tradeoff ಸಾಧಿಸಬಹುದು ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಒಂದು operation ya ಯಾವ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ ಗುಣ ಅದನ್ನೂ recomputation ಗೆ ಒಂದು ಒಳ್ಳೆಯ ಅಭ್ಯರ್ಥಿಯಾಗಿಸುತ್ತದೆ ಎಂದು ವಿವರಿಸಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'intermediate', title: 'Genuinely Implementing Selective Checkpointing Based on Operation Cost Analysis',
      titleKn: 'Operation Cost ವಿಶ್ಲೇಷಣೆ ಆಧಾರಿತ Selective Checkpointing ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "Genuinely implement selective checkpointing on your mini-GPT, targeting specific operation types rather than uniform layer boundaries.\n\n1) Genuinely profile your mini-GPT's transformer block to measure the actual compute time AND actual output-tensor memory size of each distinct operation type within it (e.g. the QKV projection matrix multiplication, the attention-score computation, the attention-output projection, the feed-forward layer's matrix multiplications, and any activation functions/normalization operations) -- genuinely calculate the compute-time-to-memory-size ratio for each operation type, identifying which are the cheapest to recompute relative to their memory footprint.\n\n2) Genuinely implement a selective checkpointing scheme that stores the output of your identified EXPENSIVE-to-recompute operations (e.g. the large matrix multiplications) while discarding-and-recomputing the outputs of your identified CHEAP-to-recompute operations (e.g. activation functions), rather than checkpointing at uniform layer boundaries.\n\n3) Genuinely measure and compare your selective scheme's peak activation memory and total training-step time against your uniform every-K-th-layer scheme from the previous lesson's advanced exercise, using a comparable overall memory budget for both -- report whether selective checkpointing genuinely achieved a better time-for-the-same-memory (or memory-for-the-same-time) tradeoff than uniform checkpointing in your specific implementation.",
      problemKn: "ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ಮೇಲೆ selective checkpointing ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ, uniform layer boundaries ಬದಲಿಗೆ ನಿರ್ದಿಷ್ಟ operation types ಗುರಿಯಾಗಿಸಿ.\n\n1) ನಿಮ್ಮ mini-GPT ya transformer block ಅನ್ನೂ ಪ್ರೊಫೈಲ್ ಮಾಡಿ, ಪ್ರತಿ ವಿಭಿನ್ನ operation type ya ನಿಜ compute time ಮತ್ತು ನಿಜ output-tensor memory size ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n2) ದುಬಾರಿ operations ya output ಇಡುವ ಮತ್ತು ಅಗ್ಗದ operations ಅನ್ನೂ ತಿರಸ್ಕರಿಸಿ-ಮರುಲೆಕ್ಕಹಾಕುವ ಒಂದು selective checkpointing ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ನಿಮ್ಮ selective ಯೋಜನೆಯನ್ನೂ ಹಿಂದಿನ ಪಾಠದ uniform ಯೋಜನೆ ವಿರುದ್ಧ ಹೋಲಿಸಿ ಮತ್ತು ಫಲಿತಾಂಶ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ." },
    { difficulty: 'advanced', title: 'Genuinely Combining Selective Checkpointing with Every Other Memory Technique from This Phase',
      titleKn: 'Selective Checkpointing ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ Phase ya ಪ್ರತಿ ಇತರ Memory Technique ಜೊತೆ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸುವುದೂ',
      problem: "As the final exercise of this phase, genuinely combine gradient checkpointing with the OTHER major memory-saving techniques you've built across this phase's distributed-training lessons (FSDP/ZeRO parameter sharding, and if feasible your mixed-precision/FP8 training from the DeepSeek-V3 module) into one comprehensive memory-optimized training configuration for your mini-GPT, and genuinely measure the combined effect.\n\n1) Genuinely construct a training configuration that simultaneously applies: your selective gradient checkpointing (from the intermediate exercise), FSDP-style parameter/gradient/optimizer-state sharding (from the distributed-training module), and mixed-precision forward computation (from the DeepSeek-V3 module), all active together in a single training loop.\n\n2) Genuinely measure the peak per-device memory footprint of this fully-combined configuration, and separately measure it with each technique REMOVED one at a time (checkpointing alone removed, then FSDP alone removed, then mixed-precision alone removed), to genuinely attribute how much memory savings each individual technique contributed within the combined system.\n\n3) Genuinely report a complete summary table of your measured findings across ALL of Phase 13's memory- and compute-efficiency techniques you've implemented, and give your own honest, evidence-based reflection on which 2-3 techniques provided the most memory savings for the least added complexity/time-cost in your own concrete experiments -- explicitly connecting this final reflection back to the very first lesson of this phase's distributed-training module, discussing how far your own hands-on measurements throughout this entire phase have taken you from a naive single-technique understanding to a genuinely informed, evidence-grounded view of how real frontier-scale models are actually trained and served.",
      problemKn: "ಈ phase ya ಅಂತಿಮ exercise ಆಗಿ, gradient checkpointing ಅನ್ನೂ ಈ phase ya distributed-training ಪಾಠಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ನೀವು ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿದ ಇತರ ಪ್ರಮುಖ memory-saving techniques ಜೊತೆ (FSDP/ZeRO sharding, ಮತ್ತು mixed-precision/FP8 training) ಒಂದು ಸಮಗ್ರ memory-optimized training configuration ಆಗಿ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಸಂಯೋಜಿಸಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ ಪರಿಣಾಮವನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ.\n\n1) selective gradient checkpointing, FSDP-ಶೈಲಿಯ sharding, ಮತ್ತು mixed-precision computation ಎಲ್ಲವನ್ನೂ ಏಕಕಾಲದಲ್ಲಿ ಅನ್ವಯಿಸುವ ಒಂದು training configuration ಅನ್ನೂ ನಿಜವಾಗಿ ನಿರ್ಮಿಸಿ.\n\n2) ಈ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ-ಸಂಯೋಜಿತ configuration ya peak per-device memory footprint ಅನ್ನೂ ಅಳೆಯಿರಿ, ಮತ್ತು ಪ್ರತಿ technique ಅನ್ನೂ ಒಂದೊಂದಾಗಿ ತೆಗೆದು ಅದರ ವೈಯಕ್ತಿಕ ಕೊಡುಗೆಯನ್ನೂ ಆರೋಪಿಸಿ.\n\n3) ಈ ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ phase ya ನೀವು ಜಾರಿಗೊಳಿಸಿದ ಎಲ್ಲಾ memory ಮತ್ತು compute-efficiency techniques ಗಳಾದ್ಯಂತ ಒಂದು ಸಂಪೂರ್ಣ ಸಾರಾಂಶ ಕೋಷ್ಟಕವನ್ನೂ ವರದಿ ಮಾಡಿ ಮತ್ತು ಯಾವ 2-3 techniques ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಕಡಿಮೆ ಸೇರಿಸಿದ ಸಂಕೀರ್ಣತೆಗೆ ಅತ್ಯಂತ ಹೆಚ್ಚು memory savings ನೀಡಿದವು ಎಂದು ನಿಮ್ಮ ಸ್ವಂತ ಪ್ರಾಮಾಣಿಕ, ಪುರಾವೆ-ಆಧಾರಿತ ಪ್ರತಿಬಿಂಬ ನೀಡಿ." },
  ]),
];
